 ''subject'':294,482 ''suit'':49 ''sweet'':127 ''tabl'':539 ''temptat'':299 ''therefor'':310 ''togeth'':138,143,150,195,325 ''treat'':555 ''trial'':298 ''true'':410 ''uncoven'':578 ''union'':476,494 ''unit'':248 ''uniti'':140,163,252,264,327,330 ''upon'':6 ''use'':63 ''utter'':22 ''valid'':508 ''voic'':131 ''walk'':441 ''word'':62,97,156 ''world'':340,357,488 ''worship'':197 ''worthi'':442 ''would'':375,399,412,426 ''yahweh'':107', NULL, '611');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('612', '2', '612', NULL, 'Behold - As if calling attention to the fact that they were there, or had come.Bless ye the Lord - Praise Yahweh. Making known their desire that God should be praised, and calling on those who presided over the public worship of the sanctuary to engage now in that service as expressive of their feelings.All ye servants of the Lord - The priests or ministers of religion, appointed especially to this service.Which by night stand in the house of the Lord - There was a class of singers in the temple who devoted the night, or a part of the night, to praise; and it is possible that this service may have been, as it was subsequently in some of the monasteries, continued by succeeding choirs, during the entire night. Thus in 1 Chronicles 9:33, it is said, "And these are the singers, chief of the fathers of the Levites, who remaining in the chambers were free, for they were employed in that work day and night." This class is particularly addressed in this psalm, as if they were especially favored, or as if they especially possessed the ear of God in the silence of the night, and when the world slumbered around them. There is something favorable to devotion in the silence of the night; when the world sleeps; when we are alone with God; when it seems as if God would more particularly attend to our cry since the rest of the world is still, and does not (as it were) need his care. All this may be fancy; but the effect may be to make the mind more solemn, and better suited for devotion.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':129 ''33'':132 ''9'':131 ''address'':169 ''alon'':221 ''appoint'':65 ''around'':200 ''attend'':233 ''attent'':5 ''behold'':1 ''better'':271 ''call'':4,31 ''care'':253 ''chamber'':152 ''chief'':141 ''choir'':122 ''chronicl'':130 ''class'':82,166 ''come.bless'':15 ''continu'':119 ''cri'':236 ''day'':162 ''desir'':24 ''devot'':89,207,274 ''ear'':186 ''effect'':261 ''employ'':158 ''engag'':44 ''entir'':125 ''especi'':66,177,183 ''express'':50 ''fact'':8 ''fanci'':258 ''father'':144 ''favor'':178,205 ''feelings.all'':53 ''free'':154 ''god'':26,188,223,229 ''hous'':75 ''known'':22 ''levit'':147 ''lord'':18,58,78 ''make'':21,265 ''may'':107,256,262 ''mind'':267 ''minist'':62 ''monasteri'':118 ''need'':251 ''night'':71,91,97,126,164,194,213 ''part'':94 ''particular'':168,232 ''possess'':184 ''possibl'':103 ''prais'':19,29,99 ''presid'':35 ''priest'':60 ''psalm'':172 ''public'':38 ''religion'':64 ''remain'':149 ''rest'':239 ''said'':135 ''sanctuari'':42 ''seem'':226 ''servant'':55 ''servic'':48,106 ''service.which'':69 ''silenc'':191,210 ''sinc'':237 ''singer'':84,140 ''sleep'':217 ''slumber'':199 ''solemn'':269 ''someth'':204 ''stand'':72 ''still'':244 ''subsequ'':113 ''succeed'':121 ''suit'':272 ''templ'':87 ''thus'':127 ''work'':161 ''world'':198,216,242 ''worship'':39 ''would'':230 ''yahweh'':20 ''ye'':16,54', NULL, '612');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('613', '2', '613', NULL, 'Praise ye the Lord - Hebrew, Hallelu-jah. Literally, "Praise Jah," an abridged name for Yahweh. See the notes at Psalm 68:4.Praise ye the name of the Lord - The same as praising God himself.Praise him, O ye servants of the Lord - You who are especially designated or appointed to this service, Psalm 134:1.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':55 ''134'':54 ''4.praise'':23 ''68'':22 ''abridg'':13 ''appoint'':49 ''design'':47 ''especi'':46 ''god'':34 ''hallelu'':7 ''hallelu-jah'':6 ''hebrew'':5 ''himself.praise'':35 ''jah'':8,11 ''liter'':9 ''lord'':4,29,42 ''name'':14,26 ''note'':19 ''o'':37 ''prais'':1,10,33 ''psalm'':21,53 ''see'':17 ''servant'':39 ''servic'':52 ''yahweh'':16 ''ye'':2,24,38', NULL, '613');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('614', '2', '614', NULL, 'To him which smote great kings - On this passage see the notes at Psalm 135:10-12. There is little difference in the two places, except that here the statement is divided by the refrain, "For his mercy endureth forever." The idea in the whole passage, in view of the divine interposition in slaying the mighty kings, and in giving their land for a possession to the Hebrew people, is, that it was a proof of mercy and benevolence. It is benevolence to mankind and to the church of God - it is in the interests of humanity, of domestic peace, and of the charities of life, to remove wicked people from the world. This mercy may be manifested further, not merely in removing the wicked, but in transferring their possessions to those who will make a better use of them. Thus the possessions of these mighty kings, Sihon and Og, were transferred to the people of God, and lands which had been devoted to the service of blood, ambition, crime, pollution, and idolatry, became devoted to the service of religion and righteousness. In like manner, through the removal of a wicked man from the world by death, God may cause his wealth, accumulated by avarice and dishonesty, to be transferred to the hands of children who will make a good use of it - children converted as if in anticipation of this, and with a view to this. Among the highest expressions of mercy to the world may be, therefore, the removal of wicked princes in war - or the removal of wicked people, in other ranks of life, by death in any form.', NULL, NULL, '''-12'':17 ''10'':16 ''135'':15 ''accumul'':203 ''ambit'':169 ''among'':238 ''anticip'':229 ''avaric'':205 ''becam'':174 ''benevol'':79,82 ''better'':137 ''blood'':168 ''caus'':200 ''chariti'':104 ''children'':215,224 ''church'':88 ''convert'':225 ''crime'':170 ''death'':197,269 ''devot'':163,175 ''differ'':21 ''dishonesti'':207 ''divid'':32 ''divin'':51 ''domest'':99 ''endureth'':39 ''except'':26 ''express'':241 ''forev'':40 ''form'':272 ''give'':60 ''god'':90,157,198 ''good'':220 ''great'':5 ''hand'':213 ''hebrew'':68 ''highest'':240 ''human'':97 ''idea'':42 ''idolatri'':173 ''interest'':95 ''interposit'':52 ''king'':6,57,147 ''land'':62,159 ''life'':106,267 ''like'':184 ''littl'':20 ''make'':135,218 ''man'':192 ''manifest'':118 ''mankind'':84 ''manner'':185 ''may'':116,199,247 ''merci'':38,77,115,243 ''mere'':121 ''mighti'':56,146 ''note'':12 ''og'':150 ''passag'':9,46 ''peac'':100 ''peopl'':69,110,155,262 ''place'':25 ''pollut'':171 ''possess'':65,130,143 ''princ'':254 ''proof'':75 ''psalm'':14 ''rank'':265 ''refrain'':35 ''religion'':180 ''remov'':108,123,188,251,259 ''righteous'':182 ''see'':10 ''servic'':166,178 ''sihon'':148 ''slay'':54 ''smote'':4 ''statement'':30 ''therefor'':249 ''thus'':141 ''transfer'':128,152,210 ''two'':24 ''use'':138,221 ''view'':48,235 ''war'':256 ''wealth'':202 ''whole'':45 ''wick'':109,125,191,253,261 ''world'':113,195,246', NULL, '614');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('615', '2', '615', NULL, 'By the rivers of Babylon - The streams, the water-courses, the rivulets. There was properly only one river flowing through Babylon - the Euphrates; but the city was watered, as Damascus now is, by means of canals or water-courses cut from the main river, and conveying the water to different parts of the city. For a description of Babylon, see the introductory notes to Isaiah 13. If the reference here is to Babylon proper, or the city, the allusion would be to the Euphrates flowing through it; if to Babylonia, the allusion would be to the Euphrates, and the other rivers which watered the country, as the Tigris, the Chaboras, and the Ulai. As it is most probable that the captive Hebrews were not scattered through the empire, but were concentrated in one or a few places, it is, perhaps, not improper to understand this of Babylon itself.There we sat down - There we were sitting. Perhaps a little company of friends; perhaps those assembled for worship; perhaps those who happened to come together on some special occasion; or, perhaps, a poetic representation of the general condition of the Hebrew captives, as sitting and meditating on the desolations of their native land.Yea, we wept - We sat there; we meditated; we wept. Our emotions overpowered us, and we poured forth tears. So now, there is a place in Jerusalem, at the southwest corner of the area on which the temple was built, where the Jews resort on set occasions to weep over the ruins of their city and nation.When we remembered Zion - When we thought on our native land; its former glory; the wrongs done to it; the desolations there; when we thought of the temple in ruins, and our homes as devastated; when we thought of the happy days which we had spent there, and when we contrasted them with our condition now.', NULL, NULL, '''13'':68 ''allus'':81,94 ''area'':236 ''assembl'':166 ''babylon'':5,22,61,75,149 ''babylonia'':92 ''built'':242 ''canal'':37 ''captiv'':123,192 ''chabora'':112 ''citi'':27,56,79,257 ''come'':174 ''compani'':161 ''concentr'':133 ''condit'':188,313 ''contrast'':309 ''convey'':48 ''corner'':233 ''countri'':107 ''cours'':11,41 ''cut'':42 ''damascus'':31 ''day'':300 ''descript'':59 ''desol'':199,279 ''devast'':293 ''differ'':52 ''done'':275 ''emot'':214 ''empir'':130 ''euphrat'':24,86,99 ''flow'':20,87 ''former'':271 ''forth'':220 ''friend'':163 ''general'':187 ''glori'':272 ''happen'':172 ''happi'':299 ''hebrew'':124,191 ''home'':291 ''improp'':144 ''introductori'':64 ''isaiah'':67 ''itself.there'':150 ''jerusalem'':229 ''jew'':245 ''land'':269 ''land.yea'':203 ''littl'':160 ''main'':45 ''mean'':35 ''medit'':196,210 ''nation.when'':259 ''nativ'':202,268 ''note'':65 ''occas'':179,249 ''one'':18,135 ''overpow'':215 ''part'':53 ''perhap'':142,158,164,169,181 ''place'':139,227 ''poetic'':183 ''pour'':219 ''probabl'':120 ''proper'':16,76 ''refer'':71 ''rememb'':261 ''represent'':184 ''resort'':246 ''river'':3,19,46,103 ''rivulet'':13 ''ruin'':254,288 ''sat'':152,207 ''scatter'':127 ''see'':62 ''set'':248 ''sit'':157,194 ''southwest'':232 ''special'':178 ''spent'':304 ''stream'':7 ''tear'':221 ''templ'':240,286 ''thought'':265,283,296 ''tigri'':110 ''togeth'':175 ''ulai'':115 ''understand'':146 ''us'':216 ''water'':10,29,40,50,105 ''water-cours'':9,39 ''weep'':251 ''wept'':205,212 ''worship'':168 ''would'':82,95 ''wrong'':274 ''zion'':262', NULL, '615');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('616', '2', '616', NULL, 'I will praise thee with my whole heart - Reserving nothing m my heart to give to idols or to other gods. All that constitutes praise to God as God, he would address to him alone. He would use no language, and cherish no feeling, which implied a belief that there was any other God; he would indulge in no attachment which would be inconsistent with supreme attachment to God, or which would tend to draw away his affections from him. See the notes at Psalm 9:1.Before the gods will I sing praise unto thee - The idols; all idols; in preference to them all. This does not mean that he would do this in the presence of other gods; but that Yahweh should be acknowledged to be God in preference to any or all of them.', NULL, NULL, '''1.before'':87 ''9'':86 ''acknowledg'':125 ''address'':32 ''affect'':78 ''alon'':35 ''attach'':60,67 ''away'':76 ''belief'':48 ''cherish'':42 ''constitut'':24 ''draw'':75 ''feel'':44 ''give'':15 ''god'':21,27,29,54,69,89,119,128 ''heart'':8,13 ''idol'':17,97,99 ''impli'':46 ''inconsist'':64 ''indulg'':57 ''languag'':40 ''m'':11 ''mean'':108 ''note'':83 ''noth'':10 ''prais'':3,25,93 ''prefer'':101,130 ''presenc'':116 ''psalm'':85 ''reserv'':9 ''see'':81 ''sing'':92 ''suprem'':66 ''tend'':73 ''thee'':4,95 ''unto'':94 ''use'':38 ''whole'':7 ''would'':31,37,56,62,72,111 ''yahweh'':122', NULL, '616');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('668', '2', '668', NULL, 'A good man''s trust in God is set forth as a counterpoise to our Ignorance of the ways of Providence.In the hand of God - Under His special protection (Deuteronomy 33:3 ff) as righteous, and under His direction Proverbs 21:1 as people.No man ... - literally, both love and also hatred man knoweth not: all are before them. Love and hatred here mean the ordinary outward tokens of God''s favor or displeasure, i. e., prosperity and adversity. "Man knoweth not" probably means: "man knows not whether to expect prosperity or adversity from God; all his earthly future is in obscurity."', NULL, NULL, '''1'':42 ''21'':41 ''3'':32 ''33'':31 ''advers'':78,92 ''also'':50 ''counterpois'':13 ''deuteronomi'':30 ''direct'':39 ''displeasur'':73 ''e'':75 ''earth'':97 ''expect'':89 ''favor'':71 ''ff'':33 ''forth'':10 ''futur'':98 ''god'':7,25,69,94 ''good'':2 ''hand'':23 ''hatr'':51,61 ''ignor'':16 ''know'':85 ''knoweth'':53,80 ''liter'':46 ''love'':48,59 ''man'':3,45,52,79,84 ''mean'':63,83 ''obscur'':101 ''ordinari'':65 ''outward'':66 ''people.no'':44 ''probabl'':82 ''prosper'':76,90 ''protect'':29 ''proverb'':40 ''providence.in'':21 ''righteous'':35 ''set'':9 ''special'':28 ''token'':67 ''trust'':5 ''way'':19 ''whether'':87', NULL, '668');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('669', '2', '669', NULL, 'This verse is by its meaning so closely connected with Ecclesiastes 9:18 that the selection of it for the beginning of a new chapter seems unfortunate.Apothecary - Rather: a dealer in spices and perfumes (compare Exodus 30:25). The swarms of flies in the East very soon corrupt and destroy any moist unguent or mixture left uncovered, and pollute a dish of food in a few minutes.So doth ... - literally, more weighty than wisdom, than honor, is a little folly.', NULL, NULL, '''18'':13 ''25'':38 ''30'':37 ''9'':12 ''begin'':21 ''chapter'':25 ''close'':8 ''compar'':35 ''connect'':9 ''corrupt'':48 ''dealer'':30 ''destroy'':50 ''dish'':61 ''doth'':68 ''east'':45 ''ecclesiast'':11 ''exodus'':36 ''fli'':42 ''folli'':79 ''food'':63 ''honor'':75 ''left'':56 ''liter'':69 ''littl'':78 ''mean'':6 ''minutes.so'':67 ''mixtur'':55 ''moist'':52 ''new'':24 ''perfum'':34 ''pollut'':59 ''rather'':28 ''seem'':26 ''select'':16 ''soon'':47 ''spice'':32 ''swarm'':40 ''uncov'':57 ''unfortunate.apothecary'':27 ''unguent'':53 ''vers'':2 ''weighti'':71 ''wisdom'':73', NULL, '669');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('617', '2', '617', NULL, 'O Lord, thou hast searched me - The word rendered searched, has a primary reference to searching the earth by boring or digging, as for water or metals. See Job 28:3. Then it means to search accurately or closely.And known me - As the result of that search, or that close investigation. Thou seest all that is in my heart. Nothing is, or can be, concealed from thee. It is with this deep consciousness that the psalm begins; and all that follows is but an expansion and application of this idea. It is of much advantage in suggesting right reflections on our own character, to have this full consciousness that God knows us altogether; that he sees all that there is in our heart; that he has been fully acquainted with our past life.', NULL, NULL, '''28'':30 ''3'':31 ''accur'':37 ''acquaint'':129 ''advantag'':95 ''altogeth'':113 ''applic'':87 ''begin'':77 ''bore'':20 ''charact'':103 ''close'':50 ''closely.and'':39 ''conceal'':65 ''conscious'':73,108 ''deep'':72 ''dig'':22 ''earth'':18 ''expans'':85 ''follow'':81 ''full'':107 ''fulli'':128 ''god'':110 ''hast'':4 ''heart'':59,123 ''idea'':90 ''investig'':51 ''job'':29 ''know'':111 ''known'':40 ''life'':133 ''lord'':2 ''mean'':34 ''metal'':27 ''much'':94 ''noth'':60 ''o'':1 ''past'':132 ''primari'':13 ''psalm'':76 ''refer'':14 ''reflect'':99 ''render'':9 ''result'':44 ''right'':98 ''search'':5,10,16,36,47 ''see'':28,116 ''seest'':53 ''suggest'':97 ''thee'':67 ''thou'':3,52 ''us'':112 ''water'':25 ''word'':8', NULL, '617');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('670', '2', '670', NULL, 'The verse means: "Show hospitality, even though the corresponding return of hospitality to you may seem improbable; nevertheless, be hospitable in faith." Compare Luke 14:13-14; Hebrews 13:2. Some interpreters (not unreasonably) understand by "bread" the seed from the produce of which bread is made. Seed cast upon the fertile soil flooded by the early rains would be returned to the sower in autumn with large increase.', NULL, NULL, '''-14'':27 ''13'':26,29 ''14'':25 ''2'':30 ''autumn'':66 ''bread'':37,45 ''cast'':49 ''compar'':23 ''correspond'':9 ''earli'':57 ''even'':6 ''faith'':22 ''fertil'':52 ''flood'':54 ''hebrew'':28 ''hospit'':5,12,20 ''improb'':17 ''increas'':69 ''interpret'':32 ''larg'':68 ''luke'':24 ''made'':47 ''may'':15 ''mean'':3 ''nevertheless'':18 ''produc'':42 ''rain'':58 ''return'':10,61 ''seed'':39,48 ''seem'':16 ''show'':4 ''soil'':53 ''sower'':64 ''though'':7 ''understand'':35 ''unreason'':34 ''upon'':50 ''vers'':2 ''would'':59', NULL, '670');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('618', '2', '618', NULL, 'Deliver me, O Lord, from the evil man - That is, evidently from some particular man who was endeavoring to injure him; some personal enemy. All the circumstances mentioned agree well with the supposition that Saul is intended.Preserve me from the violent man - Margin, as in Hebrew, "man of violences." That is, one who has committed violence so often, who has so frequently done wrong, that this may be considered a characteristic of the man. This would apply well to the repeated acts of Saul in persecuting David, and endeavoring to do him injury.', NULL, NULL, '''act'':82 ''agre'':29 ''appli'':77 ''characterist'':71 ''circumst'':27 ''commit'':55 ''consid'':69 ''david'':87 ''deliv'':1 ''done'':63 ''endeavor'':18,89 ''enemi'':24 ''evid'':11 ''evil'':7 ''frequent'':62 ''hebrew'':46 ''injur'':20 ''injuri'':93 ''intended.preserve'':37 ''lord'':4 ''man'':8,15,42,47,74 ''margin'':43 ''may'':67 ''mention'':28 ''o'':3 ''often'':58 ''one'':52 ''particular'':14 ''persecut'':86 ''person'':23 ''repeat'':81 ''saul'':35,84 ''supposit'':33 ''violenc'':49,56 ''violent'':41 ''well'':30,78 ''would'':76 ''wrong'':64', NULL, '618');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('619', '2', '619', NULL, 'Lord, I cry unto thee - In view of my perils; in view of the suggestions of my friends; in view of my temptation to do a wrong thing at their advice, and with the prospect of the advantage which it might seem to be to me.Make haste unto me - To save me from all this danger: the danger from my enemies; the danger from the counsels of my friends. See the notes at Psalm 22:19; compare Psalm 40:13; Psalm 70:1, Psalm 70:5; Psalm 71:12. The meaning is, that there is need of immediate interposition. There is danger that I shall be overcome; that I may be tempted to do a wrong thing; that I may be ruined if there is any delay.Give ear unto my voice ... - See the notes at Psalm 5:1.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':83,138 ''12'':89 ''13'':80 ''19'':76 ''22'':75 ''40'':79 ''5'':86,137 ''70'':82,85 ''71'':88 ''advantag'':38 ''advic'':31 ''compar'':77 ''counsel'':66 ''cri'':3 ''danger'':56,58,63,102 ''delay.give'':127 ''ear'':128 ''enemi'':61 ''friend'':18,69 ''hast'':47 ''immedi'':98 ''interposit'':99 ''lord'':1 ''may'':110,120 ''me.make'':46 ''mean'':91 ''might'':41 ''need'':96 ''note'':72,134 ''overcom'':107 ''peril'':10 ''prospect'':35 ''psalm'':74,78,81,84,87,136 ''ruin'':122 ''save'':51 ''see'':70,132 ''seem'':42 ''shall'':105 ''suggest'':15 ''tempt'':112 ''temptat'':23 ''thee'':5 ''thing'':28,117 ''unto'':4,48,129 ''view'':7,12,20 ''voic'':131 ''wrong'':27,116', NULL, '619');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('620', '2', '620', NULL, 'I cried unto the Lord with my voice - See the notes at Psalm 3:4, where the language is the same. He uttered a loud and audible prayer, though he was alone. It was not a mental ejaculation, but he gave expression to his desires.With my voice unto the Lord did I make my supplication - See Psalm 30:8. The Hebrew word rendered "did make my supplication," means to implore favor or mercy. It denotes the language of petition and entreaty, not the language of claim.', NULL, NULL, '''3'':14 ''30'':58 ''4'':15 ''8'':59 ''alon'':32 ''audibl'':27 ''claim'':86 ''cri'':2 ''denot'':75 ''desires.with'':45 ''ejacul'':38 ''entreati'':81 ''express'':42 ''favor'':71 ''gave'':41 ''hebrew'':61 ''implor'':70 ''languag'':18,77,84 ''lord'':5,50 ''loud'':25 ''make'':53,65 ''mean'':68 ''mental'':37 ''merci'':73 ''note'':11 ''petit'':79 ''prayer'':28 ''psalm'':13,57 ''render'':63 ''see'':9,56 ''supplic'':55,67 ''though'':29 ''unto'':3,48 ''utter'':23 ''voic'':8,47 ''word'':62', NULL, '620');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('671', '2', '671', NULL, 'Remember now - Rather, And remember. The connection between this verse and the preceding one is unfortunately interrupted by our division of chapters.Creator - Gratitude to God as Creator is here inculcated, as just previously Ecclesiastes 11:9 fear of God as Judge. Godliness, acquired as a habit in youth, is recommended as the proper compensation for that natural cessation of youthful happiness which makes the days of old age more or less evil; more evil in proportion since there is less of godliness in the heart, and less evil where there is more godliness.While the evil days come not - Rather, before the evil days come.', NULL, NULL, '''11'':35 ''9'':36 ''acquir'':43 ''age'':68 ''cessat'':58 ''chapters.creator'':22 ''come'':97,104 ''compens'':54 ''connect'':7 ''creator'':27 ''day'':65,96,103 ''divis'':20 ''ecclesiast'':34 ''evil'':72,74,88,95,102 ''fear'':37 ''god'':25,39 ''godli'':42,82 ''godliness.while'':93 ''gratitud'':23 ''habit'':46 ''happi'':61 ''heart'':85 ''inculc'':30 ''interrupt'':17 ''judg'':41 ''less'':71,80,87 ''make'':63 ''natur'':57 ''old'':67 ''one'':14 ''preced'':13 ''previous'':33 ''proper'':53 ''proport'':76 ''rather'':3,99 ''recommend'':50 ''rememb'':1,5 ''sinc'':77 ''unfortun'':16 ''vers'':10 ''youth'':48,60', NULL, '671');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('672', '2', '672', NULL, 'The "Song of songs," i. e., the best or most excellent of songs.Which is Solomon''s - literally, "to" or "for Solomon," i. e., belonging to Solomon as its author or concerning him as its subject. In a title or inscription, the former interpretation is to be preferred.', NULL, NULL, '''author'':29 ''belong'':24 ''best'':8 ''concern'':31 ''e'':6,23 ''excel'':11 ''former'':42 ''inscript'':40 ''interpret'':43 ''liter'':17 ''prefer'':47 ''solomon'':15,21,26 ''song'':2,4 ''songs.which'':13 ''subject'':35 ''titl'':38', NULL, '672');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('673', '2', '673', NULL, 'The division of the chapters is unfortunate; Cant. 2 ought to have begun at Sol 1:15, or Cant. 1 to have been continued to Sol 2:7. The bride replies, "And I am like a lovely wild flower springing at the root of the stately forest-trees." The majority of Christian fathers assigned this verse to the King (Christ). Hebrew commentators generally assign it to the bride. It is quite uncertain what flower is meant by the word rendered (here and Isaiah 35:1) "rose." The etymology is in favor of its being a bulbous plant (the white narcissus, Conder). "Sharon" is usually the proper name of the celebrated plain from Joppa to Caesarea, between the hill-country and the sea, and travelers have remarked the abundance of flowers with which this plain is still carpeted in spring. But in the time of Eusebius and Jerome there was a smaller plain of Sharon (Saron) situated between Mount Tabor and the sea of Tiberias, which would be very near the bride''s native home if that were Shunem.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':16,20,86 ''15'':17 ''2'':9,27 ''35'':85 ''7'':28 ''abund'':130 ''assign'':55,65 ''begun'':13 ''bride'':30,69,173 ''bulbous'':97 ''caesarea'':116 ''cant'':8,19 ''carpet'':139 ''celebr'':111 ''chapter'':5 ''christ'':61 ''christian'':53 ''comment'':63 ''conder'':102 ''continu'':24 ''countri'':121 ''divis'':2 ''etymolog'':89 ''eusebius'':147 ''father'':54 ''favor'':92 ''flower'':39,75,132 ''forest'':48 ''forest-tre'':47 ''general'':64 ''hebrew'':62 ''hill'':120 ''hill-countri'':119 ''home'':176 ''isaiah'':84 ''jerom'':149 ''joppa'':114 ''king'':60 ''like'':35 ''love'':37 ''major'':51 ''meant'':77 ''mount'':160 ''name'':108 ''narcissus'':101 ''nativ'':175 ''near'':171 ''ought'':10 ''plain'':112,136,154 ''plant'':98 ''proper'':107 ''quit'':72 ''remark'':128 ''render'':81 ''repli'':31 ''root'':43 ''rose'':87 ''saron'':157 ''sea'':124,164 ''sharon'':103,156 ''shunem'':180 ''situat'':158 ''smaller'':153 ''sol'':15,26 ''spring'':40,141 ''state'':46 ''still'':138 ''tabor'':161 ''tiberia'':166 ''time'':145 ''travel'':126 ''tree'':49 ''uncertain'':73 ''unfortun'':7 ''usual'':105 ''vers'':57 ''white'':100 ''wild'':38 ''word'':80 ''would'':168', NULL, '673');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('674', '2', '674', NULL, 'I held him - This begins the fourth stanza. The bride''s mother is mentioned again in Sol 6:9; Sol 8:2.', NULL, NULL, '''2'':22 ''6'':18 ''8'':21 ''9'':19 ''begin'':5 ''bride'':10 ''fourth'':7 ''held'':2 ''mention'':14 ''mother'':12 ''sol'':17,20 ''stanza'':8', NULL, '674');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1129', '2', '1129', NULL, 'The sea of Tiberias - Called also the Sea of Galilee, being situated in Galilee. See the notes at Matthew 4:18. In this place Jesus had promised to meet them, Mark 14:28; Mark 16:7; Matthew 26:32; Matthew 28:10. This interview of Jesus is but just mentioned by Matthew Mat 28:16, and is omitted by both Mark and Luke. This is the reason why John relates so particularly what occurred there. Galilee was a retired place where they would be free from danger, and was therefore a safe and convenient situation for Jesus to meet them, in order to give them his last instructions.On this wise - Thus. In this manner.', NULL, NULL, '''10'':42 ''14'':32 ''16'':35,55 ''18'':21 ''26'':38 ''28'':33,41,54 ''32'':39 ''4'':20 ''7'':36 ''also'':6 ''call'':5 ''conveni'':94 ''danger'':87 ''free'':85 ''galile'':10,14,76 ''give'':104 ''instructions.on'':108 ''interview'':44 ''jesus'':25,46,97 ''john'':69 ''last'':107 ''luke'':63 ''manner'':114 ''mark'':31,34,61 ''mat'':53 ''matthew'':19,37,40,52 ''meet'':29,99 ''mention'':50 ''note'':17 ''occur'':74 ''omit'':58 ''order'':102 ''particular'':72 ''place'':24,80 ''promis'':27 ''reason'':67 ''relat'':70 ''retir'':79 ''safe'':92 ''sea'':2,8 ''see'':15 ''situat'':12,95 ''therefor'':90 ''thus'':111 ''tiberia'':4 ''wise'':110 ''would'':83', NULL, '1018');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1132', '2', '1132', NULL, 'Peter and John went up ... - In Luke 24:53, it is said that the apostles were continually in the temple, praising and blessing God. From Acts 2:46, it is clear that all the disciples were accustomed daily to resort to the temple for devotion. Whether they joined in the sacrifices of the temple-service is not said; but the thing is not improbable. This was the place and the manner in which they and their fathers had worshipped. They came slowly to the conclusion that they were to leave the temple, and they would naturally resort there with their countrymen to worship the God of their fathers. In the previous chapter Acts 2:43 we are told in general that many wonders and signs were done by the hands of the apostles. From the many miracles which were performed, Luke selects one of which he gives a more full account, and especially as it gives him occasion to record another of the addresses of Peter to the Jews. An impostor would have been satisfied with the general statement that many miracles were performed. The sacred writers descend to particulars, and tell us where, and in relation to whom, they were performed. This is a proof that they were honest people, and did not intend to deceive.Into the temple - Not into the edifice properly called the temple, but into the court of the temple, where prayer was accustomed to be made. See the notes on Matthew 21:12.At the hour of prayer ... - The Jewish day was divided into twelve equal parts; of course, the ninth hour would be about three o''clock p. m. This was the hour of evening prayer. Morning prayer was offered at nine o''clock. Compare Psalm 55:17; Daniel 6:10.', NULL, NULL, '''10'':297 ''12.at'':249 ''17'':294 ''2'':27,115 ''21'':248 ''24'':8 ''43'':116 ''46'':28 ''53'':9 ''55'':293 ''6'':296 ''account'':152 ''accustom'':37,239 ''act'':26,114 ''address'':165 ''anoth'':162 ''apostl'':15,134 ''bless'':23 ''call'':226 ''came'':82 ''chapter'':113 ''clear'':31 ''clock'':273,290 ''compar'':291 ''conclus'':86 ''continu'':17 ''countrymen'':102 ''cours'':264 ''court'':232 ''daili'':38 ''daniel'':295 ''day'':256 ''deceive.into'':218 ''descend'':189 ''devot'':45 ''discipl'':35 ''divid'':258 ''done'':128 ''edific'':224 ''equal'':261 ''especi'':154 ''even'':281 ''father'':78,109 ''full'':151 ''general'':121,179 ''give'':148,157 ''god'':24,106 ''hand'':131 ''honest'':211 ''hour'':251,267,279 ''impostor'':172 ''improb'':65 ''intend'':216 ''jew'':170 ''jewish'':255 ''john'':3 ''join'':48 ''leav'':91 ''luke'':7,142 ''m'':275 ''made'':242 ''mani'':123,137,182 ''manner'':72 ''matthew'':247 ''miracl'':138,183 ''morn'':283 ''natur'':97 ''nine'':288 ''ninth'':266 ''note'':245 ''o'':272,289 ''occas'':159 ''offer'':286 ''one'':144 ''p'':274 ''part'':262 ''particular'':191 ''peopl'':212 ''perform'':141,185,203 ''peter'':1,167 ''place'':69 ''prais'':21 ''prayer'':237,253,282,284 ''previous'':112 ''proof'':207 ''proper'':225 ''psalm'':292 ''record'':161 ''relat'':198 ''resort'':40,98 ''sacr'':187 ''sacrific'':51 ''said'':12,59 ''satisfi'':176 ''see'':243 ''select'':143 ''servic'':56 ''sign'':126 ''slowli'':83 ''statement'':180 ''tell'':193 ''templ'':20,43,55,93,220,228,235 ''temple-servic'':54 ''thing'':62 ''three'':271 ''told'':119 ''twelv'':260 ''us'':194 ''went'':4 ''whether'':46 ''wonder'':124 ''worship'':80,104 ''would'':96,173,268 ''writer'':188', NULL, '1021');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1140', '2', '1140', NULL, 'And the apostles and brethren - The Christians who Were in Judea.Heard ... - So extraordinary an occurrence as that at Caesarea, the descent of the Holy Spirit on the Gentiles, and their reception into the church, would excite attention, and be likely to produce much sensitiveness in regard to the conduct of Peter and those with him. It was so contrary to all the ideas of the Jews, that it is not to be wondered at that it led to contention.', NULL, NULL, '''apostl'':3 ''attent'':37 ''brethren'':5 ''caesarea'':19 ''christian'':7 ''church'':34 ''conduct'':49 ''content'':79 ''contrari'':59 ''descent'':21 ''excit'':36 ''extraordinari'':13 ''gentil'':28 ''holi'':24 ''idea'':63 ''jew'':66 ''judea.heard'':11 ''led'':77 ''like'':40 ''much'':43 ''occurr'':15 ''peter'':51 ''produc'':42 ''recept'':31 ''regard'':46 ''sensit'':44 ''spirit'':25 ''wonder'':73 ''would'':35', NULL, '1029');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('675', '2', '675', NULL, 'Orchard - This is the renderlng here and in Ecclesiastes 2:5 of "pardes" (see Nehemiah 2:8 note). The pomegranate was for the Jews a sacred fruit, and a characteristic product of the land of promise (compare Exodus 28:33-34; Numbers 20:5; Deuteronomy 8:8; 1 Kings 7:18, 1 Kings 7:20). It is frequently mentioned in the Song, and always in connection with the bride. It abounds to this day in the ravines of the Lebanon.Camphire - Cyprus. See Sol 1:14 note.Songs 4:13-15Seven kinds of spices (some of them with Indian names, e. g. aloes, spikenard, saffron) are enumerated as found in this symbolic garden. They are for the most part pure exotics which have formed for countless ages articles of commerce in the East, and were brought at that time in Solomon''s ships from southern Arabia, the great Indian Peninsula, and perhaps the islands of the Indian Archipelago. The picture here is best regarded as a purely ideal one, having no corresponding reality but in the bride herself. The beauties and attractions of both north and south - of Lebanon with its streams of sparkling water and fresh mountain air, of Engedi with its tropical climate and henna plantations, of the spice-groves of Arabia Felix, and of the rarest products of the distant mysterious Ophir - all combine to furnish one glorious representation, "Thou art all fair!"', NULL, NULL, '''-15'':89 ''-34'':41 ''1'':48,52,84 ''13'':88 ''14'':85 ''18'':51 ''2'':10,16 ''20'':43,55 ''28'':39 ''33'':40 ''4'':87 ''5'':11,44 ''7'':50,54 ''8'':17,46,47 ''abound'':71 ''age'':126 ''air'':198 ''alo'':102 ''alway'':64 ''arabia'':145,214 ''archipelago'':157 ''art'':234 ''articl'':127 ''attract'':181 ''beauti'':179 ''best'':162 ''bride'':69,176 ''brought'':135 ''characterist'':30 ''climat'':204 ''combin'':227 ''commerc'':129 ''compar'':37 ''connect'':66 ''correspond'':171 ''countless'':125 ''cyprus'':81 ''day'':74 ''deuteronomi'':45 ''distant'':223 ''e'':100 ''east'':132 ''ecclesiast'':9 ''engedi'':200 ''enumer'':106 ''exodus'':38 ''exot'':120 ''fair'':236 ''felix'':215 ''form'':123 ''found'':108 ''frequent'':58 ''fresh'':196 ''fruit'':27 ''furnish'':229 ''g'':101 ''garden'':112 ''glorious'':231 ''great'':147 ''grove'':212 ''henna'':206 ''ideal'':167 ''indian'':98,148,156 ''island'':153 ''jew'':24 ''kind'':91 ''king'':49,53 ''land'':34 ''lebanon'':188 ''lebanon.camphire'':80 ''mention'':59 ''mountain'':197 ''mysteri'':224 ''name'':99 ''nehemiah'':15 ''north'':184 ''note'':18 ''note.songs'':86 ''number'':42 ''one'':168,230 ''ophir'':225 ''orchard'':1 ''pard'':13 ''part'':118 ''peninsula'':149 ''perhap'':151 ''pictur'':159 ''plantat'':207 ''pomegran'':20 ''product'':31,220 ''promis'':36 ''pure'':119,166 ''rarest'':219 ''ravin'':77 ''realiti'':172 ''regard'':163 ''renderlng'':5 ''represent'':232 ''sacr'':26 ''saffron'':104 ''see'':14,82 ''seven'':90 ''ship'':142 ''sol'':83 ''solomon'':140 ''song'':62 ''south'':186 ''southern'':144 ''sparkl'':193 ''spice'':93,211 ''spice-grov'':210 ''spikenard'':103 ''stream'':191 ''symbol'':111 ''thou'':233 ''time'':138 ''tropic'':203 ''water'':194', NULL, '675');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('699', '2', '699', NULL, 'In the year that Tartan came unto Ashdod - Tartan was one of the generals of Sennacherib. Ashdod, called by the Greeks Azotus, was a seaport on the Mediterranean, between Askelon and Ekron, and not far from Gaza (Reland''s "Palestine," iii.) It was one of the five cities of the Philistines, assigned to the tribe of Judah, but never conquered by them Joshua 13:8; Joshua 15:46-47. The temple of Dagon stood here; and here the ark of God was brought after the fatal battle of Eben-ezer (1 Samuel 5:1, following.) It sustained many sieges, and was regarded as an important place in respect to Palestine, and also to Egypt. It was taken by Tartan, and remained in the possession of the Assyrians until it was besieged by Psammetichus, the Egyptian king, who took it after a siege of twenty-nine years (Herod. ii. 157). It was about thirty miles from Gaza. It is now a small village, and is called "Esdud." It was besieged and taken by Tartan as preparatory to the conquest of Egypt; and if the king who is here called "Sargon" was Sennacherib, it probable that it was taken before he threatened Jerusalem.Sargon the king of Assyria - Who this "Sargon" was is not certainly known. Some have supposed that it was Sennacherib; others that it was Shalmaneser the father of Sennacherib, and others that it was Esar-haddon the successor of Sennacherib - (Michaelis). Rosenmuller and Gesenius suppose that it was a king who reigned "between" Sbalmaneser and Sennacherib. Tartan is known to have been a general of Sennacherib 2 Kings 18:17, and it is natural to suppose that he is here intended. Jerome says that Senacherib had seven names, and Kimchi says that he had eight; and it is not improbable that "Sargon" was one of those names. Oriental princes often had several names; and hence, the difficulty of identifying them. See Vitringa on this place.', NULL, NULL, '''-47'':69 ''1'':92,95 ''13'':64 ''15'':67 ''157'':151 ''17'':273 ''18'':272 ''2'':270 ''46'':68 ''5'':94 ''8'':65 ''also'':113 ''ark'':79 ''ashdod'':8,17 ''askelon'':30 ''assign'':52 ''assyria'':207 ''assyrian'':128 ''azotus'':22 ''battl'':87 ''besieg'':132,171 ''brought'':83 ''call'':18,167,190 ''came'':6 ''certain'':214 ''citi'':48 ''conquer'':60 ''conquest'':180 ''dagon'':73 ''difficulti'':320 ''eben'':90 ''eben-ez'':89 ''egypt'':115,182 ''egyptian'':136 ''eight'':298 ''ekron'':32 ''esar'':238 ''esar-haddon'':237 ''esdud'':168 ''ezer'':91 ''far'':35 ''fatal'':86 ''father'':229 ''five'':47 ''follow'':96 ''gaza'':37,158 ''general'':14,267 ''gesenius'':247 ''god'':81 ''greek'':21 ''haddon'':239 ''henc'':318 ''herod'':149 ''identifi'':322 ''ii'':150 ''iii'':41 ''import'':106 ''improb'':303 ''intend'':284 ''jerom'':285 ''jerusalem.sargon'':203 ''joshua'':63,66 ''judah'':57 ''kimchi'':293 ''king'':137,186,205,253,271 ''known'':215,262 ''mani'':99 ''mediterranean'':28 ''micha'':244 ''mile'':156 ''name'':291,310,316 ''natur'':277 ''never'':59 ''nine'':147 ''often'':313 ''one'':11,44,307 ''orient'':311 ''other'':223,233 ''palestin'':40,111 ''philistin'':51 ''place'':107,328 ''possess'':125 ''preparatori'':177 ''princ'':312 ''probabl'':195 ''psammetichus'':134 ''regard'':103 ''reign'':255 ''reland'':38 ''remain'':122 ''respect'':109 ''rosenmul'':245 ''samuel'':93 ''sargon'':191,210,305 ''say'':286,294 ''sbalmanes'':257 ''seaport'':25 ''see'':324 ''senacherib'':288 ''sennacherib'':16,193,222,231,243,259,269 ''seven'':290 ''sever'':315 ''shalmanes'':227 ''sieg'':100,143 ''small'':163 ''stood'':74 ''successor'':241 ''suppos'':218,248,279 ''sustain'':98 ''taken'':118,173,199 ''tartan'':5,9,120,175,260 ''templ'':71 ''thirti'':155 ''threaten'':202 ''took'':139 ''tribe'':55 ''twenti'':146 ''twenty-nin'':145 ''unto'':7 ''villag'':164 ''vitringa'':325 ''year'':3,148', NULL, '699');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('701', '2', '701', NULL, 'The burden - (see the note at Isaiah 13:1). "The valley"   gay''. Septuagint, &#x3a6;&#x3b1;&#x3c1;&#x3b1;&#x3b3;&#x3b3;&#x3bf;&#x3c2;  Pharangos - ''Valley.'' Chaldee, ''The burden of the prophecy respecting the city which dwells (that is, is built) in the valley, which the prophets have prophesied concerning it.'' There can be no doubt that Jerusalem is intended (see Isaiah 22:9-10). It is not usual to call it "a valley," but it may be so called, either(1) because there were several valleys "within" the city and adjacent to it, as the vale between mount Zion and Moriah; the vale between mount Moriah and mount Ophel; between these and mount Bezetha; and the valley of Jehoshaphat, without the walls of the city; or(2) more probably it was called "a valley" in reference to its being "encompassed with hills," rising to a considerable elevation above the city.Thus mount Olivet was on the east, and overlooked the city. Jerusalem is also called a "valley," and a "plain," in Jeremiah 21:13 : ''Behold, I am against thee, O inhabitant of the valley, and rock of the plain, saith the Lord.'' Thus it is described in Reland''s "Palestine:" - ''The city was in the mountain region of Judea, in an elevated place, yet so that in respect to the mountains by which it was surrounded, it seemed to be situated in a humble place, because mount Olivet, and other mountains surrounding it, were more elevated.'' So Phocas says, ''The holy city is placed in the midst of various valleys and hills, and this is wonderful (&#x398;&#x3b1;&#x3c5;&#x3bc;&#x3b1;&#x3c3;&#x3c4;&#x3bf;&#x3bd;  Thaumaston) in it, that at the same time the city seems to be elevated and depressed, for it is elevated in respect to the region of Judea, and depressed in respect to the hills around it.'' (Reland''s "Palestine," iii. 802, in Ugolini''s "Thesaurus," vi.) It was common with Isaiah and the other prophets to designate Jerusalem and other places, not by their proper names, but by some appellation that would be descriptive (see Isaiah 21:1; Isaiah 29:1).Of vision - (see the note at Isaiah 1:1). The word here means that Jerusalem was eminently the place where God made known his will to the prophets, and manifested himself to his people by "visions."What aileth thee now? - What is the cause of the commotion and tumult that exists in the city? The prophets throws himself at once into the midst of the excitement; sees the agitation and tumult, and the preparations for defense which were made, and asks the "cause" of all this confusion.That thou art wholly gone up to the house-tops - That all classes of the people had fled to the house-tops, so much that it might be said that all the city had gone up. Houses in the East were built in a uniform manner in ancient times, and are so to this day. (See a description of the mode of building in the notes at Matthew 9:1 ff.) The roofs were always flat, and were made either of earth that was trodden hard, or with large flat stones. This roof was surrounded with a balustrade Deuteronomy 22:8, and furnished a convenient place for walking, or even for eating and sleeping. Whenever, therefore, anything was to be seen in the street, or at a distance; or when there was any cause of alarm, they would naturally resort to the roof of the house. When there was a tower in the city, the inhabitants fled to that, and took refuge on its top (see Judges 9:50-53). The image here is, therefore, one of consternation and alarm, as if on the sudden approach of an enemy.', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':54 ''-53'':594 ''1'':9,71,333,336,344,345,494 ''13'':8,163 ''2'':117 ''21'':162,332 ''22'':52,524 ''29'':335 ''50'':593 ''8'':525 ''802'':296 ''9'':53,493,592 ''adjac'':81 ''agit'':405 ''aileth'':374 ''alarm'':560,604 ''also'':153 ''alway'':499 ''ancient'':472 ''anyth'':541 ''appel'':325 ''approach'':610 ''around'':290 ''art'':425 ''ask'':417 ''balustrad'':522 ''behold'':164 ''bezetha'':104 ''build'':487 ''built'':30,466 ''burden'':2,18 ''call'':60,69,122,154 ''caus'':380,419,558 ''chalde'':16 ''citi'':24,79,115,150,191,241,265,390,457,578 ''city.thus'':140 ''class'':436 ''common'':304 ''commot'':383 ''concern'':39 ''confusion.that'':423 ''consider'':136 ''constern'':602 ''conveni'':529 ''day'':479 ''defens'':412 ''depress'':271,284 ''describ'':185 ''descript'':329,482 ''design'':312 ''deuteronomi'':523 ''distanc'':552 ''doubt'':45 ''dwell'':26 ''earth'':506 ''east'':146,464 ''eat'':536 ''either'':70,504 ''elev'':137,201,235,269,275 ''emin'':353 ''encompass'':130 ''enemi'':613 ''even'':534 ''excit'':402 ''exist'':387 ''ff'':495 ''flat'':500,514 ''fled'':441,581 ''furnish'':527 ''gay'':12 ''god'':357 ''gone'':427,459 ''hard'':510 ''hill'':132,251,289 ''holi'':240 ''hous'':432,445,461,570 ''house-top'':431,444 ''humbl'':223 ''iii'':295 ''imag'':596 ''inhabit'':170,580 ''intend'':49 ''isaiah'':7,51,306,331,334,343 ''jehoshaphat'':109 ''jeremiah'':161 ''jerusalem'':47,151,313,351 ''judea'':198,282 ''judg'':591 ''known'':359 ''larg'':513 ''lord'':181 ''made'':358,415,503 ''manifest'':366 ''manner'':470 ''matthew'':492 ''may'':66 ''mean'':349 ''midst'':246,399 ''might'':451 ''mode'':485 ''moriah'':91,96 ''mount'':88,95,98,103,141,226 ''mountain'':195,210,230 ''much'':448 ''name'':321 ''natur'':563 ''note'':5,341,490 ''o'':169 ''olivet'':142,227 ''one'':600 ''ophel'':99 ''overlook'':148 ''palestin'':189,294 ''peopl'':370,439 ''pharango'':14 ''phoca'':237 ''place'':202,224,243,316,355,530 ''plain'':159,178 ''prepar'':410 ''probabl'':119 ''proper'':320 ''propheci'':21 ''prophesi'':38 ''prophet'':36,310,364,392 ''refer'':126 ''refug'':586 ''region'':196,280 ''reland'':187,292 ''resort'':564 ''respect'':22,207,277,286 ''rise'':133 ''rock'':175 ''roof'':497,517,567 ''said'':453 ''saith'':179 ''say'':238 ''see'':3,50,330,339,403,480,590 ''seem'':217,266 ''seen'':545 ''septuagint'':13 ''sev