INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('309', '2', '309', NULL, 'The third year - i. e., in the third year of his sojourn with the widow. The whole period of drought was three years and a half Luke 4:25; James 5:17 : of this, probably about one year was passed by Elijah in the torrent-course of Cherith, and two years and a half at Sarepta.', NULL, NULL, '''17'':32 ''25'':29 ''4'':28 ''5'':31 ''cherith'':49 ''cours'':47 ''drought'':20 ''e'':5 ''elijah'':42 ''half'':26,55 ''jame'':30 ''luke'':27 ''one'':37 ''pass'':40 ''period'':18 ''probabl'':35 ''sarepta'':57 ''sojourn'':12 ''third'':2,8 ''three'':22 ''torrent'':46 ''torrent-cours'':45 ''two'':51 ''whole'':17 ''widow'':15 ''year'':3,9,23,38,52', NULL, '309');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('562', '2', '562', NULL, 'How amiable - How much to he loved; how lovely. The word amiable is now used to denote a quality of mind or disposition - as gentle, affectionate, kind. The word used here, however in the original, means rather dear, beloved - as a token of endearment. Compare the notes at the title to Psalm 45. The idea here is, that the place of public worship is dear to the heart, as a beloved freind - a child - a wife - is. There is a strong and tender love for it.Are thy tabernacles - Thy dwelling-places. This word might be applied either to the tabernacle or the temple, or to any place where God was supposed to reside, or where his worship was celebrated. The plural form is used here probably because the tabernacle and the temple were divided into two parts or rooms, and each might be regarded as in a proper sense the dwelling-place of God. See the notes at Matthew 21:12, following.O Lord of hosts! - Yahweh of hosts; Yahweh, controlling - ruling - guiding - marshalling - all the armies of heaven and earth: compare the notes at Isaiah 1:9; notes at Psalm 24:10.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':188 ''10'':194 ''12'':163 ''21'':162 ''24'':193 ''45'':53 ''9'':189 ''affection'':26 ''amiabl'':2,12 ''appli'':97 ''armi'':178 ''belov'':39,71 ''celebr'':120 ''child'':74 ''compar'':45,183 ''control'':172 ''dear'':38,65 ''denot'':17 ''disposit'':23 ''divid'':135 ''dwell'':91,153 ''dwelling-plac'':90,152 ''earth'':182 ''either'':98 ''endear'':44 ''following.o'':164 ''form'':123 ''freind'':72 ''gentl'':25 ''god'':110,156 ''guid'':174 ''heart'':68 ''heaven'':180 ''host'':167,170 ''howev'':32 ''idea'':55 ''isaiah'':187 ''it.are'':86 ''kind'':27 ''lord'':165 ''love'':7,9,84 ''marshal'':175 ''matthew'':161 ''mean'':36 ''might'':95,143 ''mind'':21 ''much'':4 ''note'':47,159,185,190 ''origin'':35 ''part'':138 ''place'':60,92,108,154 ''plural'':122 ''probabl'':127 ''proper'':149 ''psalm'':52,192 ''public'':62 ''qualiti'':19 ''rather'':37 ''regard'':145 ''resid'':114 ''room'':140 ''rule'':173 ''see'':157 ''sens'':150 ''strong'':81 ''suppos'':112 ''tabernacl'':88,101,130 ''templ'':104,133 ''tender'':83 ''thi'':87,89 ''titl'':50 ''token'':42 ''two'':137 ''use'':15,30,125 ''wife'':76 ''word'':11,29,94 ''worship'':63,118 ''yahweh'':168,171', NULL, '562');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('310', '2', '310', NULL, 'The prophet had not long to wait before learning the intentions of the queen. A priest''s daughter herself, she would avenge the slaughtered priests; a king''s wife and a king''s child, she would not quail before a subject. That very night a messenger declared her determination to compass the prophet''s death within the space of a day.So let the gods ... - A common oath about this time (marginal references). The Greek Version prefixes to this another clause, which makes the oath even more forcible, "As surely as thou art Elijah and I am Jezebel, so let the gods," etc.', NULL, NULL, '''anoth'':79 ''art'':92 ''aveng'':22 ''child'':34 ''claus'':80 ''common'':66 ''compass'':51 ''daughter'':18 ''day.so'':61 ''death'':55 ''declar'':47 ''determin'':49 ''elijah'':93 ''etc'':102 ''even'':85 ''forcibl'':87 ''god'':64,101 ''greek'':74 ''intent'':11 ''jezebel'':97 ''king'':27,32 ''learn'':9 ''let'':62,99 ''long'':5 ''make'':82 ''margin'':71 ''messeng'':46 ''night'':44 ''oath'':67,84 ''prefix'':76 ''priest'':16,25 ''prophet'':2,53 ''quail'':38 ''queen'':14 ''refer'':72 ''slaughter'':24 ''space'':58 ''subject'':41 ''sure'':89 ''thou'':91 ''time'':70 ''version'':75 ''wait'':7 ''wife'':29 ''within'':56 ''would'':21,36', NULL, '310');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('311', '2', '311', NULL, 'Ben-hadad, the king of Syria - Probably the son of the Ben-hadad who assisted Asa against Baasha (1 Kings 15:18 note).Thirty and two kings with him - Not allies, but feudatories 1 Kings 20:24. Damascus had in the reign of this Ben-hadad become the center of an important monarchy, which may not improbably have extended from the Euphrates to the northern border of Israel. The Assyrian inscriptions show that this country was about the period in question parcelled out into a multitude of petty kingdoms, the chief tribes who possessed it being the Hittites, the Hamathites, and the Syrians of Damascus.Horses and chariots - The Assyrian inscriptions show us how very important an arm of the service the chariot force was reckoned by the Syrians. A king, who has been identified with this Ben-hadad, brought into the field against Assyria nearly four thousand chariots.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':21,36 ''15'':23 ''18'':24 ''20'':38 ''24'':39 ''alli'':33 ''arm'':120 ''asa'':18 ''assist'':17 ''assyria'':148 ''assyrian'':73,112 ''baasha'':20 ''becom'':50 ''ben'':2,14,48,141 ''ben-hadad'':1,13,47,140 ''border'':69 ''brought'':143 ''center'':52 ''chariot'':110,125,152 ''chief'':94 ''countri'':78 ''damascus'':40 ''damascus.horses'':108 ''euphrat'':65 ''extend'':62 ''feudatori'':35 ''field'':146 ''forc'':126 ''four'':150 ''hadad'':3,15,49,142 ''hamathit'':103 ''hittit'':101 ''identifi'':137 ''import'':55,118 ''improb'':60 ''inscript'':74,113 ''israel'':71 ''king'':5,22,29,37,133 ''kingdom'':92 ''may'':58 ''monarchi'':56 ''multitud'':89 ''near'':149 ''northern'':68 ''note'':25 ''parcel'':85 ''period'':82 ''petti'':91 ''possess'':97 ''probabl'':8 ''question'':84 ''reckon'':128 ''reign'':44 ''servic'':123 ''show'':75,114 ''son'':10 ''syria'':7 ''syrian'':106,131 ''thirti'':26 ''thousand'':151 ''tribe'':95 ''two'':28 ''us'':115', NULL, '311');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('312', '2', '312', NULL, 'A vineyard ... in Jezreel - The name Jezreel is applied in Scripture, not merely to the town 1 Kings 18:46, but also to the valley or plain which lies below it, between Mount Gilboa and Little Hermon (2 Samuel 2:9; 2 Kings 9:10; Hosea 1:5; etc.).The palace of Ahab at Jezreel was on the eastern side of the city, looking toward the Jordan down the valley above described. It abutted on the town wall 2 Kings 9:30-31. Immediately below it was a dry moat. Beyond, in the valley, either adjoining the moat, or at any rate at no great distance, was the plot of ground belonging to Naboth 2 Kings 9:21.', NULL, NULL, '''-31'':83 ''1'':17,47 ''10'':45 ''18'':19 ''2'':38,40,42,79,115 ''21'':118 ''30'':82 ''46'':20 ''5'':48 ''9'':41,44,81,117 ''abut'':74 ''adjoin'':96 ''ahab'':53 ''also'':22 ''appli'':9 ''belong'':112 ''beyond'':91 ''citi'':63 ''describ'':72 ''distanc'':106 ''dri'':89 ''eastern'':59 ''either'':95 ''etc'':49 ''gilboa'':34 ''great'':105 ''ground'':111 ''hermon'':37 ''hosea'':46 ''immedi'':84 ''jezreel'':4,7,55 ''jordan'':67 ''king'':18,43,80,116 ''lie'':29 ''littl'':36 ''look'':64 ''mere'':13 ''moat'':90,98 ''mount'':33 ''naboth'':114 ''name'':6 ''palac'':51 ''plain'':27 ''plot'':109 ''rate'':102 ''samuel'':39 ''scriptur'':11 ''side'':60 ''toward'':65 ''town'':16,77 ''valley'':25,70,94 ''vineyard'':2 ''wall'':78', NULL, '312');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('313', '2', '313', NULL, 'Three years - These must be counted from the close of the second campaign of Ben-hadad 1 Kings 20:34. They were not full years, as is evident from the next verse. Probably the first year is that of Ben-hadad''s dismissal after his defeat; the second is a year of actual peace; while the third is that in which Jehoshaphat paid his visit, and the Ramoth-Gilead expedition took place. The pause, here noticed, in the war between Israel and Syria was perhaps the result of a common danger. It was probably in the year following Ben-hadad''s dismissal by Ahab, that the first great Assyrian expedition took place into these parts. Shalmaneser II relates that on his first invasion of southern Syria, he was met by the combined forces of Ben-hadad, Ahab, the king of Hamath, the kings of the Hittites, and others, who gave him battle, but suffered a defeat.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':18 ''20'':20 ''34'':21 ''actual'':55 ''ahab'':108,142 ''assyrian'':113 ''battl'':157 ''ben'':16,42,103,140 ''ben-hadad'':15,41,102,139 ''campaign'':13 ''close'':9 ''combin'':136 ''common'':93 ''count'':6 ''danger'':94 ''defeat'':48,161 ''dismiss'':45,106 ''evid'':29 ''expedit'':73,114 ''first'':36,111,126 ''follow'':101 ''forc'':137 ''full'':25 ''gave'':155 ''gilead'':72 ''great'':112 ''hadad'':17,43,104,141 ''hamath'':146 ''hittit'':151 ''ii'':121 ''invas'':127 ''israel'':84 ''jehoshaphat'':64 ''king'':19,144,148 ''met'':133 ''must'':4 ''next'':32 ''notic'':79 ''other'':153 ''paid'':65 ''part'':119 ''paus'':77 ''peac'':56 ''perhap'':88 ''place'':75,116 ''probabl'':34,97 ''ramoth'':71 ''ramoth-gilead'':70 ''relat'':122 ''result'':90 ''second'':12,50 ''shalmanes'':120 ''southern'':129 ''suffer'':159 ''syria'':86,130 ''third'':59 ''three'':1 ''took'':74,115 ''vers'':33 ''visit'':67 ''war'':82 ''year'':2,26,37,53,100', NULL, '313');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('314', '2', '314', NULL, 'The Moabites, who had once lorded over Israel Judges 3:12-14, were reduced to subjection by David, and treated with extreme severity (marginal reference). In the time of Ahab they were dependent on the kingdom of Israel, to which it has been generally supposed that they fell at the separation of Israel from Judah. The Moabite monument (see 2 Kings 3:4), discovered in 1869, has now given reason to believe that they then recovered their independence, but were again reduced by Omri, who, with his son Ahab, is said (in round numbers) to have "oppressed" them for "forty years." Ahab''s death was seized upon as an occasion for revolt, and Moab (perhaps owing to Ahaziah''s sickness) easily regained her independence.', NULL, NULL, '''-14'':12 ''12'':11 ''1869'':66 ''2'':60 ''3'':10,62 ''4'':63 ''ahab'':30,89,102 ''ahaziah'':118 ''believ'':72 ''david'':18 ''death'':104 ''depend'':33 ''discov'':64 ''easili'':121 ''extrem'':22 ''fell'':48 ''forti'':100 ''general'':44 ''given'':69 ''independ'':78,124 ''israel'':8,38,53 ''judah'':55 ''judg'':9 ''king'':61 ''kingdom'':36 ''lord'':6 ''margin'':24 ''moab'':114 ''moabit'':2,57 ''monument'':58 ''number'':94 ''occas'':110 ''omri'':84 ''oppress'':97 ''owe'':116 ''perhap'':115 ''reason'':70 ''recov'':76 ''reduc'':14,82 ''refer'':25 ''regain'':122 ''revolt'':112 ''round'':93 ''said'':91 ''see'':59 ''seiz'':106 ''separ'':51 ''sever'':23 ''sick'':120 ''son'':88 ''subject'':16 ''suppos'':45 ''time'':28 ''treat'':20 ''upon'':107 ''year'':101', NULL, '314');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('315', '2', '315', NULL, 'The events of this chapter are related out of their chronological order. Elijah''s translation did not take place until after the accession of Jehoram in Judah 2 Chronicles 21:12, which was not until the fifth year of Jehoram of Israel 2 Kings 8:16. The writer of Kings, having concluded his notices of the ministry of Elijah in chapter 1, and being about to pass in 2 Kings 3 to the ministry of Elisha, thought it best to insert at this point the final scene of Elijah''s life, though it did not occur until several years later.Gilgal - The modern Jiljilieh, on the highland between Nablous and Beitin (Bethel), about eight and a half miles from the latter, is now commonly supposed to be the Gilgal here mentioned. Some regard it as the ordinary residence of Elisha 2 Kings 4:38.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':62 ''12'':31 ''16'':46 ''2'':28,43,69,140 ''21'':30 ''3'':71 ''38'':143 ''4'':142 ''8'':45 ''access'':23 ''beitin'':110 ''best'':79 ''bethel'':111 ''chapter'':5,61 ''chronicl'':29 ''chronolog'':11 ''common'':123 ''conclud'':52 ''eight'':113 ''elijah'':13,59,89 ''elisha'':76,139 ''event'':2 ''fifth'':37 ''final'':86 ''gilgal'':128 ''half'':116 ''highland'':106 ''insert'':81 ''israel'':42 ''jehoram'':25,40 ''jiljilieh'':103 ''judah'':27 ''king'':44,50,70,141 ''later.gilgal'':100 ''latter'':120 ''life'':91 ''mention'':130 ''mile'':117 ''ministri'':57,74 ''modern'':102 ''nablous'':108 ''notic'':54 ''occur'':96 ''order'':12 ''ordinari'':136 ''pass'':67 ''place'':19 ''point'':84 ''regard'':132 ''relat'':7 ''resid'':137 ''scene'':87 ''sever'':98 ''suppos'':124 ''take'':18 ''though'':92 ''thought'':77 ''translat'':15 ''writer'':48 ''year'':38,99', NULL, '315');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('316', '2', '316', NULL, 'In the eighteenth year of Jehoshaphat - This date agrees exactly with the statements that Jehoshaphat began to reign in the fourth year of Ahab 1 Kings 22:41, and Ahaziah in the 17th year of Jehoshaphat 1 Kings 22:51.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':25,37 ''17th'':33 ''22'':27,39 ''41'':28 ''51'':40 ''agre'':9 ''ahab'':24 ''ahaziah'':30 ''began'':16 ''date'':8 ''eighteenth'':3 ''exact'':10 ''fourth'':21 ''jehoshaphat'':6,15,36 ''king'':26,38 ''reign'':18 ''statement'':13 ''year'':4,22,34', NULL, '316');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('317', '2', '317', NULL, 'The creditor is come ... - The Law of Moses, like the Athenian and the Roman law, recognized servitude for debt, and allowed that pledging of the debtor''s person, which, in a rude state of society, is regarded as the safest and the most natural security (see the marginal reference). In the present case it would seem that, so long as the debtor lived, the creditor had not enforced his right over his sons, but now on his death he claimed their services, to which he was by law entitled.', NULL, NULL, '''allow'':21 ''athenian'':11 ''case'':53 ''claim'':80 ''come'':4 ''creditor'':2,65 ''death'':78 ''debt'':19 ''debtor'':26,62 ''enforc'':68 ''entitl'':89 ''law'':6,15,88 ''like'':9 ''live'':63 ''long'':59 ''margin'':48 ''mose'':8 ''natur'':44 ''person'':28 ''pledg'':23 ''present'':52 ''recogn'':16 ''refer'':49 ''regard'':37 ''right'':70 ''roman'':14 ''rude'':32 ''safest'':40 ''secur'':45 ''see'':46 ''seem'':56 ''servic'':82 ''servitud'':17 ''societi'':35 ''son'':73 ''state'':33 ''would'':55', NULL, '317');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('318', '2', '318', NULL, 'By him the Lord had given deliverance unto Syria - An Assyrian monarch had pushed his conquests as far as Syria exactly at this period, bringing into subjection all the kings of these parts. But Syria revolted after a few years and once more made herself independent. It was probably in this war of independence that Naaman had distinguished himself.But he was a leper - leprosy admitted of various kinds and degrees Leviticus 13; 14 Some of the lighter forms would not incapacitate a man from discharging the duties of a courtier and warrior.', NULL, NULL, '''13'':72 ''14'':73 ''admit'':65 ''assyrian'':11 ''bring'':25 ''conquest'':16 ''courtier'':90 ''degre'':70 ''deliver'':7 ''discharg'':85 ''distinguish'':58 ''duti'':87 ''exact'':21 ''far'':18 ''form'':78 ''given'':6 ''himself.but'':59 ''incapacit'':81 ''independ'':46,54 ''kind'':68 ''king'':30 ''leper'':63 ''leprosi'':64 ''leviticus'':71 ''lighter'':77 ''lord'':4 ''made'':44 ''man'':83 ''monarch'':12 ''naaman'':56 ''part'':33 ''period'':24 ''probabl'':49 ''push'':14 ''revolt'':36 ''subject'':27 ''syria'':9,20,35 ''unto'':8 ''various'':67 ''war'':52 ''warrior'':92 ''would'':79 ''year'':40', NULL, '318');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('319', '2', '319', NULL, 'The writer returns here to the series of miracles which Elisha performed for the benefit of the prophetical schools under his care. The connection, in this point of view, is with 2 Kings 4:44.The place where we dwell with thee - literally, "the place where we sit before thee," i. e. "the place where we assemble and sit to bear thy teaching." Elisha visited the sons of the prophets in circuit, staying a short time at each place where a "school" was established. Perhaps he was now visiting Jericho. Compare 2 Kings 2:5.', NULL, NULL, '''2'':32,91,93 ''4'':34 ''44.the'':35 ''5'':94 ''assembl'':56 ''bear'':60 ''benefit'':15 ''care'':22 ''circuit'':71 ''compar'':90 ''connect'':24 ''dwell'':39 ''e'':51 ''elisha'':11,63 ''establish'':83 ''jericho'':89 ''king'':33,92 ''liter'':42 ''miracl'':9 ''perform'':12 ''perhap'':84 ''place'':36,44,53,78 ''point'':27 ''prophet'':18,69 ''return'':3 ''school'':19,81 ''seri'':7 ''short'':74 ''sit'':47,58 ''son'':66 ''stay'':72 ''teach'':62 ''thee'':41,49 ''thi'':61 ''time'':75 ''view'':29 ''visit'':64,88 ''writer'':2', NULL, '319');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('320', '2', '320', NULL, 'The division between the chapters is most awkward here. Elisha, in this verse, replies to the king''s challenge in 2 Kings 6:33 - that his God, Yahweh, will give deliverance in the space of a day. On the morrow, by the same time in the day, the famine will have ceased, and food will be even cheaper than usual.A measure of fine flour - literally, "a seah of fine flour;" about a peck and a half.For a shekel - About 2 shillings 8 12 d.Two measures of burley - Or, "two seahs of barley;" about three pecks.In the gate - The "gates," or "gateways," of Eastern towns are favorite places for the despatch of various kinds of business. It would seem that at Samaria one of the gates was used for the grain market.', NULL, NULL, '''12'':83 ''2'':21,80 ''33'':24 ''6'':23 ''8'':82 ''awkward'':8 ''barley'':92 ''burley'':87 ''busi'':115 ''ceas'':52 ''challeng'':19 ''chapter'':5 ''cheaper'':58 ''d.two'':84 ''day'':37,47 ''deliver'':31 ''despatch'':110 ''divis'':2 ''eastern'':103 ''elisha'':10 ''even'':57 ''famin'':49 ''favorit'':106 ''fine'':63,69 ''flour'':64,70 ''food'':54 ''gate'':97,99,125 ''gateway'':101 ''give'':30 ''god'':27 ''grain'':130 ''half.for'':76 ''kind'':113 ''king'':17,22 ''liter'':65 ''market'':131 ''measur'':61,85 ''morrow'':40 ''one'':122 ''peck'':73 ''pecks.in'':95 ''place'':107 ''repli'':14 ''samaria'':121 ''seah'':67,90 ''seem'':118 ''shekel'':78 ''shill'':81 ''space'':34 ''three'':94 ''time'':44 ''town'':104 ''two'':89 ''use'':127 ''usual.a'':60 ''various'':112 ''vers'':13 ''would'':117 ''yahweh'':28', NULL, '320');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('321', '2', '321', NULL, 'The famine here recorded, and the conversation of the monarch with Gehazi, must have been anterior to the events related in 2 Kings 5 since we may be sure that a king of Israel would not have entered into familiar conversation with a confirmed leper. The writer of Kings probably col ected the miracles of Elisha from various sources, and did not always arrange them chronologically. Here the link of connection is to be found in the nature of the miracle. As Elisha on one occasion prophesied plenty, so on another he had prophesied a famine.Called for a famine - A frequent expression (compare the marginal references). God''s "calling for" anything is the same as His producing it (see Ezekiel 36:29; Romans 4:17).', NULL, NULL, '''17'':125 ''2'':22 ''29'':122 ''36'':121 ''4'':124 ''5'':24 ''alway'':63 ''anoth'':91 ''anterior'':16 ''anyth'':111 ''arrang'':64 ''call'':109 ''chronolog'':66 ''col'':51 ''compar'':103 ''confirm'':44 ''connect'':71 ''convers'':7,41 ''ect'':52 ''elisha'':56,83 ''enter'':38 ''event'':19 ''express'':102 ''ezekiel'':120 ''familiar'':40 ''famin'':2,99 ''famine.called'':96 ''found'':75 ''frequent'':101 ''gehazi'':12 ''god'':107 ''israel'':34 ''king'':23,32,49 ''leper'':45 ''link'':69 ''margin'':105 ''may'':27 ''miracl'':54,81 ''monarch'':10 ''must'':13 ''natur'':78 ''occas'':86 ''one'':85 ''plenti'':88 ''probabl'':50 ''produc'':117 ''prophesi'':87,94 ''record'':4 ''refer'':106 ''relat'':20 ''roman'':123 ''see'':119 ''sinc'':25 ''sourc'':59 ''sure'':29 ''various'':58 ''would'':35 ''writer'':47', NULL, '321');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('322', '2', '322', NULL, 'Box - Rather, "flask," or "vial" 1 Samuel 10:1. Oil and ointment were commonly kept in open-mouthed jars, vases, or bottles made of glass, alabaster, or earthen-ware. Many such vessels have been found both in Egypt and Assyria. The "oil" was the holy oil, compounded after the receipt given in Exodus Exo 30:23-25.', NULL, NULL, '''-25'':59 ''1'':6,9 ''10'':8 ''23'':58 ''30'':57 ''alabast'':27 ''assyria'':42 ''bottl'':23 ''box'':1 ''common'':14 ''compound'':49 ''earthen'':30 ''earthen-war'':29 ''egypt'':40 ''exo'':56 ''exodus'':55 ''flask'':3 ''found'':37 ''given'':53 ''glass'':26 ''holi'':47 ''jar'':20 ''kept'':15 ''made'':24 ''mani'':32 ''mouth'':19 ''oil'':10,44,48 ''ointment'':12 ''open'':18 ''open-mouth'':17 ''rather'':2 ''receipt'':52 ''samuel'':7 ''vase'':21 ''vessel'':34 ''vial'':5 ''ware'':31', NULL, '322');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('323', '2', '323', NULL, 'Seventy sons - i. e., descendants; there were included among them children of Jehoram (2 Kings 10:2-3, etc.).', NULL, NULL, '''-3'':18 ''10'':16 ''2'':14,17 ''among'':9 ''children'':11 ''descend'':5 ''e'':4 ''etc'':19 ''includ'':8 ''jehoram'':13 ''king'':15 ''seventi'':1 ''son'':2', NULL, '323');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('324', '2', '324', NULL, 'Athaliah, as wife of Joram and mother of Ahaziah, had guided both the internal and the external policy of the Jewish kingdom; she had procured the establishmeut of the worship of Baal in Judaea 2 Kings 8:18, 2 Kings 8:27, and had maintained a close alliance with the sister kingdom 2 Kings 8:29; 2 Kings 10:13. The revolution effected by Jehu touched her nearly. It struck away from her the support of her relatives; it isolated her religious system, severing the communication with Phoenicia; and the death of Ahaziah deprived her of her legal status in Judaea, which was that of queen-mother (the 1 Kings 15:13 note), and trausferred that position to the chief wife of her deceased son. Athaliah, instead of yielding to the storm, or merely standing on the defensive, resolved to become the assailant, and strike before any plans could be formed against her. In the absence of her son, hers was probably the chief anthority at Jerusalem. She used it to command the immediate destruction of all the family of David, already thinned by previous massacres 2 Kings 10:14; 2 Chronicles 21:4, 2 Chronicles 21:17, and then seized the throne.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':110 ''10'':59,190 ''13'':60,113 ''14'':191 ''15'':112 ''17'':199 ''18'':38 ''2'':35,39,53,57,188,192,196 ''21'':194,198 ''27'':42 ''29'':56 ''4'':195 ''8'':37,41,55 ''absenc'':157 ''ahaziah'':9,93 ''allianc'':48 ''alreadi'':183 ''anthor'':166 ''assail'':144 ''athaliah'':1,127 ''away'':71 ''baal'':32 ''becom'':142 ''chief'':121,165 ''chronicl'':193,197 ''close'':47 ''command'':173 ''communic'':86 ''could'':150 ''david'':182 ''death'':91 ''deceas'':125 ''defens'':139 ''depriv'':94 ''destruct'':176 ''effect'':63 ''establishmeut'':27 ''extern'':17 ''famili'':180 ''form'':152 ''guid'':11 ''immedi'':175 ''instead'':128 ''intern'':14 ''isol'':80 ''jehu'':65 ''jerusalem'':168 ''jewish'':21 ''joram'':5 ''judaea'':34,101 ''king'':36,40,54,58,111,189 ''kingdom'':22,52 ''legal'':98 ''maintain'':45 ''massacr'':187 ''mere'':135 ''mother'':7,108 ''near'':68 ''note'':114 ''phoenicia'':88 ''plan'':149 ''polici'':18 ''posit'':118 ''previous'':186 ''probabl'':163 ''procur'':25 ''queen'':107 ''queen-moth'':106 ''relat'':78 ''religi'':82 ''resolv'':140 ''revolut'':62 ''seiz'':202 ''sever'':84 ''sister'':51 ''son'':126,160 ''stand'':136 ''status'':99 ''storm'':133 ''strike'':146 ''struck'':70 ''support'':75 ''system'':83 ''thin'':184 ''throne'':204 ''touch'':66 ''trausfer'':116 ''use'':170 ''wife'':3,122 ''worship'':30 ''yield'':130', NULL, '324');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('325', '2', '325', NULL, 'All his days ... - i. e., so long as Jehoiada was his adviser" (compare 2 Chronicles 24:15-22). Jehoida was, practically speaking, regent during the minority of Jehoash, i. e., 10 or 12 years. An increase of power to the priestly order was the natural consequence. Jehoiada bore the title of "high priest" 2 Kings 12:10, which had been dropped since the time of Eleazar Joshua 20:6, and the Levitical order from this time became more mixed up with public affairs and possessed greater influence than previously. Jehoiada''s successors traced their office to him rather than to Aaron Jeremiah 29:26.', NULL, NULL, '''-22'':18 ''10'':31,57 ''12'':33,56 ''15'':17 ''2'':14,54 ''20'':68 ''24'':16 ''26'':104 ''29'':103 ''6'':69 ''aaron'':101 ''advis'':12 ''affair'':83 ''becam'':77 ''bore'':48 ''chronicl'':15 ''compar'':13 ''consequ'':46 ''day'':3 ''drop'':61 ''e'':5,30 ''eleazar'':66 ''greater'':86 ''high'':52 ''increas'':36 ''influenc'':87 ''jehoash'':28 ''jehoiada'':9,47,90 ''jehoida'':19 ''jeremiah'':102 ''joshua'':67 ''king'':55 ''levit'':72 ''long'':7 ''minor'':26 ''mix'':79 ''natur'':45 ''offic'':95 ''order'':42,73 ''possess'':85 ''power'':38 ''practic'':21 ''previous'':89 ''priest'':41,53 ''public'':82 ''rather'':98 ''regent'':23 ''sinc'':62 ''speak'':22 ''successor'':92 ''time'':64,76 ''titl'':50 ''trace'':93 ''year'':34', NULL, '325');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('326', '2', '326', NULL, 'In this chapter the history of the kingdom of Israel is traced through the two reigns of Jehoahaz and Jehoash. In 2 Kings 14 the history of Judah is resumed.In the three and twentieth year - Rather, the "one and twentieth year." See 2 Kings 13:10.', NULL, NULL, '''10'':46 ''13'':45 ''14'':24 ''2'':22,43 ''chapter'':3 ''histori'':5,26 ''israel'':10 ''jehoahaz'':18 ''jehoash'':20 ''judah'':28 ''king'':23,44 ''kingdom'':8 ''one'':38 ''rather'':36 ''reign'':16 ''resumed.in'':30 ''see'':42 ''three'':32 ''trace'':12 ''twentieth'':34,40 ''two'':15 ''year'':35,41', NULL, '326');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('327', '2', '327', NULL, 'The history of Judah is resumed 2 Kings 14:1-22, followed by a brief account of the contemporary history of Israel under Jeroboam II 2 Kings 14:23-29. The earlier narrative runs parallel with 2 Chronicles 25.', NULL, NULL, '''-22'':11 ''-29'':30 ''1'':10 ''14'':9,28 ''2'':7,26,37 ''23'':29 ''25'':39 ''account'':16 ''brief'':15 ''chronicl'':38 ''contemporari'':19 ''earlier'':32 ''follow'':12 ''histori'':2,20 ''ii'':25 ''israel'':22 ''jeroboam'':24 ''judah'':4 ''king'':8,27 ''narrat'':33 ''parallel'':35 ''resum'':6 ''run'':34', NULL, '327');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('328', '2', '328', NULL, 'The Lord smote the king, so that he was a leper - The circumstances under which this terrible affliction befel one of the greatest of the Jewish kings, are given at some length by the author of Chronicles (marginal reference), who supplies us with a tolerably full account of this important reign, which the writer of Kings dismisses in half-a-dozen verses.A several house - "A house of liberation," or, freedom. On the necessity, under which the Law placed lepers, of living apart from other men, see marginal reference Jotham became regent in his father''s room, and exercised the functions of judge (1 Kings 3:9 note), from the time that his father became a leper.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':104 ''3'':106 ''9'':107 ''account'':47 ''afflict'':18 ''apart'':83 ''author'':35 ''becam'':91,115 ''befel'':19 ''chronicl'':37 ''circumst'':13 ''dismiss'':57 ''dozen'':62 ''exercis'':99 ''father'':95,114 ''freedom'':71 ''full'':46 ''function'':101 ''given'':29 ''greatest'':23 ''half'':60 ''half-a-dozen'':59 ''hous'':65,67 ''import'':50 ''jewish'':26 ''jotham'':90 ''judg'':103 ''king'':5,27,56,105 ''law'':78 ''length'':32 ''leper'':11,80,117 ''liber'':69 ''live'':82 ''lord'':2 ''margin'':38,88 ''men'':86 ''necess'':74 ''note'':108 ''one'':20 ''place'':79 ''refer'':39,89 ''regent'':92 ''reign'':51 ''room'':97 ''see'':87 ''sever'':64 ''smote'':3 ''suppli'':41 ''terribl'':17 ''time'':111 ''toler'':45 ''us'':42 ''verses.a'':63 ''writer'':54', NULL, '328');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('329', '2', '329', NULL, 'Ahaz was the worst of all the kings of Judah. He imitated the worst of the Israelite kings - Ahab and Ahaziah - by a re-introduction of the Baal worship, which had been rooted out of Israel by Jehu and out of Judah by Jehoiada.And made Iris son to pass through the fire - i. e. Ahaz adopted the Moloch worship of the Ammonites and Moabites 2 Kings 3:27; Micah 6:7, and sacrificed at least one son, probably his firstborn, according to the horrid rites of those nations, and the Canaanite tribes Deuteronomy 12:31; Psalm 106:37-38. Hereto, apparently, the Jews had been guiltless of this abomination. They had been warned against it by Moses (marginal reference; Deuteronomy 18:10); and if (as some think) they had practiced it in the wilderness Ezekiel 20:26; Amos 5:26, the sin must have been rare and exceptional; from the date of their entrance into the promised land they had wholly put it away. Now, however, it became so frequent (compare 2 Kings 17:17; 2 Kings 21:6) as to meet with the strongest protest from Jeremiah and Ezekiel (Jeremiah 7:31-32; Jeremiah 19:2-6; Jeremiah 32:35; Ezekiel 16:20; Ezekiel 20:26; Ezekiel 23:37, etc.).', NULL, NULL, '''-32'':195 ''-38'':100 ''-6'':199 ''10'':123 ''106'':98 ''12'':95 ''16'':204 ''17'':175,176 ''18'':122 ''19'':197 ''2'':66,173,177,198 ''20'':137,205,207 ''21'':179 ''23'':210 ''26'':138,141,208 ''27'':69 ''3'':68 ''31'':96,194 ''32'':201 ''35'':202 ''37'':99,211 ''5'':140 ''6'':71,180 ''7'':72,193 ''abomin'':110 ''accord'':82 ''adopt'':57 ''ahab'':19 ''ahaz'':1,56 ''ahaziah'':21 ''ammonit'':63 ''amo'':139 ''appar'':102 ''away'':165 ''baal'':29 ''becam'':169 ''canaanit'':92 ''compar'':172 ''date'':152 ''deuteronomi'':94,121 ''e'':55 ''entranc'':155 ''etc'':212 ''except'':149 ''ezekiel'':136,191,203,206,209 ''fire'':53 ''firstborn'':81 ''frequent'':171 ''guiltless'':107 ''hereto'':101 ''horrid'':85 ''howev'':167 ''imit'':12 ''introduct'':26 ''iri'':47 ''israel'':37 ''israelit'':17 ''jehoiada.and'':45 ''jehu'':39 ''jeremiah'':189,192,196,200 ''jew'':104 ''judah'':10,43 ''king'':8,18,67,174,178 ''land'':159 ''least'':76 ''made'':46 ''margin'':119 ''meet'':183 ''micah'':70 ''moabit'':65 ''moloch'':59 ''mose'':118 ''must'':144 ''nation'':89 ''one'':77 ''pass'':50 ''practic'':131 ''probabl'':79 ''promis'':158 ''protest'':187 ''psalm'':97 ''put'':163 ''rare'':147 ''re'':25 ''re-introduct'':24 ''refer'':120 ''rite'':86 ''root'':34 ''sacrif'':74 ''sin'':143 ''son'':48,78 ''strongest'':186 ''think'':128 ''tribe'':93 ''warn'':114 ''wholli'':162 ''wilder'':135 ''worship'':30,60 ''worst'':4,14', NULL, '329');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('330', '2', '330', NULL, 'In the twelfth year - Compare 2 Kings 15:30 note. The history of the kingdom of Israel is in this chapter brought to a close.', NULL, NULL, '''15'':8 ''2'':6 ''30'':9 ''brought'':22 ''chapter'':21 ''close'':25 ''compar'':5 ''histori'':12 ''israel'':17 ''king'':7 ''kingdom'':15 ''note'':10 ''twelfth'':3 ''year'':4', NULL, '330');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('331', '2', '331', NULL, 'In the third year - If Hoshea ascended the throne toward the close of the 12th year of Ahaz 2 Kings 17:1, and if Ahaz reigned not much more than 15 years 2 Kings 16:2, the first of Hezekiah might synchronise in part with Hoshea''s third year.Hezekiah - The name given by our translators follows the Greek form, &#x395;&#x3b6;&#x3b5;&#x3ba;&#x3b9;&#x3b1;&#x3c2;  Ezekias, rather than the Hebrew, which is Hizkiah. Its meaning is "strength of Yahweh."', NULL, NULL, '''1'':22 ''12th'':15 ''15'':31 ''16'':35 ''17'':21 ''2'':19,33,36 ''ahaz'':18,25 ''ascend'':7 ''close'':12 ''ezekia'':60 ''first'':38 ''follow'':56 ''form'':59 ''given'':52 ''greek'':58 ''hebrew'':64 ''hezekiah'':40 ''hizkiah'':67 ''hoshea'':6,46 ''king'':20,34 ''mean'':69 ''might'':41 ''much'':28 ''name'':51 ''part'':44 ''rather'':61 ''reign'':26 ''strength'':71 ''synchronis'':42 ''third'':3,48 ''throne'':9 ''toward'':10 ''translat'':55 ''yahweh'':73 ''year'':4,16,32 ''year.hezekiah'':49', NULL, '331');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('506', '2', '506', NULL, 'Unto thee will I cry - That is, under the consciousness of the danger to which I am exposed - the danger of being drawn away into the society of the wicked. In such circumstances his reliance was not on his own strength; or on his own resolutions; on his own heart; or on his fellowmen. He felt that he was safe only in God, and he appeals to Him, therefore, in this earnest manner, to save him.O Lord my rock - See the notes at Psalm 18:2.Be not silent to me - Margin, "from me." So the Hebrew. The idea is that of one who will not speak to us, or who will not attend to us. We pray, and we look for an "answer" to our prayers, or, as it were, we expect God to "speak" to us; to utter words of kindness; to assure us of His favor; to declare our sins forgiven.Lest, if thou be silent to me - If thou dost not answer my supplications.I become like unto them that go down into the pit - Like those who die; or, lest I be crushed by anxiety and distress, and die. The word "pit" here refers to the grave. So it is used in Psalm 30:3; Psalm 88:4; Isaiah 38:18; Isaiah 14:15, Isaiah 14:19. The meaning is, that if he did not obtain help from God he despaired of life. His troubles would overwhelm and crush him. He could not bear up under them.', NULL, NULL, '''14'':215,218 ''15'':216 ''18'':85,213 ''19'':219 ''2.be'':86 ''3'':207 ''30'':206 ''38'':212 ''4'':210 ''88'':209 ''answer'':123,164 ''anxieti'':187 ''appeal'':66 ''assur'':144 ''attend'':113 ''away'':24 ''bear'':246 ''becom'':167 ''circumst'':33 ''conscious'':10 ''could'':244 ''cri'':5 ''crush'':185,241 ''danger'':13,20 ''declar'':150 ''despair'':233 ''die'':180,191 ''distress'':189 ''dost'':162 ''drawn'':23 ''earnest'':72 ''expect'':132 ''expos'':18 ''favor'':148 ''fellowmen'':54 ''felt'':56 ''forgiven.lest'':153 ''go'':172 ''god'':63,133,231 ''grave'':199 ''heart'':50 ''hebrew'':96 ''help'':229 ''him.o'':76 ''idea'':98 ''isaiah'':211,214,217 ''kind'':142 ''lest'':182 ''life'':235 ''like'':168,177 ''look'':120 ''lord'':77 ''manner'':73 ''margin'':91 ''mean'':221 ''note'':82 ''obtain'':228 ''one'':102 ''overwhelm'':239 ''pit'':176,194 ''pray'':117 ''prayer'':126 ''psalm'':84,205,208 ''refer'':196 ''relianc'':35 ''resolut'':46 ''rock'':79 ''safe'':60 ''save'':75 ''see'':80 ''silent'':88,157 ''sin'':152 ''societi'':27 ''speak'':106,135 ''strength'':41 ''supplications.i'':166 ''thee'':2 ''therefor'':69 ''thou'':155,161 ''troubl'':237 ''unto'':1,169 ''us'':108,115,137,145 ''use'':203 ''utter'':139 ''wick'':30 ''word'':140,193 ''would'':238', NULL, '506');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('332', '2', '332', NULL, 'Hezekiah, like his officers, probably rent his clothes on account of Rab-shakeh''s blasphemies: and he put on sackcloth in self-humiliation and in grief. The only hope left was in Yahweh, for Egypt could not be trusted to effect anything of importance. Rab-shakeh''s boldness had told upon Hezekiah. He was dispirited and dejected. He perhaps began to doubt whether he had done right in yielding to the bolder counsels of Eliakim and Isaiah. He had not lost his faith in God; but his faith was being severely tried. He wisely went and strove by prayer to strengthen it.', NULL, NULL, '''account'':10 ''anyth'':44 ''began'':63 ''blasphemi'':16 ''bold'':51 ''bolder'':75 ''cloth'':8 ''could'':38 ''counsel'':76 ''deject'':60 ''dispirit'':58 ''done'':69 ''doubt'':65 ''effect'':43 ''egypt'':37 ''eliakim'':78 ''faith'':86,91 ''god'':88 ''grief'':28 ''hezekiah'':1,55 ''hope'':31 ''humili'':25 ''import'':46 ''isaiah'':80 ''left'':32 ''like'':2 ''lost'':84 ''offic'':4 ''perhap'':62 ''prayer'':102 ''probabl'':5 ''put'':19 ''rab'':13,48 ''rab-shakeh'':12,47 ''rent'':6 ''right'':70 ''sackcloth'':21 ''self'':24 ''self-humili'':23 ''sever'':94 ''shakeh'':14,49 ''strengthen'':104 ''strove'':100 ''told'':53 ''tri'':95 ''trust'':41 ''upon'':54 ''went'':98 ''whether'':66 ''wise'':97 ''yahweh'':35 ''yield'':72', NULL, '332');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('333', '2', '333', NULL, 'In those days - Hezekiah seems to have died 697 B.C.; and his illness must belong to 713 or 714 B.C. (compare 2 Kings 20:6), a date which falls early in the reign of Sargon. The true chronological place of this narrative is therefore prior to all the other facts related of Hezekiah except his religious reforms.The prophet Isaiah the son of Amoz - This full description of Isaiah (compare 2 Kings 19:2), by the addition of his father''s name and of his office, marks the original independence of this narrative. The writer of Kings may have found it altogether separate from the other records of Hezekiah, and added it in the state in which he found it.This history (compare Jonah 3:4-10) shows that the prophetic denunciations were often not absolute predictions of what was certainly about to happen, but designed primarily to prove, or to lead to repentance, those against whom they were uttered, and only obtaining accomplishment if this primary design failed.', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':125 ''19'':72 ''2'':22,70,73 ''20'':24 ''3'':123 ''4'':124 ''6'':25 ''697'':9 ''713'':17 ''714'':19 ''absolut'':134 ''accomplish'':162 ''add'':110 ''addit'':76 ''altogeth'':101 ''amoz'':63 ''b.c'':10,20 ''belong'':15 ''certain'':139 ''chronolog'':38 ''compar'':21,69,121 ''date'':27 ''day'':3 ''denunci'':130 ''descript'':66 ''design'':144,166 ''die'':8 ''earli'':30 ''except'':54 ''fact'':50 ''fail'':167 ''fall'':29 ''father'':79 ''found'':99,118 ''full'':65 ''happen'':142 ''hezekiah'':4,53,108 ''histori'':120 ''ill'':13 ''independ'':89 ''isaiah'':59,68 ''it.this'':119 ''jonah'':122 ''king'':23,71,96 ''lead'':150 ''mark'':86 ''may'':97 ''must'':14 ''name'':81 ''narrat'':42,92 ''obtain'':161 ''offic'':85 ''often'':132 ''origin'':88 ''place'':39 ''predict'':135 ''primari'':165 ''primarili'':145 ''prior'':45 ''prophet'':58,129 ''prove'':147 ''record'':106 ''reforms.the'':57 ''reign'':33 ''relat'':51 ''religi'':56 ''repent'':152 ''sargon'':35 ''seem'':5 ''separ'':102 ''show'':126 ''son'':61 ''state'':114 ''therefor'':44 ''true'':37 ''utter'':158 ''writer'':94', NULL, '333');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('334', '2', '334', NULL, 'Manasseh was twelve years old - Manasseh, therefore, was not born at the time of Hezekiah''s dangerous illness; and it is probable that Hezekiah had at that time no son to succeed him. According to Josephus, this was the principal cause of his grief.Hephzibah - Jewish tradition makes Hephzibah, Hezekiah''s wife, the daughter of Isaiah; but this is scarcely probable. She was, however, no doubt, known to the prophet, and it may well have been in special compliment to her that Isaiah introduced her name Isaiah 62:4 as one that Jerusalem would bear after her restoration to God''s favor. The name means, "My delight (is) in her."', NULL, NULL, '''4'':88 ''62'':87 ''accord'':34 ''bear'':94 ''born'':10 ''caus'':41 ''compliment'':78 ''danger'':17 ''daughter'':53 ''delight'':106 ''doubt'':65 ''favor'':101 ''god'':99 ''grief.hephzibah'':44 ''hephzibah'':48 ''hezekiah'':15,24,49 ''howev'':63 ''ill'':18 ''introduc'':83 ''isaiah'':55,82,86 ''jerusalem'':92 ''jewish'':45 ''josephus'':36 ''known'':66 ''make'':47 ''manasseh'':1,6 ''may'':72 ''mean'':104 ''name'':85,103 ''old'':5 ''one'':90 ''princip'':40 ''probabl'':22,60 ''prophet'':69 ''restor'':97 ''scarc'':59 ''son'':30 ''special'':77 ''succeed'':32 ''therefor'':7 ''time'':13,28 ''tradit'':46 ''twelv'':3 ''well'':73 ''wife'':51 ''would'':93 ''year'':4', NULL, '334');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('335', '2', '335', NULL, 'In the eighteenth year - This is the date of the finding of the Book of the Law and of the Passover (marginal reference, and 2 Kings 23:23), but is not meant to apply to all the various reforms of Josiah as related in 2 Kings 23:4-20. The true chronology of Josiah''s reign is to be learned from 2 Chronicles 34:3-8; 2 Chronicles 35:1. From these places it appear that at least the greater part of his reforms preceded the finding of the Book of the Law. He began them in the 12th year of his reign, at the age of 20, and had accomplishied all, or the greater part, by his 18th year, when the Book of the Law was found.Shaphan is mentioned frequently by Jeremiah. He was the father of Ahikam, Jeremiah''s friend and protector at the court of Jehoiakim Jeremiah 26:24, and the grandfather of Gedaliah, who was made governor of Judaea by the Babylonians after the destruction of Jeruslem 2 Kings 25:22. Several others of his sons and grandsons were in favor with the later Jewish kings Jeremiah 29:3; Jeremiah 36:10-12, Jeremiah 36:25; Ezekiel 8:11. Shaphan''s office was one of great importance, involving very confidential relations with the king 1 Kings 4:3.', NULL, NULL, '''-12'':197 ''-20'':49 ''-8'':66 ''1'':70,219 ''10'':196 ''11'':203 ''12th'':99 ''18th'':119 ''2'':25,45,62,67,172 ''20'':108 ''22'':175 ''23'':27,28,47 ''24'':152 ''25'':174,200 ''26'':151 ''29'':192 ''3'':65,193,222 ''34'':64 ''35'':69 ''36'':195,199 ''4'':48,221 ''8'':202 ''accomplishi'':111 ''age'':106 ''ahikam'':139 ''appear'':75 ''appli'':34 ''babylonian'':166 ''began'':95 ''book'':14,90,123 ''chronicl'':63,68 ''chronolog'':52 ''confidenti'':214 ''court'':147 ''date'':8 ''destruct'':169 ''eighteenth'':3 ''ezekiel'':201 ''father'':137 ''favor'':185 ''find'':11,87 ''found.shaphan'':128 ''frequent'':131 ''friend'':142 ''gedaliah'':157 ''governor'':161 ''grandfath'':155 ''grandson'':182 ''great'':210 ''greater'':80,115 ''import'':211 ''involv'':212 ''jehoiakim'':149 ''jeremiah'':133,140,150,191,194,198 ''jeruslem'':171 ''jewish'':189 ''josiah'':41,54 ''judaea'':163 ''king'':26,46,173,190,218,220 ''later'':188 ''law'':17,93,126 ''learn'':60 ''least'':78 ''made'':160 ''margin'':22 ''meant'':32 ''mention'':130 ''offic'':206 ''one'':208 ''other'':177 ''part'':81,116 ''passov'':21 ''place'':73 ''preced'':85 ''protector'':144 ''refer'':23 ''reform'':39,84 ''reign'':56,103 ''relat'':43,215 ''sever'':176 ''shaphan'':204 ''son'':180 ''true'':51 ''various'':38 ''year'':4,100,120', NULL, '335');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('336', '2', '336', NULL, 'The prophets - The suggestion to regard this word an error of the pen for "Levites," which occurs in Chronicles (marginal reference), is unnecessary. For though Zephaniah, Urijah, and Jeremiah are all that we can name as belonging to the order at the time, there is no reason to doubt that Judaea contained others whom we cannot name. "Schools of the prophets" were as common in Judah as in Israel.He read - The present passage is strong evidence that the Jewish kings could read. The solemn reading of the Law - a practice commanded in the Law itself once in seven years Deuteronomy 31:10-13 - had been intermitted, at least for the last 75 years, from the date of the accession of Manasseh.', NULL, NULL, '''-13'':103 ''10'':102 ''31'':101 ''75'':112 ''access'':119 ''belong'':37 ''cannot'':56 ''chronicl'':19 ''command'':91 ''common'':64 ''contain'':52 ''could'':81 ''date'':116 ''deuteronomi'':100 ''doubt'':49 ''error'':10 ''evid'':76 ''intermit'':106 ''israel.he'':69 ''jeremiah'':29 ''jewish'':79 ''judaea'':51 ''judah'':66 ''king'':80 ''last'':111 ''law'':88,94 ''least'':108 ''levit'':15 ''manasseh'':121 ''margin'':20 ''name'':35,57 ''occur'':17 ''order'':40 ''other'':53 ''passag'':73 ''pen'':13 ''practic'':90 ''present'':72 ''prophet'':2,61 ''read'':70,82,85 ''reason'':47 ''refer'':21 ''regard'':6 ''school'':58 ''seven'':98 ''solemn'':84 ''strong'':75 ''suggest'':4 ''though'':25 ''time'':43 ''unnecessari'':23 ''urijah'':27 ''word'':8 ''year'':99,113 ''zephaniah'':26', NULL, '336');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('337', '2', '337', NULL, 'In his days - i. e., 605 B.C., which was the third completed Daniel 1:1, and fourth commencing Jeremiah 25:1, year of Jehoiakim.Nebuchadnezzar - or Nebuchadrezzar, which is closer to the original, Nabu-kudurri-uzur. This name, like most Babylonian names, is made up of three elements, Nebo, the well-known god Isaiah 46:1, kudur, of doubtful signification (perhaps "crown" perhaps "landmark"), and uzur "protects." Nebuchadnezzar, the son of Nabopolassar, and second monarch of the Babylonian empire, ascended the throne, 604 B.C., and reigned 43 years, dying 561 B.C. He married Amuhia (or Amyitis), daughter of Cyaxares, king of the Medes, and was the most celebrated of all the Babylonian sovereigns. No other pagan king occupies so much space in Scripture. He was not actual king at this time, but only Crown Prince and leader of the army under his father. As he would be surrounded with all the state and magnficence of a monarch, the Jews would naturally look upon him as actual king.Came up - Nebuchadnezzar began his campaign by attacking and defeating Neco''s Egyptians at Carchemish Jeremiah 46:2. He then pressed forward toward the south, overran Syria, Phoenicia, and Judaea, took Jerusalem, and carried off a portion of the inhabitants as prisoners Daniel 1:1-4 : after which he proceeded southward, and had reached the borders of Egypt when he was suddenly recalled to Babylon by the death of his father.Three years - Probably from 605 B.C. to 602 B.C. Jehoiakim rebelled because he knew Nebuchadnezzar to be engaged in important wars in some other part of Asia.', NULL, NULL, '''-4'':213 ''1'':14,15,21,57,211,212 ''2'':185 ''25'':20 ''43'':88 ''46'':56,184 ''561'':91 ''602'':245 ''604'':84 ''605'':6,242 ''actual'':128,167 ''amuhia'':95 ''amyiti'':97 ''armi'':141 ''ascend'':81 ''asia'':264 ''attack'':175 ''b.c'':7,85,92,243,246 ''babylon'':232 ''babylonian'':41,79,113 ''began'':171 ''border'':223 ''campaign'':173 ''carchemish'':182 ''carri'':201 ''celebr'':109 ''closer'':29 ''commenc'':18 ''complet'':12 ''crown'':63,135 ''cyaxar'':100 ''daniel'':13,210 ''daughter'':98 ''day'':3 ''death'':235 ''defeat'':177 ''die'':90 ''doubt'':60 ''e'':5 ''egypt'':225 ''egyptian'':180 ''element'':48 ''empir'':80 ''engag'':255 ''father'':144 ''father.three'':238 ''forward'':189 ''fourth'':17 ''god'':54 ''import'':257 ''inhabit'':207 ''isaiah'':55 ''jehoiakim'':247 ''jehoiakim.nebuchadnezzar'':24 ''jeremiah'':19,183 ''jerusalem'':199 ''jew'':160 ''judaea'':197 ''king'':101,118,129 ''king.came'':168 ''knew'':251 ''known'':53 ''kudur'':58 ''kudurri'':35 ''landmark'':65 ''leader'':138 ''like'':39 ''look'':163 ''made'':44 ''magnfic'':155 ''marri'':94 ''mede'':104 ''monarch'':76,158 ''much'':121 ''nabopolassar'':73 ''nabu'':34 ''nabu-kudurri-uzur'':33 ''name'':38,42 ''natur'':162 ''nebo'':49 ''nebuchadnezzar'':69,170,252 ''nebuchadrezzar'':26 ''neco'':178 ''occupi'':119 ''origin'':32 ''overran'':193 ''pagan'':117 ''part'':262 ''perhap'':62,64 ''phoenicia'':195 ''portion'':204 ''press'':188 ''princ'':136 ''prison'':209 ''probabl'':240 ''proceed'':217 ''protect'':68 ''reach'':221 ''rebel'':248 ''recal'':230 ''reign'':87 ''scriptur'':124 ''second'':75 ''signif'':61 ''son'':71 ''south'':192 ''southward'':218 ''sovereign'':114 ''space'':122 ''state'':153 ''sudden'':229 ''surround'':149 ''syria'':194 ''third'':11 ''three'':47 ''throne'':83 ''time'':132 ''took'':198 ''toward'':190 ''upon'':164 ''uzur'':36,67 ''war'':258 ''well'':52 ''well-known'':51 ''would'':147,161 ''year'':22,89,239', NULL, '337');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('338', '2', '338', NULL, 'In the ninth year ... - As the final catastrophe approaches, the historian becomes more close and exact in his dates, marking not only the year, but the month and the day, on which the siege began, no less than those on which it closed 2 Kings 25:3. From Ezekiel 24:1 we find that on the very day when the host of Nebuchadnezzar made its appearance before Jerusalem the fact was revealed to Ezekiel in Babylonia, and the fate of the city announced to him Ezekiel 24:6-14. The army seems to have at first spread itself over all Judaea. It fought, not only against Jerusalem, but especially against Lachish and Azekah Jeremiah 34:7, two cities of the south 2 Chronicles 11:9, which had probably been strongly garrisoned in order to maintain the communication with Egypt. This division of the Babylonian forces encouraged Hophra to put his troops in motion and advance to the relief of his Jewish allies Jeremiah 37:5. On hearing this, Nebuchadnezzar broke up from before Jerusalem and marched probably to Azekah and Lachish. The Egyptians shrank back, returned into their own country Jeremiah 37:7; Ezekiel 17:17, and took no further part in the war. Nebuchadnezzar then led back his army, and once more invested the city. (It is uncertain whether the date at the beginning of this verse refers to the first or to the second investment.)Forts - Probably moveable towers, sometimes provided with battering-rams, which the besiegers advanced against the walls, thus bringing their fighting men on a level with their antagonists. Such towers are seen in the Assyrian sculptures.', NULL, NULL, '''-14'':89 ''1'':51 ''11'':124 ''17'':195,196 ''2'':44,122 ''24'':50,87 ''25'':46 ''3'':47 ''34'':115 ''37'':164,192 ''5'':165 ''6'':88 ''7'':116,193 ''9'':125 ''advanc'':155,251 ''alli'':162 ''announc'':83 ''antagonist'':265 ''appear'':66 ''approach'':9 ''armi'':91,210 ''assyrian'':272 ''azekah'':113,179 ''babylonia'':76 ''babylonian'':144 ''back'':185,208 ''batter'':246 ''battering-ram'':245 ''becom'':12 ''began'':35 ''begin'':225 ''besieg'':250 ''bring'':256 ''broke'':170 ''catastroph'':8 ''chronicl'':123 ''citi'':82,118,216 ''close'':14,43 ''communic'':137 ''countri'':190 ''date'':19,222 ''day'':30,58 ''divis'':141 ''egypt'':139 ''egyptian'':183 ''encourag'':146 ''especi'':109 ''exact'':16 ''ezekiel'':49,74,86,194 ''fact'':70 ''fate'':79 ''fight'':258 ''final'':7 ''find'':53 ''first'':96,232 ''forc'':145 ''fort'':238 ''fought'':103 ''garrison'':131 ''hear'':167 ''historian'':11 ''hophra'':147 ''host'':61 ''invest'':214,237 ''jeremiah'':114,163,191 ''jerusalem'':68,107,174 ''jewish'':161 ''judaea'':101 ''king'':45 ''lachish'':111,181 ''led'':207 ''less'':37 ''level'':262 ''made'':64 ''maintain'':135 ''march'':176 ''mark'':20 ''men'':259 ''month'':27 ''motion'':153 ''moveabl'':240 ''nebuchadnezzar'':63,169,205 ''ninth'':3 ''order'':133 ''part'':201 ''probabl'':128,177,239 ''provid'':243 ''put'':149 ''ram'':247 ''refer'':229 ''relief'':158 ''return'':186 ''reveal'':72 ''sculptur'':273 ''second'':236 ''seem'':92 ''seen'':269 ''shrank'':184 ''sieg'':34 ''sometim'':242 ''south'':121 ''spread'':97 ''strong'':130 ''thus'':255 ''took'':198 ''tower'':241,267 ''troop'':151 ''two'':117 ''uncertain'':219 ''vers'':228 ''wall'':254 ''war'':204 ''whether'':220 ''year'':4,24', NULL, '338');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('339', '2', '339', NULL, 'The Zemarite - See Genesis 10:18 note. The inscriptions of the Assyrian monarch, Sargon, (720 B.C.) mention Zimira, which is joined with Arpad (Arvad); and there can be little doubt that it is the city indicated by the term "Zemarite."', NULL, NULL, '''10'':5 ''18'':6 ''720'':15 ''arpad'':23 ''arvad'':24 ''assyrian'':12 ''b.c'':16 ''citi'':35 ''doubt'':30 ''genesi'':4 ''indic'':36 ''inscript'':9 ''join'':21 ''littl'':29 ''mention'':17 ''monarch'':13 ''note'':7 ''sargon'':14 ''see'':3 ''term'':39 ''zemarit'':2,40 ''zimira'':18', NULL, '339');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('340', '2', '340', NULL, 'The sons of Israel - The order of the names here approximates to an order determined by legitimacy of birth. A single change - the removal of Dan to the place after Benjamin - would give the following result:(1) The six sons of the first wife, Leah.(2) the two sons of the second wife, Rachel.(3) the two sons of the first concubine, Bilhah.(4) the two sons of the second concubine, Zilpah.Dan''s undue prominency may, perhaps, be accounted for by his occupying the seventh place in the "blessing of Jacob" Genesis 49:16.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':37 ''16'':94 ''2'':46 ''3'':55 ''4'':64 ''49'':93 ''account'':79 ''approxim'':11 ''benjamin'':31 ''bilhah'':63 ''birth'':19 ''bless'':89 ''chang'':22 ''concubin'':62,71 ''dan'':26 ''determin'':15 ''first'':43,61 ''follow'':35 ''genesi'':92 ''give'':33 ''israel'':4 ''jacob'':91 ''leah'':45 ''legitimaci'':17 ''may'':76 ''name'':9 ''occupi'':83 ''order'':6,14 ''perhap'':77 ''place'':29,86 ''promin'':75 ''rachel'':54 ''remov'':24 ''result'':36 ''second'':52,70 ''seventh'':85 ''singl'':21 ''six'':39 ''son'':2,40,49,58,67 ''two'':48,57,66 ''undu'':74 ''wife'':44,53 ''would'':32 ''zilpah.dan'':72', NULL, '340');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('341', '2', '341', NULL, 'The sons of David - The writer returns to the point at which he had left the posterity of Ram 1 Chronicles 2:9, 1 Chronicles 2:15, and traces out the family of David - the royal house of the tribe of Judah.Daniel - See the marginal note and reference.There are three lists of the sons of David, born in Jerusalem.2 Samuel 5:14-16 1 Chronicles 3:5-8 1 Chronicles 14:4-7  1. Shammuah Shimeah* Shammuah  2. Shobab Shobab Shobab  3. Nathan Nathan Nathan  4. Solomon Solomon Solomon  5. Ibhar Ibhar Ibhar  6. Elishua Elishama* Elishua  7. a  Eliphelet* Elpalet*  8. a  Nogah Nogah  9. Nepheg Nepheg Nepheg  10. Japhia Japhia Japhia  11. Elishama Elishama Elishama  12. Eliada Eliada Beeliada*  13. Eliphelet Eliphelet Eliphelet  (Differences are marked with an asterick).  A comparison of the three lists serves to show:(1) that "Shimeah" and the first "Elishama" in the list of this chapter are corruptions;(2) that David had really 13 sons born in Jerusalem, of whom two - the first Eliphelet and Nogah - probably died in their childhood; and(3) that Eliada, the twelfth son, was also called Beeliada, the term Baal, "lord," not having (previous to the introduction of the Baal worship) a bad sense, but being regarded as an equivalent with El, "God."', NULL, NULL, '''-16'':63 ''-7'':73 ''-8'':68 ''1'':20,24,64,69,74,141 ''10'':110 ''11'':114 ''12'':118 ''13'':122,161 ''14'':62,71 ''15'':27 ''2'':22,26,78,156 ''3'':66,82,180 ''4'':72,86 ''5'':61,67,90 ''6'':94 ''7'':98 ''8'':102 ''9'':23,106 ''also'':187 ''asterick'':131 ''baal'':192,202 ''bad'':205 ''beeliada'':121,189 ''born'':57,163 ''call'':188 ''chapter'':153 ''childhood'':178 ''chronicl'':21,25,65,70 ''comparison'':133 ''corrupt'':155 ''david'':4,34,56,158 ''die'':175 ''differ'':126 ''el'':214 ''eliada'':119,120,182 ''eliphelet'':100,123,124,125,171 ''elishama'':96,115,116,117,147 ''elishua'':95,97 ''elpalet'':101 ''equival'':212 ''famili'':32 ''first'':146,170 ''god'':215 ''hous'':37 ''ibhar'':91,92,93 ''introduct'':199 ''japhia'':111,112,113 ''jerusalem'':165 ''jerusalem.2'':59 ''judah.daniel'':42 ''left'':15 ''list'':51,137,150 ''lord'':193 ''margin'':45 ''mark'':128 ''nathan'':83,84,85 ''nepheg'':107,108,109 ''nogah'':104,105,173 ''note'':46 ''point'':10 ''poster'':17 ''previous'':196 ''probabl'':174 ''ram'':19 ''realli'':160 ''reference.there'':48 ''regard'':209 ''return'':7 ''royal'':36 ''samuel'':60 ''see'':43 ''sens'':206 ''serv'':138 ''shammuah'':75,77 ''shimeah'':76,143 ''shobab'':79,80,81 ''show'':140 ''solomon'':87,88,89 ''son'':2,54,162,185 ''term'':191 ''three'':50,136 ''trace'':29 ''tribe'':40 ''twelfth'':184 ''two'':168 ''worship'':203 ''writer'':6', NULL, '341');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('342', '2', '342', NULL, 'Read, "These are the sons of the father (i. e. chief) of Etam" 2 Chronicles 11:6, a city of Judah, not far from Bethlehem.', NULL, NULL, '''11'':16 ''2'':14 ''6'':17 ''bethlehem'':25 ''chief'':11 ''chronicl'':15 ''citi'':19 ''e'':10 ''etam'':13 ''far'':23 ''father'':8 ''judah'':21 ''read'':1 ''son'':5', NULL, '342');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('343', '2', '343', NULL, 'His birthright was given ... - In particular, the right of the first-born to a double inheritance Deuteronomy 21:17 was conferred on Joseph, both by the expressed will of Jacob Genesis 48:22 and in the actual partition of Canaan Joshua 16-17. But though the birthright, as respecting its material privileges, passed to Joseph, its other rights, those of dignity and pre-eminence, fell to Judah; of whom came the chief ruler, an allusion especially to David, though it may reach further, and include a glance at the Messiah, the true "Ruler" of Israel Micah 5:2.', NULL, NULL, '''-17'':44 ''16'':43 ''17'':20 ''2'':100 ''21'':19 ''22'':34 ''48'':33 ''5'':99 ''actual'':38 ''allus'':77 ''birthright'':2,48 ''born'':13 ''came'':72 ''canaan'':41 ''chief'':74 ''confer'':22 ''david'':80 ''deuteronomi'':18 ''digniti'':62 ''doubl'':16 ''emin'':66 ''especi'':78 ''express'':28 ''fell'':67 ''first'':12 ''first-born'':11 ''genesi'':32 ''given'':4 ''glanc'':89 ''includ'':87 ''inherit'':17 ''israel'':97 ''jacob'':31 ''joseph'':24,56 ''joshua'':42 ''judah'':69 ''materi'':52 ''may'':83 ''messiah'':92 ''micah'':98 ''particular'':6 ''partit'':39 ''pass'':54 ''pre'':65 ''pre-emin'':64 ''privileg'':53 ''reach'':84 ''respect'':50 ''right'':8,59 ''ruler'':75,95 ''though'':46,81 ''true'':94', NULL, '343');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('344', '2', '344', NULL, 'Ahimaaz begat Azariah - It must, apparently, be this Azariah, and not the son of Johanan 1 Chronicles 6:10, who was high priest at the dedication of Solomon''s Temple. For Zadok, who lived into the reign of Solomon 1 Kings 4:4 cannot have been succeeded by a great-great-grandson. The notice in 1 Chronicles 6:10, which is attached to the second Azariah, must, beyond a doubt, belong properly to the first.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':16,40,57 ''10'':19,60 ''4'':42,43 ''6'':18,59 ''ahimaaz'':1 ''appar'':6 ''attach'':63 ''azariah'':3,9,67 ''begat'':2 ''belong'':72 ''beyond'':69 ''cannot'':44 ''chronicl'':17,58 ''dedic'':26 ''doubt'':71 ''first'':76 ''grandson'':53 ''great'':51,52 ''great-great-grandson'':50 ''high'':22 ''johanan'':15 ''king'':41 ''live'':34 ''must'':5,68 ''notic'':55 ''priest'':23 ''proper'':73 ''reign'':37 ''second'':66 ''solomon'':28,39 ''son'':13 ''succeed'':47 ''templ'':30 ''zadok'':32', NULL, '344');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('345', '2', '345', NULL, 'Whose number was in the days of David ... - The writer would seem by this passage to have had access to the statistics of the tribes collected by David, when he sinfully "numbered the people" (marginal reference). The numbers given in 1 Chronicles 7:4-5 probably came from the same source.', NULL, NULL, '''-5'':45 ''1'':41 ''4'':44 ''7'':43 ''access'':19 ''came'':47 ''chronicl'':42 ''collect'':26 ''david'':8,28 ''day'':6 ''given'':39 ''margin'':35 ''number'':2,32,38 ''passag'':15 ''peopl'':34 ''probabl'':46 ''refer'':36 ''seem'':12 ''sin'':31 ''sourc'':51 ''statist'':22 ''tribe'':25 ''whose'':1 ''would'':11 ''writer'':10', NULL, '345');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('346', '2', '346', NULL, 'The reason of this return to the genealogy of the Benjamites seems to be the desire to connect the genealogical introduction with the historical body of the work. As the history is to begin with Saul, the genealogical portion is made to end with an account of the family of this Benjamite monarch.', NULL, NULL, '''account'':46 ''begin'':34 ''benjamit'':11,52 ''bodi'':25 ''connect'':18 ''desir'':16 ''end'':43 ''famili'':49 ''genealog'':8,20,38 ''histor'':24 ''histori'':31 ''introduct'':21 ''made'':41 ''monarch'':53 ''portion'':39 ''reason'':2 ''return'':5 ''saul'':36 ''seem'':12 ''work'':28', NULL, '346');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('347', '2', '347', NULL, 'Rather, "So all Israel were reckoned ... the kings of Israel. And Judah was carried away captive to Babylon for their transgressions."', NULL, NULL, '''away'':15 ''babylon'':18 ''captiv'':16 ''carri'':14 ''israel'':4,10 ''judah'':12 ''king'':8 ''rather'':1 ''reckon'':6 ''transgress'':21', NULL, '347');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('348', '2', '348', NULL, 'The present chapter contains two facts not found in 1 Samuel 31:1-13 - the fastening of Saul''s head in the temple of Dagon 1 Chronicles 10:10, and the burial of his bones, and those of his sons, under an oak 1 Chronicles 10:12. Otherwise the narrative differs from 1 Samuel 31:1-13 only by being abbreviated (see especially 1 Chronicles 10:6-7, 1 Chronicles 10:11-12), and by having some moral reflections attached to it 1 Chronicles 10:13-14.', NULL, NULL, '''-12'':73 ''-13'':14,57 ''-14'':87 ''-7'':68 ''1'':10,13,26,44,53,56,64,69,83 ''10'':28,29,46,66,71,85 ''11'':72 ''12'':47 ''13'':86 ''31'':12,55 ''6'':67 ''abbrevi'':61 ''attach'':80 ''bone'':35 ''burial'':32 ''chapter'':3 ''chronicl'':27,45,65,70,84 ''contain'':4 ''dagon'':25 ''differ'':51 ''especi'':63 ''fact'':6 ''fasten'':16 ''found'':8 ''head'':20 ''moral'':78 ''narrat'':50 ''oak'':43 ''otherwis'':48 ''present'':2 ''reflect'':79 ''samuel'':11,54 ''saul'':18 ''see'':62 ''son'':40 ''templ'':23 ''two'':5', NULL, '348');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('349', '2', '349', NULL, 'This chapter runs parallel with 2 Samuel 5 as far as 1 Chronicles 11:9, after which it is to be compared with 2 Samuel 23:8-39 as far as 1 Chronicles 11:40, the remainder 1 Chronicles 11:41-47 being an addition, to which Samuel has nothing corresponding. Compare throughout the notes in Samuel.', NULL, NULL, '''-39'':28 ''-47'':42 ''1'':12,32,38 ''11'':14,34,40 ''2'':6,24 ''23'':26 ''40'':35 ''41'':41 ''5'':8 ''8'':27 ''9'':15 ''addit'':45 ''chapter'':2 ''chronicl'':13,33,39 ''compar'':22,52 ''correspond'':51 ''far'':10,30 ''note'':55 ''noth'':50 ''parallel'':4 ''remaind'':37 ''run'':3 ''samuel'':7,25,48,57 ''throughout'':53', NULL, '349');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('350', '2', '350', NULL, 'This chapter is composed wholly of matter that is new to us, no corresponding accounts occurring in Samuel. It comprises four lists:(1) One of men, chiefly Benjamites, who joined David at Ziklag 1 Chronicles 12:1-7;(2) A second of Gadites who united themselves to him when he was in a stronghold near the desert 1 Chronicles 12:8-15;(3) A third of Manassites who came to him when he was dismissed by the Philistines upon suspicion 1 Chronicles 12:19-22; and(4) A fourth of the numbers from the different tribes who attended and made him king at Hebron 1 Chronicles 12:23-40.', NULL, NULL, '''-15'':62 ''-22'':85 ''-40'':109 ''-7'':38 ''1'':23,34,37,58,81,105 ''12'':36,60,83,107 ''19'':84 ''2'':39 ''23'':108 ''3'':63 ''4'':87 ''8'':61 ''account'':15 ''attend'':98 ''benjamit'':28 ''came'':69 ''chapter'':2 ''chiefli'':27 ''chronicl'':35,59,82,106 ''compos'':4 ''compris'':20 ''correspond'':14 ''david'':31 ''desert'':57 ''differ'':95 ''dismiss'':75 ''four'':21 ''fourth'':89 ''gadit'':43 ''hebron'':104 ''join'':30 ''king'':102 ''list'':22 ''made'':100 ''manassit'':67 ''matter'':7 ''men'':26 ''near'':55 ''new'':10 ''number'':92 ''occur'':16 ''one'':24 ''philistin'':78 ''samuel'':18 ''second'':41 ''stronghold'':54 ''suspicion'':80 ''third'':65 ''tribe'':96 ''unit'':45 ''upon'':79 ''us'':12 ''wholli'':5 ''ziklag'':33', NULL, '350');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('351', '2', '351', NULL, 'The captains ... - Such an organisation had probably been established generally through the tribes prior to the time of David: but David seems to have been the first to recognize in these officers of the host representatives of the people, to consult them on public affairs, and to give them a certain political position.', NULL, NULL, '''affair'':45 ''captain'':2 ''certain'':51 ''consult'':41 ''david'':19,21 ''establish'':9 ''first'':27 ''general'':10 ''give'':48 ''host'':35 ''offic'':32 ''organis'':5 ''peopl'':39 ''polit'':52 ''posit'':53 ''prior'':14 ''probabl'':7 ''public'':44 ''recogn'':29 ''repres'':36 ''seem'':22 ''time'':17 ''tribe'':13', NULL, '351');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('352', '2', '352', NULL, 'Compare 2 Samuel 5:11-25, the only important variations from which are in 1 Chronicles 14:4-7, the list of the sons of David (see 1 Chronicles 3:1 note), and in 1 Chronicles 14:12, where the fact is added that the idols taken from the Philistines were burned.', NULL, NULL, '''-25'':6 ''-7'':19 ''1'':15,28,31,35 ''11'':5 ''12'':38 ''14'':17,37 ''2'':2 ''3'':30 ''4'':18 ''5'':4 ''add'':43 ''burn'':52 ''chronicl'':16,29,36 ''compar'':1 ''david'':26 ''fact'':41 ''idol'':46 ''import'':9 ''list'':21 ''note'':32 ''philistin'':50 ''samuel'':3 ''see'':27 ''son'':24 ''taken'':47 ''variat'':10', NULL, '352');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('353', '2', '353', NULL, 'And pitched for it a tent - The old "tent" or "tabernacle" was still in existence at Gibeon 1 Chronicles 16:39; 2 Chronicles 1:3; but the ark had long been separated from it, and David probably thought that something newer and more magnificent was requisite. He therefore allowed the former tabernacle to keep its place, and had another made and erected.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':18,24 ''16'':20 ''2'':22 ''3'':25 ''39'':21 ''allow'':49 ''anoth'':59 ''ark'':28 ''chronicl'':19,23 ''david'':36 ''erect'':62 ''exist'':15 ''former'':51 ''gibeon'':17 ''keep'':54 ''long'':30 ''made'':60 ''magnific'':44 ''newer'':41 ''old'':8 ''pitch'':2 ''place'':56 ''probabl'':37 ''requisit'':46 ''separ'':32 ''someth'':40 ''still'':13 ''tabernacl'':11,52 ''tent'':6,9 ''therefor'':48 ''thought'':38', NULL, '353');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('354', '2', '354', NULL, 'The first three verses form part of the narrative commenced at 1 Chronicles 15:25. Compare 2 Samuel 6:17-19, where the passage is not torn from its proper context.', NULL, NULL, '''-19'':21 ''1'':12 ''15'':14 ''17'':20 ''2'':17 ''25'':15 ''6'':19 ''chronicl'':13 ''commenc'':10 ''compar'':16 ''context'':31 ''first'':2 ''form'':5 ''narrat'':9 ''part'':6 ''passag'':24 ''proper'':30 ''samuel'':18 ''three'':3 ''torn'':27 ''vers'':4', NULL, '354');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('355', '2', '355', NULL, 'My son - The minatory clause which occurs after this in Samuel is here omitted, because the writer is not about to record the sins of Solomon, or the sufferings 1 Kings 11:9-40 which he thereby brought upon himself.', NULL, NULL, '''-40'':34 ''1'':30 ''11'':32 ''9'':33 ''brought'':38 ''claus'':5 ''king'':31 ''minatori'':4 ''occur'':7 ''omit'':14 ''record'':22 ''samuel'':11 ''sin'':24 ''solomon'':26 ''son'':2 ''suffer'':29 ''therebi'':37 ''upon'':39 ''writer'':17', NULL, '355');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('356', '2', '356', NULL, 'Gath and her towns - In Samuel, Methegammah (see the marginal reference note). Compare the marginal references and notes. The writer here adds one or two touches, and varies in one or two of the numbers.', NULL, NULL, '''add'':22 ''compar'':13 ''gath'':1 ''margin'':10,15 ''methegammah'':7 ''note'':12,18 ''number'':35 ''one'':23,30 ''refer'':11,16 ''samuel'':6 ''see'':8 ''touch'':26 ''town'':4 ''two'':25,32 ''vari'':28 ''writer'':20', NULL, '356');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('357', '2', '357', NULL, 'Hanun - A Philistine king of this name is mentioned in the Assyrian inscriptions as paying tribute to Tiglath-pileser and warring with Sargon.', NULL, NULL, '''assyrian'':12 ''hanun'':1 ''inscript'':13 ''king'':4 ''mention'':9 ''name'':7 ''pay'':15 ''philistin'':3 ''piles'':20 ''sargon'':24 ''tiglath'':19 ''tiglath-piles'':18 ''tribut'':16 ''war'':22', NULL, '357');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('358', '2', '358', NULL, 'This chapter, containing such other warlike exploits belonging to David''s reign as the writer of Chronicles thinks it important to put on record, is to be compared with the passages of Samuel noted in the marginal references.', NULL, NULL, '''belong'':8 ''chapter'':2 ''chronicl'':17 ''compar'':28 ''contain'':3 ''david'':10 ''exploit'':7 ''import'':20 ''margin'':37 ''note'':34 ''passag'':31 ''put'':22 ''record'':24 ''refer'':38 ''reign'':12 ''samuel'':33 ''think'':18 ''warlik'':6 ''writer'':15', NULL, '358');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('359', '2', '359', NULL, 'As the books of Scripture are arranged in our Version, Satan is here for the first time by name introduced to us. He appears not merely as an "adversary" who seeks to injure man from without, but as a Tempter able to ruin him by suggesting sinful acts and thoughts from within. In this point of view, the revelation made of him here is the most advanced that we find in the Old Testament.The difficulty in reconciling the statement here, "Satan provoked David," etc. with that of Samuel, "the Lord moved David," etc. 2 Samuel 24:1 is not serious. All temptation is permitted by God. When evil spirits tempt us, they do so by permission (Job 1:12; Job 2:6; Luke 22:31, etc.). If Satan therefore provoked David to number the peopIe, God allowed him. And what God allows, He may be said to do. (Another view is maintained in the 2 Samuel 24:1 note).', NULL, NULL, '''1'':97,118,158 ''12'':119 ''2'':94,121,155 ''22'':124 ''24'':96,157 ''31'':125 ''6'':122 ''abl'':41 ''act'':48 ''advanc'':67 ''adversari'':29 ''allow'':137,142 ''anoth'':149 ''appear'':24 ''arrang'':7 ''book'':3 ''david'':83,92,131 ''difficulti'':75 ''etc'':84,93,126 ''evil'':108 ''find'':70 ''first'':16 ''god'':106,136,141 ''injur'':33 ''introduc'':20 ''job'':117,120 ''lord'':90 ''luke'':123 ''made'':60 ''maintain'':152 ''man'':34 ''may'':144 ''mere'':26 ''move'':91 ''name'':19 ''note'':159 ''number'':133 ''old'':73 ''peopi'':135 ''permiss'':116 ''permit'':104 ''point'':55 ''provok'':82,130 ''reconcil'':77 ''revel'':59 ''ruin'':43 ''said'':146 ''samuel'':88,95,156 ''satan'':11,81,128 ''scriptur'':5 ''seek'':31 ''serious'':100 ''sin'':47 ''spirit'':109 ''statement'':79 ''suggest'':46 ''tempt'':110 ''temptat'':102 ''tempter'':40 ''testament.the'':74 ''therefor'':129 ''thought'':50 ''time'':17 ''us'':22,111 ''version'':10 ''view'':57,150 ''within'':52 ''without'':36', NULL, '359');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('360', '2', '360', NULL, 'This is the house of the Lord God - The double miracle - that of the angelic appearance and that of the fire from heaven - had convinced David that here he had found the destined site of that "house" which it had been told him that his son should build 1 Chronicles 22:10. Hence, this public announcement.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':49 ''10'':52 ''22'':51 ''angel'':15 ''announc'':56 ''appear'':16 ''build'':48 ''chronicl'':50 ''convinc'':25 ''david'':26 ''destin'':33 ''doubl'':10 ''fire'':21 ''found'':31 ''god'':8 ''heaven'':23 ''henc'':53 ''hous'':4,37 ''lord'':7 ''miracl'':11 ''public'':55 ''site'':34 ''son'':46 ''told'':42', NULL, '360');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('361', '2', '361', NULL, 'See the marginal references and notes. 1 Chronicles 23:28-32 give the most complete account in Scripture of the nature of the Levitical office.', NULL, NULL, '''-32'':11 ''1'':7 ''23'':9 ''28'':10 ''account'':16 ''chronicl'':8 ''complet'':15 ''give'':12 ''levit'':24 ''margin'':3 ''natur'':21 ''note'':6 ''offic'':25 ''refer'':4 ''scriptur'':18 ''see'':1', NULL, '361');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('362', '2', '362', NULL, 'Zadok and Ahimelech (rather Abiathar, see 1 Chronicles 24:6) assisted David in drawing up the priestly courses, as the "captains of the host" assisted him in making the divisions of the singers 1 Chronicles 25:1.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':7,34,37 ''24'':9 ''25'':36 ''6'':10 ''abiathar'':5 ''ahimelech'':3 ''assist'':11,25 ''captain'':21 ''chronicl'':8,35 ''cours'':18 ''david'':12 ''divis'':30 ''draw'':14 ''host'':24 ''make'':28 ''priest'':17 ''rather'':4 ''see'':6 ''singer'':33 ''zadok'':1', NULL, '362');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('363', '2', '363', NULL, 'Under the hands of Asaph ... - That is to say, "under the direction of Asaph" - who himself "prophesied," or performed the sacred services, "under the direction of the king."', NULL, NULL, '''asaph'':5,14 ''direct'':12,25 ''hand'':3 ''king'':28 ''perform'':19 ''prophesi'':17 ''sacr'':21 ''say'':9 ''servic'':22', NULL, '363');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('364', '2', '364', NULL, 'Obed-edom and Hosah 1 Chronicles 26:10 had been "porters," or door - keepers, from the time of the bringing up of the ark into Jerusalem 1 Chronicles 15:24; 1 Chronicles 16:38.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':6,28,32 ''10'':9 ''15'':30 ''16'':34 ''24'':31 ''26'':8 ''38'':35 ''ark'':25 ''bring'':21 ''chronicl'':7,29,33 ''door'':14 ''edom'':3 ''hosah'':5 ''jerusalem'':27 ''keeper'':15 ''obe'':2 ''obed-edom'':1 ''porter'':12 ''time'':18', NULL, '364');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('365', '2', '365', NULL, 'This verse is a general heading to the list 1 Chronicles 27:2-15. The heading has been taken from some fuller and more elaborate description of David''s army, whereof the writer of Chronicles gives us only an abridgement. Omitting the captains of thousands, the captains of hundreds, and the officers (probably "scribes") who served the king, he contents himself with recording the "chief fathers" or heads of the divisions 1 Chronicles 28:1, and the number of Iraelites in each course.', NULL, NULL, '''-15'':14 ''1'':10,72,75 ''2'':13 ''27'':12 ''28'':74 ''abridg'':40 ''armi'':30 ''captain'':43,47 ''chief'':65 ''chronicl'':11,35,73 ''content'':60 ''cours'':83 ''david'':28 ''descript'':26 ''divis'':71 ''elabor'':25 ''father'':66 ''fuller'':22 ''general'':5 ''give'':36 ''head'':6,16,68 ''hundr'':49 ''iraelit'':80 ''king'':58 ''list'':9 ''number'':78 ''offic'':52 ''omit'':41 ''probabl'':53 ''record'':63 ''scribe'':54 ''serv'':56 ''taken'':19 ''thousand'':45 ''us'':37 ''vers'':2 ''whereof'':31 ''writer'':33', NULL, '365');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('366', '2', '366', NULL, 'Officer - literally, as in the margin. This is the only occasion in which eunuchs are mentioned in connection with David''s reign; and it is to be remarked that they occupy, during the earlier period of the Jewish kingdom, a very subordinate position.', NULL, NULL, '''connect'':18 ''david'':20 ''earlier'':34 ''eunuch'':14 ''jewish'':38 ''kingdom'':39 ''liter'':2 ''margin'':6 ''mention'':16 ''occas'':11 ''occupi'':31 ''offic'':1 ''period'':35 ''posit'':43 ''reign'':22 ''remark'':28 ''subordin'':42', NULL, '366');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('367', '2', '367', NULL, 'The palace - The original word here used is the Hebrew form of a Persian word, and generally designates the residence of the Persian monarch Esther 1:2, Esther 1:5; Esther 2:3, Esther 2:8; Nehemiah 1:1; Daniel 8:2. It is only here and in 1 Chronicles 29:19 that it is applied to the temple.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':26,29,38,39,49 ''19'':52 ''2'':27,32,35,42 ''29'':51 ''3'':33 ''5'':30 ''8'':36,41 ''appli'':56 ''chronicl'':50 ''daniel'':40 ''design'':18 ''esther'':25,28,31,34 ''form'':11 ''general'':17 ''hebrew'':10 ''monarch'':24 ''nehemiah'':37 ''origin'':4 ''palac'':2 ''persian'':14,23 ''resid'':20 ''templ'':59 ''use'':7 ''word'':5,15', NULL, '367');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('368', '2', '368', NULL, 'The narrative here corresponds with 1 Kings 3:4; but is very much fuller. We learn from the present passage:(1) that Solomon''s sacrifice at Gibeon was a great public festivity, to which he collected vast numbers of the people;(2) that it was made upon the brass altar of Bezaleel, which(3) stood before the tabernacle; and(4) that Solomon''s vision was on the night of his sacrifice. Consult the marginal references', NULL, NULL, '''1'':6,21 ''2'':42 ''3'':8,54 ''4'':9,60 ''altar'':50 ''bezaleel'':52 ''brass'':49 ''collect'':36 ''consult'':72 ''correspond'':4 ''festiv'':32 ''fuller'':14 ''gibeon'':27 ''great'':30 ''king'':7 ''learn'':16 ''made'':46 ''margin'':74 ''much'':13 ''narrat'':2 ''night'':68 ''number'':38 ''passag'':20 ''peopl'':41 ''present'':19 ''public'':31 ''refer'':75 ''sacrific'':25,71 ''solomon'':23,62 ''stood'':55 ''tabernacl'':58 ''upon'':47 ''vast'':37 ''vision'':64', NULL, '368');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('369', '2', '369', NULL, 'Huram, the form used throughout Chronicles (except 1 Chronicles 14:1) for the name both of the king and of the artisan whom he lent to Solomon 2 Chronicles 2:13; 2 Chronicles 4:11, 2 Chronicles 4:16, is a late corruption of the true native word, Hiram (marginal note and reference).', NULL, NULL, '''1'':8,11 ''11'':35 ''13'':31 ''14'':10 ''16'':39 ''2'':28,30,32,36 ''4'':34,38 ''artisan'':22 ''chronicl'':6,9,29,33,37 ''corrupt'':43 ''except'':7 ''form'':3 ''hiram'':49 ''huram'':1 ''king'':18 ''late'':42 ''lent'':25 ''margin'':50 ''name'':14 ''nativ'':47 ''note'':51 ''refer'':53 ''solomon'':27 ''throughout'':5 ''true'':46 ''use'':4 ''word'':48', NULL, '369');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('370', '2', '370', NULL, 'Where the Lord appeared unto David - The marginal rendering, or "which was shown to David," is preferred by some; and the expression is understood to point out to David the proper site for the temple by the appearance of the Angels and the command to build an altar 2 Samuel 24:17-25; 1 Chronicles 21:16-26.In the place that David had prepared - This seems to be the true meaning of the passage, though the order of the words in the original has been accidentally deranged.', NULL, NULL, '''-25'':53 ''1'':54 ''16-26.in'':57 ''17'':52 ''2'':49 ''21'':56 ''24'':51 ''accident'':85 ''altar'':48 ''angel'':41 ''appear'':4,38 ''build'':46 ''chronicl'':55 ''command'':44 ''david'':6,15,29,61 ''derang'':86 ''express'':22 ''lord'':3 ''margin'':8 ''mean'':70 ''order'':76 ''origin'':82 ''passag'':73 ''place'':59 ''point'':26 ''prefer'':17 ''prepar'':63 ''proper'':31 ''render'':9 ''samuel'':50 ''seem'':65 ''shown'':13 ''site'':32 ''templ'':35 ''though'':74 ''true'':69 ''understood'':24 ''unto'':5 ''word'':79', NULL, '370');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('371', '2', '371', NULL, 'The supplementary character of Chronicles is here once more apparent. The author of Kings had omitted to record the dimensions of the brass altar. It stood in the great court 2 Chronicles 6:12-13.', NULL, NULL, '''-13'':35 ''12'':34 ''2'':31 ''6'':33 ''altar'':24 ''appar'':10 ''author'':12 ''brass'':23 ''charact'':3 ''chronicl'':5,32 ''court'':30 ''dimens'':20 ''great'':29 ''king'':14 ''omit'':16 ''record'':18 ''stood'':26 ''supplementari'':2', NULL, '371');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('372', '2', '372', NULL, 'This chapter contains one important addition only to the narrative of Kings (see the marginal references); namely, the account of the circumstances under which the manifestation of the Divine Presence took place 2 Chronicles 5:11-13.', NULL, NULL, '''-13'':37 ''11'':36 ''2'':33 ''5'':35 ''account'':19 ''addit'':6 ''chapter'':2 ''chronicl'':34 ''circumst'':22 ''contain'':3 ''divin'':29 ''import'':5 ''king'':12 ''manifest'':26 ''margin'':15 ''name'':17 ''narrat'':10 ''one'':4 ''place'':32 ''presenc'':30 ''refer'':16 ''see'':13 ''took'':31', NULL, '372');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('373', '2', '373', NULL, 'In Kings, a different conclusion takes the place of these verses. The document from which both writers copied contained the full prayer of dedication, which each givcs in a somewhat abbreviated form.', NULL, NULL, '''abbrevi'':31 ''conclus'':5 ''contain'':19 ''copi'':18 ''dedic'':24 ''differ'':4 ''document'':13 ''form'':32 ''full'':21 ''givc'':27 ''king'':2 ''place'':8 ''prayer'':22 ''somewhat'':30 ''take'':6 ''vers'':11 ''writer'':17', NULL, '373');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('374', '2', '374', NULL, 'The fire came down from heaven - As in the time of Moses on the dedication of the tabernacle Leviticus 9:24 The fact is omitted from the narrative of Kings; but omission is not contradiction.', NULL, NULL, '''24'':21 ''9'':20 ''came'':3 ''contradict'':35 ''dedic'':15 ''fact'':23 ''fire'':2 ''heaven'':6 ''king'':30 ''leviticus'':19 ''mose'':12 ''narrat'':28 ''omiss'':32 ''omit'':25 ''tabernacl'':18 ''time'':10', NULL, '374');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('375', '2', '375', NULL, 'The cities which Huram had restored to Solomon - These cities had not been mentioned previously by the writer of Chronicles, who, however, seems to assume that the fact of their having been given by Hiram to Solomon is known to his readers. See 1 Kings 9:11-13.', NULL, NULL, '''-13'':48 ''1'':44 ''11'':47 ''9'':46 ''assum'':25 ''chronicl'':20 ''citi'':2,10 ''fact'':28 ''given'':33 ''hiram'':35 ''howev'':22 ''huram'':4 ''king'':45 ''known'':39 ''mention'':14 ''previous'':15 ''reader'':42 ''restor'':6 ''see'':43 ''seem'':23 ''solomon'':8,37 ''writer'':18', NULL, '375');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('377', '2', '377', NULL, 'Notes on the Bible by Albert Barnes [1834].
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('378', '2', '378', NULL, 'Rehoboam was between two dangers: on the north he might be attacked by Jeroboam, on the south by Jeroboam''s ally, Egypt. From this side was the greater peril, and therefore out of the 15 cities fortified, all but three were on the southern or western frontier, where Egypt would be most likely to attack.', NULL, NULL, '''15'':35 ''alli'':21 ''attack'':12,55 ''citi'':36 ''danger'':5 ''egypt'':22,49 ''fortifi'':37 ''frontier'':47 ''greater'':28 ''jeroboam'':14,19 ''like'':53 ''might'':10 ''north'':8 ''peril'':29 ''rehoboam'':1 ''side'':25 ''south'':17 ''southern'':44 ''therefor'':31 ''three'':40 ''two'':4 ''western'':46 ''would'':50', NULL, '378');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('379', '2', '379', NULL, 'Shishak ... came up ... because they had transgressed - The writer speaks from a divine, not a human, point of view. Shishak''s motive in coming up was to help Jeroboam, and to extend his own influence.', NULL, NULL, '''came'':2 ''come'':24 ''divin'':13 ''extend'':32 ''help'':28 ''human'':16 ''influenc'':35 ''jeroboam'':29 ''motiv'':22 ''point'':17 ''shishak'':1,20 ''speak'':10 ''transgress'':7 ''view'':19 ''writer'':9', NULL, '379');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('380', '2', '380', NULL, 'The history of Abijah''s reign is here related far more fully than in Kings (marginal reference), especially as regards his war with Jeroboam.', NULL, NULL, '''abijah'':4 ''especi'':18 ''far'':10 ''fulli'':12 ''histori'':2 ''jeroboam'':24 ''king'':15 ''margin'':16 ''refer'':17 ''regard'':20 ''reign'':6 ''relat'':9 ''war'':22', NULL, '380');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('381', '2', '381', NULL, 'Asa his son reigned - If Rehoboam was (1 Kings 12:8 note) not more than 21 years old at his accession, Asa, when he mounted the throne, must have been a mere boy, not more than 10 or 11 years of age.The land was quiet ten years - The great blow struck by Abijah 2 Chronicles 13:15-19, his alliance with Syria 1 Kings 15:19, and the rapid succession of sovereigns in Israel during the earlier part of Asa''s reign 1 Kings 15:25-33, would naturally prevent disturbance on the part of the northern kingdom. The tender age of Asa himself would be a bar to warlike enterprises on the part of Judah.', NULL, NULL, '''-19'':58 ''-33'':87 ''1'':8,63,83 ''10'':37 ''11'':39 ''12'':10 ''13'':56 ''15'':57,65,85 ''19'':66 ''2'':54 ''21'':16 ''25'':86 ''8'':11 ''abijah'':53 ''access'':21 ''age'':101 ''age.the'':42 ''allianc'':60 ''asa'':1,22,80,103 ''bar'':108 ''blow'':50 ''boy'':33 ''chronicl'':55 ''disturb'':91 ''earlier'':77 ''enterpris'':111 ''great'':49 ''israel'':74 ''judah'':116 ''king'':9,64,84 ''kingdom'':98 ''land'':43 ''mere'':32 ''mount'':25 ''must'':28 ''natur'':89 ''northern'':97 ''note'':12 ''old'':18 ''part'':78,94,114 ''prevent'':90 ''quiet'':45 ''rapid'':69 ''rehoboam'':6 ''reign'':4,82 ''son'':3 ''sovereign'':72 ''struck'':51 ''success'':70 ''syria'':62 ''ten'':46 ''tender'':100 ''throne'':27 ''warlik'':110 ''would'':88,105 ''year'':17,40,47', NULL, '381');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('382', '2', '382', NULL, 'Oded is by some identified with Iddo, the prophet and historian of the two preceding reigns. In the Hebrew the two names differ very slightly.', NULL, NULL, '''differ'':23 ''hebrew'':19 ''historian'':11 ''iddo'':7 ''identifi'':5 ''name'':22 ''ode'':1 ''preced'':15 ''prophet'':9 ''reign'':16 ''slight'':25 ''two'':14,21', NULL, '382');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('383', '2', '383', NULL, 'Abel-maim - or, "Abel-beth-maachah" 1 Kings 15:20. It was one of the towns most exposed to attack when an invader entered Israel from the north, and was taken from Pekah by Tiglath-pileser 2 Kings 15:29.Store cities - See 1 Kings 9:19 note.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':9,46 ''15'':11,42 ''19'':49 ''2'':40 ''20'':12 ''29.store'':43 ''9'':48 ''abel'':2,6 ''abel-beth-maachah'':5 ''abel-maim'':1 ''attack'':22 ''beth'':7 ''citi'':44 ''enter'':26 ''expos'':20 ''invad'':25 ''israel'':27 ''king'':10,41,47 ''maachah'':8 ''maim'':3 ''north'':30 ''note'':50 ''one'':15 ''pekah'':35 ''piles'':39 ''see'':45 ''taken'':33 ''tiglath'':38 ''tiglath-piles'':37 ''town'':18', NULL, '383');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('384', '2', '384', NULL, 'Jehoshaphat ascended the throne in the fourth year of Ahab 1 Kings 22:41, probably after that monarch had contracted his alliance with the royal family of Sidon, and before he was engaged in war with Syria. It was thus not unnatural that Jehoshaphat should begin his reign by strengthening himself against a possible attack on the part of his northern neighbor.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':11 ''22'':13 ''41'':14 ''ahab'':10 ''allianc'':22 ''ascend'':2 ''attack'':55 ''begin'':46 ''contract'':20 ''engag'':33 ''famili'':26 ''fourth'':7 ''jehoshaphat'':1,44 ''king'':12 ''monarch'':18 ''neighbor'':62 ''northern'':61 ''part'':58 ''possibl'':54 ''probabl'':15 ''reign'':48 ''royal'':25 ''sidon'':28 ''strengthen'':50 ''syria'':37 ''throne'':4 ''thus'':40 ''unnatur'':42 ''war'':35 ''year'':8', NULL, '384');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('385', '2', '385', NULL, 'After certain years - In Jehoshaphat''s 17th year 1 Kings 22:51, not less than 8 years after the marriage (marginal reference note).', NULL, NULL, '''1'':9 ''17th'':7 ''22'':11 ''51'':12 ''8'':16 ''certain'':2 ''jehoshaphat'':5 ''king'':10 ''less'':14 ''margin'':21 ''marriag'':20 ''note'':23 ''refer'':22 ''year'':3,8,17', NULL, '385');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('386', '2', '386', NULL, 'Jehoshaphat ... returned to his house in peace - With the battle of Ramoth-Gilead, and the death of Ahab, the war came to an end. The combined attack of the two kings having failed, their troops had been withdrawn, and the enterprise in which they had joined relinquished. The Syrians, satisfied with their victory, did not press on the retreating foe, or carry the war into their enemies'' country.', NULL, NULL, '''ahab'':19 ''attack'':28 ''battl'':10 ''came'':22 ''carri'':63 ''combin'':27 ''countri'':69 ''death'':17 ''end'':25 ''enemi'':68 ''enterpris'':42 ''fail'':34 ''foe'':61 ''gilead'':14 ''hous'':5 ''jehoshaphat'':1 ''join'':47 ''king'':32 ''peac'':7 ''press'':57 ''ramoth'':13 ''ramoth-gilead'':12 ''relinquish'':48 ''retreat'':60 ''return'':2 ''satisfi'':51 ''syrian'':50 ''troop'':36 ''two'':31 ''victori'':54 ''war'':21,65 ''withdrawn'':39', NULL, '386');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('387', '2', '387', NULL, 'The present Hebrew (and English) text mentions the Ammonites twice over. Hence, some adopt a different reading and translate "the children of Ammon, and with them certain of the Maonites," etc. Compare 2 Chronicles 20:10, note; Judges 10:12, note; 1 Chronicles 4:41, note.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':42 ''10'':36,39 ''12'':40 ''2'':33 ''20'':35 ''4'':44 ''41'':45 ''adopt'':14 ''ammon'':23 ''ammonit'':9 ''certain'':27 ''children'':21 ''chronicl'':34,43 ''compar'':32 ''differ'':16 ''english'':5 ''etc'':31 ''hebrew'':3 ''henc'':12 ''judg'':38 ''maonit'':30 ''mention'':7 ''note'':37,41,46 ''present'':2 ''read'':17 ''text'':6 ''translat'':19 ''twice'':10', NULL, '387');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('388', '2', '388', NULL, 'Jehoram''s sole reign now began. (See 2 Kings 8:16 note). His eight years 2 Chronicles 21:5 must be counted from the time of his association, in his father''s 23rd year.', NULL, NULL, '''16'':11 ''2'':8,16 ''21'':18 ''23rd'':33 ''5'':19 ''8'':10 ''associ'':28 ''began'':6 ''chronicl'':17 ''count'':22 ''eight'':14 ''father'':31 ''jehoram'':1 ''king'':9 ''must'':20 ''note'':12 ''reign'':4 ''see'':7 ''sole'':3 ''time'':25 ''year'':15,34', NULL, '388');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('389', '2', '389', NULL, 'For "42" read "22" (see the marginal reference). Ahaziah''s father, Jehoram, was but 40 when be died 2 Chronicles 21:20.', NULL, NULL, '''2'':19 ''20'':22 ''21'':21 ''22'':4 ''40'':15 ''42'':2 ''ahaziah'':9 ''chronicl'':20 ''die'':18 ''father'':11 ''jehoram'':12 ''margin'':7 ''read'':3 ''refer'':8 ''see'':5', NULL, '389');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('390', '2', '390', NULL, 'The five names do not occur in Kings; only, and incidentally, the five divisions of the royal guard (2 Kings 11:5 note).', NULL, NULL, '''11'':21 ''2'':19 ''5'':22 ''divis'':14 ''five'':2,13 ''guard'':18 ''incident'':11 ''king'':8,20 ''name'':3 ''note'':23 ''occur'':6 ''royal'':17', NULL, '390');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('391', '2', '391', NULL, 'This chapter is parallel with 2 Kings 12, but treats the matters common to both narratives in a different and, apparently, supplemental way.', NULL, NULL, '''12'':8 ''2'':6 ''appar'':21 ''chapter'':2 ''common'':13 ''differ'':19 ''king'':7 ''matter'':12 ''narrat'':16 ''parallel'':4 ''supplement'':22 ''treat'':10 ''way'':23', NULL, '391');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('392', '2', '392', NULL, 'This chapter is evidently taken to a large extent from the same document as Kings (see the marginal reference and the notes). At the same time it contains large and important additions; e. g. 2 Chronicles 25:5-10, 2 Chronicles 25:13-16.', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':39 ''-16'':44 ''13'':43 ''2'':35,40 ''25'':37,42 ''5'':38 ''addit'':32 ''chapter'':2 ''chronicl'':36,41 ''contain'':28 ''document'':13 ''e'':33 ''evid'':4 ''extent'':9 ''g'':34 ''import'':31 ''king'':15 ''larg'':8,29 ''margin'':18 ''note'':22 ''refer'':19 ''see'':16 ''taken'':5 ''time'':26', NULL, '392');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('393', '2', '393', NULL, 'Uzziah - This form of the name is found uniformly in Chronicles (except 1 Chronicles 3:12) and in the prophets. The writer of Kings prefers the form Azariah. Uzziah has been regarded as a phonetic corruption of the real name used by the common people.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':13 ''12'':16 ''3'':15 ''azariah'':28 ''chronicl'':11,14 ''common'':44 ''corrupt'':36 ''except'':12 ''form'':3,27 ''found'':8 ''king'':24 ''name'':6,40 ''peopl'':45 ''phonet'':35 ''prefer'':25 ''prophet'':20 ''real'':39 ''regard'':32 ''uniform'':9 ''use'':41 ''uzziah'':1,29 ''writer'':22', NULL, '393');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('394', '2', '394', NULL, 'This short chapter runs parallel with 2 Kings marginal reference), and is taken mainly from the same source or sources which it amplifies.', NULL, NULL, '''2'':7 ''amplifi'':23 ''chapter'':3 ''king'':8 ''main'':14 ''margin'':9 ''parallel'':5 ''refer'':10 ''run'':4 ''short'':2 ''sourc'':18,20 ''taken'':13', NULL, '394');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('395', '2', '395', NULL, 'This chapter is supplemental in character, The writer seems to assume that the narrative of Kings (marginal reference) is known, and is mainly anxious to add points which the author of that narrative has omitted.', NULL, NULL, '''add'':26 ''anxious'':24 ''assum'':11 ''author'':30 ''chapter'':2 ''charact'':6 ''king'':16 ''known'':20 ''main'':23 ''margin'':17 ''narrat'':14,33 ''omit'':35 ''point'':27 ''refer'':18 ''seem'':9 ''supplement'':4 ''writer'':8', NULL, '395');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('396', '2', '396', NULL, 'The treatment of Hezekiah''s reign by the author of Chronicles is in marked contrast with that followed in the Book of Kings. The writer of Kings describes mainly civil affairs; the author of Chronicles gives a full account of Hezekiah''s religious reformation. 2 Chronicles 29-31 contain matter, therefore, which is almost wholly new.', NULL, NULL, '''-31'':48 ''2'':45 ''29'':47 ''account'':39 ''affair'':31 ''almost'':54 ''author'':9,33 ''book'':21 ''chronicl'':11,35,46 ''civil'':30 ''contain'':49 ''contrast'':15 ''describ'':28 ''follow'':18 ''full'':38 ''give'':36 ''hezekiah'':4,41 ''king'':23,27 ''main'':29 ''mark'':14 ''matter'':50 ''new'':56 ''reform'':44 ''reign'':6 ''religi'':43 ''therefor'':51 ''treatment'':2 ''wholli'':55 ''writer'':25', NULL, '396');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('397', '2', '397', NULL, 'In the second month - Hezekiah and his counselors considered that the permission of the Law (see the marginal reference) might, under the circumstances, be extended to the whole people. It had been found impossible to complete the cleansing of the temple until the fourteenth day of the first month was past 2 Chronicles 29:17. It was, therefore, determined to defer it to the 14th of the second month, which allowed time for the priests generally to purify themselves, and for proclamation of the festival to be made throughout all Israel.', NULL, NULL, '''14th'':65 ''17'':55 ''2'':52 ''29'':54 ''allow'':71 ''chronicl'':53 ''circumst'':23 ''cleans'':38 ''complet'':36 ''consid'':9 ''counselor'':8 ''day'':45 ''defer'':61 ''determin'':59 ''extend'':25 ''festiv'':85 ''first'':48 ''found'':33 ''fourteenth'':44 ''general'':76 ''hezekiah'':5 ''imposs'':34 ''israel'':91 ''law'':15 ''made'':88 ''margin'':18 ''might'':20 ''month'':4,49,69 ''past'':51 ''peopl'':29 ''permiss'':12 ''priest'':75 ''proclam'':82 ''purifi'':78 ''refer'':19 ''second'':3,68 ''see'':16 ''templ'':41 ''therefor'':58 ''throughout'':89 ''time'':72 ''whole'':28', NULL, '397');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('398', '2', '398', NULL, 'Jerusalem had been cleansed 2 Chronicles 30:14; now the land had to be purged. Hezekiah therefore gave his sanction to a popular movement directed as much against the "high places" which had been maintained since the times of the patriarchs, as against the remnants of the Baal-worship, or the innovations of Ahaz. See 2 Kings 18:4 note. The invasion of the northern kingdom "Ephraim and Manasseh" by a tumultuous crowd from the southern one, and the success which attended the movement, can only be explained by the state of weakness into which the northern kingdom had fallen (see the note at 2 Chronicles 29:24).', NULL, NULL, '''14'':8 ''18'':59 ''2'':5,57,106 ''24'':109 ''29'':108 ''30'':7 ''4'':60 ''ahaz'':55 ''attend'':83 ''baal'':49 ''baal-worship'':48 ''chronicl'':6,107 ''cleans'':4 ''crowd'':74 ''direct'':25 ''ephraim'':68 ''explain'':89 ''fallen'':101 ''gave'':18 ''hezekiah'':16 ''high'':30 ''innov'':53 ''invas'':63 ''jerusalem'':1 ''king'':58 ''kingdom'':67,99 ''land'':11 ''maintain'':35 ''manasseh'':70 ''movement'':24,85 ''much'':27 ''northern'':66,98 ''note'':61,104 ''one'':78 ''patriarch'':41 ''place'':31 ''popular'':23 ''purg'':15 ''remnant'':45 ''sanction'':20 ''see'':56,102 ''sinc'':36 ''southern'':77 ''state'':92 ''success'':81 ''therefor'':17 ''time'':38 ''tumultu'':73 ''weak'':94 ''worship'':50', NULL, '398');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('399', '2', '399', NULL, 'The establishment thereof - literally, "the faithfulness thereof" or, in other words, "after these things had been faithfully accomplished."2 Chronicles 32:1-8 form a passage supplementary to 2 Kings 18:13-16.', NULL, NULL, '''-16'':33 ''-8'':23 ''1'':22 ''13'':32 ''18'':31 ''2'':19,29 ''32'':21 ''accomplish'':18 ''chronicl'':20 ''establish'':2 ''faith'':6,17 ''form'':24 ''king'':30 ''liter'':4 ''passag'':26 ''supplementari'':27 ''thereof'':3,7 ''thing'':14 ''word'':11', NULL, '399');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('400', '2', '400', NULL, 'Compare references and notes. The author of Chronicles differs chiefly from Kings in additions (see the 2 Kings 21:17 note). The central part of this chapter (2 Chronicles 33:11-19) is almost entirely new matter.', NULL, NULL, '''-19'':32 ''11'':31 ''17'':20 ''2'':17,28 ''21'':19 ''33'':30 ''addit'':14 ''almost'':34 ''author'':6 ''central'':23 ''chapter'':27 ''chiefli'':10 ''chronicl'':8,29 ''compar'':1 ''differ'':9 ''entir'':35 ''king'':12,18 ''matter'':37 ''new'':36 ''note'':4,21 ''part'':24 ''refer'':2 ''see'':15', NULL, '400');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('401', '2', '401', NULL, 'Compare the parallel history of 2 Kings 22 notes; 2 Kings 23:1-30 notes; the writer here being more full on the celebration of the Passover. The only approach to a discrepancy between the two narratives is with respect to the time of the religions reformation, which the writer of Chronicles distinctly places before, the author of Kings after, the repair of the temple. The best explanation seems to be, that the author of Kings has departed from the chronological order, to which he makes no profession of adhering.', NULL, NULL, '''-30'':14 ''1'':13 ''2'':6,10 ''22'':8 ''23'':12 ''adher'':90 ''approach'':30 ''author'':57,74 ''best'':67 ''celebr'':24 ''chronicl'':52 ''chronolog'':81 ''compar'':1 ''depart'':78 ''discrep'':33 ''distinct'':53 ''explan'':68 ''full'':21 ''histori'':4 ''king'':7,11,59,76 ''make'':86 ''narrat'':37 ''note'':9,15 ''order'':82 ''parallel'':3 ''passov'':27 ''place'':54 ''profess'':88 ''reform'':47 ''religion'':46 ''repair'':62 ''respect'':40 ''seem'':69 ''templ'':65 ''time'':43 ''two'':36 ''writer'':17,50', NULL, '401');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('402', '2', '402', NULL, 'Put the holy ark etc - The ark of the covenant may have been temporarily removed from the holy of holies while Josiah effected necessary repairs.It shall not be a burden upon your shoulders - The removing and replacing the ark Josiah means "shall not henceforth be your duty. The ark shall remain undisturbed in the holy of holies. You shall return to your old employments, to the service of God and the instruction of the people."', NULL, NULL, '''ark'':4,7,39,49 ''burden'':30 ''coven'':10 ''duti'':47 ''effect'':23 ''employ'':64 ''etc'':5 ''god'':69 ''henceforth'':44 ''holi'':3,18,20,55,57 ''instruct'':72 ''josiah'':22,40 ''may'':11 ''mean'':41 ''necessari'':24 ''old'':63 ''peopl'':75 ''put'':1 ''remain'':51 ''remov'':15,35 ''repairs.it'':25 ''replac'':37 ''return'':60 ''servic'':67 ''shall'':26,42,50,59 ''shoulder'':33 ''temporarili'':14 ''undisturb'':52 ''upon'':31', NULL, '402');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('403', '2', '403', NULL, 'The narrative runs parallel with 2 Kings marginal reference) as far as 2 Chronicles 36:13. The writer then emits the events following, and substitutes a sketch in which the moral and didactic element preponderates over the historical.', NULL, NULL, '''13'':16 ''2'':6,13 ''36'':15 ''chronicl'':14 ''didact'':33 ''element'':34 ''emit'':20 ''event'':22 ''far'':11 ''follow'':23 ''histor'':38 ''king'':7 ''margin'':8 ''moral'':31 ''narrat'':2 ''parallel'':4 ''preponder'':35 ''refer'':9 ''run'':3 ''sketch'':27 ''substitut'':25 ''writer'':18', NULL, '403');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('404', '2', '404', NULL, 'By the first year of Cyrus is to be understood the first year of his sovereignty over the Jews, or 538 B.C.', NULL, NULL, '''538'':21 ''b.c'':22 ''cyrus'':6 ''first'':3,12 ''jew'':19 ''sovereignti'':16 ''understood'':10 ''year'':4,13', NULL, '404');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('405', '2', '405', NULL, 'The province - Judaea was no longer a kingdom, but a mere "province" of Persia. "The children of the province" are the Israelites who returned to Palestine, as distinct from those who remained in Babylonia and Persia.Every one unto his city - That is, to the city whereto his forefathers had belonged. Of course, in the few cases where this was not known Ezra 2:59-62, the plan could not be carried out.Two other copies of the following list have come down to us - one in Nehemiah 7:7-69, and the other in 1 Esdras 5:8-43. All seem to have been taken from the same original document, and to have suffered more or less from corruption. Where two out of the three agree, the reading should prevail over that of the third.', NULL, NULL, '''-43'':98 ''-62'':65 ''-69'':89 ''1'':94 ''2'':63 ''5'':96 ''59'':64 ''7'':87,88 ''8'':97 ''agre'':125 ''babylonia'':34 ''belong'':50 ''carri'':71 ''case'':56 ''children'':16 ''citi'':40,45 ''come'':80 ''copi'':74 ''corrupt'':118 ''could'':68 ''cours'':52 ''distinct'':28 ''document'':109 ''esdra'':95 ''ezra'':62 ''follow'':77 ''forefath'':48 ''israelit'':22 ''judaea'':3 ''kingdom'':8 ''known'':61 ''less'':116 ''list'':78 ''longer'':6 ''mere'':11 ''nehemiah'':86 ''one'':37,84 ''origin'':108 ''out.two'':72 ''palestin'':26 ''persia'':14 ''persia.every'':36 ''plan'':67 ''prevail'':129 ''provinc'':2,12,19 ''read'':127 ''remain'':32 ''return'':24 ''seem'':100 ''suffer'':113 ''taken'':104 ''third'':134 ''three'':124 ''two'':120 ''unto'':38 ''us'':83 ''whereto'':46', NULL, '405');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('406', '2', '406', NULL, 'The seventh month - i. e., the month Tisri (nearly our September), the most sacred month in the Jewish year Exodus 23:16; Leviticus 23:24-41.', NULL, NULL, '''-41'':26 ''16'':22 ''23'':21,24 ''24'':25 ''e'':5 ''exodus'':20 ''jewish'':18 ''leviticus'':23 ''month'':3,7,15 ''near'':9 ''sacr'':14 ''septemb'':11 ''seventh'':2 ''tisri'':8 ''year'':19', NULL, '406');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('407', '2', '407', NULL, 'Adversaries - i. e., the Samaritans, a mixed race, partly Israelite but chiefly foreign, which had replaced to some extent the ancient inhabitants after they were carried into captivity by Sargon (see 2 Kings 17:6 note).', NULL, NULL, '''17'':34 ''2'':32 ''6'':35 ''adversari'':1 ''ancient'':21 ''captiv'':28 ''carri'':26 ''chiefli'':12 ''e'':3 ''extent'':19 ''foreign'':13 ''inhabit'':22 ''israelit'':10 ''king'':33 ''mix'':7 ''note'':36 ''part'':9 ''race'':8 ''replac'':16 ''samaritan'':5 ''sargon'':30 ''see'':31', NULL, '407');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('408', '2', '408', NULL, 'Haggai and Zechariah stirred up Zerubbabel and Joshua Ezr 5:2; Haggai 1:14, and warned the people against neglecting the building of the temple, in order to give themselves to the beautifying of their own houses (see Haggai 1:4, Haggai 1:9). Zechariah was the son of Berechiah, and grandson of Iddo (see the marginal reference; Matthew 23:35). Compare a similar application of "son" in the case of Jehu (see the 2 Kings 9:20 note).In the name of the God of Israel, even unto them - Rather, "in the name of the God of Israel, which was upon them." The two prophets addressed the Jews, in respect of their being God''s people, or, in Hebrew phrase (see the Jeremiah 15:16 margin), "having God''s name called upon them."', NULL, NULL, '''1'':13,40,43 ''14'':14 ''15'':125 ''16'':126 ''2'':11,75 ''20'':78 ''23'':60 ''35'':61 ''4'':41 ''5'':10 ''9'':44,77 ''address'':107 ''applic'':65 ''beautifi'':33 ''berechiah'':50 ''build'':22 ''call'':132 ''case'':70 ''compar'':62 ''even'':88 ''ezr'':9 ''give'':29 ''god'':85,97,115,129 ''grandson'':52 ''haggai'':1,12,39,42 ''hebrew'':120 ''hous'':37 ''iddo'':54 ''israel'':87,99 ''jehu'':72 ''jeremiah'':124 ''jew'':109 ''joshua'':8 ''king'':76 ''margin'':57,127 ''matthew'':59 ''name'':82,94,131 ''neglect'':20 ''note'':79 ''order'':27 ''peopl'':18,117 ''phrase'':121 ''prophet'':106 ''rather'':91 ''refer'':58 ''respect'':111 ''see'':38,55,73,122 ''similar'':64 ''son'':48,67 ''stir'':4 ''templ'':25 ''two'':105 ''unto'':89 ''upon'':102,133 ''warn'':16 ''zechariah'':3,45 ''zerubbabel'':6', NULL, '408');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('409', '2', '409', NULL, 'A "house of the rolls" was discovered at Koyunjik, the ancient Nineveh, in 1850 - a set of chambers, i. e., in the palace devoted exclusively to the storing of public documents. These were in baked clay, and covered the floor to the depth of more than a foot. Such a "house" was probably that at Babylon.', NULL, NULL, '''1850'':14 ''ancient'':11 ''babylon'':56 ''bake'':35 ''chamber'':18 ''clay'':36 ''cover'':38 ''depth'':43 ''devot'':24 ''discov'':7 ''document'':31 ''e'':20 ''exclus'':25 ''floor'':40 ''foot'':48 ''hous'':2,51 ''koyunjik'':9 ''nineveh'':12 ''palac'':23 ''probabl'':53 ''public'':30 ''roll'':5 ''set'':16 ''store'':28', NULL, '409');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('410', '2', '410', NULL, 'After these things - The words mark an interval of 57 years; if, with most commentators, we take Artaxerxes to be Longimanus. See the introduction to the Book of Ezra. Three kings named Artaxerxes, the Greek rendering of the Hebrew Artakhshasta, and the Persian Artakhshatra, ruled over Persia, namely,: Longimanus, Mnemon, and Ochus. The evidence is in favor of the first being meant here: he was the grandson of Darius Hystaspis, Jeshua''s contemporary.The genealogy of Ezra here is incomplete. The time between the Exodus and Ezra must have exceeded one thousand years, and cannot have been covered by 16 generations. One gap may be filled up from 1 Chronicles 6:7-10, which supplies six names between Meraioth and Azariah Ezra 7:3 : another gap probably occurs between Seraiah Ezra 7:1 and Ezra himself; since Seraiah appears to be the high priest of Zedekiah''s time (marginal reference), who lived at least 130 years before Ezra. Three or four names are probably missing in this place. Another name (Meraioth) may be supplied from 1 Chronicles 9:11, between Zadok and Ahitub Ezra 7:2. These additions would produce 27 generations - a number nearly sufficient - instead of 16 generations.', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':112 ''1'':108,132,175 ''11'':178 ''130'':154 ''16'':99,198 ''2'':185 ''27'':190 ''3'':123 ''57'':10 ''6'':110 ''7'':111,122,131,184 ''9'':177 ''addit'':187 ''ahitub'':182 ''anoth'':124,168 ''appear'':138 ''artakhshasta'':40 ''artakhshatra'':44 ''artaxerx'':18,33 ''azariah'':120 ''book'':27 ''cannot'':94 ''chronicl'':109,176 ''comment'':15 ''contemporary.the'':73 ''cover'':97 ''darius'':69 ''evid'':54 ''exceed'':89 ''exodus'':84 ''ezra'':29,76,86,121,130,134,157,183 ''favor'':57 ''fill'':105 ''first'':60 ''four'':160 ''gap'':102,125 ''genealog'':74 ''generat'':100,191,199 ''grandson'':67 ''greek'':35 ''hebrew'':39 ''high'':142 ''hystaspi'':70 ''incomplet'':79 ''instead'':196 ''interv'':8 ''introduct'':24 ''jeshua'':71 ''king'':31 ''least'':153 ''live'':151 ''longimanus'':21,49 ''margin'':148 ''mark'':6 ''may'':103,171 ''meant'':62 ''meraioth'':118,170 ''miss'':164 ''mnemon'':50 ''must'':87 ''name'':32,48,116,161,169 ''near'':194 ''number'':193 ''occur'':127 ''ochus'':52 ''one'':90,101 ''persia'':47 ''persian'':43 ''place'':167 ''priest'':143 ''probabl'':126,163 ''produc'':189 ''refer'':149 ''render'':36 ''rule'':45 ''see'':22 ''seraiah'':129,137 ''sinc'':136 ''six'':115 ''suffici'':195 ''suppli'':114,173 ''take'':17 ''thing'':3 ''thousand'':91 ''three'':30,158 ''time'':81,147 ''word'':5 ''would'':188 ''year'':11,92,155 ''zadok'':180 ''zedekiah'':145', NULL, '410');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('411', '2', '411', NULL, 'Punctuate as follows:Ezra 8:2. ... of the sons of David, Hattush of the sons of Shechaniah.Ezra 8:3. Of the sons of Pharosh, Zechariah ....Hattush, the descendant of David, was the grandson of Shechaniah (see marginal reference).Most of these names Ezra 8:2-14 occur also as those of heads of families in the list of the Jews who returned with Zerubbabel Ezra 2:3-15. The Septuagint and Syriac versions supply omissions in Ezra 8:5, Ezra 8:10.', NULL, NULL, '''-14'':46 ''-15'':68 ''10'':82 ''2'':6,45,66 ''3'':19,67 ''5'':79 ''8'':5,18,44,78,81 ''also'':48 ''david'':11,30 ''descend'':28 ''ezra'':4,43,65,77,80 ''famili'':54 ''follow'':3 ''grandson'':33 ''hattush'':12,26 ''head'':52 ''jew'':60 ''list'':57 ''margin'':37 ''name'':42 ''occur'':47 ''omiss'':75 ''pharosh'':24 ''punctuat'':1 ''refer'':38 ''return'':62 ''see'':36 ''septuagint'':70 ''shechaniah'':35 ''shechaniah.ezra'':17 ''son'':9,15,22 ''suppli'':74 ''syriac'':72 ''version'':73 ''zechariah'':25 ''zerubbabel'':64', NULL, '411');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('412', '2', '412', NULL, 'Abominations - The mixed marriages had prevented that complete separation of the people of God from the idolatrous rites, or "abominations," which the Law required, and which was necessary for purity of religion. See 1 Kings 11:2 note.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':34 ''11'':36 ''2'':37 ''abomin'':1,20 ''complet'':8 ''god'':14 ''idolatr'':17 ''king'':35 ''law'':23 ''marriag'':4 ''mix'':3 ''necessari'':28 ''note'':38 ''peopl'':12 ''prevent'':6 ''puriti'':30 ''religion'':32 ''requir'':24 ''rite'':18 ''see'':33 ''separ'':9', NULL, '412');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('413', '2', '413', NULL, 'Before the house of God - i. e., in front of the temple, praying toward it 1 Kings 8:30, 1 Kings 8:35; Daniel 6:10, and thus in the sight of all the people who happened at the time to be in the great court.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':16,20 ''10'':26 ''30'':19 ''35'':23 ''6'':25 ''8'':18,22 ''court'':46 ''daniel'':24 ''e'':7 ''front'':9 ''god'':5 ''great'':45 ''happen'':37 ''hous'':3 ''king'':17,21 ''peopl'':35 ''pray'':13 ''sight'':31 ''templ'':12 ''thus'':28 ''time'':40 ''toward'':14', NULL, '413');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('414', '2', '414', NULL, 'The words of Nehemiah the son of Hachaliah - The prophetical books commence generally with a title of this kind (see Jeremiah 1:1); but no other extant historical book begins thus. Nehemiah, while attaching his work to Ezra, perhaps marked in this manner the point at which his own composition commenced. (See the introduction of the Book of Nehemiah.)Chisleu - The ninth month, corresponding to the end of November and beginning of December.In the twentieth year - i. e. of Artaxerxes Longimanus (465-425 B.C.). Compare Nehemiah 2:1.Shushan the palace - Compare Esther 1:2, Esther 1:5, etc.; Daniel 8:2. Shushan, or Susa, was the ordinary residence of the Persian kings. "The palace" or acropolis was a distinct quarter of the city, occupying an artificial eminence.', NULL, NULL, '''-425'':83 ''1'':22,23,93,96 ''1.shushan'':88 ''2'':87,94,101 ''465'':82 ''5'':97 ''8'':100 ''acropoli'':116 ''artaxerx'':80 ''artifici'':126 ''attach'':34 ''b.c'':84 ''begin'':30,71 ''book'':11,29,57 ''chisleu'':60 ''citi'':123 ''commenc'':12,51 ''compar'':85,91 ''composit'':50 ''correspond'':64 ''daniel'':99 ''december.in'':73 ''distinct'':119 ''e'':78 ''emin'':127 ''end'':67 ''esther'':92,95 ''etc'':98 ''extant'':27 ''ezra'':38 ''general'':13 ''hachaliah'':8 ''histor'':28 ''introduct'':54 ''jeremiah'':21 ''kind'':19 ''king'':112 ''longimanus'':81 ''manner'':43 ''mark'':40 ''month'':63 ''nehemiah'':4,32,59,86 ''ninth'':62 ''novemb'':69 ''occupi'':124 ''ordinari'':107 ''palac'':90,114 ''perhap'':39 ''persian'':111 ''point'':45 ''prophet'':10 ''quarter'':120 ''resid'':108 ''see'':20,52 ''shushan'':102 ''son'':6 ''susa'':104 ''thus'':31 ''titl'':16 ''twentieth'':75 ''word'':2 ''work'':36 ''year'':76', NULL, '414');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('415', '2', '415', NULL, 'Nisan was the name given by the Persian Jews to the month previously called "Abib," the first month of the Jewish year, or that which followed the vernal equinox. It fell four months after Chisleu Nehemiah 1:1.The twentieth year - As Artaxerxes ascended the throne in 465 B.C., his 20th year would correspond to 445-444 B.C.', NULL, NULL, '''-444'':56 ''1'':37 ''1.the'':38 ''20th'':50 ''445'':55 ''465'':47 ''abib'':15 ''artaxerx'':42 ''ascend'':43 ''b.c'':48,57 ''call'':14 ''chisleu'':35 ''correspond'':53 ''equinox'':29 ''fell'':31 ''first'':17 ''follow'':26 ''four'':32 ''given'':5 ''jew'':9 ''jewish'':21 ''month'':12,18,33 ''name'':4 ''nehemiah'':36 ''nisan'':1 ''persian'':8 ''previous'':13 ''throne'':45 ''twentieth'':39 ''vernal'':28 ''would'':52 ''year'':22,40,51', NULL, '415');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('416', '2', '416', NULL, 'Bavai - Or, "Binnui" Nehemiah 3:24; Nehemiah 10:9.The armoury at the turning of the wall - literally, "the armoury of the corner." The northwestern corner of the special wall of the "city of David" seems to be intended. See Nehemiah 3:1 note.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':42 ''10'':8 ''24'':6 ''3'':5,41 ''9.the'':9 ''armouri'':10,19 ''bavai'':1 ''binnui'':3 ''citi'':32 ''corner'':22,25 ''david'':34 ''intend'':38 ''liter'':17 ''nehemiah'':4,7,40 ''northwestern'':24 ''note'':43 ''see'':39 ''seem'':35 ''special'':28 ''turn'':13 ''wall'':16,29', NULL, '416');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('417', '2', '417', NULL, 'The parenthetical prayers of Nehemiah form one of the most striking characteristics of his history. Here we have the first. Other examples are Nehemiah 5:19; Nehemiah 6:9, Nehemiah 6:14; Nehemiah 13:14, Nehemiah 13:22, Nehemiah 13:29, Nehemiah 13:31.', NULL, NULL, '''13'':34,37,40,43 ''14'':32,35 ''19'':26 ''22'':38 ''29'':41 ''31'':44 ''5'':25 ''6'':28,31 ''9'':29 ''characterist'':12 ''exampl'':22 ''first'':20 ''form'':6 ''histori'':15 ''nehemiah'':5,24,27,30,33,36,39,42 ''one'':7 ''parenthet'':2 ''prayer'':3 ''strike'':11', NULL, '417');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('418', '2', '418', NULL, 'Are many - A slight emendation brings this verse into exact parallelism with the next, and gives the sense - "We have pledged our sons and our daughters, that we might get corn, and eat and live." Compare Nehemiah 5:5.', NULL, NULL, '''5'':38,39 ''bring'':6 ''compar'':36 ''corn'':31 ''daughter'':26 ''eat'':33 ''emend'':5 ''exact'':10 ''get'':30 ''give'':16 ''live'':35 ''mani'':2 ''might'':29 ''nehemiah'':37 ''next'':14 ''parallel'':11 ''pledg'':21 ''sens'':18 ''slight'':4 ''son'':23 ''vers'':8', NULL, '418');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('419', '2', '419', NULL, 'Noadiah is not elsewhere mentioned. The examples of Miriam, Deborah, Huldah, and Anna, show that the prophetical gift was occasionally bestowed upon women (2 Kings 22:14 note).', NULL, NULL, '''14'':27 ''2'':24 ''22'':26 ''anna'':13 ''bestow'':21 ''deborah'':10 ''elsewher'':4 ''exampl'':7 ''gift'':18 ''huldah'':11 ''king'':25 ''mention'':5 ''miriam'':9 ''noadiah'':1 ''note'':28 ''occasion'':20 ''prophet'':17 ''show'':14 ''upon'':22 ''women'':23', NULL, '419');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('420', '2', '420', NULL, 'Since the watch of the temple had hitherto been kept by porters, singers, and Levites 1 Chronicles 26:1-19, so now the watch of the entire city was committed to men of the same three classes, their experience pointing them out as the most suitable persons.', NULL, NULL, '''-19'':20 ''1'':16,19 ''26'':18 ''chronicl'':17 ''citi'':28 ''class'':37 ''commit'':30 ''entir'':27 ''experi'':39 ''hitherto'':8 ''kept'':10 ''levit'':15 ''men'':32 ''person'':47 ''point'':40 ''porter'':12 ''sinc'':1 ''singer'':13 ''suitabl'':46 ''templ'':6 ''three'':36 ''watch'':3,24', NULL, '420');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('507', '2', '507', NULL, 'Give unto the Lord - Ascribe unto Yahweh; or, recognize Him as entitled to what is here ascribed to Him. The word cannot be understood, as it is commonly with us, to denote the imparting to another, or granting to another what he does not now possess - for God is always in possession of what is here ascribed to Him.O ye mighty - Margin, as in Hebrew, "ye sons of the mighty." The Hebrew word used here -   ''Eliym - is the plural form of one of the names of God -   ''El. The word means properly "strong, mighty, a mighty one, a hero;" then, "strength, might, power;" and then it is applied to God as "the Mighty One," the Almighty. ("Gesenius.") In the plural form, the word means "mighty ones, heroes, gods:" Exodus 15:11; Exodus 18:11; Daniel 11:36. The phrase "sons of the mighty" is used only here and in Psalm 89:6. The allusion is undoubtedly to the angels as being in an eminent sense the sons of God, or of the mighty ones; and they are referred to here under that appellation as being themselves endowed with power or strength. Compare Psalm 103:20, "Bless the Lord, ye his angels, that excel in strength;" margin: "mighty in strength." In view of the wonderful exhibitions of God''s power in the storm - exhibitions far above the power of the most exalted of His creatures, the psalmist calls upon the angels, the most exalted of them, to acknowledge the existence of a power so much beyond their own.Glory and strength - Majesty and might. Acknowledge Him as the God of glory; as endowed with power. That is, learn from the manifestations of the power evinced in the storm how great is the power and the glory of God.', NULL, NULL, '''103'':194 ''11'':131,134,136 ''15'':130 ''18'':133 ''20'':195 ''36'':137 ''6'':152 ''89'':151 ''acknowledg'':247,263 ''allus'':154 ''almighti'':116 ''alway'':50 ''angel'':159,201,240 ''anoth'':36,40 ''appel'':183 ''appli'':108 ''ascrib'':5,17,57 ''beyond'':255 ''bless'':196 ''call'':237 ''cannot'':22 ''common'':28 ''compar'':192 ''creatur'':234 ''daniel'':135 ''denot'':32 ''el'':88 ''eliym'':76 ''emin'':164 ''endow'':187,271 ''entitl'':12 ''evinc'':283 ''exalt'':231,243 ''excel'':203 ''exhibit'':215,223 ''exist'':249 ''exodus'':129,132 ''far'':224 ''form'':80,121 ''gesenius'':117 ''give'':1 ''glori'':269,294 ''god'':48,87,110,128,169,217,267,296 ''grant'':38 ''great'':288 ''hebrew'':65,72 ''hero'':99,127 ''him.o'':59 ''impart'':34 ''learn'':276 ''lord'':4,198 ''majesti'':260 ''manifest'':279 ''margin'':62,206 ''mean'':91,124 ''might'':102,262 ''mighti'':61,70,94,96,113,125,143,173,207 ''much'':254 ''name'':85 ''one'':82,97,114,126,174 ''own.glory'':257 ''phrase'':139 ''plural'':79,120 ''possess'':46,52 ''power'':103,189,219,227,252,273,282,291 ''proper'':92 ''psalm'':150,193 ''psalmist'':236 ''recogn'':9 ''refer'':178 ''sens'':165 ''son'':67,140,167 ''storm'':222,286 ''strength'':101,191,205,209,259 ''strong'':93 ''understood'':24 ''undoubt'':156 ''unto'':2,6 ''upon'':238 ''us'':30 ''use'':74,145 ''view'':211 ''wonder'':214 ''word'':21,73,90,123 ''yahweh'':7 ''ye'':60,66,199', NULL, '507');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('421', '2', '421', NULL, 'The street - Rather, "the square" or "court." So in Nehemiah 8:16 (compare Ezra 10:9). The court seems to have been one between the eastern gate of the temple and the watergate in the city-wall. It would thus lie within the modern Haram area.Ezra the scribe - This is the first mention of Ezra in the present book, and the first proof we have had that he was contemporary with Nehemiah. Probably he returned to the court of Artaxerxes soon after effecting the reforms which he relates in Ezra 10, and did not revisit Jerusalem until about the time when the walls were completed, or after an absence of more than ten years. It was natural for the people to request him to resume the work of exposition of the Law to which he had accustomed them on his former visit Ezra 7:10, Ezra 7:25.', NULL, NULL, '''10'':15,92,146 ''16'':12 ''25'':149 ''7'':145,148 ''8'':11 ''9'':16 ''absenc'':110 ''accustom'':138 ''area.ezra'':47 ''artaxerx'':81 ''book'':60 ''citi'':37 ''city-wal'':36 ''compar'':13 ''complet'':106 ''contemporari'':71 ''court'':7,18,79 ''eastern'':26 ''effect'':84 ''exposit'':130 ''ezra'':14,56,91,144,147 ''first'':53,63 ''former'':142 ''gate'':27 ''haram'':46 ''jerusalem'':97 ''law'':133 ''lie'':42 ''mention'':54 ''modern'':45 ''natur'':118 ''nehemiah'':10,73 ''one'':23 ''peopl'':121 ''present'':59 ''probabl'':74 ''proof'':64 ''rather'':3 ''reform'':86 ''relat'':89 ''request'':123 ''resum'':126 ''return'':76 ''revisit'':96 ''scribe'':49 ''seem'':19 ''soon'':82 ''squar'':5 ''street'':2 ''templ'':30 ''ten'':114 ''thus'':41 ''time'':101 ''visit'':143 ''wall'':38,104 ''waterg'':33 ''within'':43 ''work'':128 ''would'':40 ''year'':115', NULL, '421');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('422', '2', '422', NULL, 'The festival lasted from the 15th day of the 7th month to the first. The 22nd day was a day of solemn observance Nehemiah 8:18. One day seems to have been allowed the people for rest; and then the work of repentance, for which they had shown themselves ready Nehemiah 8:9, was taken in hand, and a general fast was proclaimed.', NULL, NULL, '''15th'':6 ''18'':26 ''22nd'':16 ''7th'':10 ''8'':25,52 ''9'':53 ''allow'':33 ''day'':7,17,20,28 ''fast'':61 ''festiv'':2 ''first'':14 ''general'':60 ''hand'':57 ''last'':3 ''month'':11 ''nehemiah'':24,51 ''observ'':23 ''one'':27 ''peopl'':35 ''proclaim'':63 ''readi'':50 ''repent'':43 ''rest'':37 ''seem'':29 ''shown'':48 ''solemn'':22 ''taken'':55 ''work'':41', NULL, '422');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('423', '2', '423', NULL, 'The "Zidkijah" of this passage is probably the same as "Zadok" Nehemiah 13:13. "Zadok" is expressly called "the scribe," and it was probably as the scribe who drew up the document that "Zidkijah" signed it immediately after Nehemiah.', NULL, NULL, '''13'':13,14 ''call'':18 ''document'':32 ''drew'':29 ''expressli'':17 ''immedi'':37 ''nehemiah'':12,39 ''passag'':5 ''probabl'':7,24 ''scribe'':20,27 ''sign'':35 ''zadok'':11,15 ''zidkijah'':2,34', NULL, '423');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('424', '2', '424', NULL, 'To bring one of ten - Artificial enlargements of capitals by forcible transfers of population to them, were not unusual in ancient times. About 500 B.C., Syracuse became a great city in this way. Tradition ascribed the greatness of Rome, in part, to this cause.', NULL, NULL, '''500'':24 ''ancient'':21 ''artifici'':6 ''ascrib'':35 ''b.c'':25 ''becam'':27 ''bring'':2 ''capit'':9 ''caus'':44 ''citi'':30 ''enlarg'':7 ''forcibl'':11 ''great'':29,37 ''one'':3 ''part'':41 ''popul'':14 ''rome'':39 ''syracus'':26 ''ten'':5 ''time'':22 ''tradit'':34 ''transfer'':12 ''unusu'':19 ''way'':33', NULL, '424');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('425', '2', '425', NULL, 'The priests - The number of the names here given, which is 22, is probably to be connected with that of the Davidic "courses," which was 24 1 Chronicles 24:7-18. Eight names are identical with those of the heads in David''s time. On comparing the present list with that of the families who sealed to Nehemiah''s covenant Nehemiah 10:2-8, we shall find that the first sixteen recur in that document nearly in the same order; but that the last six are absent from it. It would seem that as these six declined to seal to Nehemiah''s covenant, they were placed below the rest here in a sort of supplementary list. Note especially the "and" which connects the second part of the lists with the earlier part, both in Nehemiah 12:6 and in Nehemiah 12:19.', NULL, NULL, '''-18'':31 ''-8'':64 ''1'':27 ''10'':62 ''12'':136,141 ''19'':142 ''2'':63 ''22'':12 ''24'':26,29 ''6'':137 ''7'':30 ''absent'':87 ''chronicl'':28 ''compar'':46 ''connect'':17,122 ''cours'':23 ''coven'':60,103 ''david'':22,42 ''declin'':97 ''document'':75 ''earlier'':131 ''eight'':32 ''especi'':118 ''famili'':54 ''find'':67 ''first'':70 ''given'':9 ''head'':40 ''ident'':35 ''last'':84 ''list'':49,116,128 ''name'':7,33 ''near'':76 ''nehemiah'':58,61,101,135,140 ''note'':117 ''number'':4 ''order'':80 ''part'':125,132 ''place'':106 ''present'':48 ''priest'':2 ''probabl'':14 ''recur'':72 ''rest'':109 ''seal'':56,99 ''second'':124 ''seem'':92 ''shall'':66 ''six'':85,96 ''sixteen'':71 ''sort'':113 ''supplementari'':115 ''time'':44 ''would'':91', NULL, '425');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('426', '2', '426', NULL, 'On that day - Or, "at that time," as in Nehemiah 12:44.The entire Pentateuch is probably meant by "the Book of Moses".', NULL, NULL, '''12'':11 ''44.the'':12 ''book'':20 ''day'':3 ''entir'':13 ''meant'':17 ''mose'':22 ''nehemiah'':10 ''pentateuch'':14 ''probabl'':16 ''time'':7', NULL, '426');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('427', '2', '427', NULL, 'Ahasuerus - . Xerxes, the son of Darius Hystaspis. His empire is rightly described as from India even unto Ethiopia. The satrapies of Darius Hystaspis reached 29 in number, and the nations under Xerxes were about 60. The 127 "provinces" include probably sub-satrapies and other smaller divisions of the great governments.', NULL, NULL, '''127'':37 ''29'':25 ''60'':35 ''ahasuerus'':1 ''darius'':6,22 ''describ'':12 ''divis'':47 ''empir'':9 ''ethiopia'':18 ''even'':16 ''govern'':51 ''great'':50 ''hystaspi'':7,23 ''includ'':39 ''india'':15 ''nation'':30 ''number'':27 ''probabl'':40 ''provinc'':38 ''reach'':24 ''right'':11 ''satrapi'':20,43 ''smaller'':46 ''son'':4 ''sub'':42 ''sub-satrapi'':41 ''unto'':17 ''xerx'':2,32', NULL, '427');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('428', '2', '428', NULL, 'These events must belong to the time between the great assembly held at Susa in Xerxes'' third year (483 B.C.), and the departure of the monarch on his expedition against Greece in his fifth year, 481 B.C.', NULL, NULL, '''481'':36 ''483'':19 ''assembl'':11 ''b.c'':20,37 ''belong'':4 ''departur'':23 ''event'':2 ''expedit'':29 ''fifth'':34 ''great'':10 ''greec'':31 ''held'':12 ''monarch'':26 ''must'':3 ''susa'':14 ''third'':17 ''time'':7 ''xerx'':16 ''year'':18,35', NULL, '428');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1026', '2', '1026', NULL, 'Wheresoever ... - The words in this verse are proverbial. Vultures and eagles easily ascertain where dead bodies are, and hasten to devour them. So with the Roman army. Jerusalem is like a dead and putrid corpse. Its life is gone, and it is ready to be devoured. The Roman armies will find it out, as the vultures do a dead carcass, and will come around it to devour it. This proverb also teaches a universal truth. Wherever wicked people are, there will be assembled the instruments of their chastisement. The providence of God will direct them there, as the vultures are directed to a dead carcass.This verse is connected with the preceding by the word "for," implying that this is a reason for what is said there that the Son of man would certainly come to destroy the city, and that he would come suddenly. The meaning is that he would come, by means of the Roman armies, as "certainly;" as "suddenly," and as unexpectedly as whole flocks of vultures and eagles, though unseen before, see their prey at a great distance and suddenly gather in multitudes around it. Travelers in the deserts of Arabia tell us that they sometimes witness a speck in the distant sky which for a long time is scarcely visible. At length it grows larger, it comes nearer, and they at last find that it is a vulture that has from an immense distance seen a carcass lying on the sand. So keen is their vision as aptly to represent the Roman armies, though at an immense distance, spying, as it were, Jerusalem, a putrid carcass, and hastening in multitudes to destroy it.', NULL, NULL, '''also'':71 ''apt'':252 ''arabia'':194 ''armi'':27,49,157,257 ''around'':64,187 ''ascertain'':13 ''assembl'':83 ''bodi'':16 ''carcass'':60,241,270 ''carcass.this'':105 ''certain'':133,159 ''chastis'':88 ''citi'':138 ''come'':63,134,143,151,221 ''connect'':108 ''corps'':35 ''dead'':15,32,59,104 ''desert'':192 ''destroy'':136,276 ''devour'':21,46,67 ''direct'':94,101 ''distanc'':181,238,262 ''distant'':205 ''eagl'':11,171 ''easili'':12 ''find'':51,227 ''flock'':167 ''gather'':184 ''god'':92 ''gone'':39 ''great'':180 ''grow'':218 ''hasten'':19,272 ''immens'':237,261 ''impli'':116 ''instrument'':85 ''jerusalem'':28,267 ''keen'':247 ''larger'':219 ''last'':226 ''length'':216 ''lie'':242 ''life'':37 ''like'':30 ''long'':210 ''man'':131 ''mean'':146,153 ''multitud'':186,274 ''nearer'':222 ''peopl'':78 ''preced'':111 ''prey'':177 ''proverb'':70 ''proverbi'':8 ''provid'':90 ''putrid'':34,269 ''readi'':43 ''reason'':121 ''repres'':254 ''roman'':26,48,156,256 ''said'':125 ''sand'':245 ''scarc'':213 ''see'':175 ''seen'':239 ''sky'':206 ''sometim'':199 ''son'':129 ''speck'':202 ''spi'':263 ''sudden'':144,161,183 ''teach'':72 ''tell'':195 ''though'':172,258 ''time'':211 ''travel'':189 ''truth'':75 ''unexpect'':164 ''univers'':74 ''unseen'':173 ''us'':196 ''vers'':6,106 ''visibl'':214 ''vision'':250 ''vultur'':9,56,99,169,232 ''wheresoev'':1 ''wherev'':76 ''whole'':166 ''wick'':77 ''wit'':200 ''word'':3,114 ''would'':132,142,150', NULL, '953');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('429', '2', '429', NULL, 'The name, Haman, is probably the same as the Classical Omanes, and in ancient Persian, "Umana", an exact equivalent of the Greek "Eumenes." Hammedatha is perhaps the same as "Madata" or "Mahadata", an old Persian name signifying "given by (or to) the moon."The Agagite - The Jews generally understand by this expression "the descendant of Agag," the Amalekite monarch of 1 Samuel 15. Haman, however, by his own name, and the names of his sons Esther 9:7-9 and his father, would seem to have been a genuine Persian.The Classical writers make no mention of Haman''s advancement; but their notices of the reign of Xerxes after 479 B.C. are exceedingly scanty.', NULL, NULL, '''-9'':79 ''1'':61 ''15'':63 ''479'':109 ''7'':78 ''9'':77 ''advanc'':99 ''agag'':56 ''agagit'':45 ''amalekit'':58 ''ancient'':14 ''b.c'':110 ''classic'':10,91 ''descend'':54 ''equival'':19 ''esther'':76 ''eumen'':23 ''exact'':18 ''exceed'':112 ''express'':52 ''father'':82 ''general'':48 ''genuin'':89 ''given'':38 ''greek'':22 ''haman'':3,64,97 ''hammedatha'':24 ''howev'':65 ''jew'':47 ''madata'':30 ''mahadata'':32 ''make'':93 ''mention'':95 ''monarch'':59 ''moon'':43 ''name'':2,36,69,72 ''notic'':102 ''old'':34 ''oman'':11 ''perhap'':26 ''persian'':15,35 ''persian.the'':90 ''probabl'':5 ''reign'':105 ''samuel'':62 ''scanti'':113 ''seem'':84 ''signifi'':37 ''son'':75 ''umana'':16 ''understand'':49 ''would'':83 ''writer'':92 ''xerx'':107', NULL, '429');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('430', '2', '430', NULL, 'None might enter into the king''s gate clothed with sackcloth - This law is not elsewhere mentioned; but its principle - that nothing of evil omen is to be obtruded on the monarch - has been recognized throughout the East in all ages.', NULL, NULL, '''age'':41 ''cloth'':9 ''east'':38 ''elsewher'':16 ''enter'':3 ''evil'':24 ''gate'':8 ''king'':6 ''law'':13 ''mention'':17 ''might'':2 ''monarch'':32 ''none'':1 ''noth'':22 ''obtrud'':29 ''omen'':25 ''principl'':20 ''recogn'':35 ''sackcloth'':11 ''throughout'':36', NULL, '430');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('431', '2', '431', NULL, 'Over against the gate - This is the usual situation of the throne in the "throne-room" of an Oriental palace. The monarch, from his raised position, can see into the court through the doorway opposite to him, which is kept open.', NULL, NULL, '''court'':32 ''doorway'':35 ''gate'':4 ''kept'':41 ''monarch'':23 ''open'':42 ''opposit'':36 ''orient'':20 ''palac'':21 ''posit'':27 ''rais'':26 ''room'':17 ''see'':29 ''situat'':9 ''throne'':12,16 ''throne-room'':15 ''usual'':8', NULL, '431');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('432', '2', '432', NULL, 'It was a settled principle of the Persian government that "Royal Benefactors" were to receive an adequate reward. The names of such persons were placed on a special roll, and care was taken that they should be properly recompensed, though they sometimes waited for months or years before they were rewarded.', NULL, NULL, '''adequ'':17 ''benefactor'':12 ''care'':31 ''govern'':9 ''month'':45 ''name'':20 ''persian'':8 ''person'':23 ''place'':25 ''principl'':5 ''proper'':38 ''receiv'':15 ''recompens'':39 ''reward'':18,51 ''roll'':29 ''royal'':11 ''settl'':4 ''sometim'':42 ''special'':28 ''taken'':33 ''though'':40 ''wait'':43 ''year'':47', NULL, '432');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('433', '2', '433', NULL, 'The king now learned, perhaps for the first time, that his favorite was a Jewess.Although the enemy ... - i. e. "although the enemy (Haman) would not (even in that case) compensate (by his payment to the treasury) for the king''s loss of so many subjects."', NULL, NULL, '''although'':20 ''case'':29 ''compens'':30 ''e'':19 ''enemi'':17,22 ''even'':26 ''favorit'':12 ''first'':8 ''haman'':23 ''jewess.although'':15 ''king'':2,39 ''learn'':4 ''loss'':41 ''mani'':44 ''payment'':33 ''perhap'':5 ''subject'':45 ''time'':9 ''treasuri'':36 ''would'':24', NULL, '433');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('434', '2', '434', NULL, 'Give the house of Haman - Confiscation of goods accompanied public execution in Persia as in other Oriental countries.', NULL, NULL, '''accompani'':9 ''confisc'':6 ''countri'':18 ''execut'':11 ''give'':1 ''good'':8 ''haman'':5 ''hous'':3 ''orient'':17 ''persia'':13 ''public'':10', NULL, '434');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('435', '2', '435', NULL, 'All the rulers ... helped the Jews - i. e. the Persians, who formed the standing army which kept the Empire in subjection, and were at the disposal of the various governors of provinces, took the Jews'' side. The enemies of the Jews (e. g. Esther 9:16) were almost entirely to be found among the idolatrous people of the subject nations, for whose lives neither the Persians generally, nor their monarchs, cared greatly.', NULL, NULL, '''16'':46 ''9'':45 ''almost'':48 ''among'':53 ''armi'':15 ''care'':71 ''dispos'':26 ''e'':8,42 ''empir'':19 ''enemi'':38 ''entir'':49 ''esther'':44 ''form'':12 ''found'':52 ''g'':43 ''general'':67 ''governor'':30 ''great'':72 ''help'':4 ''idolatr'':55 ''jew'':6,35,41 ''kept'':17 ''live'':63 ''monarch'':70 ''nation'':60 ''neither'':64 ''peopl'':56 ''persian'':10,66 ''provinc'':32 ''ruler'':3 ''side'':36 ''stand'':14 ''subject'':21,59 ''took'':33 ''various'':29 ''whose'':62', NULL, '435');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('436', '2', '436', NULL, 'A tribute - Perhaps an allusion to some fresh arrangement of the tribute likely to have followed on the return of Xerxes from Greece.Upon the isles of the sea - Cyprus, Aradus, the island of Tyre, Platea, etc., remained in the hands of the Persians after the victories of the Greeks, and may be the "isles" here intended.', NULL, NULL, '''allus'':5 ''aradus'':30 ''arrang'':9 ''cyprus'':29 ''etc'':36 ''follow'':16 ''fresh'':8 ''greece.upon'':23 ''greek'':49 ''hand'':40 ''intend'':56 ''isl'':25,54 ''island'':32 ''like'':13 ''may'':51 ''perhap'':3 ''persian'':43 ''platea'':35 ''remain'':37 ''return'':19 ''sea'':28 ''tribut'':2,12 ''tyre'':34 ''victori'':46 ''xerx'':21', NULL, '436');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('438', '2', '438', NULL, 'Again there was a day ... - See the notes at Job 1:6. These seasons are represented as periodical, when the angels came, as it were, to make report to God of what they had observed and done. The Chaldee renders this, "And there was a day of the great judgment (    yom dyna'' raba''), a day of the remission of sins (  ) and there came bands () of angels."To present himself before the Lord - This does not occur in the former statement in Job 1:6. It here means that he came before the Lord after he had had permission to afflict; Job. The Chaldee renders it "that he might stand in judgment   dyn before the Lord."', NULL, NULL, '''1'':11,83 ''6'':12,84 ''afflict'':100 ''angel'':21,66 ''band'':64 ''came'':22,63,90 ''chalde'':39,103 ''day'':5,46,55 ''done'':37 ''dyn'':112 ''dyna'':52 ''former'':79 ''god'':30 ''great'':49 ''job'':10,82,101 ''judgment'':50,111 ''lord'':72,93,115 ''make'':27 ''mean'':87 ''might'':108 ''note'':8 ''observ'':35 ''occur'':76 ''period'':18 ''permiss'':98 ''present'':68 ''raba'':53 ''remiss'':58 ''render'':40,104 ''report'':28 ''repres'':16 ''season'':14 ''see'':6 ''sin'':60 ''stand'':109 ''statement'':80 ''yom'':51', NULL, '438');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('439', '2', '439', NULL, 'Let the day perish - "Perish the day! O that there had never been such a day! Let it be blotted from the memory of man! There is something singularly bold, sublime, and "wild" in this exclamation. It is a burst of feeling where there had been long restraint, and where now it breaks forth in the most vehement and impassioned manner. The word "perish" here   yo''bad expresses the "optative," and indicates strong desire. So the Septuagint, &#x391;&#x3c0;&#x3bf;&#x3bb;&#x3bf;&#x3b9;&#x3c4;&#x3bf;  Apoloito, "may it perish," or be destroyed; compare Job 10:18. "O that I had given up the ghost." Dr. Good says of this exclamation, "There is nothing that I know of, ia ancient or modern poetry, equal to the entire burst, whether in the wildness and horror of the imprecations. or the terrible sublimity of its imagery." The boldest and most animated of the Hebrew poets have imitated it, and have expressed themselves in almost the same language, in scenes of distress. A remarkably similar expression of feeling is made by Jeremiah.Cursed be the day wherein I was born:Let not the day wherein my mother bare me be blessed!Cursed be the man who brought tidings to my father, saying,"A man child is born unto thee,"Making him very glad.Be that man as the cities which yahweh overthrew and repented not!Yea, let him hear the outcry in the morning,And the lamentation at noon day!', NULL, NULL, '''10'':87 ''18'':88 ''almost'':153 ''ancient'':111 ''anim'':140 ''apoloito'':78 ''bad'':67 ''bare'':185 ''bless'':188 ''blot'':20 ''bold'':30 ''boldest'':137 ''born'':177,204 ''break'':53 ''brought'':194 ''burst'':40,119 ''child'':202 ''citi'':215 ''compar'':85 ''curs'':189 ''day'':3,7,16,173,181,236 ''desir'':74 ''destroy'':84 ''distress'':160 ''dr'':97 ''entir'':118 ''equal'':115 ''exclam'':36,102 ''express'':68,150,164 ''father'':198 ''feel'':42,166 ''forth'':54 ''ghost'':96 ''given'':93 ''glad.be'':210 ''good'':98 ''hear'':225 ''hebrew'':143 ''horror'':125 ''ia'':110 ''imageri'':135 ''imit'':146 ''impass'':60 ''imprec'':128 ''indic'':72 ''jeremiah.cursed'':170 ''job'':86 ''know'':108 ''lament'':233 ''languag'':156 ''let'':1,17,178,223 ''long'':47 ''made'':168 ''make'':207 ''man'':25,192,201,212 ''manner'':61 ''may'':79 ''memori'':23 ''modern'':113 ''morn'':230 ''mother'':184 ''never'':12 ''noon'':235 ''noth'':105 ''o'':8,89 ''optat'':70 ''outcri'':227 ''overthrew'':218 ''perish'':4,5,64,81 ''poet'':144 ''poetri'':114 ''remark'':162 ''repent'':220 ''restraint'':48 ''say'':99,199 ''scene'':158 ''septuagint'':77 ''similar'':163 ''singular'':29 ''someth'':28 ''strong'':73 ''sublim'':31,132 ''terribl'':131 ''thee'':206 ''tide'':195 ''unto'':205 ''vehement'':58 ''wherein'':174,182 ''whether'':120 ''wild'':33,123 ''word'':63 ''yahweh'':217 ''yea'':222 ''yo'':66', NULL, '439');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('440', '2', '440', NULL, 'If we assay to commune with thee - Margin, A word. Hebrew -    dabar hancah. "May we attempt a word with thee?" This is a gentle and polite apology at the beginning of his speech - an inquiry whether he would take it as unkind if one should adventure on a remark in the way of argument. Jahn, in characterizing the part which Job''s three friends respectively take in the controversy, says: "Eliphaz is superior to the others in discernment and delicacy. He begins by addressing Job mildly; and it is not until irritated by opposition that he reckons him among the wicked."Wilt thou be grieved? - That is, Wilt thou take it ill? Will it be offensive to you, or weary you, or tire your patience? The word used here (  la''ah) means to labor, to strive, to weary, to exhaust; and hence, to be weary, to try one''s patience, to take anything ill. Here it is the language of courtesy, and is designed to introduce the subsequent remarks in the kindest manner. Eliphaz knew that he was about to make observations which might implicate Job, and he introduced them in as kind a manner as possible. There is nothing abrupt or harsh in his beginning. All is courteous in the highest degree, and is a model for debaters.But who can withhold himself from speaking? - Margin, "Refrain from words." That is, "the subject is so important, the sentiments advanced by Job are so extraordinary, and the principles involved are so momentous, that it is impossible to refrain." There is much delicacy in this. He did not begin to speak merely to make a speech. He professes that be would not have spoken, if he had not been pressed by the importance of the subject, and had not been full of matter. To a great extent, this is a good rule to adopt: not to make a speech unless there are sentiments which weigh upon the mind, and convictions of duty which cannot be repressed.', NULL, NULL, '''abrupt'':201 ''address'':84 ''adopt'':312 ''advanc'':239 ''adventur'':46 ''ah'':131 ''among'':99 ''anyth'':153 ''apolog'':27 ''argument'':54 ''assay'':3 ''attempt'':16 ''begin'':30,82,206,267 ''cannot'':332 ''character'':57 ''commune'':5 ''controversi'':69 ''convict'':328 ''courteous'':209 ''courtesi'':161 ''dabar'':12 ''debaters.but'':219 ''degre'':213 ''delicaci'':80,261 ''design'':164 ''discern'':78 ''duti'':330 ''eliphaz'':71,174 ''exhaust'':140 ''extent'':305 ''extraordinari'':244 ''friend'':64 ''full'':299 ''gentl'':24 ''good'':309 ''great'':304 ''griev'':105 ''hancah'':13 ''harsh'':203 ''hebrew'':11 ''henc'':142 ''highest'':212 ''ill'':112,154 ''implic'':185 ''import'':236,291 ''imposs'':255 ''inquiri'':35 ''introduc'':166,189 ''involv'':248 ''irrit'':92 ''jahn'':55 ''job'':61,85,186,241 ''kind'':193 ''kindest'':172 ''knew'':175 ''la'':130 ''labor'':134 ''languag'':159 ''make'':181,272,315 ''manner'':173,195 ''margin'':8,226 ''matter'':301 ''may'':14 ''mean'':132 ''mere'':270 ''might'':184 ''mild'':86 ''mind'':326 ''model'':217 ''moment'':251 ''much'':260 ''noth'':200 ''observ'':182 ''offens'':116 ''one'':44,148 ''opposit'':94 ''other'':76 ''part'':59 ''patienc'':125,150 ''polit'':26 ''possibl'':197 ''press'':288 ''principl'':247 ''profess'':276 ''reckon'':97 ''refrain'':227,257 ''remark'':49,169 ''repress'':334 ''respect'':65 ''rule'':310 ''say'':70 ''sentiment'':238,321 ''speak'':225,269 ''speech'':33,274,317 ''spoken'':282 ''strive'':136 ''subject'':233,294 ''subsequ'':168 ''superior'':73 ''take'':39,66,110,152 ''thee'':7,20 ''thou'':103,109 ''three'':63 ''tire'':123 ''tri'':147 ''unkind'':42 ''unless'':318 ''upon'':324 ''use'':128 ''way'':52 ''weari'':120,138,145 ''weigh'':323 ''whether'':36 ''wick'':101 ''wilt'':102,108 ''withhold'':222 ''word'':10,18,127,229 ''would'':38,279', NULL, '440');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('442', '2', '442', NULL, 'O that my grief were thoroughly weighed - The word rendered "grief" here (  kaas) may mean either vexation, trouble, grief; Ecclesiastes 1:18; Ecclesiastes 2:23; or it may mean anger; Deuteronomy 32:19; Ezekiel 20:28. It is rendered by the Septuagint here, &#x3bf;&#x3c1;&#x3b3;&#x3b7;  orge - anger; by Jerome, peccata - sins. The sense of the whole passage may either be, that Job wished his anger or his complaints to be laid in the balance with his calamity, to see if one was more weighty than the other - meaning that he had not complained unreasonably or unjustly (Rosenmuller); or that he wished that his afflictions might be put into one scale and the sands of the sea into another, and the one weighed against the other (Noyes); or simply, that he desired that his sorrows should be accurately estimated. This latter is, I think, the true sense of the passage. He supposed his friends had not understood and appreciated his sufferings; that they were disposed to blame him without understanding the extent of his sorrows, and he desires that they would estimate them aright before they condemned him. In particular, he seems to have supposed that Eliphaz had not done justice to the depth of his sorrows in the remarks which he had just made. The figure of weighing actions or sorrows, is not uncommon or unnatural. It means to take an exact estimate of their amount. So we speak of heavy calamities, of afflictions that crush us by their weight. etc.Laid in the balances - Margin, "lifted up." That is, raised up and put in the scales, or put in the scales and then raised up - as is common in weighing.Together -   yachad. At the same time; that all my sorrows, griefs, and woes, were piled on the scales, and then weighed. He supposed that only a partial estimate had been formed of the extent of his calamities.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':21 ''18'':22 ''19'':33 ''2'':24 ''20'':35 ''23'':25 ''28'':36 ''32'':32 ''accur'':135 ''action'':217 ''afflict'':102,242 ''amount'':234 ''anger'':30,45,63 ''anoth'':116 ''appreci'':156 ''aright'':181 ''balanc'':72,252 ''blame'':164 ''calam'':75,240,314 ''common'':276 ''complain'':91 ''complaint'':66 ''condemn'':184 ''crush'':244 ''depth'':201 ''desir'':129,175 ''deuteronomi'':31 ''dispos'':162 ''done'':197 ''ecclesiast'':20,23 ''either'':16,57 ''eliphaz'':194 ''estim'':136,179,231,305 ''etc.laid'':249 ''exact'':230 ''extent'':169,311 ''ezekiel'':34 ''figur'':214 ''form'':308 ''friend'':151 ''grief'':4,11,19,288 ''heavi'':239 ''jerom'':47 ''job'':60 ''justic'':198 ''kaa'':13 ''laid'':69 ''latter'':138 ''lift'':254 ''made'':212 ''margin'':253 ''may'':14,28,56 ''mean'':15,29,86,226 ''might'':103 ''noy'':124 ''o'':1 ''one'':79,107,119 ''org'':44 ''partial'':304 ''particular'':187 ''passag'':55,147 ''peccata'':48 ''pile'':292 ''put'':105,261,266 ''rais'':258,272 ''remark'':207 ''render'':10,39 ''rosenmul'':95 ''sand'':111 ''scale'':108,264,269,295 ''sea'':114 ''see'':77 ''seem'':189 ''sens'':51,144 ''septuagint'':42 ''simpli'':126 ''sin'':49 ''sorrow'':132,172,204,219,287 ''speak'':237 ''suffer'':158 ''suppos'':149,192,300 ''take'':228 ''think'':141 ''thorough'':6 ''time'':283 ''troubl'':18 ''true'':143 ''uncommon'':222 ''understand'':167 ''understood'':154 ''unjust'':94 ''unnatur'':224 ''unreason'':92 ''us'':245 ''vexat'':17 ''weigh'':7,120,216,298 ''weighing.together'':278 ''weight'':248 ''weighti'':82 ''whole'':54 ''wish'':61,99 ''without'':166 ''woe'':290 ''word'':9 ''would'':178 ''yachad'':279', NULL, '442');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('443', '2', '443', NULL, 'Is there not an appointed time to man upon earth? - Margin, or, warfare. The word used here   tsaba'' means properly a host, an army, see the notes, Isaiah 1:9; then it means warfare, or the hard service of a soldier; notes, Isaiah 40:2. Here it means that man on the earth was enlisted, so to speak, for a certain time. He had a certain and definite hard service to perform, and which he must continue to discharge until he was relieved by death. It was a service of hazard, like the life of a soldier, or of toil, like that of one who had been hired for a certain time, and who anxiously looked for the period of his release. The object of Job in introducing this remark evidently is, to vindicate himself for the wish to die which he had expressed. He maintains that it is as natural and proper for man in his circumstances to wish to be released by death, as for a soldier to desire that his term of service might be accomplished, or a weary servant to long for the shades of the evening. The Septuagint renders it, "Is not the life of man upon the earth peirateerion " - explained by Schleusner and rendered by Good, as meaning a band of pirates. The Vulgate renders it, militia - miltary service. The sense is, that the life of man was like the hard service of a soldier; and this is one of the points of justification to which Job referred in Job 6:29-30. He maintains that it is not improper to desire that such a service should close.The days of an hireling - A man who has been hired to perform some service with a promise of a reward, and who is not unnaturally impatient to receive it. Job maintained that such was the life of man. He was looking forward to a reward, and it was not unnatural or improper to desire that that reward should be given to him. Job 7:1But if a man die - shall he indeed live again?All the days of my appointed time (Hebrew, my warfare) will Iwait,Till my change come.', NULL, NULL, '''-30'':259 ''1'':29 ''1but'':339 ''2'':45 ''29'':258 ''40'':44 ''6'':257 ''7'':338 ''9'':30 ''accomplish'':179 ''anxious'':115 ''appoint'':5,354 ''armi'':24 ''band'':216 ''certain'':61,66,111 ''chang'':363 ''circumst'':158 ''close.the'':274 ''come'':364 ''continu'':77 ''day'':275,351 ''death'':85,165 ''definit'':68 ''desir'':171,268,328 ''die'':140,343 ''discharg'':79 ''earth'':10,53,204 ''enlist'':55 ''even'':191 ''evid'':131 ''explain'':206 ''express'':144 ''forward'':316 ''given'':334 ''good'':212 ''hard'':37,69,237 ''hazard'':91 ''hebrew'':356 ''hire'':108,284 ''hirel'':278 ''host'':22 ''impati'':300 ''improp'':266,326 ''inde'':346 ''introduc'':128 ''isaiah'':28,43 ''iwait'':360 ''job'':126,253,256,304,337 ''justif'':250 ''life'':94,199,231,310 ''like'':92,101,235 ''live'':347 ''long'':185 ''look'':116,315 ''maintain'':146,261,305 ''man'':8,50,155,201,233,280,312,342 ''margin'':11 ''mean'':19,33,48,214 ''might'':177 ''militia'':223 ''miltari'':224 ''must'':76 ''natur'':151 ''note'':27,42 ''object'':124 ''one'':104,245 ''peirateerion'':205 ''perform'':72,286 ''period'':119 ''pirat'':218 ''point'':248 ''promis'':291 ''proper'':20,153 ''receiv'':302 ''refer'':254 ''releas'':122,163 ''reliev'':83 ''remark'':130 ''render'':194,210,221 ''reward'':294,319,331 ''schleusner'':208 ''see'':25 ''sens'':227 ''septuagint'':193 ''servant'':183 ''servic'':38,70,89,176,225,238,272,288 ''shade'':188 ''shall'':344 ''soldier'':41,97,169,241 ''speak'':58 ''term'':174 ''till'':361 ''time'':6,62,112,355 ''toil'':100 ''tsaba'':18 ''unnatur'':299,324 ''upon'':9,202 ''use'':16 ''vindic'':134 ''vulgat'':220 ''warfar'':13,34,358 ''weari'':182 ''wish'':138,160 ''word'':15', NULL, '443');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('444', '2', '444', NULL, 'Whilst it is yet in his greenness - That is, while it seems to be in its vigor.And is not cut down - Even when it is not cut down. If suffered to stand by itself, and if undisturbed, it will wither away. The application of this is obvious and beautiful. Such plants have no self sustaining power. They are dependent on moisture for their support. If that is withheld, they droop and die. So with the prosperous sinner and the hypocrite. His piety, compared with that which is genuine, is like the spongy texture of the paper-reed compared with the solid oak. He is sustained in his professed religion by outward prosperity, as the rush is nourished by moisture; and the moment his prosperity is withdrawn, his religion droops and dies like the flag without water.', NULL, NULL, '''applic'':43 ''away'':41 ''beauti'':49 ''compar'':83,99 ''cut'':20,27 ''depend'':59 ''die'':72,132 ''droop'':70,130 ''even'':22 ''flag'':135 ''genuin'':88 ''green'':7 ''hypocrit'':80 ''like'':90,133 ''moistur'':61,120 ''moment'':123 ''nourish'':118 ''oak'':103 ''obvious'':47 ''outward'':112 ''paper'':97 ''paper-re'':96 ''pieti'':82 ''plant'':51 ''power'':56 ''profess'':109 ''prosper'':76,113,125 ''reed'':98 ''religion'':110,129 ''rush'':116 ''seem'':12 ''self'':54 ''sinner'':77 ''solid'':102 ''spongi'':92 ''stand'':32 ''suffer'':30 ''support'':64 ''sustain'':55,106 ''textur'':93 ''undisturb'':37 ''vigor.and'':17 ''water'':137 ''whilst'':1 ''withdrawn'':127 ''wither'':40 ''withheld'':68 ''without'':136 ''yet'':4', NULL, '444');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('445', '2', '445', NULL, 'Which doeth great things - This is almost the sentiment which had been expressed by Eliphaz; see the notes, Job 5:9. It was evidently a proverb, and as such was used by both Eliphaz and Job.', NULL, NULL, '''5'':20 ''9'':21 ''almost'':7 ''doeth'':2 ''eliphaz'':15,34 ''evid'':24 ''express'':13 ''great'':3 ''job'':19,36 ''note'':18 ''proverb'':26 ''see'':16 ''sentiment'':9 ''thing'':4 ''use'':31', NULL, '445');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('446', '2', '446', NULL, 'Thou knowest that I am not wicked - That is, that I am not a hypocrite, or an impenitent sinner. Job did not claim perfection (see the note at Job 9:20), but he maintained through all this argument that he was not a wicked man, in the sense in which his friends regarded him as such, and for the truth of this he could boldly appeal to God. The margin is, "It is upon thy knowledge." This is a literal translation of the Hebrew, but the sense is well expressed in the text. The meaning of the verse is, "Why dost thou thus afflict me, when thou knowest that I am not wicked? Why am I treated as if I were the worst of men? Why is occasion thus furnished for my friends to construct an argument as if I were a man of singular depravity?"There is none that can deliver out of thine hand - I have no power to release myself. Job felt hat God had almighty power; and he seems to have felt that his sufferings were rather the simple exertion of power, than the exercise of justice. It was this that laid the foundation for his complaint.', NULL, NULL, '''20'':31 ''9'':30 ''afflict'':104 ''almighti'':169 ''appeal'':66 ''argument'':38,137 ''bold'':65 ''claim'':23 ''complaint'':201 ''construct'':135 ''could'':64 ''deliv'':152 ''deprav'':146 ''dost'':101 ''exercis'':189 ''exert'':184 ''express'':90 ''felt'':165,176 ''foundat'':198 ''friend'':52,133 ''furnish'':130 ''god'':68,167 ''hand'':156 ''hat'':166 ''hebrew'':84 ''hypocrit'':15 ''impenit'':18 ''job'':20,29,164 ''justic'':191 ''knowest'':2,108 ''knowledg'':76 ''laid'':196 ''liter'':80 ''maintain'':34 ''man'':45,143 ''margin'':70 ''mean'':95 ''men'':125 ''none'':149 ''note'':27 ''occas'':128 ''perfect'':24 ''power'':160,170,186 ''rather'':181 ''regard'':53 ''releas'':162 ''see'':25 ''seem'':173 ''sens'':48,87 ''simpl'':183 ''singular'':145 ''sinner'':19 ''suffer'':179 ''text'':93 ''thi'':75 ''thine'':155 ''thou'':1,102,107 ''thus'':103,129 ''translat'':81 ''treat'':117 ''truth'':60 ''upon'':74 ''vers'':98 ''well'':89 ''wick'':7,44,113 ''worst'':123', NULL, '446');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('447', '2', '447', NULL, 'Should not the multitude of words be answered? - As if all that Job had said had been mere words; or as if he was remarkable for mere garrulity.And should a man full of talk be justified - Margin, as in Hebrew "of lips." The phrase is evidently a Hebraism, to denote a great talker - a man of mere lips, or empty sound. Zophar asks whether such a man could be justified or vindicated. It will be recollected that taciturnity was with the Orientals a much greater virtue than with us, and that it was regarded as one of the proofs of wisdom. The wise man with them was he who sat down at the feet of age, and desired to learn; who carefully collected the maxims of former times; who diligently observed the course of events; and who deliberated with care on what others had to say. Thus, Solomon says, "In the multitude of words there wanteth not sin: but he that refraineth his lips is wise;" Proverbs 10:19; so James 1:19, "let every man be swift to hear, slow to speak." It was supposed that a man who said much would say some foolish or improper things, and hence, it was regarded as a proof of prudence to be distinguished for silence. In Oriental countries, and it may be added also, in all countries that we regard as uncivilized, it is unusual and disrespectful to be hasty in offering counsel, to be forward to speak, or to be confident and bold in opinion; see the notes at Job 32:6-7. It was for reasons such as these that Zophar maintained that a man who was full of talk could not be justified in it; that there was presumptive proof that he was not a safe man, or a man who could be vindicated in all that he said.', NULL, NULL, '''-7'':263 ''1'':172 ''10'':168 ''19'':169,173 ''32'':261 ''6'':262 ''add'':222 ''age'':116 ''also'':223 ''answer'':8 ''ask'':63 ''bold'':253 ''care'':122,140 ''collect'':123 ''confid'':251 ''could'':68,282,304 ''counsel'':242 ''countri'':217,226 ''cours'':133 ''deliber'':138 ''denot'':50 ''desir'':118 ''dilig'':130 ''disrespect'':236 ''distinguish'':212 ''empti'':60 ''event'':135 ''everi'':175 ''evid'':46 ''feet'':114 ''foolish'':196 ''former'':127 ''forward'':245 ''full'':32,279 ''garrulity.and'':28 ''great'':52 ''greater'':85 ''hasti'':239 ''hear'':180 ''hebraism'':48 ''hebrew'':40 ''henc'':201 ''improp'':198 ''jame'':171 ''job'':13,260 ''justifi'':36,70,285 ''learn'':120 ''let'':174 ''lip'':42,58,164 ''maintain'':273 ''man'':31,55,67,104,176,189,276,299,302 ''margin'':37 ''maxim'':125 ''may'':220 ''mere'':18,27,57 ''much'':84,192 ''multitud'':4,152 ''note'':258 ''observ'':131 ''offer'':241 ''one'':96 ''opinion'':255 ''orient'':82,216 ''other'':143 ''phrase'':44 ''presumpt'':291 ''proof'':99,207,292 ''proverb'':167 ''prudenc'':209 ''reason'':267 ''recollect'':76 ''refraineth'':162 ''regard'':94,204,229 ''remark'':25 ''safe'':298 ''said'':15,191,311 ''sat'':110 ''say'':146,149,194 ''see'':256 ''silenc'':214 ''sin'':158 ''slow'':181 ''solomon'':148 ''sound'':61 ''speak'':183,247 ''suppos'':186 ''swift'':178 ''taciturn'':78 ''talk'':34,281 ''talker'':53 ''thing'':199 ''thus'':147 ''time'':128 ''uncivil'':231 ''unusu'':234 ''us'':89 ''vindic'':72,306 ''virtu'':86 ''wanteth'':156 ''whether'':64 ''wisdom'':101 ''wise'':103,166 ''word'':6,19,154 ''would'':193 ''zophar'':62,272', NULL, '447');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('448', '2', '448', NULL, 'No doubt but ye are the people - That is, the only wise people. You have engrossed all the wisdom of the world, and all else are to be regarded as fools. This is evidently the language of severe sarcasm; and it shows a spirit fretted and chafed by their reproaches. Job felt contempt for their reasoning. and meant to intimate that their maxims, on which they placed so much reliance, were common-place, and such as every one was familar with.And wisdom shall die with you - This is ironical, but it is language such as is common perhaps every where. "The people of the East," says Roberts, "take great pleasure in irony, and some of their satirical sayings are very cutting. When a sage intimates that he has superior wisdom or when he is disposed to rally another for his meagrc attainments, he says, ''Yes, yes, you are the man! '' ''Your wisdom is like the sea.'' ''When you die, whither will wisdom go?''" In a serious sense, language like this is used by the Classical writers to describe the death of eminently great or good men. They speak of wisdom, bravery, piety, or music, as dying with them. Thus, Moschus, Idyll. iii.12.&#x39f;&#x3c4;&#x3c4;&#x3b9; &#x3b2;&#x3b9;&#x3c9;&#x3bd; &#x3c4;&#x3b5;&#x3b8;&#x3bd;&#x3b7;&#x3ba;&#x3b5;&#x3bd; &#x3bf; &#x3b2;&#x3c9;&#x3ba;&#x3bf;&#x3bb;&#x3bf;&#x3c2;, &#x3b5;&#x3c4;&#x3c4;&#x3b9; &#x3c3;&#x3c5;&#x3bd; &#x3b1;&#x3c5;&#x3c4;&#x3c9;&#x39a;&#x3b1;&#x3b9; &#x3c4;&#x3bf; &#x3bc;&#x3b5;&#x3bb;&#x3bf;&#x3c2; &#x3c4;&#x3b5;&#x3b8;&#x3bd;&#x3b1;&#x3ba;&#x3b5;, &#x3ba;&#x3b1;&#x3b9; &#x3c9;&#x3bb;&#x3b5;&#x3c4;&#x3bf; &#x394;&#x3c9;&#x3c1;&#x3b9;&#x3c2; &#x3b1;&#x3b5;&#x3b9;&#x3b4;&#x3bf;&#x3c2;.Hotti bion tethneken ho bokolos, esti sun autoKai to melos tethnake, kai oleto Doris aeidos."Bion the swain is dead, and with him songHas died, and the Doric muse has perished."Expressions like these are common. Thus, in the "Pleasures of Hope" it is said:And Freedom shrieked when Kosciusko fell.', NULL, NULL, '''aeido'':218 ''anoth'':139 ''attain'':143 ''autokai'':211 ''bion'':205,219 ''bokolo'':208 ''braveri'':192 ''chafe'':47 ''classic'':176 ''common'':73,98,239 ''common-plac'':72 ''contempt'':53 ''cut'':122 ''dead'':223 ''death'':181 ''describ'':179 ''die'':85,160,197,228 ''dispos'':136 ''dori'':217 ''doric'':231 ''doubt'':2 ''east'':106 ''els'':25 ''emin'':183 ''engross'':16 ''esti'':209 ''everi'':78,100 ''evid'':34 ''express'':235 ''familar'':81 ''fell'':254 ''felt'':52 ''fool'':31 ''freedom'':250 ''fret'':45 ''go'':164 ''good'':186 ''great'':110,184 ''ho'':207 ''hope'':245 ''hotti'':204 ''idyl'':202 ''iii.12'':203 ''intim'':60,126 ''iron'':90 ''ironi'':113 ''job'':51 ''kai'':215 ''kosciusko'':253 ''languag'':36,94,169 ''like'':155,170,236 ''man'':151 ''maxim'':63 ''meagrc'':142 ''meant'':58 ''melo'':213 ''men'':187 ''moschus'':201 ''much'':69 ''muse'':232 ''music'':195 ''oleto'':216 ''one'':79 ''peopl'':7,13,103 ''perhap'':99 ''perish'':234 ''pieti'':193 ''place'':67,74 ''pleasur'':111,243 ''ralli'':138 ''reason'':56 ''regard'':29 ''relianc'':70 ''reproach'':50 ''robert'':108 ''sage'':125 ''said'':248 ''sarcasm'':39 ''satir'':118 ''say'':107,119,145 ''sea'':157 ''sens'':168 ''serious'':167 ''sever'':38 ''shall'':84 ''show'':42 ''shriek'':251 ''songha'':227 ''speak'':189 ''spirit'':44 ''sun'':210 ''superior'':130 ''swain'':221 ''take'':109 ''tethnak'':214 ''tethneken'':206 ''thus'':200,240 ''use'':173 ''whither'':161 ''wisdom'':19,83,131,153,163,191 ''wise'':12 ''with.and'':82 ''world'':22 ''writer'':177 ''ye'':4 ''yes'':146,147', NULL, '448');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('449', '2', '449', NULL, 'Lo, mine eye hath seen all this - I have seen illustrations of all that I have said, or that you have said about the methods of divine providence.', NULL, NULL, '''divin'':27 ''eye'':3 ''hath'':4 ''illustr'':11 ''lo'':1 ''method'':25 ''mine'':2 ''provid'':28 ''said'':17,22 ''seen'':5,10', NULL, '449');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('450', '2', '450', NULL, 'Who can bring a clean - thing "out of an unclean?" This is evidently a proverb or an adage; but its connection here is not very apparent. Probably, however, it is designed as a plea of mitigation for his conscious frailties and infirmities. He could not but admit that he had faults. But he asks, how could it be expected to be otherwise? He belonged to a race that was sinful and depraved. Connected with such a race, how could it be otherwise than that he should be prone to evil? Why then did God follow him with so much severity, and hold him with a grasp so close and so unrelenting? Why did he treat him as if he ought to be expected to be perfectly pure, or as if it were reasonable to suppose he would be otherwise than unholy? This passage is of great value as showing the early opinion of the world in regard to the native character of man. The sentiment was undoubtedly common - so common as to have passed into a proverb - that man was a sinner; and that it could not be expected that anyone of the race should be pure and holy.The sentiment is as true as it is obvious - like will beget like all over the world. The nature of the lion, the tiger, the hyaena, the serpent is propagated, and so the same thing is true of man. It is a great law, that the offspring will resemble the parentage; and as the offspring of the lion is not a lamb but a young lion; of a wolf is not a kid but a young wolf, so the offspring of man is not an angel, but is a man with the same nature, the same moral character, the same proneness to evil with the parent. The Chaldee renders this: "Who will give one pure from a man polluted in sin, except God, who is one, and who forgiveth him?" But this is manifestly a departure from the sense of the passage. Jerome, however, has adopted nearly the same translation. As a historical record, this passage proves that the doctrine of original sin was early held in the world. Still it is true that the same great law prevails, that the off-spring of woman is a sinner - no matter where he may be born, or in what circumstances he may be placed. No art, no philosophy, no system of religion can prevent the operation of this great law under which we live, and by which we die; compare the notes at Romans 5:19.', NULL, NULL, '''19'':434 ''5'':433 ''adag'':18 ''admit'':47 ''adopt'':344 ''angel'':284 ''anyon'':191 ''appar'':26 ''art'':404 ''ask'':54 ''beget'':210 ''belong'':64 ''born'':394 ''bring'':3 ''chalde'':306 ''charact'':161,296 ''circumst'':398 ''clean'':5 ''close'':108 ''common'':168,170 ''compar'':428 ''connect'':21,73 ''conscious'':39 ''could'':44,56,79,186 ''departur'':334 ''deprav'':72 ''design'':31 ''die'':427 ''doctrin'':358 ''earli'':151,363 ''evid'':13 ''evil'':90,301 ''except'':320 ''expect'':59,123,189 ''fault'':51 ''follow'':95 ''forgiveth'':327 ''frailti'':40 ''give'':311 ''god'':94,321 ''grasp'':106 ''great'':146,241,375,417 ''held'':364 ''histor'':351 ''hold'':102 ''holy.the'':199 ''howev'':28,342 ''hyaena'':224 ''infirm'':42 ''jerom'':341 ''kid'':271 ''lamb'':260 ''law'':242,376,418 ''like'':208,211 ''lion'':220,256,264 ''live'':422 ''man'':163,179,237,280,288,316 ''manifest'':332 ''matter'':389 ''may'':392,400 ''mitig'':36 ''moral'':295 ''much'':99 ''nativ'':160 ''natur'':217,292 ''near'':345 ''note'':430 ''obvious'':207 ''off-spr'':380 ''offspr'':245,253,278 ''one'':312,324 ''oper'':414 ''opinion'':152 ''origin'':360 ''otherwis'':62,82,139 ''ought'':120 ''parent'':304 ''parentag'':249 ''pass'':174 ''passag'':143,340,354 ''perfect'':126 ''philosophi'':406 ''place'':402 ''plea'':34 ''pollut'':317 ''prevail'':377 ''prevent'':412 ''probabl'':27 ''prone'':88,299 ''propag'':228 ''prove'':355 ''proverb'':15,177 ''pure'':127,197,313 ''race'':67,77,194 ''reason'':133 ''record'':352 ''regard'':157 ''religion'':410 ''render'':307 ''resembl'':247 ''roman'':432 ''sens'':337 ''sentiment'':165,200 ''serpent'':226 ''sever'':100 ''show'':149 ''sin'':70,319,361 ''sinner'':182,387 ''spring'':382 ''still'':368 ''suppos'':135 ''system'':408 ''thing'':6,233 ''tiger'':222 ''translat'':348 ''treat'':115 ''true'':203,235,371 ''unclean'':10 ''undoubt'':167 ''unholi'':141 ''unrel'':111 ''valu'':147 ''wolf'':267,275 ''woman'':384 ''world'':155,215,367 ''would'':137 ''young'':263,274', NULL, '450');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('451', '2', '451', NULL, 'Because he covereth his face with his fatness - That is, he not only stretches out his hand against God Job 15:25 and rushes upon him as an armed foe Job 15:26, but he gives himself up to a life of luxury, gluttony, and licentiousness; and therefore, these calamities must come upon him. This is designed to be a description of a luxurious and licentious person - a man who is an enemy of God, and who, therefore, must incur his displeasure.And maketh collops of fat - Like an ox that is fattened. The word collop properly means "a small slice of meat, a piece of flesh" (Webster), but here it means a thick piece, or a mass. The word is used in this sense in New England. The sense is, that he becomes excessively fat and gross - as they usually do who live in sensual indulgence and who forget God.', NULL, NULL, '''15'':21,32 ''25'':22 ''26'':33 ''arm'':29 ''becom'':133 ''calam'':50 ''collop'':84,95 ''come'':52 ''covereth'':3 ''descript'':61 ''design'':57 ''displeasure.and'':82 ''enemi'':73 ''england'':127 ''excess'':134 ''face'':5 ''fat'':8,86,135 ''fatten'':92 ''flesh'':106 ''foe'':30 ''forget'':149 ''give'':36 ''gluttoni'':44 ''god'':19,75,150 ''gross'':137 ''hand'':17 ''incur'':80 ''indulg'':146 ''job'':20,31 ''licenti'':46,66 ''life'':41 ''like'':87 ''live'':143 ''luxuri'':43,64 ''maketh'':83 ''man'':69 ''mass'':117 ''mean'':97,111 ''meat'':102 ''must'':51,79 ''new'':126 ''ox'':89 ''person'':67 ''piec'':104,114 ''proper'':96 ''rush'':24 ''sens'':124,129 ''sensual'':145 ''slice'':100 ''small'':99 ''stretch'':14 ''therefor'':48,78 ''thick'':113 ''upon'':25,53 ''use'':121 ''usual'':140 ''webster'':107 ''word'':94,119', NULL, '451');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('452', '2', '452', NULL, 'Many such things - That is, either things fitted to provoke and irritate, or sentiments that are common-place. There was nothing new in what they said, and nothing to the purpose.Miserable comforters - Compare Job 13:4. They had come professedly to condole with him. Now all that they said was adapted only to irritate, and to deepen his distress. He was disappointed; and he was deeply wounded and grieved.', NULL, NULL, '''13'':36 ''4'':37 ''adapt'':52 ''come'':40 ''comfort'':33 ''common'':18 ''common-plac'':17 ''compar'':34 ''condol'':43 ''deepen'':58 ''deepli'':67 ''disappoint'':63 ''distress'':60 ''either'':6 ''fit'':8 ''griev'':70 ''irrit'':12,55 ''job'':35 ''mani'':1 ''new'':23 ''noth'':22,29 ''place'':19 ''profess'':41 ''provok'':10 ''purpose.miserable'':32 ''said'':27,50 ''sentiment'':14 ''thing'':3,7 ''wound'':68', NULL, '452');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('453', '2', '453', NULL, 'My breath is corrupt - Margin or "spirit is spent." The idea is, that his vital powers were nearly extinct; his breath failed; his power was weakened, and he was ready to die. This is connected with the previous chapter, and should not have been separated from it. There was no necessity of making a new chapter here, and we have one of those unfortunate breaks in the middle of a paragraph, and almost of a sentence, which are too common in the Scriptures.The graves are ready for me - The Hebrew is plural, but why so used I know not. The Vulgate is singular - sepulchrum. The Septuagint renders it, "I pray for a tomb (singular, &#x3c4;&#x3b1;&#x3c6;&#x3b7;&#x3c2;  taphes), but I cannot obtain it." Possibly the meaning is, "I am about to be united "to the graves," or "to tombs."" Schultens remarks that the plural form is common in Arabic poetry, as well as in poetry in general.', NULL, NULL, '''almost'':73 ''arab'':146 ''break'':65 ''breath'':2,21 ''cannot'':118 ''chapter'':39,56 ''common'':80,144 ''connect'':35 ''corrupt'':4 ''die'':32 ''extinct'':19 ''fail'':22 ''form'':142 ''general'':154 ''grave'':84,133 ''hebrew'':90 ''idea'':11 ''know'':98 ''make'':53 ''margin'':5 ''mean'':123 ''middl'':68 ''near'':18 ''necess'':51 ''new'':55 ''obtain'':119 ''one'':61 ''paragraph'':71 ''plural'':92,141 ''poetri'':147,152 ''possibl'':121 ''power'':16,24 ''pray'':110 ''previous'':38 ''readi'':30,86 ''remark'':138 ''render'':107 ''schulten'':137 ''scriptures.the'':83 ''sentenc'':76 ''separ'':45 ''septuagint'':106 ''sepulchrum'':104 ''singular'':103,114 ''spent'':9 ''spirit'':7 ''taph'':115 ''tomb'':113,136 ''unfortun'':64 ''unit'':130 ''use'':96 ''vital'':15 ''vulgat'':101 ''weaken'':26 ''well'':149', NULL, '453');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('454', '2', '454', NULL, 'How long will it be ere ye make an end of words? - It has been made a question to whom this is addressed. It is in the plural number, and it is not usual in Hebrew when addressing an individual to make use of the plural form. Some have supposed that it is addressed to Job and to Eliphaz, as being both "long-winded" and tedious in their remarks. Others have supposed that it refers to Job "and the members of his family," who possibly interposed remarks, and joined Job in his complaints. Others suppose that it refers to Eliphaz and Zophar, as being silent during the speech of Job, and not arresting his remarks as they ought to have done. Rosenmuller supposes that it refers to Job and those similar to him, who were mere feigners of piety, and that Bildad means to ask how long it would be before they would be effectually silenced, and their complaints hushed. I see no great difficulty in supposing that the reference is to Job. The whole strain of the discourse evidently supposes it; and there is no evidence that any of the family of Job had spoken, nor does it seem at all probable that Bildad would reprove his own friends either for the length of their speeches, or for not interrupting an other. The custom in the East is to allow a man to utter all that he has to say without interruption.Mark - Hebrew understand; or be intelligent -   tabynu; that is, either speak distinctly, clearly, intelligently; or consider and weigh our arguments. The former is the interpretation of Schultens, and seems to me to be the true one. The idea is this: "You, Job, have been altering mere words. They are words of complaint, without argument. Speak now in a different manner; show that you understand the case; advance arguments that are worthy of attention, and then we will reply."', NULL, NULL, '''address'':23,38,54 ''advanc'':310 ''allow'':232 ''alter'':288 ''argument'':263,297,311 ''arrest'':114 ''ask'':146 ''attent'':316 ''bildad'':143,206 ''case'':309 ''clear'':256 ''complaint'':94,160,295 ''consid'':259 ''custom'':226 ''differ'':302 ''difficulti'':166 ''discours'':180 ''distinct'':255 ''done'':122 ''east'':229 ''effectu'':156 ''either'':212,253 ''eliphaz'':59,101 ''end'':10 ''ere'':6 ''evid'':181,188 ''famili'':84,193 ''feigner'':138 ''form'':47 ''former'':265 ''friend'':211 ''great'':165 ''hebrew'':36,245 ''hush'':161 ''idea'':281 ''individu'':40 ''intellig'':249,257 ''interpos'':87 ''interpret'':268 ''interrupt'':222 ''interruption.mark'':244 ''job'':56,78,91,111,129,174,195,285 ''join'':90 ''length'':215 ''long'':2,64,148 ''long-wind'':63 ''made'':16 ''make'':8,42 ''man'':234 ''manner'':303 ''mean'':144 ''member'':81 ''mere'':137,289 ''number'':29 ''one'':279 ''other'':71,95 ''ought'':119 ''pieti'':140 ''plural'':28,46 ''possibl'':86 ''probabl'':204 ''question'':18 ''refer'':76,99,127,171 ''remark'':70,88,116 ''repli'':321 ''reprov'':208 ''rosenmul'':123 ''say'':242 ''schulten'':270 ''see'':163 ''seem'':201,272 ''show'':304 ''silenc'':157 ''silent'':106 ''similar'':132 ''speak'':254,298 ''speech'':109,218 ''spoken'':197 ''strain'':177 ''suppos'':50,73,96,124,168,182 ''tabynu'':250 ''tedious'':67 ''true'':278 ''understand'':246,307 ''use'':43 ''usual'':34 ''utter'':236 ''weigh'':261 ''whole'':176 ''wind'':65 ''without'':243,296 ''word'':12,290,293 ''worthi'':314 ''would'':150,154,207 ''ye'':7 ''zophar'':103', NULL, '454');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('455', '2', '455', NULL, 'How long will ye vex my soul? - Perhaps designing to reply to the taunting speech of Bildad; Job 18:2. "He" had asked "how long it would be ere Job would make an end of empty talk?" "Job" asks, in reply, "how long" they would torture and afflict his soul? Or whether there was on hope that this would ever come to an end!And break me in pieces - Crush me, or bruise me - like breaking any thing in a mortar, or breaking rocks by repeated blows of the hammer. "Noyes." He says they had crushed him, as if by repeated blows.', NULL, NULL, '''18'':19 ''2'':20 ''afflict'':48 ''ask'':23,39 ''bildad'':17 ''blow'':87,102 ''break'':66,76,83 ''bruis'':73 ''come'':61 ''crush'':70,96 ''design'':9 ''empti'':36 ''end'':34,64 ''ere'':29 ''ever'':60 ''hammer'':90 ''hope'':56 ''job'':18,30,38 ''like'':75 ''long'':2,25,43 ''make'':32 ''mortar'':81 ''noy'':91 ''perhap'':8 ''piec'':69 ''repeat'':86,101 ''repli'':11,41 ''rock'':84 ''say'':93 ''soul'':7,50 ''speech'':15 ''talk'':37 ''taunt'':14 ''thing'':78 ''tortur'':46 ''vex'':5 ''whether'':52 ''would'':27,31,45,59 ''ye'':4', NULL, '455');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('456', '2', '456', NULL, 'In the fulness of his sufficiency - When he seems to have an abundance.He shall be in straits - Either by the dread of calamity, or because calamity shall come suddenly upon him, and his property shall be swept away. When everything seemed to be abundant he should be reduced to want.Every hand of the wicked shall come upon him - Margin, "or, troublesome" The meaning is, that all that the wretched or miserable endure should come suddenly upon him. Rosenmuller suggests, however, that it means that all the poor, and all who had been oppressed and robbed by him, would suddenly come upon him to recover their own property, and would scatter all that he had. The general meaning is clear, that he would be involved in misery from every quarter, or on every hand.', NULL, NULL, '''abund'':44 ''abundance.he'':13 ''away'':38 ''calam'':23,26 ''clear'':119 ''come'':28,56,74,100 ''dread'':21 ''either'':18 ''endur'':72 ''everi'':128,132 ''everyth'':40 ''ful'':3 ''general'':116 ''hand'':51,133 ''howev'':80 ''involv'':124 ''margin'':59 ''mean'':63,83,117 ''miser'':71 ''miseri'':126 ''oppress'':93 ''poor'':87 ''properti'':34,107 ''quarter'':129 ''recov'':104 ''reduc'':48 ''rob'':95 ''rosenmul'':78 ''scatter'':110 ''seem'':9,41 ''shall'':14,27,35,55 ''strait'':17 ''sudden'':29,75,99 ''suffici'':6 ''suggest'':79 ''swept'':37 ''troublesom'':61 ''upon'':30,57,76,101 ''want.every'':50 ''wick'':54 ''would'':98,109,122 ''wretch'':69', NULL, '456');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('457', '2', '457', NULL, 'Hear diligently - Hebrew "Hearing hear" - that is, hear attentively. What he was about to say was worthy of their solemn consideration.And let this be your consolations - That is, "You came to me for the professed purpose of giving "me" consolation. In that you have wholly failed. You have done nothing to sustain or comfort me; but all that you have said has only tended to exasperate me, and to increase my sorrow. If you will now hear me attentively, I will take that as a consolation, and it shall be in the place of what I had a right to expect from you. It will be "some" comfort if I am permitted to express my sentiments without interruption, and I will accept it as a proof of kindness on your part."', NULL, NULL, '''accept'':122 ''attent'':9,79 ''came'':30 ''comfort'':54,108 ''consideration.and'':21 ''consol'':26,40,86 ''dilig'':2 ''done'':49 ''exasper'':66 ''expect'':101 ''express'':114 ''fail'':46 ''give'':38 ''hear'':1,4,5,8,77 ''hebrew'':3 ''increas'':70 ''interrupt'':118 ''kind'':128 ''let'':22 ''noth'':50 ''part'':131 ''permit'':112 ''place'':93 ''profess'':35 ''proof'':126 ''purpos'':36 ''right'':99 ''said'':61 ''say'':15 ''sentiment'':116 ''shall'':89 ''solemn'':20 ''sorrow'':72 ''sustain'':52 ''take'':82 ''tend'':64 ''wholli'':45 ''without'':117 ''worthi'':17', NULL, '457');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('458', '2', '458', NULL, 'Is not God in the height of heaven? - In the highest heaven. That is, Is not God exalted over all worlds? This seems to be intended to refer to the sentiments of Job, as if he had maintained that God was so exalted that he could not notice what was occurring on earth. It should, therefore, be read in connection with the following verse: "God is so exalted, that thou sayest, How can he know? Can he look down through the thick clouds which intervene between him and man?" Job had maintained no such opinion, but the process of thought in the mind of Eliphaz seems to have been this. Job had maintained that God did "not" punish the wicked in this life as they deserved, but that they lived and prospered. Eliphaz "inferred" that he could hold that opinion only because he supposed that God was so exalted that he could not attend to worldly affairs. He knew no other way in which the opinion could be held, and he proceeds to argue "as if" it were so.Job had in the previous chapter appealed to plain "facts," and had rested his whole argument on them. Eliphaz, instead of meeting the "facts" in the case, or showing that they did not exist as Job said they did, considered his discourse as a denial of Divine Providence, and as representing God to be so far above the earth that he could not notice what was occurring here. How common is this in theological controversy! One man, in defending his opinions, or in searching for the truth, appeals to "facts," and endeavors to ascertain their nature and bearing. His adversary, instead of meeting them, or showing that they are not so, at once appeals to some admitted doctrine, to some established article of a creed, or to some tradition of the fathers, and says that the appeal to fact is but a denial of an important doctrine of revelation. It is easier to charge a man with denying the doctrine of Providence, or to call him by a harsh name, than it is to meet an argument drawn from fact and from the plain meaning of the Bible.And behold the height of the stars - Margin, as in Hebrew "head" -   ro''sh. God is more exalted than the highest of the stars. The stars are the highest objects in view, and the sense, therefore, is, that God is infinitely exalted.', NULL, NULL, '''admit'':295 ''adversari'':278 ''affair'':157 ''appeal'':185,266,292,315 ''argu'':174 ''argument'':194,355 ''articl'':300 ''ascertain'':272 ''attend'':154 ''bear'':276 ''behold'':367 ''bible.and'':366 ''call'':343 ''case'':205 ''chapter'':184 ''charg'':332 ''cloud'':83 ''common'':248 ''connect'':60 ''consid'':218 ''controversi'':253 ''could'':46,137,152,167,240 ''creed'':303 ''defend'':257 ''deni'':336 ''denial'':223,321 ''deserv'':126 ''discours'':220 ''divin'':225 ''doctrin'':296,325,338 ''drawn'':356 ''earth'':53,237 ''easier'':330 ''eliphaz'':105,133,197 ''endeavor'':270 ''establish'':299 ''exalt'':18,43,68,149,383,407 ''exist'':212 ''fact'':188,202,268,317,358 ''far'':234 ''father'':310 ''follow'':63 ''god'':3,17,40,65,115,146,230,380,404 ''harsh'':347 ''head'':377 ''heaven'':8,12 ''hebrew'':376 ''height'':6,369 ''held'':169 ''highest'':11,386,394 ''hold'':138 ''import'':324 ''infer'':134 ''infinit'':406 ''instead'':198,279 ''intend'':26 ''interven'':85 ''job'':33,90,111,214 ''knew'':159 ''know'':75 ''life'':123 ''live'':130 ''look'':78 ''maintain'':38,92,113 ''man'':89,255,334 ''margin'':373 ''mean'':363 ''meet'':200,281,353 ''mind'':103 ''name'':348 ''natur'':274 ''notic'':48,242 ''object'':395 ''occur'':51,245 ''one'':254 ''opinion'':95,140,166,259 ''plain'':187,362 ''previous'':183 ''proceed'':172 ''process'':98 ''prosper'':132 ''provid'':226,340 ''punish'':118 ''read'':58 ''refer'':28 ''repres'':229 ''rest'':191 ''revel'':327 ''ro'':378 ''said'':215 ''say'':312 ''sayest'':71 ''search'':262 ''seem'':23,106 ''sens'':400 ''sentiment'':31 ''sh'':379 ''show'':207,284 ''so.job'':179 ''star'':372,389,391 ''suppos'':144 ''theolog'':252 ''therefor'':56,401 ''thick'':82 ''thou'':70 ''thought'':100 ''tradit'':307 ''truth'':265 ''vers'':64 ''view'':397 ''way'':162 ''whole'':193 ''wick'':120 ''world'':21,156', NULL, '458');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('459', '2', '459', NULL, 'Even to-day - At the present time. I am not relieved. You afford me no consolation. All that you say only aggravates my woes.My complaint - See the notes at Job 21:3.Bitter - Sad, melancholy, distressing. The meaning is, not that he made bitter complaints in the sense which those words would naturally convey, or that he meant to find fault with God, but that his case was a hard one. His friends furnished him no relief, and he had in vain endeavored to bring his cause before God. This is now, as he proceeds to state, the principal cause of his difficulty. He knows not where to find God; he cannot get his cause before him.My stroke - Margin, as in Hebrew "hand;" that is, the hand that is upon me, or the calamity that is inflicted upon me. The hand is represented as the instrument of inflicting punishment, or causing affliction; see the notes at Job 19:21.Heavier than my groaning - My sighs bear no proportion to my sufferings. They are no adequate expression of my woes. If you think I complain; if I am heard to groan, yet the sufferings which I endure are far beyond what these would secm to indicate. Sighs and groans are not improper. They are prompted by nature, and they furnish "some" relief to a sufferer. But they should not be:(1) with a spirit of murmuring or complaining;(2) they should not be beyond what our sufferings demand, or the proper expression of our sufferings. They should not be such as to lead others to suppose we suffer more than we actually do.(3) they should - when they are extorted from us by the severity of suffering - lead us go look to that world where no groan will ever be heard.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':229 ''19'':158 ''2'':237 ''21'':32 ''21.heavier'':159 ''3'':272 ''3.bitter'':33 ''actual'':270 ''adequ'':174 ''afflict'':152 ''afford'':14 ''aggrav'':23 ''bear'':165 ''beyond'':198,242 ''bitter'':44 ''bring'':85 ''calam'':134 ''cannot'':112 ''case'':67 ''caus'':87,100,115,151 ''complain'':183,236 ''complaint'':26,45 ''consol'':17 ''convey'':54 ''day'':4 ''demand'':246 ''difficulti'':103 ''distress'':36 ''endeavor'':83 ''endur'':195 ''even'':1 ''ever'':297 ''express'':175,250 ''extort'':278 ''far'':197 ''fault'':61 ''find'':60,109 ''friend'':73 ''furnish'':74,218 ''get'':113 ''go'':288 ''god'':63,89,110 ''groan'':162,189,207,295 ''hand'':123,127,141 ''hard'':70 ''heard'':187,299 ''hebrew'':122 ''him.my'':117 ''improp'':210 ''indic'':204 ''inflict'':137,148 ''instrument'':146 ''job'':31,157 ''know'':105 ''lead'':261,286 ''look'':289 ''made'':43 ''margin'':119 ''mean'':38 ''meant'':58 ''melancholi'':35 ''murmur'':234 ''natur'':53,215 ''note'':29,155 ''one'':71 ''other'':262 ''present'':7 ''princip'':99 ''proceed'':95 ''prompt'':213 ''proper'':249 ''proport'':167 ''punish'':149 ''relief'':77,220 ''reliev'':12 ''repres'':143 ''sad'':34 ''say'':21 ''secm'':202 ''see'':27,153 ''sens'':48 ''sever'':283 ''sigh'':164,205 ''spirit'':232 ''state'':97 ''stroke'':118 ''suffer'':170,192,223,245,253,266,285 ''suppos'':264 ''think'':181 ''time'':8 ''to-day'':2 ''upon'':130,138 ''us'':280,287 ''vain'':82 ''woe'':178 ''woes.my'':25 ''word'':51 ''world'':292 ''would'':52,201 ''yet'':190', NULL, '459');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('508', '2', '508', NULL, 'I will extol thee - literally, "I will exalt thee;" that is, he would make God first and supreme in his thoughts and affections; he would do what he could to make Him known; he would elevate Him high in his praises.For thou hast lifted me up - To wit, from the state of danger in which I was Psalm 30:2-3. The Hebrew word used here means properly to draw out, as from a well; and then, to deliver, to set free. As God had thus lifted him up, it was proper that he should show his gratitude by "lifting up" or extolling the name of God.And hast not made my foes to rejoice over me - Hast not suffered them to triumph over me; that is, thou hast delivered me from them. He refers to the fact that he had been saved from a dangerous illness, and that his enemies had not been allowed to exult over his death. Compare the notes at Psalm 41:5.', NULL, NULL, '''-3'':61 ''2'':60 ''30'':59 ''41'':165 ''5'':166 ''affect'':23 ''allow'':154 ''compar'':160 ''could'':29 ''danger'':53,145 ''death'':159 ''deliv'':79,129 ''draw'':70 ''elev'':36 ''enemi'':150 ''exalt'':8 ''extol'':3,103 ''exult'':156 ''fact'':137 ''first'':16 ''foe'':112 ''free'':82 ''god'':15,84 ''god.and'':107 ''gratitud'':98 ''hast'':43,108,117,128 ''hebrew'':63 ''high'':38 ''ill'':146 ''known'':33 ''lift'':44,87,100 ''liter'':5 ''made'':110 ''make'':14,31 ''mean'':67 ''name'':105 ''note'':162 ''praises.for'':41 ''proper'':68,92 ''psalm'':58,164 ''refer'':134 ''rejoic'':114 ''save'':142 ''set'':81 ''show'':96 ''state'':51 ''suffer'':119 ''suprem'':18 ''thee'':4,9 ''thou'':42,127 ''thought'':21 ''thus'':86 ''triumph'':122 ''use'':65 ''well'':75 ''wit'':48 ''word'':64 ''would'':13,25,35', NULL, '508');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('460', '2', '460', NULL, 'They are wet with the showers of the mountains - That is, the poor persons, or the travelers whom they have robbed. Hills collect the clouds, and showers seem to pour down from the mountains. These showers often collect and pour down so suddenly that there is scarcely time to seek a shelter.And embrace the rock for want of a shelter - Take refuge beneath a projecting rock. The robbers drive them away from their homes, or plunder them of their tents, and leave them to find a shelter from the storm, or at night, beneath a rock. This agrees exactly with what Niebuhr says of the wandering Arabs near mount Sinai: "Those who cannot afford a tent, spread out a cloth upon four or six stakes; and others spread their cloth near a tree, or endeavor to shelter themselves from the heat and the rain in the cavities of the rocks. Reisebeschreib. i. Thes s. 233.', NULL, NULL, '''233'':155 ''afford'':114 ''agre'':98 ''arab'':107 ''away'':71 ''beneath'':63,94 ''cannot'':113 ''caviti'':147 ''cloth'':120,130 ''cloud'':25 ''collect'':23,38 ''drive'':69 ''embrac'':53 ''endeavor'':135 ''exact'':99 ''find'':85 ''four'':122 ''heat'':141 ''hill'':22 ''home'':74 ''leav'':82 ''mount'':109 ''mountain'':9,34 ''near'':108,131 ''niebuhr'':102 ''night'':93 ''often'':37 ''other'':127 ''person'':14 ''plunder'':76 ''poor'':13 ''pour'':30,40 ''project'':65 ''rain'':144 ''refug'':62 ''reisebeschreib'':151 ''rob'':21 ''robber'':68 ''rock'':55,66,96,150 ''say'':103 ''scarc'':47 ''seek'':50 ''seem'':28 ''shelter'':60,87,137 ''shelter.and'':52 ''shower'':6,27,36 ''sinai'':110 ''six'':124 ''spread'':117,128 ''stake'':125 ''storm'':90 ''sudden'':43 ''take'':61 ''tent'':80,116 ''thes'':153 ''time'':48 ''travel'':17 ''tree'':133 ''upon'':121 ''wander'':106 ''want'':57 ''wet'':3', NULL, '460');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('461', '2', '461', NULL, 'Dominion and fear are with him - That is, God has a right to rule, and he ought to be regarded with reverence. The object of Bildad is to show that He is so great and glorious that it is impossible that man should be regarded as pure in his sight. He begins, therefore, by saying, that he is a Sovereign; that he is clothed with majesty, and that he is worthy of profound veneration.He maketh peace in his high places - "High places," here refer to the heavenly worlds. The idea is, that he preserves peace and concord among the hosts of heaven. Numerous and mighty as are the armies of the skies, yet he keeps them in order and in awe. The object is to present an image of the majesty and power of that Being who thus controls a vast number of minds. The phrase does not necessarily imply that there had been variance or strife, and that then God had made peace, but that he preserved or kept them in peace.', NULL, NULL, '''among'':98 ''armi'':109 ''awe'':121 ''begin'':52 ''bildad'':26 ''cloth'':64 ''concord'':97 ''control'':139 ''dominion'':1 ''fear'':3 ''glorious'':36 ''god'':9,161 ''great'':34 ''heaven'':87,102 ''high'':79,81 ''host'':100 ''idea'':90 ''imag'':128 ''impli'':150 ''imposs'':40 ''keep'':115 ''kept'':170 ''made'':163 ''majesti'':66,131 ''maketh'':75 ''man'':42 ''mighti'':105 ''mind'':144 ''necessarili'':149 ''number'':142 ''numer'':103 ''object'':24,123 ''order'':118 ''ought'':17 ''peac'':76,95,164,173 ''phrase'':146 ''place'':80,82 ''power'':133 ''present'':126 ''preserv'':94,168 ''profound'':73 ''pure'':47 ''refer'':84 ''regard'':20,45 ''rever'':22 ''right'':12 ''rule'':14 ''say'':55 ''show'':29 ''sight'':50 ''sky'':112 ''sovereign'':60 ''strife'':157 ''therefor'':53 ''thus'':138 ''varianc'':155 ''vast'':141 ''veneration.he'':74 ''world'':88 ''worthi'':71 ''yet'':113', NULL, '461');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('462', '2', '462', NULL, 'How hast thou helped him that is without power? - It has been doubted whether this refers to Job himself, the two friends of Bildad, or to the Deity. Rosenmuller. The connection, however, seems to demand that it should be referred to Job himself. It is sarcastical. Bildad had come as a friend and comforter. He had, also, in common with Eliphaz and Zophar, taken upon himself the office of teacher and counsellor. He had regarded Job as manifesting great weakness in his views of God and of his government; as destitute of all strength to bear up aright under trials, and now all that he had done to aid one so weak was found in the impertinent and irrelevant generalities of his brief speech. Job is indignant that one with such pretensions should have said nothing more to the purpose. Herder, however, renders this as if it related wholly to God, and it cannot be denied that the Hebrew would bear this:"Whom helpest thou? Him who hath no strength?Whom dost thou vindicate? Him whose arm hath no power?To whom give counsel? One without wisdom?Truly much wisdom hast thou taught him."How savest thou the arm that hath no strength? - That is, your remarks are not adapted to invigorate the feeble. He had come professedly to comfort and support his afflicted friend in his trials. Yet Job asks what there was in his observations that was fitted to produce this effect? Instead of declaiming on the majesty and greatness of God, he should have said something that was adapted to relieve an afflicted and a troubled soul.', NULL, NULL, '''adapt'':210,262 ''afflict'':224,266 ''aid'':109 ''also'':57 ''aright'':98 ''arm'':177,199 ''ask'':231 ''bear'':96,161 ''bildad'':24,47 ''brief'':123 ''cannot'':154 ''come'':49,217 ''comfort'':54,220 ''common'':59 ''connect'':31 ''counsel'':184 ''counsellor'':72 ''declaim'':247 ''deiti'':28 ''demand'':35 ''deni'':156 ''destitut'':91 ''done'':107 ''dost'':172 ''doubt'':13 ''effect'':244 ''eliphaz'':61 ''feebl'':214 ''fit'':240 ''found'':114 ''friend'':22,52,225 ''general'':120 ''give'':183 ''god'':85,151,254 ''govern'':89 ''great'':79,252 ''hast'':2,191 ''hath'':168,178,201 ''hebrew'':159 ''help'':4 ''helpest'':164 ''herder'':141 ''howev'':32,142 ''impertin'':117 ''indign'':127 ''instead'':245 ''invigor'':212 ''irrelev'':119 ''job'':18,42,76,125,230 ''majesti'':250 ''manifest'':78 ''much'':189 ''noth'':136 ''observ'':237 ''offic'':68 ''one'':110,129,185 ''power'':9,180 ''pretens'':132 ''produc'':242 ''profess'':218 ''purpos'':140 ''refer'':16,40 ''regard'':75 ''relat'':148 ''reliev'':264 ''remark'':207 ''render'':143 ''rosenmul'':29 ''said'':135,258 ''sarcast'':46 ''savest'':196 ''seem'':33 ''someth'':259 ''soul'':270 ''speech'':124 ''strength'':94,170,203 ''support'':222 ''taken'':64 ''taught'':193 ''teacher'':70 ''thou'':3,165,173,192,197 ''trial'':100,228 ''troubl'':269 ''truli'':188 ''two'':21 ''upon'':65 ''view'':83 ''vindic'':174 ''weak'':80,112 ''whether'':14 ''wholli'':149 ''whose'':176 ''wisdom'':187,190 ''without'':8,186 ''would'':160 ''yet'':229 ''zophar'':63', NULL, '462');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('463', '2', '463', NULL, 'The east wind carrieth him away - He is swept off as by the violence of a tempest. Severe storms are represented in this book as coming from the East; compare the notes at Job 15:2. The ancients believed that people might be carried away by a tempest or whirlwind; compare Isaiah 41:16; see also Homer, Odyssey xx. 63ff:"Snatch me, ye whirlwinds far from human race,Test through the void illimitable space;Or if dismounted from the rapid cloud,Me with his whelming wave let Ocean shroud!"PopeCompare the notes at Job 30:22. The parallelism here would seem to imply that the wind referred to was violent, but it is possible that the allusion may be to the burning winds of the desert, so well known in the East, and so frequently described by travelers. The Vulgate here renders the Hebrew word   qadym, ventus urens, "burning wind;" the Septuagint in like manner, &#x3ba;&#x3b1;&#x3c5;&#x3c3;&#x3c9;&#x3bd;  kauson; the Syriac simply wind. This east wind, or burning wind, is what the Arabians call Samum. It is a hot wind which passes over the desert, and which was formerly supposed to be destructive of life. More recent travelers however, tell us that it is not fatal to life, though exceedingly oppressive.And as a storm - See Psalm 58:9.Hurleth him out of his place - Takes him entirely away, or removes him from the earth.', NULL, NULL, '''15'':35 ''16'':54 ''2'':36 ''22'':96 ''30'':95 ''41'':53 ''58'':214 ''63ff'':60 ''9.hurleth'':215 ''allus'':117 ''also'':56 ''ancient'':38 ''arabian'':170 ''away'':6,45,224 ''believ'':39 ''book'':24 ''burn'':122,149,165 ''call'':171 ''carri'':44 ''carrieth'':4 ''cloud'':81 ''come'':26 ''compar'':30,51 ''describ'':136 ''desert'':126,182 ''destruct'':190 ''dismount'':77 ''earth'':230 ''east'':2,29,132,162 ''entir'':223 ''exceed'':207 ''far'':65 ''fatal'':203 ''former'':186 ''frequent'':135 ''hebrew'':144 ''homer'':57 ''hot'':176 ''howev'':196 ''human'':67 ''illimit'':73 ''impli'':103 ''isaiah'':52 ''job'':34,94 ''kauson'':156 ''known'':129 ''let'':87 ''life'':192,205 ''like'':154 ''manner'':155 ''may'':118 ''might'':42 ''note'':32,92 ''ocean'':88 ''odyssey'':58 ''oppressive.and'':208 ''parallel'':98 ''pass'':179 ''peopl'':41 ''place'':220 ''popecompar'':90 ''possibl'':114 ''psalm'':213 ''qadym'':146 ''race'':68 ''rapid'':80 ''recent'':194 ''refer'':107 ''remov'':226 ''render'':142 ''repres'':21 ''samum'':172 ''see'':55,212 ''seem'':101 ''septuagint'':152 ''sever'':18 ''shroud'':89 ''simpli'':159 ''snatch'':61 ''space'':74 ''storm'':19,211 ''suppos'':187 ''swept'':9 ''syriac'':158 ''take'':221 ''tell'':197 ''tempest'':17,48 ''test'':69 ''though'':206 ''travel'':138,195 ''uren'':148 ''us'':198 ''ventus'':147 ''violenc'':14 ''violent'':110 ''void'':72 ''vulgat'':140 ''wave'':86 ''well'':128 ''whelm'':85 ''whirlwind'':50,64 ''wind'':3,106,123,150,160,163,166,177 ''word'':145 ''would'':100 ''xx'':59 ''ye'':63', NULL, '463');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('464', '2', '464', NULL, 'Whence then cometh wisdom? - This question is now repeated from Job 28:12, in order to give it greater emphasis. It is designed to fix the attention on the inquiry as one which found no solution in the discoveries of science, and whose solution was hidden from the most penetrating human intellect.', NULL, NULL, '''12'':13 ''28'':12 ''attent'':27 ''cometh'':3 ''design'':23 ''discoveri'':39 ''emphasi'':20 ''fix'':25 ''found'':34 ''give'':17 ''greater'':19 ''hidden'':46 ''human'':51 ''inquiri'':30 ''intellect'':52 ''job'':11 ''one'':32 ''order'':15 ''penetr'':50 ''question'':6 ''repeat'':9 ''scienc'':41 ''solut'':36,44 ''whenc'':1 ''whose'':43 ''wisdom'':4', NULL, '464');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('465', '2', '465', NULL, 'Moreover, Job continued his parable - See the notes at Job 27:1. It is probable that Job had paused to see if anyone would attempt a reply. As his friends were silent, he resumed his remarks and went into a more full statement of his sufferings. The fact that Job more than once paused in his addresses to give his friends an opportunity to speak, and that they were silent when they seemed called upon to vindicate their former sentiments, was what particularly roused the wrath of Elihu and induced him to answer; Job 32:2-5.', NULL, NULL, '''-5'':97 ''1'':12 ''2'':96 ''27'':11 ''32'':95 ''address'':57 ''answer'':93 ''anyon'':23 ''attempt'':25 ''call'':74 ''continu'':3 ''elihu'':88 ''fact'':48 ''former'':79 ''friend'':30,61 ''full'':42 ''give'':59 ''induc'':90 ''job'':2,10,17,50,94 ''moreov'':1 ''note'':8 ''opportun'':63 ''parabl'':5 ''particular'':83 ''paus'':19,54 ''probabl'':15 ''remark'':36 ''repli'':27 ''resum'':34 ''rous'':84 ''see'':6,21 ''seem'':73 ''sentiment'':80 ''silent'':32,70 ''speak'':65 ''statement'':43 ''suffer'':46 ''upon'':75 ''vindic'':77 ''went'':38 ''would'':24 ''wrath'':86', NULL, '465');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('466', '2', '466', NULL, 'But now they that are younger than I-- Margin, "of fewer days." It is not probable that Job here refers to his three friends. It is not possible to determine their age with accuracy, but in Job 15:10, they claim that there were with them old and very aged men, much older than the father of Job. Though that place may possibly refer not to themselves but to those who held the same opinions with them, yet none of those who engaged in the discussion, except Ehhu Job 32:6, are represented as young men. They were the contemporaries of Job; men who are ranked as his friends; and men who showed that they had had oppoptunities for long and careful observation. The reference here, therefore, is to the fact that while, in the days of his prosperity, even the aged and the honorable rose up to do him reverence, now he was the object of contempt even by the young and the worthless. The Orientals would feel this much. It was among the chief virtues with them to show respect to the aged, and their sensibilites were especially keen in regard to any indignity shown to them by the young.Whose fathers I would have disdained - Who are the children of the lowest and most degraded of the community. How deep the calamity to be so fallen as to be the subject of derision by such men!To have set with the dogs of my flock - To have associated with my dogs in guarding my flock. That is, they were held in less esteem than his dogs. This was the lowest conceivable point of debasement. The Orientals had no language that would express greater contempt of anyone than to call him a dog; compare Deuteronomy 23:18; 1 Samuel 17:43; 1 Samuel 24:14; 2 Samuel 3:8; 2 Samuel 9:8; 2 Samuel 16:9; 2 Kings 8:13; Note Isaiah 66:3.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':299,303 ''10'':39 ''13'':322 ''14'':306 ''15'':38 ''16'':317 ''17'':301 ''18'':298 ''2'':307,311,315,319 ''23'':297 ''24'':305 ''3'':309,326 ''32'':90 ''43'':302 ''6'':91 ''66'':325 ''8'':310,314,321 ''9'':313,318 ''accuraci'':34 ''age'':32,50,142,185 ''among'':174 ''anyon'':288 ''associ'':250 ''calam'':224 ''call'':291 ''care'':122 ''chief'':176 ''children'':211 ''claim'':41 ''communiti'':220 ''compar'':295 ''conceiv'':273 ''contemporari'':100 ''contempt'':158,286 ''day'':12,136 ''debas'':276 ''deep'':222 ''degrad'':217 ''deris'':235 ''determin'':30 ''deuteronomi'':296 ''discuss'':86 ''disdain'':207 ''dog'':244,253,268,294 ''ehhu'':88 ''engag'':83 ''especi'':190 ''esteem'':265 ''even'':140,159 ''except'':87 ''express'':284 ''fact'':131 ''fallen'':228 ''father'':56,203 ''feel'':169 ''fewer'':11 ''flock'':247,257 ''friend'':24,109 ''greater'':285 ''guard'':255 ''held'':72,262 ''honor'':145 ''indign'':196 ''isaiah'':324 ''job'':18,37,58,89,102 ''keen'':191 ''king'':320 ''languag'':281 ''less'':264 ''long'':120 ''lowest'':214,272 ''margin'':9 ''may'':62 ''men'':51,96,103,111,238 ''much'':52,171 ''none'':79 ''note'':323 ''object'':156 ''observ'':123 ''old'':47 ''older'':53 ''opinion'':75 ''oppoptun'':118 ''orient'':167,278 ''place'':61 ''point'':274 ''possibl'':28,63 ''probabl'':16 ''prosper'':139 ''rank'':106 ''refer'':20,64,125 ''regard'':193 ''repres'':93 ''respect'':182 ''rever'':151 ''rose'':146 ''samuel'':300,304,308,312,316 ''sensibilit'':188 ''set'':241 ''show'':113,181 ''shown'':197 ''subject'':233 ''therefor'':127 ''though'':59 ''three'':23 ''virtu'':177 ''worthless'':165 ''would'':168,205,283 ''yet'':78 ''young'':95,162 ''young.whose'':202 ''younger'':6', NULL, '466');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('468', '2', '468', NULL, 'So these three men ceased to answer Job - Each had had three opportunities of replying to him, though in the last series of the controversy Zophar had been silent. Now all were silent; and though they do not appear in the least to have been convinced, or to have changed their opinion, yet they found no arguments with which to sustain their views. It was this, among other things, which induced Elihu to take up the subject.Because he was righteous in his own eyes - Umbreit expresses the sense of this by adding, "and they could not convince him of his unrighteousness." It was not merely because he was righteous in his own estimation, that they ceased to answer him; it was because their arguments had no effect in convincing him, and they had nothing new to say. He seemed to be obstinately bent on maintaining his own good opinion of himself in spite of all their reasoning, and they sat down in silence.', NULL, NULL, '''add'':92 ''among'':67 ''answer'':7,118 ''appear'':39 ''argument'':57,124 ''bent'':143 ''ceas'':5,116 ''chang'':50 ''controversi'':25 ''convinc'':46,97,129 ''could'':95 ''effect'':127 ''elihu'':72 ''estim'':113 ''express'':86 ''eye'':84 ''found'':55 ''good'':148 ''induc'':71 ''job'':8 ''last'':21 ''least'':42 ''maintain'':145 ''men'':4 ''mere'':105 ''new'':135 ''noth'':134 ''obstin'':142 ''opinion'':52,149 ''opportun'':13 ''reason'':157 ''repli'':15 ''righteous'':80,109 ''sat'':160 ''say'':137 ''seem'':139 ''sens'':88 ''seri'':22 ''silenc'':163 ''silent'':29,33 ''spite'':153 ''subject.because'':77 ''sustain'':61 ''take'':74 ''thing'':69 ''though'':18,35 ''three'':3,12 ''umbreit'':85 ''unright'':101 ''view'':63 ''yet'':53 ''zophar'':26', NULL, '468');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('469', '2', '469', NULL, 'Wherefore, Job, I pray thee - In the next chapter he addresses the three friends of Job. This is addressed particularly to him.My speeches - Hebrew, "my words" -   millah. This is the usual word in the Aramaen languages to express a saying or discourse, though in Hebrew it is only a poetic form. The meaning is, not that he would address separate speeches, or distinct discourses, to Job, but that he called on him to attend to what he had to say.', NULL, NULL, '''address'':11,19,59 ''aramaen'':35 ''attend'':74 ''call'':70 ''chapter'':9 ''discours'':42,64 ''distinct'':63 ''express'':38 ''form'':51 ''friend'':14 ''hebrew'':24,45 ''him.my'':22 ''job'':2,16,66 ''languag'':36 ''mean'':53 ''millah'':27 ''next'':8 ''particular'':20 ''poetic'':50 ''pray'':4 ''say'':40,80 ''separ'':60 ''speech'':23,61 ''thee'':5 ''though'':43 ''three'':13 ''usual'':31 ''wherefor'':1 ''word'':26,32 ''would'':58', NULL, '469');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('470', '2', '470', NULL, 'My desire is - Margin, "or, "my father, let Job be tried."" This variation between the text and the margin, arises from the different interpretations affixed to the Hebrew word   ''abiy. The Hebrew word commonly means "father," and some have supposed that that sense is to be retained here, and then it would be a solemn appeal to God as his Father - expressing the earnest prayer of Elihu that Job might be fully tried. But the difficulties in this interpretation are obvious:(1) Such a mode of appeal to God occurs nowhere else in the book, and it is little in the spirit of the poem. No particular reason can be assigned why that solemn appeal should be made here, rather than in many other places.(2) The name "Father," though often given to God in the Scriptures, is not elsewhere given to him in this book.The probability is, therefore, that the word is from   ''abah - "to breathe after, to desire," and means that Elihu "desired" that Job should have a fair trial. No other similar form of the word, however, occurs The Vulgate renders it, "Pater mi, my father;" the Septuagint, "But learn, Job, no more to make reply like the foolish;" the Chaldee,  - "I desire."May be tried - That his views may be fully canvassed and examined. He had expressed sentiments which Elihu thought should not be allowed to pass without the most careful examination into their truth and bearing. "Unto the end." In the most full and free manner; that the matter should be pursued as far as possible, so that it might be wholly understood. Literally, it means "forever" -   ad-netsach.Because of his answers for wicked men - Because of the views which he has expressed, which seem to favor the wicked. Elihu refers to the opinions advanced by Job that God did not punish people in this life, or did not deal with them according to their characters, which "he" interpreted as giving countenance to wickedness, or as affirming the God was not the enemy of impiety. The Vulgate renders this, "My Father, let Job be tried to the end; do not cease from the man of iniquity;" but the true meaning doubtless is, that Job had uttered sentiments which Elihu understood to favor the wicked, and he was desirous that every trial should be applied to him which would tend to correct his erroneous views.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':82 ''2'':126 ''abah'':155 ''abiy'':30 ''accord'':317 ''ad-netsach.because'':273 ''advanc'':299 ''affirm'':331 ''affix'':25 ''allow'':229 ''answer'':276 ''appeal'':56,87,115 ''appli'':388 ''aris'':20 ''assign'':111 ''bear'':241 ''book'':95 ''book.the'':146 ''breath'':157 ''canvass'':216 ''care'':235 ''ceas'':355 ''chalde'':204 ''charact'':320 ''common'':34 ''correct'':395 ''counten'':326 ''deal'':314 ''desir'':2,160,165,206,382 ''differ'':23 ''difficulti'':76 ''doubtless'':365 ''earnest'':64 ''elihu'':67,164,224,294,373 ''els'':92 ''elsewher'':140 ''end'':244,352 ''enemi'':337 ''erron'':397 ''everi'':384 ''examin'':218,236 ''express'':62,221,287 ''fair'':171 ''far'':259 ''father'':7,36,61,129,189,345 ''favor'':291,376 ''foolish'':202 ''forev'':272 ''form'':176 ''free'':250 ''full'':248 ''fulli'':72,215 ''give'':325 ''given'':132,141 ''god'':58,89,134,303,333 ''hebrew'':28,32 ''howev'':180 ''impieti'':339 ''iniqu'':360 ''interpret'':24,79,323 ''job'':9,69,167,194,301,347,368 ''learn'':193 ''let'':8,346 ''life'':310 ''like'':200 ''liter'':269 ''littl'':99 ''made'':118 ''make'':198 ''man'':358 ''mani'':123 ''manner'':251 ''margin'':4,19 ''matter'':254 ''may'':207,213 ''mean'':35,162,271,364 ''men'':279 ''mi'':187 ''might'':70,265 ''mode'':85 ''name'':128 ''nowher'':91 ''obvious'':81 ''occur'':90,181 ''often'':131 ''opinion'':298 ''particular'':107 ''pass'':231 ''pater'':186 ''peopl'':307 ''place'':125 ''poem'':105 ''possibl'':261 ''prayer'':65 ''probabl'':147 ''punish'':306 ''pursu'':257 ''rather'':120 ''reason'':108 ''refer'':295 ''render'':184,342 ''repli'':199 ''retain'':47 ''scriptur'':137 ''seem'':289 ''sens'':43 ''sentiment'':222,371 ''septuagint'':191 ''similar'':175 ''solemn'':55,114 ''spirit'':102 ''suppos'':40 ''tend'':393 ''text'':16 ''therefor'':149 ''though'':130 ''thought'':225 ''tri'':11,73,209,349 ''trial'':172,385 ''true'':363 ''truth'':239 ''understood'':268,374 ''unto'':242 ''utter'':370 ''variat'':13 ''view'':212,283,398 ''vulgat'':183,341 ''wholli'':267 ''wick'':278,293,378 ''wicked'':328 ''without'':232 ''word'':29,33,152,179 ''would'':52,392', NULL, '470');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('471', '2', '471', NULL, 'Elihu spake - Hebrew,   vayaan "And he answered"; the word "answer" being used, as it is often in the Scriptures, to denote the commencement of a discourse. We may suppose that Elihu had paused at the close of his second discourse, possibly with a view to see whether there was any disposition to reply.', NULL, NULL, '''answer'':7,10 ''close'':36 ''commenc'':23 ''denot'':21 ''discours'':26,40 ''disposit'':51 ''elihu'':1,31 ''hebrew'':3 ''may'':28 ''often'':16 ''paus'':33 ''possibl'':41 ''repli'':53 ''scriptur'':19 ''second'':39 ''see'':46 ''spake'':2 ''suppos'':29 ''use'':12 ''vayaan'':4 ''view'':44 ''whether'':47 ''word'':9', NULL, '471');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('472', '2', '472', NULL, 'Elihu also proceeded - Hebrew added -   vayasaph. Vulgate "addens;" Septuagint, &#x397;&#x3c1;&#x3bf;&#x3c3;&#x3b8;&#x3b5;&#x3b9;&#x3c2;  Eerostheis - "adding, or proceeding." The Hebrew commentators remark that this word is used because this speech is "added" to the number which it might be supposed he would make. There had been "three" series of speeches, by Job and his friends, and in each one of them Job had spoken three times. Each one of the three friends had also spoken thrice, except Zophar, who failed to reply when it came to his turn. Elihu had also now made three speeches, and here he would naturally have closed, but it is remarked that he "added" this to the usual number.', NULL, NULL, '''add'':5,11,27,104 ''adden'':8 ''also'':2,69,86 ''came'':80 ''close'':97 ''comment'':16 ''eerosthei'':10 ''elihu'':1,84 ''except'':72 ''fail'':75 ''friend'':50,67 ''hebrew'':4,15 ''job'':47,57 ''made'':88 ''make'':38 ''might'':33 ''natur'':95 ''number'':30,109 ''one'':54,63 ''proceed'':3,13 ''remark'':17,101 ''repli'':77 ''septuagint'':9 ''seri'':43 ''speech'':25,45,90 ''spoken'':59,70 ''suppos'':35 ''three'':42,60,66,89 ''thrice'':71 ''time'':61 ''turn'':83 ''use'':22 ''usual'':108 ''vayasaph'':6 ''vulgat'':7 ''word'':20 ''would'':37,94 ''zophar'':73', NULL, '472');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('473', '2', '473', NULL, 'Hast thou with him spread out the sky? - That is, wert thou employed with God in performing that vast work, that thou canst explain how it was done? Elihu here speaks of the sky as it appears, and as it is often spoken of, as an expanse or solid body spread out over our heads, and as sustained by some cause which is unknown. Sometimes in the Scriptures it is spoken of as a curtain (Notes, Isaiah 40:22); sometimes as a "firmament," or a solid body spread out (Septuagint, Genesis 1:6-7); sometimes as a fixture in which the stars are placed (Notes, Isaiah 34:4), and sometimes as a scroll that may be rolled up, or as a garment, Psalm 102:26. There is no reason to suppose that the true cause of the appearance of an expanse was understood at that time, but probably the prevailing impression was that the sky was solid and was a fixture in which the stars were held. Many of the ancients supposed that there were concentric spheres, which were transparent but solid, and that these spheres revolved around the earth carrying the heavenly bodies with them. In one of these spheres, they supposed, was the sun; in another the moon; in another the fixed stars; in another the planets; and it was the harmonious movement of these concentric and transparent orbs which it was supposed produced the "music of the spheres."Which is strong - Firm, compact. Elihu evidently supposed that it was solid. It was so firm that it was self-sustained.And as a molten looking-glass - As a mirror that is made by being fused or cast. The word "glass" is not in the original, the Hebrew denoting simply "seeing," or a "mirror" (  re''y). Mirrors were commonly made of plates of metal highly polished; see the notes at Isaiah 3:23; compare Wilkinson''s Manners and Customs of the Ancient Egyptians, vol. iii. p. 365. Ancient mirrors were so highly polished that in some which have been discovered at Thebes the luster has been partially restored, though they have been buried for many centuries. There can be no doubt that the early apprehension in regard to the sky was, that it was a solid expanse, and that it is often so spoken of in the Bible. There is, however, no direct declaration that it is so, and whenever it is so spoken of, it is to be understood as popular language, as we speak still of the rising or setting of the sun, though we know that the language is not philosophically correct. The design of the Bible is not to teach science, but religion, and the speakers in the Bible were allowed to use the language of common life - just as scientific men in fact do now.', NULL, NULL, '''-7'':94 ''1'':92 ''102'':124 ''22'':79 ''23'':313 ''26'':125 ''3'':312 ''34'':107 ''365'':327 ''4'':108 ''40'':78 ''6'':93 ''allow'':455 ''ancient'':171,322,328 ''anoth'':208,212,217 ''appear'':37,138 ''apprehens'':365 ''around'':188 ''bibl'':388,440,453 ''bodi'':50,87,194 ''buri'':353 ''canst'':23 ''carri'':191 ''cast'':278 ''caus'':61,135 ''centuri'':356 ''common'':299,461 ''compact'':246 ''compar'':314 ''concentr'':176,228 ''correct'':435 ''curtain'':75 ''custom'':319 ''declar'':394 ''denot'':289 ''design'':437 ''direct'':393 ''discov'':340 ''done'':28 ''doubt'':361 ''earli'':364 ''earth'':190 ''egyptian'':323 ''elihu'':29,247 ''employ'':13 ''evid'':248 ''expans'':47,141,377 ''explain'':24 ''fact'':468 ''firm'':245,257 ''firmament'':83 ''fix'':214 ''fixtur'':98,161 ''fuse'':276 ''garment'':122 ''genesi'':91 ''glass'':267,281 ''god'':15 ''harmoni'':224 ''hast'':1 ''head'':55 ''heaven'':193 ''hebrew'':288 ''held'':167 ''high'':305,332 ''howev'':391 ''iii'':325 ''impress'':151 ''isaiah'':77,106,311 ''know'':428 ''languag'':413,431,459 ''life'':462 ''look'':266 ''looking-glass'':265 ''luster'':344 ''made'':273,300 ''mani'':168,355 ''manner'':317 ''may'':115 ''men'':466 ''metal'':304 ''mirror'':270,294,297,329 ''molten'':264 ''moon'':2