INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('744', '2', '744', NULL, 'Shall sing for joy of heart - They who serve me shall have abundant occasion of rejoicing. But ye - shall howl. You shall shriek under the anguish and distress that shall come upon you.For vexation of spirit - Margin, as in Hebrew, ''Breaking.'' That is, your spirit shall be broken and crushed under the weight of the calamities that shall come upon you.', NULL, NULL, '''abund'':13 ''anguish'':26 ''break'':41 ''broken'':48 ''calam'':56 ''come'':31,59 ''crush'':50 ''distress'':28 ''heart'':6 ''hebrew'':40 ''howl'':20 ''joy'':4 ''margin'':37 ''occas'':14 ''rejoic'':16 ''serv'':9 ''shall'':1,11,19,22,30,46,58 ''shriek'':23 ''sing'':2 ''spirit'':36,45 ''upon'':32,60 ''vexat'':34 ''weight'':53 ''ye'':18 ''you.for'':33', NULL, '744');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('745', '2', '745', NULL, 'The heaven is my throne - (See the notes at Isaiah 57:15). Here he is represented as having his seat or throne there. He speaks as a king. heaven is the place where he holds his court; from where he dispenses his commands; and from where he surveys all his works (compare 2 Chronicles 6:18; Matthew 5:34). The idea here is, that as God dwelt in the vast and distant heavens, no house that could be built on earth could be magnificent enough to be his abode.The earth is my footstool - A footstool is that which is placed under the feet when we sit. The idea here is, that God was so glorious that even the earth itself could be regarded only as his footstool. It is probable that the Saviour had this passage in his eye in his declaration in the sermon on the mount, ''Swear not at all; neither by heaven, for it is God''s throne; nor by the earth, for it is his footstool'' Matthew 5:34-35.Where is the house that ye build unto me? - What house can you build that will be an appropriate dwelling for him who fills heaven and earth? The same idea, substantially, was expressed by Solomon when he dedicated the temple: ''But will God indeed dwell on the earth? Behold, the heaven, and heaven of heavens cannot contain thee; how much less this house that I have builded!'' 1 Kings 8:27. Substantially the same thought is found in the address of Paul at Athens: ''God, that made the world, and all things therein, seeing that he is Lord of heaven and earth, dwelleth not in temples made with hands'' Acts 17:24.And where is the place of my rest? - It has already been intimated (in the analysis) that this refers probably to the time subsequent to the captivity. Lowth supposes that it refers to the time of the rebuilding of the temple by Herod. So also Vitringa understands it, and supposes that it refers to the pride and self-confidence of those who then imagined that they were rearing a structure that was worthy of being a dwelling-place of Yahweh. Grotius supposes that it refers to the time of the Maccabees, and that it was designed to give consolation to the pious of those times when they were about to witness the profanation of the temple by Antiochus, and the cessation of the sacrifices for three years and a half. ''God therefore shows,'' says he, ''that there was no reason why they should be offended in this thing. The most acceptable temple to him was a pious mind; and from that the value of all sacrifices was to be estimated.'' Abarbanel supposes that it refers to the times of redemption.His words are these: ''I greatly wonder at the words of the learned interpreting this prophecy, when they say that the prophet in this accuses the people of his own time on account of sacrifices offered with impure hands, for lo! all these prophecies which the prophet utters in the end of his book have respect to future redemption.'' See Vitringa. That it refers to some future time when the temple should be rebuilt seems to me to be evident. But what precise period it refers to - whether to times not far succeeding the captivity, or to the times of the Maccabees, or to the time of the rebuilding of the temple by Herod, it is difficult to find any data by which we can determine. From the whole strain of the prophecy, and particularly from Isaiah 66:3-5, it seems probable that it refers to the time when the temple which Herod had reared was finishing; when the nation was full of pride, self-righteousness, and hypocrisy; and when all sacrifices were about to be superseded by the one great sacrifice which the Messiah was to make for the sins of the world. At that time, God says that the spirit which would be evinced by the nation would be abominable in his sight; and to offer sacrifice then, and with the spirit which they would manifest, would be as offensive as murder or the sacrifice of a dog (see the notes at Isaiah 66:3).', NULL, NULL, '''-5'':604 ''1'':240 ''15'':12 ''17'':283 ''18'':56 ''2'':53 ''24.and'':284 ''27'':243 ''3'':603,713 ''34'':59 ''34-35.where'':173 ''5'':58,172 ''57'':11 ''6'':55 ''66'':602,712 ''8'':242 ''abarbanel'':456 ''abode.the'':89 ''abomin'':678 ''accept'':436 ''account'':497 ''accus'':489 ''act'':282 ''address'':252 ''alreadi'':294 ''also'':328 ''analysi'':299 ''antiochus'':403 ''appropri'':191 ''athen'':256 ''behold'':221 ''book'':518 ''build'':179,186,239 ''built'':79 ''cannot'':228 ''captiv'':310,559 ''cessat'':406 ''chronicl'':54 ''command'':43 ''compar'':52 ''confid'':343 ''consol'':384 ''contain'':229 ''could'':77,82,121 ''court'':37 ''data'':585 ''declar'':142 ''dedic'':210 ''design'':381 ''determin'':590 ''difficult'':581 ''dispens'':41 ''distant'':72 ''dog'':706 ''dwell'':192,217,362 ''dwelleth'':275 ''dwelling-plac'':361 ''dwelt'':67 ''earth'':81,90,119,165,199,220,274 ''end'':515 ''enough'':85 ''estim'':455 ''even'':117 ''evid'':544 ''evinc'':672 ''express'':205 ''eye'':139 ''far'':556 ''feet'':103 ''fill'':196 ''find'':583 ''finish'':622 ''footstool'':93,95,127,170 ''found'':249 ''full'':627 ''futur'':522,531 ''give'':383 ''glorious'':115 ''god'':66,112,159,215,257,416,664 ''great'':470,647 ''grotius'':366 ''half'':415 ''hand'':281,503 ''heaven'':2,29,73,155,197,223,225,227,272 ''herod'':326,578,618 ''hold'':35 ''hous'':75,176,183,235 ''hypocrisi'':634 ''idea'':61,108,202 ''imagin'':348 ''impur'':502 ''inde'':216 ''interpret'':478 ''intim'':296 ''isaiah'':10,601,711 ''king'':28,241 ''learn'':477 ''less'':233 ''lo'':505 ''lord'':270 ''lowth'':311 ''maccabe'':376,566 ''made'':259,279 ''magnific'':84 ''make'':654 ''manifest'':694 ''matthew'':57,171 ''messiah'':651 ''mind'':443 ''mount'':148 ''much'':232 ''murder'':700 ''nation'':625,675 ''neither'':153 ''note'':8,709 ''offend'':430 ''offens'':698 ''offer'':500,684 ''one'':646 ''particular'':599 ''passag'':136 ''paul'':254 ''peopl'':491 ''period'':548 ''pious'':387,442 ''place'':32,100,288,363 ''precis'':547 ''pride'':339,629 ''probabl'':130,303,607 ''profan'':398 ''propheci'':480,508,597 ''prophet'':486,511 ''rear'':352,620 ''reason'':425 ''rebuild'':321,573 ''rebuilt'':538 ''redempt'':523 ''redemption.his'':465 ''refer'':302,315,336,370,460,528,550,610 ''regard'':123 ''repres'':16 ''respect'':520 ''rest'':291 ''righteous'':632 ''sacrific'':409,451,499,638,648,685,703 ''saviour'':133 ''say'':419,483,665 ''seat'':20 ''see'':6,266,524,707 ''seem'':539,606 ''self'':342,631 ''self-confid'':341 ''self-right'':630 ''sermon'':145 ''show'':418 ''sight'':681 ''sin'':657 ''sit'':106 ''solomon'':207 ''speak'':25 ''spirit'':668,690 ''strain'':594 ''structur'':354 ''subsequ'':307 ''substanti'':203,244 ''succeed'':557 ''supersed'':643 ''suppos'':312,333,367,457 ''survey'':48 ''swear'':149 ''templ'':212,278,324,401,437,535,576,616 ''thee'':230 ''therefor'':417 ''therein'':265 ''thing'':264,433 ''thought'':247 ''three'':411 ''throne'':5,22,161 ''time'':306,318,373,390,463,495,532,554,563,570,613,663 ''understand'':330 ''unto'':180 ''utter'':512 ''valu'':448 ''vast'':70 ''vitringa'':329,525 ''whether'':552 ''whole'':593 ''wit'':396 ''wonder'':471 ''word'':466,474 ''work'':51 ''world'':261,660 ''worthi'':357 ''would'':670,676,693,695 ''yahweh'':365 ''ye'':178 ''year'':412', NULL, '745');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('746', '2', '746', NULL, 'The words of Jeremiah - The usual title of the prophetic books is "the Word of the Lord," but the two books of Amos and Jeremiah are called the words of those prophets, probably because they contain not merely the words of those prophets, probably because they contain not merely prophecies, but also the record of much which belongs to the personal history of the writers. This title might therefore be translated the "life of Jeremiah" or "acts of Jeremiah," though some understand by it a collection of the prophecies of Jeremiah. One derivation of Jeremiah''s name is "God exalteth."Hilkiah, may have been the high priest of that name.That were - Or, who was, i. e., dwelt. The meaning is, that Jeremiah was a priest who dwelt at Anathoth.', NULL, NULL, '''act'':77 ''also'':52 ''amo'':23 ''anathoth'':129 ''belong'':58 ''book'':11,21 ''call'':27 ''collect'':86 ''contain'':36,47 ''deriv'':93 ''dwelt'':117,127 ''e'':116 ''exalteth'':100 ''god'':99 ''high'':106 ''hilkiah'':101 ''histori'':62 ''jeremiah'':4,25,75,79,91,95,122 ''life'':73 ''lord'':17 ''may'':102 ''mean'':119 ''mere'':38,49 ''might'':68 ''much'':56 ''name'':97 ''name.that'':110 ''one'':92 ''person'':61 ''priest'':107,125 ''probabl'':33,44 ''propheci'':50,89 ''prophet'':10,32,43 ''record'':54 ''therefor'':69 ''though'':80 ''titl'':7,67 ''translat'':71 ''two'':20 ''understand'':82 ''usual'':6 ''word'':2,14,29,40 ''writer'':65', NULL, '746');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('747', '2', '747', NULL, 'Up to this time Jeremiah had lived at Anathoth, he is now to make Jerusalem the scene of his ministrations.I remember ... - Or, I have remembered for thee the grace "of thy youth, the love of thine espousals," thy going "after me in the wilderness" in an unsown land. Jeremiah contrasts the present unfriendly relations between Yahweh and His people with their past love. Israel, as often elsewhere, is represented as a young bride Ezekiel 16:8; Hosea 2:20; Joel 1:8. The walking after God in the wilderness was an act of love on Israel''s part. Israel did leave Egypt at Moses'' bidding, and at Sinai was solemnly espoused to Yahweh.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':81 ''16'':75 ''2'':78 ''20'':79 ''8'':76,82 ''act'':92 ''anathoth'':9 ''bid'':105 ''bride'':73 ''contrast'':50 ''egypt'':102 ''elsewher'':67 ''espous'':37,111 ''ezekiel'':74 ''go'':39 ''god'':86 ''grace'':29 ''hosea'':77 ''israel'':64,96,99 ''jeremiah'':5,49 ''jerusalem'':15 ''joel'':80 ''land'':48 ''leav'':101 ''live'':7 ''love'':34,63,94 ''make'':14 ''ministrations.i'':20 ''mose'':104 ''often'':66 ''part'':98 ''past'':62 ''peopl'':59 ''present'':52 ''relat'':54 ''rememb'':21,25 ''repres'':69 ''scene'':17 ''sinai'':108 ''solemn'':110 ''thee'':27 ''thi'':31,38 ''thine'':36 ''time'':4 ''unfriend'':53 ''unsown'':47 ''walk'':84 ''wilder'':44,89 ''yahweh'':56,113 ''young'':72 ''youth'':32', NULL, '747');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('748', '2', '748', NULL, 'They say - Or, That is to say. The prophet has completed his survey of Israel''s conduct, and draws the conclusion that as an adulterous wife could not be taken back by her husband, so Israel has forfeited her part in the covenant with God. Apparently the opening word, which literally means "to say," only introduces the quotation in the margin.Yet return again to me - Or, "and thinkest thou to return unto me!" The whole argument is not of mercy, but is the proof that after her repeated adulteries, Israel could not again take her place as wife. To think of returning to God, with the marriage-law unrepealed, was folly.', NULL, NULL, '''adulter'':25 ''adulteri'':89 ''appar'':46 ''argument'':76 ''back'':31 ''complet'':11 ''conclus'':21 ''conduct'':17 ''could'':27,91 ''coven'':43 ''draw'':19 ''folli'':112 ''forfeit'':38 ''god'':45,104 ''husband'':34 ''introduc'':56 ''israel'':15,36,90 ''law'':109 ''liter'':51 ''margin.yet'':61 ''marriag'':108 ''marriage-law'':107 ''mean'':52 ''merci'':80 ''open'':48 ''part'':40 ''place'':96 ''proof'':84 ''prophet'':9 ''quotat'':58 ''repeat'':88 ''return'':62,71,102 ''say'':2,7,54 ''survey'':13 ''take'':94 ''taken'':30 ''think'':100 ''thinkest'':68 ''thou'':69 ''unrep'':110 ''unto'':72 ''whole'':75 ''wife'':26,98 ''word'':49', NULL, '748');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('774', '2', '774', NULL, 'Elasah - Probably brother of Ahikam Jeremiah 26:24, and therefore an acceptable person at the Chaldaean court. As Zedekiah had to go in person to Babylon in his fourth year Jeremiah 51:59, this embassy was probably sent two or three years earlier. Its date, however, was subsequent to the vision in Jeremiah 24:1-10. It is appended therefore to Jeremiah 28, not as later in point of time, but because of the similarity of subject.', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':56 ''1'':55 ''24'':8,54 ''26'':7 ''28'':63 ''51'':32 ''59'':33 ''accept'':12 ''ahikam'':5 ''append'':59 ''babylon'':26 ''brother'':3 ''chaldaean'':16 ''court'':17 ''date'':45 ''earlier'':43 ''elasah'':1 ''embassi'':35 ''fourth'':29 ''go'':22 ''howev'':46 ''jeremiah'':6,31,53,62 ''later'':66 ''person'':13,24 ''point'':68 ''probabl'':2,37 ''sent'':38 ''similar'':75 ''subject'':77 ''subsequ'':48 ''therefor'':10,60 ''three'':41 ''time'':70 ''two'':39 ''vision'':51 ''year'':30,42 ''zedekiah'':19', NULL, '774');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('749', '2', '749', NULL, 'Return - The repentance of Israel described in Jeremiah 3:21-25 was a hope, and not a reality. The return, literally, would be their restoration to their land; spiritually, their abandoning their sins.Jeremiah 4:1-2 should be translated as follows:If thou wouldst return, O Israel, saith Yahweh.Unto Me thou shalt return:And if thou wouldst remove thy abominations from before Me,And not wander to and fro,But wouldst swear truly, uprightly; and justlyBy the living Yahweh;Then shall the pagan bless themselves ... - In him - In Yahweh. Two great truths are taught in this verse;(1) that the Gentiles were to be members of the Church of the Messiah;(2) that Israel''s special office was to be God''s mediator in this great work.Thus, Jeremiah is in exact accord with the evangelical teaching of Isaiah.', NULL, NULL, '''-2'':36 ''-25'':11 ''1'':35,98 ''2'':112 ''21'':10 ''3'':9 ''4'':34 ''abandon'':31 ''abomin'':60 ''accord'':132 ''bless'':84 ''church'':108 ''describ'':6 ''evangel'':135 ''exact'':131 ''follow'':41 ''fro'':69 ''gentil'':101 ''god'':121 ''great'':91,126 ''hope'':14 ''isaiah'':138 ''israel'':5,47,114 ''jeremiah'':8,128 ''justlybi'':76 ''land'':28 ''liter'':21 ''live'':78 ''mediat'':123 ''member'':105 ''messiah'':111 ''o'':46 ''offic'':117 ''pagan'':83 ''realiti'':18 ''remov'':58 ''repent'':3 ''restor'':25 ''return'':1,20,45,53 ''saith'':48 ''shall'':81 ''shalt'':52 ''sins.jeremiah'':33 ''special'':116 ''spiritu'':29 ''swear'':72 ''taught'':94 ''teach'':136 ''thi'':59 ''thou'':43,51,56 ''translat'':39 ''truli'':73 ''truth'':92 ''two'':90 ''upright'':74 ''vers'':97 ''wander'':66 ''work.thus'':127 ''would'':22 ''wouldst'':44,57,71 ''yahweh'':79,89 ''yahweh.unto'':49', NULL, '749');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('750', '2', '750', NULL, 'The broad places - The open spaces next the gates, and other places of concourse.A man - Or, anyone.That executeth - "That" practiceth.Truth - uprightness, probity (so in Jeremiah 5:3).', NULL, NULL, '''3'':27 ''5'':26 ''anyone.that'':17 ''broad'':2 ''concourse.a'':14 ''executeth'':18 ''gate'':9 ''jeremiah'':25 ''man'':15 ''next'':7 ''open'':5 ''place'':3,12 ''practiceth.truth'':20 ''probiti'':22 ''space'':6 ''upright'':21', NULL, '750');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('751', '2', '751', NULL, 'Jeremiah addresses the men of Benjamin, either as being his own tribesmen, or as a name appropriate to the people of Jerusalem, which also was situate in the tribe of Benjamin.Gather yourselves to flee - Gather your goods together to remove them to a place of safety.Blow the trumpet in Tekoa - The name of Tekoa is almost identical with the verb "to blow": but it was not chosen merely for the alliteration, but because it was the last town in Judaea (about 11 miles south of Jerusalem), upon the very border of the desert, where the fugitives would halt.A sign - Rather, a signal.Beth-haccerem - Or, the "Vineyard-House," which was situated halfway between Jerusalem and Tekoa.Appeareth - "Is bending over;" is bending forward in eagerness to seize its prey.', NULL, NULL, '''11'':82 ''address'':2 ''alliter'':71 ''almost'':56 ''also'':24 ''appropri'':17 ''bend'':118,121 ''benjamin'':6 ''benjamin.gather'':31 ''blow'':62 ''border'':90 ''chosen'':67 ''desert'':93 ''eager'':124 ''either'':7 ''flee'':34 ''forward'':122 ''fugit'':96 ''gather'':35 ''good'':37 ''haccerem'':103 ''halfway'':112 ''halt.a'':98 ''hous'':108 ''ident'':57 ''jeremiah'':1 ''jerusalem'':22,86,114 ''judaea'':80 ''last'':77 ''men'':4 ''mere'':68 ''mile'':83 ''name'':16,52 ''peopl'':20 ''place'':44 ''prey'':128 ''rather'':100 ''remov'':40 ''safety.blow'':46 ''seiz'':126 ''sign'':99 ''signal.beth'':102 ''situat'':26,111 ''south'':84 ''tekoa'':50,54 ''tekoa.appeareth'':116 ''togeth'':38 ''town'':78 ''tribe'':29 ''tribesmen'':12 ''trumpet'':48 ''upon'':87 ''verb'':60 ''vineyard'':107 ''vineyard-hous'':106 ''would'':97', NULL, '751');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('752', '2', '752', NULL, 'In Jeremiah 7-10 he addresses the people as they flocked into Jerusalem from the country, to attend the solemn services in the temple upon a fastday. Jehoiakim Jeremiah 26 had just ascended the throne, and was so incensed at this sermon that he would have put Jeremiah to death but for the influence of Ahikam. With the accession of Jehoiakim all hope of averting the ruin of the country had passed away. He represented the reverse of his father''s policy, and belonged to that faction, who placed their sole hope of deliverance in a close alliance with Pharaoh-Necho. As this party rejected the distinctive principles of the theocracy, and the king was personally an irreligious man, the maintenance of the worship of Yahweh was no longer an object of the public care. At this time upon a public fast-day, appointed probably because of the calamities under which the nation was laboring, Jeremiah was commanded by Yahweh to stand at the gate of the temple, and address to the people as they entered words of solemn warning. The whole sermon divides itself into three parts;In Jeremiah 7-10 he addresses the people as they flocked into Jerusalem from the country, to attend the solemn services in the temple upon a fastday. Jehoiakim Jeremiah 26 had just ascended the throne, and was so incensed at this sermon that he would have put Jeremiah to death but for the influence of Ahikam. With the accession of Jehoiakim all hope of averting the ruin of the country had passed away. He represented the reverse of his father''s policy, and belonged to that faction, who placed their sole hope of deliverance in a close alliance with Pharaoh-Necho. As this party rejected the distinctive principles of the theocracy, and the king was personally an irreligious man, the maintenance of the worship of Yahweh was no longer an object of the public care. At this time upon a public fast-day, appointed probably because of the calamities under which the nation was laboring, Jeremiah was commanded by Yahweh to stand at the gate of the temple, and address to the people as they entered words of solemn warning. The whole sermon divides itself into three parts;(1) It points out the folly of the superstitious confidence placed by the people in the temple, while they neglect the sole sure foundation of a nation''s hope. A sanctuary long polluted by immorality must inevitably be destroyed Jeremiah 7:2-8:3.(2) complaints follow of a more general character, in which the growing wickedness of the nation and especially of the leaders is pointed out Jeremiah 8:4-9:24.(3) lastly the prophet shows the possibility of averting the evils impending upon the nation Jeremiah 9:25-10:25.Jeremiah 10:1-2. The temple had several entrances 2 Chronicles 4:9; and the gate or door here mentioned is probably that of the inner court, where Baruch read Jeremiah''s scroll Jeremiah 36:10. The prophet stood in the doorway, and addressed the people assembled in the outer court.All ye of Judah - Better, literally all Judah (compare Jeremiah 26:2).', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':4,194,472 ''-2'':476 ''-8'':423 ''-9'':452 ''1'':381,475 ''10'':474,508 ''2'':422,425,482,534 ''24'':453 ''25'':471 ''25.jeremiah'':473 ''26'':30,220,533 ''3'':424,454 ''36'':507 ''4'':451,484 ''7'':3,193,421 ''8'':450 ''9'':470,485 ''access'':59,249 ''address'':6,172,196,362,516 ''ahikam'':56,246 ''allianc'':98,288 ''appoint'':146,336 ''ascend'':33,223 ''assembl'':519 ''attend'':18,208 ''avert'':65,255,462 ''away'':73,263 ''baruch'':501 ''belong'':84,274 ''better'':527 ''calam'':151,341 ''care'':136,326 ''charact'':432 ''chronicl'':483 ''close'':97,287 ''command'':160,350 ''compar'':531 ''complaint'':426 ''confid'':390 ''countri'':16,70,206,260 ''court'':499 ''court.all'':523 ''day'':145,335 ''death'':50,240 ''deliver'':94,284 ''destroy'':419 ''distinct'':108,298 ''divid'':186,376 ''door'':490 ''doorway'':514 ''enter'':178,368 ''entranc'':481 ''especi'':442 ''evil'':464 ''faction'':87,277 ''fast'':144,334 ''fast-day'':143,333 ''fastday'':27,217 ''father'':80,270 ''flock'':11,201 ''folli'':386 ''follow'':427 ''foundat'':404 ''gate'':167,357,488 ''general'':431 ''grow'':436 ''hope'':63,92,253,282,409 ''immor'':415 ''impend'':465 ''incens'':39,229 ''inevit'':417 ''influenc'':54,244 ''inner'':498 ''irreligi'':119,309 ''jehoiakim'':28,61,218,251 ''jeremiah'':2,29,48,158,192,219,238,348,420,449,469,503,506,532 ''jerusalem'':13,203 ''judah'':526,530 ''king'':115,305 ''labor'':157,347 ''last'':455 ''leader'':445 ''liter'':528 ''long'':412 ''longer'':130,320 ''mainten'':122,312 ''man'':120,310 ''mention'':492 ''must'':416 ''nation'':155,345,407,440,468 ''necho'':102,292 ''neglect'':400 ''object'':132,322 ''outer'':522 ''part'':190,380 ''parti'':105,295 ''pass'':72,262 ''peopl'':8,175,198,365,394,518 ''person'':117,307 ''pharaoh'':101,291 ''pharaoh-necho'':100,290 ''place'':89,279,391 ''point'':383,447 ''polici'':82,272 ''pollut'':413 ''possibl'':460 ''principl'':109,299 ''probabl'':147,337,494 ''prophet'':457,510 ''public'':135,142,325,332 ''put'':47,237 ''read'':502 ''reject'':106,296 ''repres'':75,265 ''revers'':77,267 ''ruin'':67,257 ''sanctuari'':411 ''scroll'':505 ''sermon'':42,185,232,375 ''servic'':21,211 ''sever'':480 ''show'':458 ''sole'':91,281,402 ''solemn'':20,181,210,371 ''stand'':164,354 ''stood'':511 ''superstiti'':389 ''sure'':403 ''templ'':24,170,214,360,397,478 ''theocraci'':112,302 ''three'':189,379 ''throne'':35,225 ''time'':139,329 ''upon'':25,140,215,330,466 ''warn'':182,372 ''whole'':184,374 ''wicked'':437 ''word'':179,369 ''worship'':125,315 ''would'':45,235 ''yahweh'':127,162,317,352 ''ye'':524', NULL, '752');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('753', '2', '753', NULL, 'Not the living only but the dead shall be exposed to the ruthless violence of the enemy, who will ransack the graves of the wealthier classes.', NULL, NULL, '''class'':26 ''dead'':7 ''enemi'':17 ''expos'':10 ''grave'':22 ''live'':3 ''ransack'':20 ''ruthless'':13 ''shall'':8 ''violenc'':14 ''wealthier'':25', NULL, '753');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('754', '2', '754', NULL, 'This verse is joined in the Hebrew to the preceding chapter. But any break at all here interrupts the meaning.A fountain - Rather, "a reservoir," in which tears had been stored up, so that the prophet might weep abundantly.', NULL, NULL, '''abund'':38 ''break'':14 ''chapter'':11 ''fountain'':21 ''hebrew'':7 ''interrupt'':18 ''join'':4 ''meaning.a'':20 ''might'':36 ''preced'':10 ''prophet'':35 ''rather'':22 ''reservoir'':24 ''store'':30 ''tear'':27 ''vers'':2 ''weep'':37', NULL, '754');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('755', '2', '755', NULL, 'Therefore they shall not prosper - Rather, "therefore they have not governed wisely." "The pastors," i. e., the kings and rulers Jeremiah 2:8, having sunk to the condition of barbarous and untutored men, could not govern wisely.', NULL, NULL, '''2'':22 ''8'':23 ''barbar'':30 ''condit'':28 ''could'':34 ''e'':16 ''govern'':11,36 ''jeremiah'':21 ''king'':18 ''men'':33 ''pastor'':14 ''prosper'':5 ''rather'':6 ''ruler'':20 ''shall'':3 ''sunk'':25 ''therefor'':1,7 ''untutor'':32 ''wise'':12,37', NULL, '755');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('783', '2', '783', NULL, 'For thus ... - Because he makes the men of war dispirited. No doubt this was true. Jeremiah, however, did not speak as a private person, but as the representative of the government; the temporal ruler in a theocracy being responsible directly to God.', NULL, NULL, '''direct'':40 ''dispirit'':10 ''doubt'':12 ''god'':42 ''govern'':31 ''howev'':17 ''jeremiah'':16 ''make'':5 ''men'':7 ''person'':24 ''privat'':23 ''repres'':28 ''respons'':39 ''ruler'':34 ''speak'':20 ''tempor'':33 ''theocraci'':37 ''thus'':2 ''true'':15 ''war'':9', NULL, '783');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('756', '2', '756', NULL, 'The prophecy contained in Jeremiah 11-12 seems to belong to an early period of Jeremiah''s life. The covenant Jeremiah 11:2 was that renewed by Josiah in his 18th year, after the discovery of the Book of the Law in the temple 2 Kings 23:3; while Jeremiah 11:13 apparently refers to the public establishment of idolatry by Manasseh Jeremiah 21:3. The people took no hearty part in Josiah''s reformation, and the prophet therefore sets before them the consequences that will inevitably fellow upon their disloyalty to their covenant-God. The prophecy was probably called forth by the conspiracy of the men of Judah and of his own relatives of Anathoth to murder Jeremiah Jer 11:18-23; Jeremiah 12:1-6, for such deeds, which but too well represented the nation''s whole course, punishment must come, if unrepented of.', NULL, NULL, '''-12'':7 ''-23'':124 ''-6'':128 ''1'':127 ''11'':6,22,51,122 ''12'':126 ''13'':52 ''18'':123 ''18th'':31 ''2'':23,45 ''21'':64 ''23'':47 ''3'':48,65 ''anathoth'':117 ''appar'':53 ''belong'':10 ''book'':38 ''call'':101 ''come'':144 ''consequ'':84 ''conspiraci'':105 ''contain'':3 ''cours'':141 ''coven'':20,95 ''covenant-god'':94 ''deed'':131 ''discoveri'':35 ''disloyalti'':91 ''earli'':13 ''establish'':58 ''fellow'':88 ''forth'':102 ''god'':96 ''hearti'':70 ''idolatri'':60 ''inevit'':87 ''jer'':121 ''jeremiah'':5,16,21,50,63,120,125 ''josiah'':28,73 ''judah'':110 ''king'':46 ''law'':41 ''life'':18 ''manasseh'':62 ''men'':108 ''murder'':119 ''must'':143 ''nation'':138 ''part'':71 ''peopl'':67 ''period'':14 ''probabl'':100 ''propheci'':2,98 ''prophet'':78 ''public'':57 ''punish'':142 ''refer'':54 ''reform'':75 ''relat'':115 ''renew'':26 ''repres'':136 ''seem'':8 ''set'':80 ''templ'':44 ''therefor'':79 ''took'':68 ''unrep'':146 ''upon'':89 ''well'':135 ''whole'':140 ''year'':32', NULL, '756');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('757', '2', '757', NULL, 'Yet let me talk ... - Rather, yet will I speak with thee on a matter of right. This sense is well given in the margin. The prophet acknowledges the general righteousness of God''s dealings, but cannot reconcile with it the properity of the conspirators of Anathoth This difficulty was often present to the minds of the saints of the Old Testament, see Job 21:7 ff; Psalm 37; Psalm 73.Happy - Rather, secure, tranquil.', NULL, NULL, '''21'':64 ''37'':68 ''7'':65 ''73.happy'':70 ''acknowledg'':27 ''anathoth'':46 ''cannot'':36 ''conspir'':44 ''deal'':34 ''difficulti'':48 ''ff'':66 ''general'':29 ''given'':21 ''god'':32 ''job'':63 ''let'':2 ''margin'':24 ''matter'':14 ''mind'':54 ''often'':50 ''old'':60 ''present'':51 ''proper'':41 ''prophet'':26 ''psalm'':67,69 ''rather'':5,71 ''reconcil'':37 ''right'':16 ''righteous'':30 ''saint'':57 ''secur'':72 ''see'':62 ''sens'':18 ''speak'':9 ''talk'':4 ''testament'':61 ''thee'':11 ''tranquil'':73 ''well'':20 ''yet'':1,6', NULL, '757');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('758', '2', '758', NULL, 'A linen girdle - The appointed dress of the priestly order (Leviticus 16:4, ...).Put it not in water - i. e., do not wash it, and so let it represent the deep-grained pollution of the people.', NULL, NULL, '''16'':12 ''4'':13 ''appoint'':5 ''deep'':32 ''deep-grain'':31 ''dress'':6 ''e'':20 ''girdl'':3 ''grain'':33 ''let'':27 ''leviticus'':11 ''linen'':2 ''order'':10 ''peopl'':37 ''pollut'':34 ''priest'':9 ''put'':14 ''repres'':29 ''wash'':23 ''water'':18', NULL, '758');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('759', '2', '759', NULL, 'Do thou it - Rather, "deal thou, act thou for Thy Name''s sake, i. e., not according to the strict measure of right and wrong, but as a God merciful and gracious.', NULL, NULL, '''accord'':17 ''act'':7 ''deal'':5 ''e'':15 ''god'':29 ''gracious'':32 ''measur'':21 ''merci'':30 ''name'':11 ''rather'':4 ''right'':23 ''sake'':13 ''strict'':20 ''thi'':10 ''thou'':2,6,8 ''wrong'':25', NULL, '759');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('760', '2', '760', NULL, 'Cast them out of my sight - Rather, "send them out of My presence, and let them go away." The prophet is to dismiss them, because their mediators, Moses and Samuel, whose intercession had been accepted in old times (marginal references), would intercede now in vain.', NULL, NULL, '''accept'':35 ''away'':18 ''cast'':1 ''dismiss'':23 ''go'':17 ''interced'':42 ''intercess'':32 ''let'':15 ''margin'':39 ''mediat'':27 ''mose'':28 ''old'':37 ''presenc'':13 ''prophet'':20 ''rather'':7 ''refer'':40 ''samuel'':30 ''send'':8 ''sight'':6 ''time'':38 ''vain'':45 ''whose'':31 ''would'':41', NULL, '760');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('761', '2', '761', NULL, 'In this prophecy Jeremiah 16:1-18, the punishment of the people is set forth in even sterner terms than in the last. The whole land is likened to a desert covered with the bodies of the dead, who lie unbemoaned and uncared for; and the prophet himself is commanded to abstain from the common usages of mankind that his motto of life, as well as his words, may warn the people of the greatness of the approaching calamity. There is, however, to be finally a return from exile, but only after the idolatry of the nation has been severely punished. The prophecy was probably written about the close of Jehoiakim''s reign.', NULL, NULL, '''-18'':7 ''1'':6 ''16'':5 ''abstain'':52 ''approach'':78 ''bodi'':35 ''calam'':79 ''close'':109 ''command'':50 ''common'':55 ''cover'':32 ''dead'':38 ''desert'':31 ''even'':17 ''exil'':89 ''final'':85 ''forth'':15 ''great'':75 ''howev'':82 ''idolatri'':94 ''jehoiakim'':111 ''jeremiah'':4 ''land'':26 ''last'':23 ''lie'':40 ''life'':63 ''liken'':28 ''mankind'':58 ''may'':69 ''motto'':61 ''nation'':97 ''peopl'':12,72 ''probabl'':105 ''propheci'':3,103 ''prophet'':47 ''punish'':9,101 ''reign'':113 ''return'':87 ''set'':14 ''sever'':100 ''sterner'':18 ''term'':19 ''unbemoan'':41 ''uncar'':43 ''usag'':56 ''warn'':70 ''well'':65 ''whole'':25 ''word'':68 ''written'':106', NULL, '761');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('762', '2', '762', NULL, 'This section Jeremiah 17:1-4 is inseparably connected with the preceding. Judah''s sin had been described Jeremiah 16:19 as one of which the very Gentiles will become ashamed. and for which she will shortly be punished by, an intervention of God''s hand more marked than anything in her previous history. Jeremiah now dwells upon the indelible nature of her sin.A pen of iron - i. e., an iron chisel for cutting inscriptions upon tables of stone.The point of a diamond - The ancients were well acquainted with the cutting powers of the diamond.Altars - Not Yahweh''s one altar, but the many altars which the Jews had set up to Baalim Jeremiah 11:13. Though Josiah had purged the land of these, yet in the eleven years of Jehoiakim''s reign they had multiplied again, and were the external proofs of Judah''s idolatry, as the table of her heart was the internal witness.', NULL, NULL, '''-4'':6 ''1'':5 ''11'':114 ''13'':115 ''16'':20 ''17'':4 ''19'':21 ''acquaint'':88 ''altar'':100,104 ''ancient'':85 ''anyth'':50 ''asham'':31 ''baalim'':112 ''becom'':30 ''chisel'':72 ''connect'':9 ''cut'':74,91 ''describ'':18 ''diamond'':83 ''diamond.altars'':95 ''dwell'':57 ''e'':69 ''eleven'':127 ''extern'':140 ''gentil'':28 ''god'':44 ''hand'':46 ''heart'':151 ''histori'':54 ''idolatri'':145 ''indel'':60 ''inscript'':75 ''insepar'':8 ''intern'':154 ''intervent'':42 ''iron'':67,71 ''jehoiakim'':130 ''jeremiah'':3,19,55,113 ''jew'':107 ''josiah'':117 ''judah'':13,143 ''land'':121 ''mani'':103 ''mark'':48 ''multipli'':135 ''natur'':61 ''one'':23,99 ''pen'':65 ''point'':80 ''power'':92 ''preced'':12 ''previous'':53 ''proof'':141 ''punish'':39 ''purg'':119 ''reign'':132 ''section'':2 ''set'':109 ''short'':37 ''sin'':15 ''sin.a'':64 ''stone.the'':79 ''tabl'':77,148 ''though'':116 ''upon'':58,76 ''well'':87 ''wit'':155 ''yahweh'':97 ''year'':128 ''yet'':124', NULL, '762');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('763', '2', '763', NULL, 'In the first prophecy of the series Jeremiah 18, the fate of Jerusalem was still undetermined; a long line of kings might yet reign there in splendor, and the city be inhabited forever. This was possible only so long as it was still undecided whether Josiah''s efforts would end in a national reformation or not, and before Jehoiakim threw the weight of the kingly office into the opposite balance. In the present prophecy mercy is still offered to the inhabitants of Jerusalem, but they reject it Jeremiah 18:11-12. They have made their final choice: and thereupon follows the third prophecy of "the broken vessel" Jeremiah 19:1-15 in which the utter overthrow of city and kingdom is foretold. We should thus place this prophecy of the potter very early in the reign of Jehoiakim; and that of the broken vessel at the commencement of his fourth year. This internal evidence is confirmed by external proof.', NULL, NULL, '''-12'':91 ''-15'':111 ''1'':110 ''11'':90 ''18'':9,89 ''19'':109 ''balanc'':70 ''broken'':106,143 ''choic'':97 ''citi'':30,118 ''commenc'':147 ''confirm'':156 ''earli'':133 ''effort'':48 ''end'':50 ''evid'':154 ''extern'':158 ''fate'':11 ''final'':96 ''first'':3 ''follow'':100 ''foretold'':122 ''forev'':33 ''fourth'':150 ''inhabit'':32,81 ''intern'':153 ''jehoiakim'':59,138 ''jeremiah'':8,88,108 ''jerusalem'':13,83 ''josiah'':46 ''king'':21,65 ''kingdom'':120 ''line'':19 ''long'':18,39 ''made'':94 ''merci'':75 ''might'':22 ''nation'':53 ''offer'':78 ''offic'':66 ''opposit'':69 ''overthrow'':116 ''place'':126 ''possibl'':36 ''potter'':131 ''present'':73 ''proof'':159 ''propheci'':4,74,103,128 ''reform'':54 ''reign'':24,136 ''reject'':86 ''seri'':7 ''splendor'':27 ''still'':15,43,77 ''thereupon'':99 ''third'':102 ''threw'':60 ''thus'':125 ''undecid'':44 ''undetermin'':16 ''utter'':115 ''vessel'':107,144 ''weight'':62 ''whether'':45 ''would'':49 ''year'':151 ''yet'':23', NULL, '763');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('764', '2', '764', NULL, 'Get (i. e., purchase) a potter''s earthen bottle - The "bottle" was a flask with a long neck, and took its name from the noise made by liquids in running out.The ancients - These "elders" were the regularly constituted representatives of the people (see Jeremiah 29:1; Numbers 11:16), and the organization lasted down to our Saviour''s time Matthew 26:47. Similarly the priests had also their representatives 2 Kings 19:2. Accompanied thus by the representatives of Church and State, the prophet was to carry the earthen bottle, the symbol of their mean origin and frail existence, outside the walls of Jerusalem.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':46 ''11'':48 ''16'':49 ''19'':72 ''2'':70,73 ''26'':61 ''29'':45 ''47'':62 ''accompani'':74 ''also'':67 ''ancient'':32 ''bottl'':9,11,90 ''carri'':87 ''church'':80 ''constitut'':38 ''e'':3 ''earthen'':8,89 ''elder'':34 ''exist'':99 ''flask'':14 ''frail'':98 ''get'':1 ''jeremiah'':44 ''jerusalem'':104 ''king'':71 ''last'':53 ''liquid'':28 ''long'':17 ''made'':26 ''matthew'':60 ''mean'':95 ''name'':22 ''neck'':18 ''nois'':25 ''number'':47 ''organ'':52 ''origin'':96 ''out.the'':31 ''outsid'':100 ''peopl'':42 ''potter'':6 ''priest'':65 ''prophet'':84 ''purchas'':4 ''regular'':37 ''repres'':39,69,78 ''run'':30 ''saviour'':57 ''see'':43 ''similar'':63 ''state'':82 ''symbol'':92 ''thus'':75 ''time'':59 ''took'':20 ''wall'':102', NULL, '764');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('765', '2', '765', NULL, 'Pashur, the father probably of the Gedaliah mentioned in Jeremiah 38:1, was the head of the 16th course (shift) of priests (marginal reference); the other Pashur Jeremiah 21:1 belonged to the fifth course, the sons of Melchiah. Both these houses returned in great strength from the exile. See Ezra 2:37-38.Chief governor - Or, "deputy governor." The Nagid or governer of the temple was the high priest 1 Chronicles 9:11, and Pashur was his Pakid, i. e., deputy (see Jeremiah 1:10 note). Zephaniah held this office Jeremiah 29:26, and his relation to the high priest is exactly defined 2 Kings 25:18; Jeremiah 52:24. The Nagid at this time was Seraiah the high priest, the grandson of Hilkiah, or (possibly) Azariah, Hilkiah''s son and Jeremiah''s brother 1 Chronicles 6:13, Ezra 7:1.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':12,30,69,83,134,140 ''10'':84 ''11'':72 ''13'':137 ''16th'':18 ''18'':106 ''2'':52,103 ''21'':29 ''24'':109 ''25'':105 ''26'':92 ''29'':91 ''37-38.chief'':53 ''38'':11 ''52'':108 ''6'':136 ''7'':139 ''9'':71 ''azariah'':126 ''belong'':31 ''brother'':133 ''chronicl'':70,135 ''cours'':19,35 ''defin'':102 ''deputi'':56,80 ''e'':79 ''exact'':101 ''exil'':49 ''ezra'':51,138 ''father'':3 ''fifth'':34 ''gedaliah'':7 ''govern'':61 ''governor'':54,57 ''grandson'':121 ''great'':45 ''head'':15 ''held'':87 ''high'':67,98,118 ''hilkiah'':123,127 ''hous'':42 ''jeremiah'':10,28,82,90,107,131 ''king'':104 ''margin'':23 ''melchiah'':39 ''mention'':8 ''nagid'':59,111 ''note'':85 ''offic'':89 ''pakid'':77 ''pashur'':1,27,74 ''possibl'':125 ''priest'':22,68,99,119 ''probabl'':4 ''refer'':24 ''relat'':95 ''return'':43 ''see'':50,81 ''seraiah'':116 ''shift'':20 ''son'':37,129 ''strength'':46 ''templ'':64 ''time'':114 ''zephaniah'':86', NULL, '765');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('766', '2', '766', NULL, 'By sending this embassy Zedekiah acknowledged that Jeremiah held the same position in the kingdom which Isaiah had held under Hezekiah 2 Kings 19:2. Pashur and Zephaniah belonged to the party who were for resisting Nebuchadnezzar by force of arms.', NULL, NULL, '''19'':24 ''2'':22,25 ''acknowledg'':6 ''arm'':41 ''belong'':29 ''embassi'':4 ''forc'':39 ''held'':9,19 ''hezekiah'':21 ''isaiah'':17 ''jeremiah'':8 ''king'':23 ''kingdom'':15 ''nebuchadnezzar'':37 ''parti'':32 ''pashur'':26 ''posit'':12 ''resist'':36 ''send'':2 ''zedekiah'':5 ''zephaniah'':28', NULL, '766');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('767', '2', '767', NULL, 'Omit and. "Thou art a Gilead unto me, a summit of Lebanon."Yet surely - literally, if not, the form of an oath with the imprecation omitted. For the full form see Numbers 14:23.A wilderness, and cities - Omit and. The meaning is: If the house of David does not hear God''s words, though it be now grand as Lebanon, God will make it a wilderness, even uninhabited cities; the house of David being regarded as equivalent to the kingdom of Judah.', NULL, NULL, '''14'':33 ''23'':34 ''art'':4 ''citi'':38,70 ''david'':48,74 ''equival'':78 ''even'':68 ''form'':19,30 ''full'':29 ''gilead'':6 ''god'':52,62 ''grand'':59 ''hear'':51 ''hous'':46,72 ''imprec'':25 ''judah'':83 ''kingdom'':81 ''lebanon'':12,61 ''liter'':15 ''make'':64 ''mean'':42 ''number'':32 ''oath'':22 ''omit'':1,26,39 ''regard'':76 ''see'':31 ''summit'':10 ''sure'':14 ''thou'':3 ''though'':55 ''uninhabit'':69 ''unto'':7 ''wilder'':36,67 ''word'':54 ''yet'':13', NULL, '767');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('768', '2', '768', NULL, 'Even with the temporal kingship abolished, David''s mercies are still sure.A righteous Branch - Or, sprout, germ (see Isaiah 4:2 note). The sprout is that in which the root springs up and grows, and which, if it be destroyed, makes the root perish also.And a king shall reign ... - Rather, and he shall reign as king. David''s family is to be dethroned (temporally), that it may reign gloriously (spiritually). But compare Jeremiah 33:17, note; Jeremiah 33:25, note.', NULL, NULL, '''17'':75 ''2'':21 ''25'':79 ''33'':74,78 ''4'':20 ''abolish'':6 ''also.and'':45 ''branch'':14 ''compar'':72 ''david'':7,57 ''destroy'':40 ''dethron'':63 ''even'':1 ''famili'':59 ''germ'':17 ''glorious'':69 ''grow'':34 ''isaiah'':19 ''jeremiah'':73,77 ''king'':47,56 ''kingship'':5 ''make'':41 ''may'':67 ''merci'':9 ''note'':22,76,80 ''perish'':44 ''rather'':50 ''reign'':49,54,68 ''righteous'':13 ''root'':30,43 ''see'':18 ''shall'':48,53 ''spiritu'':70 ''spring'':31 ''sprout'':16,24 ''still'':11 ''sure.a'':12 ''tempor'':4,64', NULL, '768');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('775', '2', '775', NULL, 'In Jeremiah 30-39, not all written at the same time, are gathered together whatsoever God had revealed to Jeremiah of happier import for the Jewish people. This subject is "the New covenant." In contrast then with the rolls of Jehoiakim and Zedekiah, we here have one containing the nation''s hope. A considerable portion was written in the 10th year of Zedekiah, when famine and pestilence were busy in the city, its capture daily more imminent, and the prophet himself in prison. Yet in this sad pressure of earthly troubles Jeremiah could bid his countrymen look courageously onward to the fulfillment of those hopes, which had so constantly in his darkest hours comforted the heart and nerved the arm of the Jew. The scroll consists of three portions:(1) "a triumphal hymn of Israel''s salvation," Jeremiah 30-31;(2) Jeremiah 32; and(3) Jeremiah 33.', NULL, NULL, '''-31'':140 ''-39'':4 ''1'':130 ''10th'':60 ''2'':141 ''3'':145 ''30'':3,139 ''32'':143 ''33'':147 ''arm'':120 ''bid'':94 ''busi'':69 ''captur'':74 ''citi'':72 ''comfort'':114 ''consider'':54 ''consist'':126 ''constant'':109 ''contain'':48 ''contrast'':35 ''could'':93 ''countrymen'':96 ''courag'':98 ''coven'':33 ''daili'':75 ''darkest'':112 ''earth'':90 ''famin'':65 ''fulfil'':102 ''gather'':13 ''god'':16 ''happier'':22 ''heart'':116 ''hope'':52,105 ''hour'':113 ''hymn'':133 ''immin'':77 ''import'':23 ''israel'':135 ''jehoiakim'':41 ''jeremiah'':2,20,92,138,142,146 ''jew'':123 ''jewish'':26 ''look'':97 ''nation'':50 ''nerv'':118 ''new'':32 ''one'':47 ''onward'':99 ''peopl'':27 ''pestil'':67 ''portion'':55,129 ''pressur'':88 ''prison'':83 ''prophet'':80 ''reveal'':18 ''roll'':39 ''sad'':87 ''salvat'':137 ''scroll'':125 ''subject'':29 ''three'':128 ''time'':11 ''togeth'':14 ''triumphal'':132 ''troubl'':91 ''whatsoev'':15 ''written'':7,57 ''year'':61 ''yet'':84 ''zedekiah'':43,63', NULL, '775');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('776', '2', '776', NULL, 'Weeping - For joy, not for sorrow.Supplications - The conviction that God is guiding them, encourages them to pray.Ephraim is My firstborn - The house of Joseph is thus to be restored to its old preeminence.', NULL, NULL, '''convict'':8 ''encourag'':14 ''firstborn'':20 ''god'':10 ''guid'':12 ''hous'':22 ''joseph'':24 ''joy'':3 ''old'':32 ''pray.ephraim'':17 ''preemin'':33 ''restor'':29 ''sorrow.supplications'':6 ''thus'':26 ''weep'':1', NULL, '776');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('777', '2', '777', NULL, 'The siege of Jerusalem began in Zedekiah''s ninth year Jeremiah 39:1, but was temporarily raised upon the approach of an Egyptian army. See Jeremiah 37; 38.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':13 ''37'':27 ''38'':28 ''39'':12 ''approach'':20 ''armi'':24 ''began'':5 ''egyptian'':23 ''jeremiah'':11,26 ''jerusalem'':4 ''ninth'':9 ''rais'':17 ''see'':25 ''sieg'':2 ''temporarili'':16 ''upon'':18 ''year'':10 ''zedekiah'':7', NULL, '777');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('778', '2', '778', NULL, 'Or, Thus saith Yahweh the doer of it, Yahweh who formeth it, that He may establish it, Yahweh is His name. The word "it" means whatsoever Yahweh wills.', NULL, NULL, '''doer'':6 ''establish'':16 ''formeth'':11 ''may'':15 ''mean'':25 ''name'':21 ''saith'':3 ''thus'':2 ''whatsoev'':26 ''will'':28 ''word'':23 ''yahweh'':4,9,18,27', NULL, '778');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('779', '2', '779', NULL, 'In peace - See Jeremiah 12:12 note.Burn odors - "Make a burning." The burning was probably that of piles of wood, and spices were added only as an special honor. It was not a Jewish custom to burn the dead. As these burnings depended upon the estimation in which the dead king was held, the verse implies a prosperous reign, such as Zedekiah might have had as an obedient vassal to BabyIon.I have pronounced the word - I have spoken the word.', NULL, NULL, '''12'':5,6 ''add'':24 ''babyion.i'':71 ''burn'':11,13,37,42 ''custom'':35 ''dead'':39,50 ''depend'':43 ''estim'':46 ''held'':53 ''honor'':29 ''impli'':56 ''jeremiah'':4 ''jewish'':34 ''king'':51 ''make'':9 ''might'':63 ''note.burn'':7 ''obedi'':68 ''odor'':8 ''peac'':2 ''pile'':18 ''probabl'':15 ''pronounc'':73 ''prosper'':58 ''reign'':59 ''see'':3 ''special'':28 ''spice'':22 ''spoken'':78 ''upon'':44 ''vassal'':69 ''vers'':55 ''wood'':20 ''word'':75,80 ''zedekiah'':62', NULL, '779');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('780', '2', '780', NULL, 'The house - The family.The Rechabites - The Rechabites were a nomadic tribe not of Jewish but of Kenite race, and connected with the Amalekites Numbers 24:21; 1 Samuel 15:6, from whom however they had separated themselves, and made a close alliance with the tribe of Judah Judges 1:16, on whose southern borders they took up their dwelling 1 Samuel 27:10. While, however, the main body of the Kenites gradually adopted settled habits, and dwelt in cities 1 Samuel 30:29, the Rechabites persisted in leading the free desert life, and in this determination they were finally confirmed by the influence and authority of Jonadab, who lived in Jehu''s reign. He was a zealous adherent of Yahweh 2 Kings 10:15-17, and possibly a religious reformer; and as the names of the men mentioned in the present narrative are all compounded with Yah, it is plain that the tribe continued their allegiance to Him.The object of Jonadab in endeavoring to preserve the nomad habits of his race was probably twofold. He wished first to maintain among them the purer morality and higher feeling of the desert contrasted with the laxity and effeminacy of the city life; and secondly he was anxious for the preservation of their freedom. Their punctilious obedience Jeremiah 35:14 to Jonadab''s precepts is employed by Jeremiah to point a useful lesson for his own people.The date of the prophecy is the interval between the battle of Carchemish and the appearance of Nebuchadnezzar at Jerusalem, Jeremiah 35:11 at the end of the same year. It is consequently 17 years earlier than the narrative in Jeremiah 34:8 ff', NULL, NULL, '''-17'':125 ''1'':27,49,60,80 ''10'':63,123 ''11'':256 ''14'':217 ''15'':29,124 ''16'':50 ''17'':267 ''2'':121 ''21'':26 ''24'':25 ''27'':62 ''29'':83 ''30'':82 ''34'':275 ''35'':216,255 ''6'':30 ''8'':276 ''adher'':118 ''adopt'':73 ''allegi'':156 ''allianc'':42 ''amalekit'':23 ''among'':180 ''anxious'':205 ''appear'':249 ''author'':105 ''battl'':244 ''bodi'':68 ''border'':54 ''carchemish'':246 ''citi'':79,199 ''close'':41 ''compound'':145 ''confirm'':100 ''connect'':20 ''consequ'':266 ''continu'':154 ''contrast'':191 ''date'':235 ''desert'':91,190 ''determin'':96 ''dwell'':59 ''dwelt'':77 ''earlier'':269 ''effeminaci'':196 ''employ'':223 ''end'':259 ''endeavor'':163 ''family.the'':4 ''feel'':187 ''ff'':277 ''final'':99 ''first'':177 ''free'':90 ''freedom'':211 ''gradual'':72 ''habit'':75,168 ''higher'':186 ''him.the'':158 ''hous'':2 ''howev'':33,65 ''influenc'':103 ''interv'':241 ''jehu'':111 ''jeremiah'':215,225,254,274 ''jerusalem'':253 ''jewish'':14 ''jonadab'':107,161,219 ''judah'':47 ''judg'':48 ''kenit'':17,71 ''king'':122 ''laxiti'':194 ''lead'':88 ''lesson'':230 ''life'':92,200 ''live'':109 ''made'':39 ''main'':67 ''maintain'':179 ''men'':137 ''mention'':138 ''moral'':184 ''name'':134 ''narrat'':142,272 ''nebuchadnezzar'':251 ''nomad'':10,167 ''number'':24 ''obedi'':214 ''object'':159 ''people.the'':234 ''persist'':86 ''plain'':150 ''point'':227 ''possibl'':127 ''precept'':221 ''present'':141 ''preserv'':165,208 ''probabl'':173 ''propheci'':238 ''punctili'':213 ''purer'':183 ''race'':18,171 ''rechabit'':5,7,85 ''reform'':130 ''reign'':113 ''religi'':129 ''samuel'':28,61,81 ''second'':202 ''separ'':36 ''settl'':74 ''southern'':53 ''took'':56 ''tribe'':11,45,153 ''twofold'':174 ''use'':229 ''whose'':52 ''wish'':176 ''yah'':147 ''yahweh'':120 ''year'':263,268 ''zealous'':117', NULL, '780');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('781', '2', '781', NULL, 'In the fourth year of Jehoiakim - See Jeremiah 25:1 note. The present chapter belongs to the very end of that year. The capture of Jerusalem by Nebuchadnezzar took place early in Jehoiakim''s fourth year, long before the writing of Jehoiakim''s scroll. The humiliation seems to have sunk deeply into the heart of Jehoiakim, and when Jeremiah prophesied extended dominion to the Chaldaeans Jeremiah 36:29, his anger knew no bounds. It was the fact that judgment had begun which made it expedient to gather Jeremiah''s predictions into one volume, with the object:(1) of inducing the people to repent, and(2) of persuading the king to be a true subject of the Chaldaean empire.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':10,97 ''2'':105 ''25'':9 ''29'':68 ''36'':67 ''anger'':70 ''begun'':81 ''belong'':15 ''bound'':73 ''captur'':24 ''chaldaean'':65,117 ''chapter'':14 ''deepli'':51 ''dominion'':62 ''earli'':31 ''empir'':118 ''end'':19 ''expedi'':85 ''extend'':61 ''fact'':77 ''fourth'':3,35 ''gather'':87 ''heart'':54 ''humili'':46 ''induc'':99 ''jehoiakim'':6,33,42,56 ''jeremiah'':8,59,66,88 ''jerusalem'':26 ''judgment'':79 ''king'':109 ''knew'':71 ''long'':37 ''made'':83 ''nebuchadnezzar'':28 ''note'':11 ''object'':96 ''one'':92 ''peopl'':101 ''persuad'':107 ''place'':30 ''predict'':90 ''present'':13 ''prophesi'':60 ''repent'':103 ''scroll'':44 ''see'':7 ''seem'':47 ''subject'':114 ''sunk'':50 ''took'':29 ''true'':113 ''volum'':93 ''write'':40 ''year'':4,22,36', NULL, '781');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('782', '2', '782', NULL, 'It is evident that Zedekiah was well affected toward Jeremiah. In Jeremiah 37-38, dealing with events during the siege of Jerusalem, we have an account of his relations with Jeremiah and of the prophet''s personal history up to the capture of the city.', NULL, NULL, '''-38'':14 ''37'':13 ''account'':26 ''affect'':8 ''captur'':42 ''citi'':45 ''deal'':15 ''event'':17 ''evid'':3 ''histori'':38 ''jeremiah'':10,12,31 ''jerusalem'':22 ''person'':37 ''prophet'':35 ''relat'':29 ''sieg'':20 ''toward'':9 ''well'':7 ''zedekiah'':5', NULL, '782');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('807', '2', '807', NULL, 'Translate it: take thee a sharp sword, for a barber''s razor thou shalt take it thee. Even if the action were literal, the use of an actual sword would best enforce the symbolic meaning. The "head" represents the chief city, the "hair" the inhabitants - its ornament and glory - the "hair cut from the head" the exiles cast forth from their homes. It adds to the force of the representation that "to shave the head" was a token of mourning Job 1:20, and was forbidden to the priests Leviticus 21:5. Thus, in many ways, this action of Ezekiel "the priest" is significant of calamity and ruin. The sword indicates the avenging power; the shaving of the head the removal of grace and glory; the scales and weights the determination of divine justice. Compare Zechariah 13:8-9.', NULL, NULL, '''-9'':139 ''1'':82 ''13'':137 ''20'':83 ''21'':91 ''5'':92 ''8'':138 ''action'':21,98 ''actual'':28 ''add'':64 ''aveng'':113 ''barber'':10 ''best'':31 ''calam'':106 ''cast'':58 ''chief'':40 ''citi'':41 ''compar'':135 ''cut'':52 ''determin'':131 ''divin'':133 ''enforc'':32 ''even'':18 ''exil'':57 ''ezekiel'':100 ''forbidden'':86 ''forc'':67 ''forth'':59 ''glori'':49,125 ''grace'':123 ''hair'':43,51 ''head'':37,55,75,119 ''home'':62 ''indic'':111 ''inhabit'':45 ''job'':81 ''justic'':134 ''leviticus'':90 ''liter'':23 ''mani'':95 ''mean'':35 ''mourn'':80 ''ornament'':47 ''power'':114 ''priest'':89,102 ''razor'':12 ''remov'':121 ''repres'':38 ''represent'':70 ''ruin'':108 ''scale'':127 ''shalt'':14 ''sharp'':6 ''shave'':73,116 ''signific'':104 ''sword'':7,29,110 ''symbol'':34 ''take'':3,15 ''thee'':4,17 ''thou'':13 ''thus'':93 ''token'':78 ''translat'':1 ''use'':25 ''way'':96 ''weight'':129 ''would'':30 ''zechariah'':136', NULL, '807');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('808', '2', '808', NULL, 'The former prophecies concerned the city of Jerusalem and the inhabitants of Judaea. The present is addressed to the whole land and people of Israel, which is to be included in a like judgment, The ground of the judgment is "idolatry," and the whole rests on Deuteronomy 12. The prophecy is against the "mountains" of Israel, because the mountains and valleys were the seats of idol-worship. It is also the proclamation of the final judgment of Israel. It is the picture of the future judgment of the world.', NULL, NULL, '''12'':48 ''address'':17 ''also'':71 ''citi'':6 ''concern'':4 ''deuteronomi'':47 ''final'':76 ''former'':2 ''futur'':86 ''ground'':36 ''idol'':67 ''idol-worship'':66 ''idolatri'':41 ''includ'':30 ''inhabit'':11 ''israel'':25,56,79 ''jerusalem'':8 ''judaea'':13 ''judgment'':34,39,77,87 ''land'':21 ''like'':33 ''mountain'':54,59 ''peopl'':23 ''pictur'':83 ''present'':15 ''proclam'':73 ''propheci'':3,50 ''rest'':45 ''seat'':64 ''valley'':61 ''whole'':20,44 ''world'':90 ''worship'':68', NULL, '808');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('809', '2', '809', NULL, 'A dirge. Supposing the date of the prophecy to be the same as that of the preceding, there were now but four, or perhaps three, years to the final overthrow of the kingdom of Judah by Nebuchadnezzar.', NULL, NULL, '''date'':5 ''dirg'':2 ''final'':29 ''four'':22 ''judah'':35 ''kingdom'':33 ''nebuchadnezzar'':37 ''overthrow'':30 ''perhap'':24 ''preced'':17 ''propheci'':8 ''suppos'':3 ''three'':25 ''year'':26', NULL, '809');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('810', '2', '810', NULL, 'The elders of Judah - The prophet''s fellow-exiles are no longer unwilling to hear him Ezekiel 2:1-10. They sat as mourners. The message here is not as in Ezekiel 6:2, but distinctly to Judah, that portion of the people whose exile Ezekiel shared.', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':21 ''1'':20 ''2'':19,35 ''6'':34 ''distinct'':37 ''elder'':2 ''exil'':10,46 ''ezekiel'':18,33,47 ''fellow'':9 ''fellow-exil'':8 ''hear'':16 ''judah'':4,39 ''longer'':13 ''messag'':27 ''mourner'':25 ''peopl'':44 ''portion'':41 ''prophet'':6 ''sat'':23 ''share'':48 ''unwil'':14 ''whose'':45', NULL, '810');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('811', '2', '811', NULL, 'Them that have charge - The angels who have charge to execute God''s sentence.Every man - "angels," not "men."', NULL, NULL, '''angel'':6,16 ''charg'':4,9 ''execut'':11 ''god'':12 ''man'':15 ''men'':18 ''sentence.every'':14', NULL, '811');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('812', '2', '812', NULL, 'As in Ezekiel 1, the vision of the glory of the Lord, the particulars given identifying the two visions.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':4 ''ezekiel'':3 ''given'':15 ''glori'':9 ''identifi'':16 ''lord'':12 ''particular'':14 ''two'':18 ''vision'':6,19', NULL, '812');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('813', '2', '813', NULL, 'The gate - The gate of the templecourt. The gate was the place of judgment.Five and twenty men - Not the same men as in Ezekiel 8:16. There they were representatives of the "priests," here of the "princes." The number is, no doubt, symbolic, made up, probably, of 24 men and the king. The number 24 points to the tribes of undivided Israel.Jaazaniah ... Pelatiah - We know nothing more of these men. The former name was probably common at that time Ezekiel 8:11. In these two names there is an allusion to the false hopes which they upheld. "Jaazaniah" (Yah (weh) listeneth) "son of Azur" (the Helper); "Pelatiah" (Yah (weh) rescues) "son of Benaiah" (Yah (weh) builds). In the latter case, death Ezekiel 11:13 turned the allusion into bitter irony.', NULL, NULL, '''11'':82,123 ''13'':124 ''16'':26 ''24'':48,55 ''8'':25,81 ''allus'':90,127 ''azur'':104 ''benaiah'':113 ''bitter'':129 ''build'':116 ''case'':120 ''common'':76 ''death'':121 ''doubt'':42 ''ezekiel'':24,80,122 ''fals'':93 ''former'':72 ''gate'':2,4,9 ''helper'':106 ''hope'':94 ''ironi'':130 ''israel.jaazaniah'':62 ''jaazaniah'':98 ''judgment.five'':14 ''king'':52 ''know'':65 ''latter'':119 ''listeneth'':101 ''made'':44 ''men'':17,21,49,70 ''name'':73,86 ''noth'':66 ''number'':39,54 ''pelatiah'':63,107 ''place'':12 ''point'':56 ''priest'':33 ''princ'':37 ''probabl'':46,75 ''repres'':30 ''rescu'':110 ''son'':102,111 ''symbol'':43 ''templecourt'':7 ''time'':79 ''tribe'':59 ''turn'':125 ''twenti'':16 ''two'':85 ''undivid'':61 ''upheld'':97 ''weh'':100,109,115 ''yah'':99,108,114', NULL, '813');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('814', '2', '814', NULL, 'Compare Deuteronomy 1:26; margin reference; Romans 10:21. The repetition of such words from age to age, shows that the prophet''s words are intended to reach beyond the generation in which he lived.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':3 ''10'':8 ''21'':9 ''26'':4 ''age'':16,18 ''beyond'':29 ''compar'':1 ''deuteronomi'':2 ''generat'':31 ''intend'':26 ''live'':35 ''margin'':5 ''prophet'':22 ''reach'':28 ''refer'':6 ''repetit'':11 ''roman'':7 ''show'':19 ''word'':14,24', NULL, '814');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('815', '2', '815', NULL, 'The identity of phrases and ideas of this chapter with Jeremiah 23 leads to the conclusion that Ezekiel took up a well-known prophecy to enforce and apply it to his companions in exile. They probably had read Jeremiah''s words as referring to others than themselves.', NULL, NULL, '''23'':12 ''appli'':29 ''chapter'':9 ''companion'':33 ''conclus'':16 ''enforc'':27 ''exil'':35 ''ezekiel'':18 ''idea'':6 ''ident'':2 ''jeremiah'':11,40 ''known'':24 ''lead'':13 ''other'':46 ''phrase'':4 ''probabl'':37 ''propheci'':25 ''read'':39 ''refer'':44 ''took'':19 ''well'':23 ''well-known'':22 ''word'':42', NULL, '815');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('816', '2', '816', NULL, 'Omit "that cometh."According to the multitiude of his idols - i. e., I will give him an answer as delusive as the idols which he serves. Compare Micaiah''s answer to Ahab 1 Kings 22:15.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':33 ''15'':36 ''22'':35 ''accord'':4 ''ahab'':32 ''answer'':18,30 ''cometh'':3 ''compar'':27 ''delus'':20 ''e'':12 ''give'':15 ''idol'':10,23 ''king'':34 ''micaiah'':28 ''multitiud'':7 ''omit'':1 ''serv'':26', NULL, '816');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('827', '2', '827', NULL, 'It was a distinct part of scriptural prophecy to address pagan nations. In Isaiah Isa. 13-19, Jeremiah Jer. 46-51, and here Ezekiel 25-32, one section is specially devoted to a collection of such prophecies. Every such prediction had the general purpose of exhibiting the conflict ever waging between the servants of God and the powers of the world, the struggle in which the Church of Christ has still to wrestle against her foes Ephesians 6:12, but in which she will surely prevail.It was a distinct part of scriptural prophecy to address pagan nations. In Isaiah Isa. 13-19, Jeremiah Jer. 46-51, and here Ezekiel 25-32, one section is specially devoted to a collection of such prophecies. Every such prediction had the general purpose of exhibiting the conflict ever waging between the servants of God and the powers of the world, the struggle in which the Church of Christ has still to wrestle against her foes Ephesians 6:12, but in which she will surely prevail.This series of prophecies, with one exception, was delivered at the time of the fall of Jerusalem; some shortly before, and some shortly after, the capture of the city. They were collected together to illustrate their original purpose of warning the nations not to exult in their neighbor''s fall. Seven nations are addressed, which have had most contact with the children of Israel - on their eastern borders Moab and Ammon, to the south, Edom, on the south-west Philistia, northward Tyre (the merchant city) and the more ancient Sidon, and lastly Egypt, alternately the scourge and the false stay of the chosen people. The number "seven" is symbolic of completeness. "Seven" prophecies against Egypt the chief of "seven" nations, denote the completeness of the overthrow of the pagan power, the antagonist of the kingdom of God. While other prophets hold out to these pagan nations some prospect of future mercy (e. g., Isaiah 16:14; Jeremiah 49:6, Jeremiah 49:11), Ezekiel speaks of their complete ruin. He was contemplating "national" ruin. In the case of Jerusalem there would be national restoration, but in the case of the pagan no such recovery. The "national" ruin was irretrievable; the remnant to whom the other prophets hold out hopes of mercy were to find it as individuals gathered into God''s Church, not as nations to be again set up. Ezekiel does not, like other prophets, prophesy against Babylon; it was his mission to show that for the moment, Babylon was the righteous instrument of the divine wrath, doing God''s work in punishing His foes. In prophesying against foreign nations, Ezekiel often adopts the language of those who preceded him.In Ezekiel 25, the four nations most closely connected with one another by geographical position and by contact, are addressed in a few brief sentences concluding with the same refrain - "Ye shall know that I am the Lord" (e. g. Ezekiel 25:5). This prophecy was delivered immediately after the capture of the city by Nebuchadnezzar, and so is later, in point of time, than some of the prophecies that follow it.The Ammonites were inveterate foes of the descendants of Abraham.', NULL, NULL, '''-19'':17,102 ''-32'':26,111 ''-51'':21,106 ''11'':333 ''12'':79,164 ''13'':16,101 ''14'':327 ''16'':326 ''25'':25,110,453,492 ''46'':20,105 ''49'':329,332 ''5'':493 ''6'':78,163,330 ''abraham'':531 ''address'':10,95,224,470 ''adopt'':444 ''altern'':265 ''ammon'':241 ''ammonit'':523 ''ancient'':260 ''anoth'':462 ''antagonist'':303 ''babylon'':409,420 ''border'':238 ''brief'':474 ''captur'':196,501 ''case'':347,358 ''chief'':288 ''children'':232 ''chosen'':274 ''christ'':69,154 ''church'':67,152,392 ''citi'':199,256,504 ''close'':458 ''collect'':34,119,202 ''complet'':282,294,338 ''conclud'':476 ''conflict'':48,133 ''connect'':459 ''contact'':229,468 ''contempl'':342 ''deliv'':179,497 ''denot'':292 ''desce