INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('950', '2', '950', NULL, 'of silk or some other costly fabric" (Hackett''s Illustrations of Scripture, p. 104).Wild honey - This was probably the honey that he found in the rocks of the wilderness. Palestine was often called th', NULL, NULL, '''104'':14 ''call'':34 ''cost'':6 ''fabric'':7 ''found'':24 ''hackett'':8 ''honey'':16,21 ''illustr'':10 ''often'':33 ''p'':13 ''palestin'':31 ''probabl'':19 ''rock'':27 ''scriptur'':12 ''silk'':2 ''th'':35 ''wild'':15 ''wilder'':30', NULL, '932');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('944', '2', '944', NULL, 'In those days - The days here referred to cannot be those mentioned in the preceding chapter, for John was but six months older than Christ. Perhaps Matthew intended to embrace in his narrative the whole time that Jesus lived at Nazareth; and the meaning is, "in those days while Jesus still dwelt at Nazareth," John began to preach. It is not probable that John began to baptize or preach long before the Saviour entered on his ministry; and, consequently, from the time that is mentioned in the close of the second chapter to that mentioned in the beginning of the third, an interval of twenty-five years or more elapsed.John the Baptist - Or John the baptizer - so called from his principal office, that of baptizing. Baptism, or the application of water, was a rite well known to the Jews, and pr', NULL, NULL, '''applic'':130 ''baptism'':127 ''baptist'':113 ''baptiz'':67,117,126 ''began'':56,65 ''begin'':98 ''call'':119 ''cannot'':9 ''chapter'':16,92 ''christ'':25 ''close'':88 ''consequ'':79 ''day'':3,5,48 ''dwelt'':52 ''elapsed.john'':111 ''embrac'':30 ''enter'':74 ''five'':107 ''intend'':28 ''interv'':103 ''jesus'':38,50 ''jew'':140 ''john'':18,55,64,115 ''known'':137 ''live'':39 ''long'':70 ''matthew'':27 ''mean'':44 ''mention'':12,85,95 ''ministri'':77 ''month'':22 ''narrat'':33 ''nazareth'':41,54 ''offic'':123 ''older'':23 ''perhap'':26 ''pr'':142 ''preach'':58,69 ''preced'':15 ''princip'':122 ''probabl'':62 ''refer'':7 ''rite'':135 ''saviour'':73 ''second'':91 ''six'':21 ''still'':51 ''third'':101 ''time'':36,82 ''twenti'':106 ''twenty-f'':105 ''water'':132 ''well'':136 ''whole'':35 ''year'':108', NULL, '932');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('945', '2', '945', NULL, 'acticed when they admitted proselytes to their religion from paganism. - Lightfoot.Preaching - The word rendered "preach" means to proclaim in the manner of a public crier; to make proclamation. The discourses recorded in the New Testament are mostly brief, sometimes consisting only of a single sentence. They were public proclamations of some great truth. Such appear to have been the discourses of John, calling people to repentance.In the wilderness of Judea - This country was situated along the Jordan and the Dead Sea, to the east of Jerusalem. The word translated "wilderness" does not denote, as with us, a place of boundless forests, entirely destitute of inhabitants; but a mountainous, rough, and thinly settled country, covered to some considerable extent with forests and rocks, and bet', NULL, NULL, '''actic'':1 ''admit'':4 ''along'':75 ''appear'':55 ''bet'':124 ''boundless'':100 ''brief'':38 ''call'':63 ''consider'':117 ''consist'':40 ''countri'':72,113 ''cover'':114 ''crier'':25 ''dead'':80 ''denot'':93 ''destitut'':103 ''discours'':30,60 ''east'':84 ''entir'':102 ''extent'':118 ''forest'':101,120 ''great'':52 ''inhabit'':105 ''jerusalem'':86 ''john'':62 ''jordan'':77 ''judea'':70 ''lightfoot.preaching'':11 ''make'':27 ''manner'':21 ''mean'':16 ''most'':37 ''mountain'':108 ''new'':34 ''pagan'':10 ''peopl'':64 ''place'':98 ''preach'':15 ''proclaim'':18 ''proclam'':28,49 ''proselyt'':5 ''public'':24,48 ''record'':31 ''religion'':8 ''render'':14 ''repentance.in'':66 ''rock'':122 ''rough'':109 ''sea'':81 ''sentenc'':45 ''settl'':112 ''singl'':44 ''situat'':74 ''sometim'':39 ''testament'':35 ''thin'':111 ''translat'':89 ''truth'':53 ''us'':96 ''wilder'':68,90 ''word'':13,88', NULL, '932');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('946', '2', '946', NULL, 'ter suited for pasture than for tilling. There were inhabitants in those places, and even villages, but they were the comparatively unsettled portions of the country, 1 Samuel 25:1-2. In the time of Joshua there were six cities in what was then called a wilderness, Joshua 15:61-62.', NULL, NULL, '''-2'':31 ''-62'':51 ''1'':27,30 ''15'':49 ''25'':29 ''61'':50 ''call'':45 ''citi'':40 ''compar'':21 ''countri'':26 ''even'':15 ''inhabit'':10 ''joshua'':36,48 ''pastur'':4 ''place'':13 ''portion'':23 ''samuel'':28 ''six'':39 ''suit'':2 ''ter'':1 ''till'':7 ''time'':34 ''unsettl'':22 ''villag'':16 ''wilder'':47', NULL, '932');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('947', '2', '947', NULL, 'purity.The phrases "kingdom of God" and "kingdom of heaven" have been supposed to have a considerable variety of meaning. Some have supposed that they refer to the state of things in heaven; others, to the personal reign of Christ on earth; others, that they mean the church, or the reign of Christ in the hearts of his people. There can be no doubt that there is reference in the words to the condition of things in heaven after this life. But the church of God is a preparatory state to that beyond the grave - a state in which Christ pre-eminently rules and reigns and there is no doubt that the phrases sometimes refer to the state of things in the church; and that they may refer, therefore, to the state of things which the Messiah was to set up his spiritual reign begun in the church on earth', NULL, NULL, '''begun'':145 ''beyond'':92 ''christ'':39,52,99 ''church'':47,83,123,148 ''condit'':73 ''consider'':16 ''doubt'':63,110 ''earth'':41,150 ''emin'':102 ''god'':5,85 ''grave'':94 ''heart'':55 ''heaven'':9,32,77 ''kingdom'':3,7 ''life'':80 ''may'':127 ''mean'':19,45 ''messiah'':137 ''other'':33,42 ''peopl'':58 ''person'':36 ''phrase'':2,113 ''pre'':101 ''pre-emin'':100 ''preparatori'':88 ''purity.the'':1 ''refer'':25,67,115,128 ''reign'':37,50,105,144 ''rule'':103 ''set'':140 ''sometim'':114 ''spiritu'':143 ''state'':28,89,96,118,132 ''suppos'':12,22 ''therefor'':129 ''thing'':30,75,120,134 ''varieti'':17 ''word'':70', NULL, '932');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('948', '2', '948', NULL, ' and completed in heaven.The expression "the kingdom of heaven is at hand" would be best translated, "the reign of God draws near." We do not say commonly of a kingdom that it is movable, or that it approaches. A reign may be said to be at hand; and it may be said with propriety that the time when Christ would reign was at hand. In this sense it is meant that the time when Christ should reign, or set up his kingdom, or begin his dominion on earth, under the Christian economy, was about to commence. The phrase, then, should not be confined to any period of that reign, but includes his whole dominion over his people on earth and in heaven.In the passage here it clearly means that the coming of the Messiah was near, or that the time of the reign of God which the Jews had expected was coming.T', NULL, NULL, '''approach'':38 ''begin'':84 ''best'':15 ''christ'':59,75 ''christian'':91 ''clear'':127 ''come'':131 ''coming.t'':152 ''commenc'':96 ''common'':27 ''complet'':2 ''confin'':103 ''dominion'':86,114 ''draw'':21 ''earth'':88,119 ''economi'':92 ''expect'':150 ''express'':5 ''god'':20,145 ''hand'':12,47,64 ''heaven'':9 ''heaven.in'':122 ''heaven.the'':4 ''includ'':111 ''jew'':148 ''kingdom'':7,30,82 ''may'':41,50 ''mean'':128 ''meant'':70 ''messiah'':134 ''movabl'':34 ''near'':22,136 ''passag'':124 ''peopl'':117 ''period'':106 ''phrase'':98 ''proprieti'':54 ''reign'':18,40,61,77,109,143 ''said'':43,52 ''say'':26 ''sens'':67 ''set'':79 ''time'':57,73,140 ''translat'':16 ''whole'':113 ''would'':13,60', NULL, '932');
