INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('217', '2', '217', NULL, 'Midian - See Genesis 25:2 note. They were remarkable not only for the vast number of their cattle Judges 6:5; Numbers 31:32-39, but also for their great wealth in gold and other metal ornaments, showing their connection with a gold country. (Compare Numbers 31:22, Numbers 31:50-54, with Judges 8:24 :26.) At this time they were allies of the Amalekites and of the Arabian tribes called collectively "the children of the East" Judges 6:3. They seem to have extended their settlements to the east of Jordan, and to have belonged to the larger section of Arabs called Ishmaelites Judges 8:24.', NULL, NULL, '''-39'':25 ''-54'':52 ''2'':5 ''22'':48 ''24'':56,108 ''25'':4 ''26'':57 ''3'':81 ''31'':23,47,50 ''32'':24 ''5'':21 ''50'':51 ''6'':20,80 ''8'':55,107 ''alli'':63 ''also'':27 ''amalekit'':66 ''arab'':103 ''arabian'':70 ''belong'':97 ''call'':72,104 ''cattl'':18 ''children'':75 ''collect'':73 ''compar'':45 ''connect'':40 ''countri'':44 ''east'':78,91 ''extend'':86 ''genesi'':3 ''gold'':33,43 ''great'':30 ''ishmaelit'':105 ''jordan'':93 ''judg'':19,54,79,106 ''larger'':100 ''metal'':36 ''midian'':1 ''note'':6 ''number'':15,22,46,49 ''ornament'':37 ''remark'':9 ''section'':101 ''see'':2 ''seem'':83 ''settlement'':88 ''show'':38 ''time'':60 ''tribe'':71 ''vast'':14 ''wealth'':31', NULL, '217');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1294', '2', '1294', NULL, 'And I heard a great voice out of the temple - A loud voice out of the temple as seen in heaven (notes on Revelation 11:19), and that came, therefore, from the very presence of God.Saying to the seven angels - That had the seven vials of wrath. See the notes on Revelation 15:1, Revelation 15:7.Go your ways - Your respective ways, to the fulfillment of the task assigned to each.And pour out the vials of the wrath of God - Empty those vials; cause to come upon the earth the plagues indicated by their contents. The order in which this was to be done is not intimated. It seems to be supposed that that would be understood by each.Upon the earth - The particular part of the earth is not here specified, but it should not be inferred that it was to be upon the earth in general, or that there were any calamities, in consequence of this pouring out of the vials of wrath, to spread over the whole world. The subsequent statements show what parts of the earth were particularly to be affected.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':54 ''11'':25 ''15'':53,56 ''19'':26 ''7.go'':57 ''affect'':184 ''angel'':40 ''assign'':69 ''calam'':153 ''came'':29 ''caus'':84 ''come'':86 ''consequ'':155 ''content'':95 ''done'':104 ''each.and'':71 ''each.upon'':119 ''earth'':89,121,127,145,179 ''empti'':81 ''fulfil'':65 ''general'':147 ''god'':80 ''god.saying'':36 ''great'':5 ''heard'':3 ''heaven'':21 ''indic'':92 ''infer'':137 ''intim'':107 ''loud'':12 ''note'':22,50 ''order'':97 ''part'':124,176 ''particular'':123,181 ''plagu'':91 ''pour'':72,158 ''presenc'':34 ''respect'':61 ''revel'':24,52,55 ''see'':48 ''seem'':109 ''seen'':19 ''seven'':39,44 ''show'':174 ''specifi'':131 ''spread'':166 ''statement'':173 ''subsequ'':172 ''suppos'':112 ''task'':68 ''templ'':10,17 ''therefor'':30 ''understood'':117 ''upon'':87,143 ''vial'':45,75,83,162 ''voic'':6,13 ''way'':59,62 ''whole'':169 ''world'':170 ''would'':115 ''wrath'':47,78,164', NULL, '1183');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1295', '2', '1295', NULL, 'And there came one of the seven angels which had the seven vials - See the notes on Revelation 15:1, Revelation 15:7. Reference is again made to these angels in the same manner in Revelation 21:9, where one of them says that he would show to John "the bride, the Lamb''s wife." No particular one is specified. The general idea seems to be, that to those seven angels was entrusted the execution of the last things, or the winding up of affairs introductory to the reign of God, and that the communications respecting those last events were properly made through them. It is clearly quite immaterial by which of these it is done. The expression "which had the seven vials," would seem to imply that though they had emptied the vials in the manner stated in the previous chapter, they still retained them in their hands.And talked with me - Spake to me. The word "talk" would imply a more protracted conversation than occurred here.Come hither - Greek, &#x3b4;&#x3b5;&#x3c5;&#x3c1;&#x3bf;  deuro - "Here, hither." This is a word merely calling the attention, as we should say now, "Here." It does not imply that John was to leave the place where he was.I will shew unto thee - Partly by symbols, and partly by express statements; for this is the way in which, in fact, he showed him.The judgment - The condemnation and calamity that will come upon her.Of the great whore - It is not uncommon in the Scriptures to represent a city under the image of a woman - a pure and holy city under the image of a virgin or chaste female; a corrupt, idolatrous, and wicked city under the image of an abandoned or lewd woman. See the notes on Isaiah 1:21; "How is the faithful city become an harlot!" Compare the notes on Isaiah 1:8. In Revelation 17:18, it is expressly said that "this woman is that great city which reigneth over the kings of the earth" - that is, as I suppose, papal Rome; and the design here is to represent it as resembling an abandoned female - fit representative of an apostate, corrupt, unfaithful church. Compare the notes on Revelation 9:21.That sitteth upon many waters - An image drawn either from Babylon, situated on the Euphrates, and encompassed by the many artificial rivers which had been made to irrigate the country, or Rome, situated on the Tiber. In Revelation 17:15 these waters are said to represent the peoples, multitudes, nations, and tongues over which the government symbolized by the woman ruled. See the notes on that verse. Waters are often used to symbolize nations.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':20,289,304 ''15'':19,22,402 ''17'':308,401 ''18'':309 ''21'':37,290 ''21.that'':363 ''7'':23 ''8'':305 ''9'':38,362 ''abandon'':280,347 ''affair'':85 ''angel'':8,30,71 ''apost'':353 ''artifici'':383 ''attent'':180 ''babylon'':373 ''becom'':296 ''bride'':51 ''calam'':229 ''call'':178 ''came'':3 ''chapter'':142 ''chast'':267 ''church'':356 ''citi'':248,259,274,295,320 ''clear'':107 ''come'':232 ''communic'':95 ''compar'':299,357 ''condemn'':227 ''convers'':164 ''corrupt'':270,354 ''countri'':392 ''design'':338 ''deuro'':170 ''done'':116 ''drawn'':370 ''earth'':328 ''either'':371 ''empti'':132 ''encompass'':379 ''entrust'':73 ''euphrat'':377 ''event'':99 ''execut'':75 ''express'':118,211 ''expressli'':312 ''fact'':221 ''faith'':294 ''femal'':268,348 ''fit'':349 ''general'':62 ''god'':91 ''govern'':418 ''great'':236,319 ''greek'':169 ''hands.and'':149 ''harlot'':298 ''her.of'':234 ''here.come'':167 ''him.the'':224 ''hither'':168,172 ''holi'':258 ''idea'':63 ''idolatr'':271 ''imag'':251,262,277,369 ''immateri'':109 ''impli'':127,160,190 ''introductori'':86 ''irrig'':390 ''isaiah'':288,303 ''john'':49,192 ''judgment'':225 ''king'':325 ''lamb'':53 ''last'':78,98 ''leav'':195 ''lewd'':282 ''made'':27,102,388 ''mani'':366,382 ''manner'':34,137 ''mere'':177 ''multitud'':411 ''nation'':412,436 ''note'':16,286,301,359,426 ''occur'':166 ''often'':432 ''one'':4,40,58 ''papal'':334 ''part'':205,209 ''particular'':57 ''peopl'':410 ''place'':197 ''previous'':141 ''proper'':101 ''protract'':163 ''pure'':256 ''quit'':108 ''refer'':24 ''reign'':89 ''reigneth'':322 ''repres'':246,342,350,408 ''resembl'':345 ''respect'':96 ''retain'':145 ''revel'':18,21,36,307,361,400 ''river'':384 ''rome'':335,394 ''rule'':423 ''said'':313,406 ''say'':43,184 ''scriptur'':244 ''see'':14,284,424 ''seem'':64,125 ''seven'':7,12,70,122 ''shew'':202 ''show'':47,223 ''sitteth'':364 ''situat'':374,395 ''spake'':153 ''specifi'':60 ''state'':138 ''statement'':212 ''still'':144 ''suppos'':333 ''symbol'':207,419,435 ''talk'':150,158 ''thee'':204 ''thing'':79 ''though'':129 ''tiber'':398 ''tongu'':414 ''uncommon'':241 ''unfaith'':355 ''unto'':203 ''upon'':233,365 ''use'':433 ''vers'':429 ''vial'':13,123,134 ''virgin'':265 ''was.i'':200 ''water'':367,404,430 ''way'':217 ''whore'':237 ''wick'':273 ''wife'':55 ''wind'':82 ''woman'':254,283,316,422 ''word'':157,176 ''would'':46,124,159', NULL, '1184');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1296', '2', '1296', NULL, 'And he cried mightily - Literally, "he cried with a strong great voice." See Revelation 10:3.Babylon the great is fallen, is fallen - See the notes on Revelation 14:8. The proclamation here is substantially the same as in that place, and no doubt the same thing is referred to.And is become the habitation of devils - Of demons - in allusion to the common opinion that the demons inhabited abandoned cities, old ruins, and deserts. See the notes on Matthew 12:43-45. The language here is taken from the description of Babylon in Isaiah 13:20-22; and for a full illustration of the meaning, see the notes on that passage.And the hold of every foul spirit - &#x3c6;&#x3c5;&#x3bb;&#x3b1;&#x3ba;&#x3b7;  phulake. A watch-post, station, haunt of such spirits - That is, they, as it were, kept guard there; were stationed there; haunted the place.And a cage of every unclean and hateful bird - That is, they would resort there, and abide there as in a cage. The word translated "cage" is the same which is rendered "hold" - &#x3c6;&#x3c5;&#x3bb;&#x3b1;&#x3ba;&#x3b7;  phulake. In Isaiah 13:21, it is said, "and owls shall dwell there"; and in Isaiah 14:23, it is said that it would be a "possession for the bittern." The idea is that of utter desolation; and the meaning here is, that spiritual Babylon - papal Rome Revelation 14:8 - will be reduced to a state of utter desolation resembling that of the real Babylon. It is not necessary to suppose this of the city of Rome itself - for that is not the object of the representation. It is the papacy, represented under the image of the city, and having its seat there. That is to be destroyed as utterly as was Babylon of old; that will become as odious, and loathsome, and detestable as the literal Babylon, the abode of monsters is.', NULL, NULL, '''-22'':96 ''-45'':81 ''10'':15 ''12'':79 ''13'':94,177 ''14'':28,190,222 ''20'':95 ''21'':178 ''23'':191 ''3.babylon'':16 ''43'':80 ''8'':29,223 ''abandon'':68 ''abid'':157 ''abod'':303 ''allus'':59 ''babylon'':91,218,238,286,301 ''becom'':51,291 ''bird'':149 ''bittern'':203 ''cage'':143,162,166 ''citi'':69,248,271 ''common'':62 ''cri'':3,7 ''demon'':57,66 ''descript'':89 ''desert'':73 ''desol'':210,232 ''destroy'':281 ''detest'':297 ''devil'':55 ''doubt'':43 ''dwell'':185 ''everi'':114,145 ''fallen'':20,22 ''foul'':115 ''full'':100 ''great'':11,18 ''guard'':134 ''habit'':53 ''hate'':148 ''haunt'':123,139 ''hold'':112,173 ''idea'':205 ''illustr'':101 ''imag'':268 ''inhabit'':67 ''isaiah'':93,176,189 ''kept'':133 ''languag'':83 ''liter'':5,300 ''loathsom'':295 ''matthew'':78 ''mean'':104,213 ''mightili'':4 ''monster'':305 ''necessari'':242 ''note'':25,76,107 ''object'':257 ''odious'':293 ''old'':70,288 ''opinion'':63 ''owl'':183 ''papaci'':264 ''papal'':219 ''passage.and'':110 ''phulak'':117,174 ''place'':40 ''place.and'':141 ''possess'':200 ''post'':121 ''proclam'':31 ''real'':237 ''reduc'':226 ''refer'':48 ''render'':172 ''repres'':265 ''represent'':260 ''resembl'':233 ''resort'':154 ''revel'':14,27,221 ''rome'':220,250 ''ruin'':71 ''said'':181,194 ''seat'':275 ''see'':13,23,74,105 ''shall'':184 ''spirit'':116,126 ''spiritu'':217 ''state'':229 ''station'':122,137 ''strong'':10 ''substanti'':34 ''suppos'':244 ''taken'':86 ''thing'':46 ''to.and'':49 ''translat'':165 ''unclean'':146 ''utter'':209,231,283 ''voic'':12 ''watch'':120 ''watch-post'':119 ''word'':164 ''would'':153,197', NULL, '1185');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1297', '2', '1297', NULL, 'And after these things - The things particularly that were exhibited in the previous chapter. See the notes on Revelation 18:1.I heard a great voice of much people in heaven - The voice of the worshippers before the throne.Saying, Alleluia - The Greek method of writing "Hallelujah." This word - &#x3b1;&#x3bb;&#x3bb;&#x3b7;&#x3bb;&#x3bf;&#x3c5;&#x3b9;&#x3b1;  allelouia - occurs in the New Testament only in this chapter, Revelation 19:1, Revelation 19:3-4, Revelation 19:6. The Hebrew phrase -    haleluw Yah "Hallelujah" - occurs often in the Old Testament. It means, properly, "Praise Yahweh," or "Praise the Lord." The occasion on which it is introduced here is very appropriate. It is uttered by the inhabitants of heaven, in the immediate presence of God himself, and in view of the final overthrow of the enemies of the church, and the triumph of the gospel. In such circumstances it was fit that heaven should render praise, and that a song of thanksgiving should be uttered in which all holy beings could unite.Salvation - That is, the salvation is to be ascribed to God. See the notes on Revelation 7:10.And glory, and honour - notes on Revelation 5:12.And power - notes on Revelation 5:13.Unto the Lord our God - That is, all that there is of honor, glory, power, in the redemption of the world belongs to God, and should be ascribed to him. This is expressive of the true feelings of piety always; this will constitute the song of heaven.', NULL, NULL, '''-4'':65 ''1'':21,61 ''10.and'':178 ''12.and'':186 ''13.unto'':192 ''18'':20 ''19'':60,63,67 ''3'':64 ''5'':185,191 ''6'':68 ''7'':177 ''allelouia'':49 ''alleluia'':40 ''alway'':231 ''appropri'':100 ''ascrib'':169,219 ''be'':159 ''belong'':213 ''chapter'':14,58 ''church'':128 ''circumst'':137 ''constitut'':234 ''could'':160 ''enemi'':125 ''exhibit'':10 ''express'':224 ''feel'':228 ''final'':121 ''fit'':140 ''glori'':179,205 ''god'':114,171,196,215 ''gospel'':134 ''great'':25 ''greek'':42 ''haleluw'':72 ''hallelujah'':46,74 ''heard'':23 ''heaven'':31,108,142,238 ''hebrew'':70 ''holi'':158 ''honor'':204 ''honour'':181 ''immedi'':111 ''inhabit'':106 ''introduc'':96 ''lord'':89,194 ''mean'':82 ''method'':43 ''much'':28 ''new'':53 ''note'':17,174,182,188 ''occas'':91 ''occur'':50,75 ''often'':76 ''old'':79 ''overthrow'':122 ''particular'':7 ''peopl'':29 ''phrase'':71 ''pieti'':230 ''power'':187,206 ''prais'':84,87,145 ''presenc'':112 ''previous'':13 ''proper'':83 ''redempt'':209 ''render'':144 ''revel'':19,59,62,66,176,184,190 ''salvat'':165 ''see'':15,172 ''song'':149,236 ''testament'':54,80 ''thanksgiv'':151 ''thing'':4,6 ''throne.saying'':39 ''triumph'':131 ''true'':227 ''unite.salvation'':161 ''utter'':103,154 ''view'':118 ''voic'':26,33 ''word'':48 ''world'':212 ''worshipp'':36 ''write'':45 ''yah'':73 ''yahweh'':85', NULL, '1186');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1298', '2', '1298', NULL, 'And I saw an angel come down from heaven - Compare the notes on Revelation 10:1. He does not say whether this angel had appeared to him before, but the impression is rather that it was a different one. The whole character of the composition of the book leads us to suppose that different angels were employed to make these communications to John, and that, in fact, in the progress of things disclosed in the book, he had contact with a considerable number of the heavenly inhabitants. The scene that is recorded here occurred after the destruction of the beast and the false prophet Revelation 19:18-21, and therefore, according to the principles expressed in the explanation of the previous chapters, what is intended to be described here will take place after the final destruction of the papal and Muhammedan powers.Having the key of the bottomless pit - See the notes on Revelation 1:18; Revelation 9:1. The fact that he has the key of that underworld is designed to denote here, that he can fasten it on Satan so that it shall become his prison.And a great chain in his hand - With which to bind the dragon, Revelation 20:2. It is called great because of the strength of him that was to be bound. The chain only appears to have been in his hand. Perhaps the key was suspended to his side.', NULL, NULL, '''-21'':108 ''1'':16,154,158 ''10'':15 ''18'':107,155 ''19'':106 ''2'':202 ''20'':201 ''9'':157 ''accord'':111 ''angel'':5,23,55 ''appear'':25,221 ''beast'':100 ''becom'':185 ''bind'':197 ''book'':48,76 ''bottomless'':147 ''bound'':217 ''call'':205 ''chain'':190,219 ''chapter'':122 ''charact'':42 ''come'':6 ''communic'':61 ''compar'':10 ''composit'':45 ''consider'':82 ''contact'':79 ''denot'':172 ''describ'':128 ''design'':170 ''destruct'':97,136 ''differ'':38,54 ''disclos'':73 ''dragon'':199 ''employ'':57 ''explan'':118 ''express'':115 ''fact'':67,160 ''fals'':103 ''fasten'':177 ''final'':135 ''great'':189,206 ''hand'':193,227 ''heaven'':9,86 ''impress'':31 ''inhabit'':87 ''intend'':125 ''john'':63 ''key'':144,165,230 ''lead'':49 ''make'':59 ''muhammedan'':141 ''note'':12,151 ''number'':83 ''occur'':94 ''one'':39 ''papal'':139 ''perhap'':228 ''pit'':148 ''place'':132 ''powers.having'':142 ''previous'':121 ''principl'':114 ''prison.and'':187 ''progress'':70 ''prophet'':104 ''rather'':33 ''record'':92 ''revel'':14,105,153,156,200 ''satan'':180 ''saw'':3 ''say'':20 ''scene'':89 ''see'':149 ''shall'':184 ''side'':235 ''strength'':210 ''suppos'':52 ''suspend'':232 ''take'':131 ''therefor'':110 ''thing'':72 ''underworld'':168 ''us'':50 ''whether'':21 ''whole'':41', NULL, '1187');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('218', '2', '218', NULL, 'The middle watch - The old Jewish division of the night was three watches of four hours each. They are alluded to in Exodus 14:24; 1 Samuel 11:11; Psalm 63:6; Psalm 90:4; Psalm 119:148; Psalm 130:6; Lamentations 2:19. After the Jews fell under the power of the Romans, they used the Roman division of four watches of three hours each Matthew 14:25; Mark 13:35."The beginning" of the watch would be about eleven o''clock at night.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':26 ''11'':28,29 ''119'':37 ''13'':71 ''130'':40 ''14'':24,68 ''148'':38 ''19'':44 ''2'':43 ''24'':25 ''25'':69 ''35'':72 ''4'':35 ''6'':32,41 ''63'':31 ''90'':34 ''allud'':20 ''begin'':74 ''clock'':83 ''divis'':7,59 ''eleven'':81 ''exodus'':23 ''fell'':48 ''four'':15,61 ''hour'':16,65 ''jew'':47 ''jewish'':6 ''lament'':42 ''mark'':70 ''matthew'':67 ''middl'':2 ''night'':10,85 ''o'':82 ''old'':5 ''power'':51 ''psalm'':30,33,36,39 ''roman'':54,58 ''samuel'':27 ''three'':12,64 ''use'':56 ''watch'':3,13,62,77 ''would'':78', NULL, '218');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1299', '2', '1299', NULL, 'And he said unto me - That is, he that sat on the throne - the Messiah.It is done - It is finished, complete; or, still more expressively, "it is" - &#x3b3;&#x3b5;&#x3b3;&#x3bf;&#x3bd;&#x3b1;&#x3bd;  gegonan. An expression remarkably similar to this occurs in John 19:30, when the Saviour on the cross said, "It is finished." The meaning in the passage before us evidently is, "The great work is accomplished; the arrangement of human affairs is complete. The redeemed are gathered in; the wicked are cut off; truth is triumphant, and all is now complete - prepared for the eternal state of things."I am Alpha and Omega, the beginning and the end - This language makes it morally certain that the speaker here is the Lord Jesus, for it is the very language which he uses of himself in Revelation 1:11. See its meaning explained in the notes on Revelation 1:8. If it is applied to him here, it proves that he is divine, for in the following verse (7) the speaker says that he would be a God to him who should "overcome." The meaning of the language as used here, regarded as spoken by the Redeemer at the consummation of all things, and as his people are about entering into the abodes of blessedness, is, "I am now indeed the Alpha and the Omega - the first and the last. The attributes implied in this language which I claimed for myself are now verified in me, and it is seen that these properly belong to me. The scheme for setting up a kingdom in the lost world began in me, and it ends in me - the glorious and triumphant king."I will give unto him that is athirst - See the Matthew 5:6 note; John 4:14; John 7:37 notes.Of the fountain of the water of life - An image often used in the Scriptures to represent salvation. It is compared with a fountain that flows in abundance, where all may freely slake their thirst.Freely - Without money and without price (Isaiah 55:1 note; John 7:37 note); the common representation in the Scriptures. The meaning here is, not that he would do this in the future, but that he had shown that this was his character, as he had claimed, in the same way as he had shown that he was the Alpha and the Omega. The freeness and the fulness of salvation will be one of the most striking things made manifest when the immense hosts of the redeemed shall be welcomed to their eternal abodes.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':133,144,338 ''11'':134 ''14'':292 ''19'':38 ''30'':39 ''37'':295,342 ''4'':291 ''5'':287 ''55'':337 ''6'':288 ''7'':164,294,341 ''8'':145 ''abod'':208,423 ''abund'':323 ''accomplish'':63 ''affair'':68 ''alpha'':98,217,389 ''appli'':149 ''arrang'':65 ''athirst'':283 ''attribut'':227 ''began'':263 ''begin'':102 ''belong'':249 ''blessed'':210 ''certain'':111 ''charact'':372 ''claim'':234,376 ''common'':345 ''compar'':316 ''complet'':21,70,88 ''consumm'':195 ''cross'':45 ''cut'':79 ''divin'':158 ''done'':17 ''end'':105,268 ''enter'':205 ''etern'':92,422 ''evid'':57 ''explain'':138 ''express'':25,30 ''finish'':20,49 ''first'':222 ''flow'':321 ''follow'':162 ''fountain'':298,319 ''freeli'':327 ''freeness'':394 ''ful'':397 ''futur'':362 ''gather'':74 ''gegonan'':28 ''give'':278 ''glorious'':272 ''god'':173 ''great'':60 ''host'':413 ''human'':67 ''imag'':305 ''immens'':412 ''impli'':228 ''inde'':215 ''isaiah'':336 ''jesus'':119 ''john'':37,290,293,340 ''king'':275 ''kingdom'':258 ''languag'':107,125,183,231 ''last'':225 ''life'':303 ''lord'':118 ''lost'':261 ''made'':408 ''make'':108 ''manifest'':409 ''matthew'':286 ''may'':326 ''mean'':51,137,180,351 ''messiah.it'':15 ''money'':332 ''moral'':110 ''note'':141,289,339,343 ''notes.of'':296 ''occur'':35 ''often'':306 ''omega'':100,220,392 ''one'':402 ''overcom'':178 ''passag'':54 ''peopl'':202 ''prepar'':89 ''price'':335 ''proper'':248 ''prove'':154 ''redeem'':72,192,416 ''regard'':187 ''remark'':31 ''repres'':312 ''represent'':346 ''revel'':132,143 ''said'':3,46 ''salvat'':313,399 ''sat'':10 ''saviour'':42 ''say'':167 ''scheme'':253 ''scriptur'':310,349 ''see'':135,284 ''seen'':245 ''set'':255 ''shall'':417 ''shown'':367,384 ''similar'':32 ''slake'':328 ''speaker'':114,166 ''spoken'':189 ''state'':93 ''still'':23 ''strike'':406 ''thing'':95,198,407 ''thirst.freely'':330 ''throne'':13 ''triumphant'':83,274 ''truth'':81 ''unto'':4,279 ''us'':56 ''use'':128,185,307 ''verifi'':239 ''vers'':163 ''water'':301 ''way'':380 ''welcom'':419 ''wick'':77 ''without'':331,334 ''work'':61 ''world'':262 ''would'':170,357', NULL, '1188');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('1300', '2', '1300', NULL, 'And he showed me a pure river of water of life - In the New Jerusalem; the happy abode of the redeemed. The phrase "water of life," means living or running water, like a spring or fountain, as contrasted with a stagnant pool. See the notes on John 4:14. The allusion here is doubtless to the first Eden, where a river watered the garden (Genesis 2:10, seq.), and as this is a description of Eden recovered, or Paradise regained, it was natural to introduce a river of water also, yet in such a way as to accord with the general description of that future abode of the redeemed. It does not spring up, therefore, from the ground, but flows from the throne of God and the Lamb. Perhaps, also, the writer had in his eye the description in Ezekiel 47:1-12, where a stream issues from under the temple, and is parted in different directions.Clear as crystal - See the notes on Revelation 4:6.Proceeding out of the throne of God and of the Lamb - Flowing from the foot of the throne. Compare Revelation 4:6. This idea is strictly in accordance with Oriental imagery. In the East, fountains and running streams constituted an essential part of the image of enjoyment and prosperity (see the notes on Isaiah 35:6), and such fountains were common in the courts of Oriental houses. Here, the river is an emblem of peace, happiness, plenty; and the essential thought in its flowing from the throne is, that all the happiness of heaven proceeds from God.', NULL, NULL, '''-12'':143 ''1'':142 ''10'':67 ''14'':49 ''2'':66 ''35'':220 ''4'':48,165,186 ''47'':141 ''6'':187,221 ''6.proceeding'':166 ''abod'':18,106 ''accord'':98,193 ''allus'':51 ''also'':90,130 ''common'':226 ''compar'':184 ''constitut'':204 ''contrast'':38 ''court'':229 ''crystal'':159 ''descript'':74,102,138 ''differ'':156 ''directions.clear'':157 ''doubtless'':54 ''east'':199 ''eden'':58,76 ''emblem'':238 ''enjoy'':212 ''essenti'':206,245 ''eye'':136 ''ezekiel'':140 ''first'':57 ''flow'':120,177,249 ''foot'':180 ''fountain'':36,200,224 ''futur'':105 ''garden'':64 ''general'':101 ''genesi'':65 ''god'':125,172,262 ''ground'':118 ''happi'':17,241,257 ''heaven'':259 ''hous'':232 ''idea'':189 ''imag'':210 ''imageri'':196 ''introduc'':85 ''isaiah'':219 ''issu'':147 ''jerusalem'':15 ''john'':47 ''lamb'':128,176 ''life'':11,26 ''like'':32 ''live'':28 ''mean'':27 ''natur'':83 ''new'':14 ''note'':45,162,217 ''orient'':195,231 ''paradis'':79 ''part'':154,207 ''peac'':240 ''perhap'':129 ''phrase'':23 ''plenti'':242 ''pool'':42 ''proceed'':260 ''prosper'':214 ''pure'':6 ''recov'':77 ''redeem'':21,109 ''regain'':80 ''revel'':164,185 ''river'':7,61,87,235 ''run'':30,202 ''see'':43,160,215 ''seq'':68 ''show'':3 ''spring'':34,113 ''stagnant'':41 ''stream'':146,203 ''strict'':191 ''templ'':151 ''therefor'':115 ''thought'':246 ''throne'':123,170,183,252 ''water'':9,24,31,62,89 ''way'':95 ''writer'':132 ''yet'':91', NULL, '1189');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('212', '2', '212', NULL, 'After the death of Joshua - But from Judges 1:1 to Judges 2:9 is a consecutive narrative, ending with the death of Joshua. Hence, the events in this chapter and in Judges 2:1-6 are to be taken as belonging to the lifetime of Joshua. See Judges 2:11 note.Asked the Lord - The phrase is only found in Judges and Samuel. It was the privilege of the civil ruler, to apply to the high priest to consult for him the Urim and Thummim (marginal reference). (Compare Joshua 14:1; Joshua 18:1, Joshua 18:10; Joshua 19:51). Here it was not Phinehas, as Josephus concludes from placing these events after the death of Joshua, but Eleazar, through whom the children of Israel inquired "who" (or, rather), "which tribe of us shall go up!"', NULL, NULL, '''-6'':36 ''1'':9,10,35,91,94 ''10'':97 ''11'':51 ''14'':90 ''18'':93,96 ''19'':99 ''2'':13,34,50 ''51'':100 ''9'':14 ''appli'':73 ''belong'':42 ''chapter'':30 ''children'':123 ''civil'':70 ''compar'':88 ''conclud'':108 ''consecut'':17 ''consult'':79 ''death'':3,22,115 ''eleazar'':119 ''end'':19 ''event'':27,112 ''found'':59 ''go'':135 ''henc'':25 ''high'':76 ''inquir'':126 ''israel'':125 ''josephus'':107 ''joshua'':5,24,47,89,92,95,98,117 ''judg'':8,12,33,49,61 ''lifetim'':45 ''lord'':54 ''margin'':86 ''narrat'':18 ''note.asked'':52 ''phineha'':105 ''phrase'':56 ''place'':110 ''priest'':77 ''privileg'':67 ''rather'':129 ''refer'':87 ''ruler'':71 ''samuel'':63 ''see'':48 ''shall'':134 ''taken'':40 ''thummim'':85 ''tribe'':131 ''urim'':83 ''us'':133', NULL, '212');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('213', '2', '213', NULL, 'The angel of the Lord (not an angel). - The phrase is used nearly 60 times to designate the Angel of God''s presence. See Genesis 12:7 note. In all cases where "the angel of the Lord" delivers a message, he does it as if God Himself were speaking, without the intervening words "Thus saith the Lord," which are used in the case of prophets. (Compare Judges 6:8; Joshua 24:2.)When the host of Israel came up from Gilgal in the plain of Jericho, near the Jordan Joshua 4:19 to Shiloh and Shechem, in the hill country of Ephraim, the Angel who had been with them at Gilgal Exodus 23:20-23; Exodus 33:1-4; Joshua 5:10-15 accompanied them. The mention of Gilgal thus fixes the transaction to the period soon after the removal of the camp from Gilgal, and the events recorded in Judges 1:1-36 (of which those related in Judges 1:1-29 took place before, and those in Judges 1:30-36, just after that removal). It also shows that it was the conduct of the Israelites, recorded in Judges 1 as in Joshua 16:1-10; 17, which provoked this rebuke.', NULL, NULL, '''-10'':198 ''-15'':123 ''-23'':115 ''-29'':163 ''-36'':154,173 ''-4'':119 ''1'':118,152,153,161,162,171,192,197 ''10'':122 ''12'':26 ''16'':196 ''17'':199 ''19'':92 ''2'':72 ''20'':114 ''23'':113 ''24'':71 ''30'':172 ''33'':117 ''4'':91 ''5'':121 ''6'':68 ''60'':14 ''7'':27 ''8'':69 ''accompani'':124 ''also'':179 ''angel'':2,8,19,34,104 ''came'':78 ''camp'':143 ''case'':31,63 ''compar'':66 ''conduct'':185 ''countri'':100 ''deliv'':38 ''design'':17 ''ephraim'':102 ''event'':148 ''exodus'':112,116 ''fix'':131 ''genesi'':25 ''gilgal'':81,111,129,145 ''god'':21,46 ''hill'':99 ''host'':75 ''interven'':52 ''israel'':77 ''israelit'':188 ''jericho'':86 ''jordan'':89 ''joshua'':70,90,120,195 ''judg'':67,151,160,170,191 ''lord'':5,37,57 ''mention'':127 ''messag'':40 ''near'':13,87 ''note'':28 ''period'':136 ''phrase'':10 ''place'':165 ''plain'':84 ''presenc'':23 ''prophet'':65 ''provok'':201 ''rebuk'':203 ''record'':149,189 ''relat'':158 ''remov'':140,177 ''saith'':55 ''see'':24 ''shechem'':96 ''shiloh'':94 ''show'':180 ''soon'':137 ''speak'':49 ''thus'':54,130 ''time'':15 ''took'':164 ''transact'':133 ''use'':12,60 ''without'':50 ''word'':53', NULL, '213');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('214', '2', '214', NULL, 'Even as many of Israel ... - These words show that the writer has especially in view the generation which came to man''s estate immediately after the close of the wars with the Canaanites Joshua 23:1. Compare Judges 2:10.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':36 ''10'':40 ''2'':39 ''23'':35 ''came'':19 ''canaanit'':33 ''close'':27 ''compar'':37 ''especi'':13 ''estat'':23 ''even'':1 ''generat'':17 ''immedi'':24 ''israel'':5 ''joshua'':34 ''judg'':38 ''man'':21 ''mani'':3 ''show'':8 ''view'':15 ''war'':30 ''word'':7 ''writer'':11', NULL, '214');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('215', '2', '215', NULL, 'See Joshua 11:1 note. Since the events there narrated, Hazor must have been rebuilt, and have resumed its position as the metropolis of the northern Canaanites; the other cities must also have resumed their independence, and restored the fallen dynasties.Harosheth (identified by Conder with El Harathlyeh, see Judges 4:6) is marked by the addition of the Gentiles, as in Galilee of the nations Genesis 14:1; Isaiah 9:1. The name Harosheth signifies workmanship, cutting and carving, whether in stone or wood Exodus 31:5, and hence, might be applied to the place where such works are carried on. It has been conjectured that this being a great timber district, rich in cedars and fir-trees, and near Great Zidon Joshua 11:8, Jabin kept a large number of oppressed Israelites at work in hewing wood, and preparing it at Harosheth for transport to Zidon; and that these woodcutters, armed with axes and hatchets, formed the soldiers of Barak''s army.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':4,68,71 ''11'':3,125 ''14'':67 ''31'':86 ''4'':50 ''5'':87 ''6'':51 ''8'':126 ''9'':70 ''addit'':56 ''also'':32 ''appli'':92 ''arm'':153 ''armi'':164 ''axe'':155 ''barak'':162 ''canaanit'':27 ''carri'':100 ''carv'':79 ''cedar'':115 ''citi'':30 ''conder'':44 ''conjectur'':105 ''cut'':77 ''district'':112 ''dynasties.harosheth'':41 ''el'':46 ''event'':8 ''exodus'':85 ''fallen'':40 ''fir'':118 ''fir-tre'':117 ''form'':158 ''galile'':62 ''genesi'':66 ''gentil'':59 ''great'':110,122 ''harathlyeh'':47 ''harosheth'':74,144 ''hatchet'':157 ''hazor'':11 ''henc'':89 ''hew'':138 ''identifi'':42 ''independ'':36 ''isaiah'':69 ''israelit'':134 ''jabin'':127 ''joshua'':2,124 ''judg'':49 ''kept'':128 ''larg'':130 ''mark'':53 ''metropoli'':23 ''might'':90 ''must'':12,31 ''name'':73 ''narrat'':10 ''nation'':65 ''near'':121 ''northern'':26 ''note'':5 ''number'':131 ''oppress'':133 ''place'':95 ''posit'':20 ''prepar'':141 ''rebuilt'':15 ''restor'':38 ''resum'':18,34 ''rich'':113 ''see'':1,48 ''signifi'':75 ''sinc'':6 ''soldier'':160 ''stone'':82 ''timber'':111 ''transport'':146 ''tree'':119 ''whether'':80 ''wood'':84,139 ''woodcutt'':152 ''work'':98,136 ''workmanship'':76 ''zidon'':123,148', NULL, '215');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('219', '2', '219', NULL, 'Succoth was in the valley or Ghor of the Jordan Judges 8:5, and Penuel apparently in the mountain. No identification of Penuel has taken place. It was south of the Brook Jabbok, and on Jacob''s way to Succoth. Gideon, journeying in the opposite direction to Jacob, comes from Succoth to Penuel.', NULL, NULL, '''5'':13 ''8'':12 ''appar'':16 ''brook'':32 ''come'':49 ''direct'':46 ''ghor'':7 ''gideon'':41 ''identif'':21 ''jabbok'':33 ''jacob'':36,48 ''jordan'':10 ''journey'':42 ''judg'':11 ''mountain'':19 ''opposit'':45 ''penuel'':15,23,53 ''place'':26 ''south'':29 ''succoth'':1,40,51 ''taken'':25 ''valley'':5 ''way'':38', NULL, '219');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('221', '2', '221', NULL, 'Defend - The marginal reading "to deliver," is far preferable. The word is the same as in Judges 2:16, Judges 2:18; Judges 3:9, Judges 3:15, Judges 3:31, etc., and is the technical word applied to the judges. Compare Nehemiah 9:27 ("saviours who saved them," the King James Version).The term "there arose," also marks Tola as one of the Judges, properly so called, raised by divine providence.Tola and Puah - Both names of heads of houses in the tribe of Issachar 1 Chronicles 7:1; Genesis 46:13.Shamir - Not the same as that mentioned in Joshua 15:48, which was in the hill country of Judah. Issaehar would seem from this to have extended into the northern part of mount Ephraim.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':86,89 ''13.shamir'':92 ''15'':28,101 ''16'':19 ''18'':22 ''2'':18,21 ''27'':45 ''3'':24,27,30 ''31'':31 ''46'':91 ''48'':102 ''7'':88 ''9'':25,44 ''also'':58 ''appli'':38 ''aros'':57 ''call'':68 ''chronicl'':87 ''compar'':42 ''countri'':108 ''defend'':1 ''deliv'':6 ''divin'':71 ''ephraim'':125 ''etc'':32 ''extend'':118 ''far'':8 ''genesi'':90 ''head'':78 ''hill'':107 ''hous'':80 ''issachar'':85 ''issaehar'':111 ''jame'':52 ''joshua'':100 ''judah'':110 ''judg'':17,20,23,26,29,41,65 ''king'':51 ''margin'':3 ''mark'':59 ''mention'':98 ''mount'':124 ''name'':76 ''nehemiah'':43 ''northern'':121 ''one'':62 ''part'':122 ''prefer'':9 ''proper'':66 ''providence.tola'':72 ''puah'':74 ''rais'':69 ''read'':4 ''save'':48 ''saviour'':46 ''seem'':113 ''technic'':36 ''term'':55 ''tola'':60 ''tribe'':83 ''version'':53 ''word'':11,37 ''would'':112', NULL, '221');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('222', '2', '222', NULL, 'The history of Jephthah appears to be an independent history inserted by the compiler of the Book of Judges. Jdg 11:4-5 introduce the Ammonite war without any apparent reference to Judges 10:17-18.A genealogy of Manasseh 1 Chronicles 7:14-17 gives the families which sprang from Gilead, and among them mention is made of an "Aramitess" concubine as the mother of one family. Jephthah, the son of Gilead by a strange woman, fled, after his father''s death, to the land of Tob Judges 11:3, presumably the land of his maternal ancestors (compare Judges 9:1) and an "Aramean" settlement (2 Samuel 10:6, 2 Samuel 10:8; 1 Macc. 5:13). It is difficult to conceive that Jephthah was literally the son of Gilead, if Gilead was the son of Machir, the son of Manasseh. Possibly "Gilead" here denotes the heir of Gilead, the head of the family, whose individual name has not been preserved, nor the time when he lived.', NULL, NULL, '''-17'':45 ''-18'':36 ''-5'':23 ''1'':41,102,115 ''10'':34,109,113 ''11'':21,90 ''13'':118 ''14'':44 ''17'':35 ''2'':107,111 ''3'':91 ''4'':22 ''5'':117 ''6'':110 ''7'':43 ''8'':114 ''9'':101 ''ammonit'':26 ''among'':54 ''ancestor'':98 ''appar'':30 ''appear'':5 ''aramean'':105 ''aramitess'':61 ''book'':17 ''chronicl'':42 ''compar'':99 ''compil'':14 ''conceiv'':123 ''concubin'':62 ''death'':83 ''denot'':146 ''difficult'':121 ''famili'':48,68,155 ''father'':81 ''fled'':78 ''genealog'':38 ''gilead'':52,73,131,133,144,150 ''give'':46 ''head'':152 ''heir'':148 ''histori'':2,10 ''independ'':9 ''individu'':157 ''insert'':11 ''introduc'':24 ''jdg'':20 ''jephthah'':4,69,125 ''judg'':19,33,89,100 ''land'':86,94 ''liter'':127 ''live'':168 ''macc'':116 ''machir'':138 ''made'':58 ''manasseh'':40,142 ''matern'':97 ''mention'':56 ''mother'':65 ''name'':158 ''one'':67 ''possibl'':143 ''preserv'':162 ''presum'':92 ''refer'':31 ''samuel'':108,112 ''settlement'':106 ''son'':71,129,136,140 ''sprang'':50 ''strang'':76 ''time'':165 ''tob'':88 ''war'':27 ''whose'':156 ''without'':28 ''woman'':77', NULL, '222');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('223', '2', '223', NULL, 'Compare the similar complaint of the Ephraimites to Gideon Judges 8:1, when a civil war was only avoided by Gideon''s wise and patriotic moderation. The overhearing pride of Ephraim comes out in both occurrences (see also Joshua 17:14-18).We will burn thine house upon thee with fire - Compare the fierce threat of the Philistines to Samson''s wife Judges 14:15, and the yet fiercer execution Judges 15:6. Burning appears as a mode of capital punishment Genesis 38:24; Joshua 7:25, and as a mode of desperate warfare (Judges 1:8; Judges 20:48; Joshua 8:8, Joshua 8:19, etc.).', NULL, NULL, '''-18'':42 ''1'':12,96 ''14'':41,64 ''15'':65,72 ''17'':40 ''19'':106 ''20'':99 ''24'':84 ''25'':87 ''38'':83 ''48'':100 ''6'':73 ''7'':86 ''8'':11,97,102,103,105 ''also'':38 ''appear'':75 ''avoid'':19 ''burn'':45,74 ''capit'':80 ''civil'':15 ''come'':32 ''compar'':1,52 ''complaint'':4 ''desper'':93 ''ephraim'':31 ''ephraimit'':7 ''etc'':107 ''execut'':70 ''fierc'':54 ''fiercer'':69 ''fire'':51 ''genesi'':82 ''gideon'':9,21 ''hous'':47 ''joshua'':39,85,101,104 ''judg'':10,63,71,95,98 ''mode'':78,91 ''moder'':26 ''occurr'':36 ''overhear'':28 ''patriot'':25 ''philistin'':58 ''pride'':29 ''punish'':81 ''samson'':60 ''see'':37 ''similar'':3 ''thee'':49 ''thine'':46 ''threat'':55 ''upon'':48 ''war'':16 ''warfar'':94 ''wife'':62 ''wise'':23 ''yet'':68', NULL, '223');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('224', '2', '224', NULL, 'The Philistines have been mentioned as oppressors of Israel in Judges 3:31; Judges 10:7, Judges 10:11; and the Israelite worship of the gods of the Philistines is spoken of in Judges 10:6. But this is the first time that we have any detailed history in connection with the Philistines. They continned to be the prominent enemies of Israel until the time of David.Forty years - The Philistine dominion began before the birth of Samson Judges 13:5, and was in force during Samson''s twenty years'' judgeship Judges 14:4; Judges 15:20. The 40 years are, therefore, about coincident with Samson''s life.', NULL, NULL, '''10'':15,18,35 ''11'':19 ''13'':79 ''14'':92 ''15'':95 ''20'':96 ''3'':12 ''31'':13 ''4'':93 ''40'':98 ''5'':80 ''6'':36 ''7'':16 ''began'':72 ''birth'':75 ''coincid'':103 ''connect'':50 ''contin'':55 ''david.forty'':67 ''detail'':47 ''dominion'':71 ''enemi'':60 ''first'':41 ''forc'':84 ''god'':26 ''histori'':48 ''israel'':9,62 ''israelit'':22 ''judg'':11,14,17,34,78,91,94 ''judgeship'':90 ''life'':107 ''mention'':5 ''oppressor'':7 ''philistin'':2,29,53,70 ''promin'':59 ''samson'':77,86,105 ''spoken'':31 ''therefor'':101 ''time'':42,65 ''twenti'':88 ''worship'':23 ''year'':68,89,99', NULL, '224');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('225', '2', '225', NULL, 'Get her for me - namely, by Paying the requisite dowry (see marginal references) and gifts to relations. Hence, the frequent mention of parents taking wives for their sons Exodus 34:16; Nehemiah 10:30, because the parents of the bridegroom conducted the negotiation, and paid the dower to the parents of the bride.', NULL, NULL, '''10'':33 ''16'':31 ''30'':34 ''34'':30 ''bride'':53 ''bridegroom'':40 ''conduct'':41 ''dower'':47 ''dowri'':10 ''exodus'':29 ''frequent'':20 ''get'':1 ''gift'':15 ''henc'':18 ''margin'':12 ''mention'':21 ''name'':5 ''negoti'':43 ''nehemiah'':32 ''paid'':45 ''parent'':23,37,50 ''pay'':7 ''refer'':13 ''relat'':17 ''requisit'':9 ''see'':11 ''son'':28 ''take'':24 ''wive'':25', NULL, '225');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('226', '2', '226', NULL, 'Visited his wife with a kid - A common present (see Genesis 38:17; Luke 15:29). From Samson''s wife being still in her father''s house, it would seem that she was only betrothed, not actually married, to his companion.', NULL, NULL, '''15'':15 ''17'':13 ''29'':16 ''38'':12 ''actual'':37 ''betroth'':35 ''common'':8 ''companion'':41 ''father'':25 ''genesi'':11 ''hous'':27 ''kid'':6 ''luke'':14 ''marri'':38 ''present'':9 ''samson'':18 ''see'':10 ''seem'':30 ''still'':22 ''visit'':1 ''wife'':3,20 ''would'':29', NULL, '226');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('227', '2', '227', NULL, 'The possession of his extraordinary strength is ascribed (e. g. Judges 13:25) to the presence of the Spirit of the Lord. Now the Lord, or the Spirit of the Lord, had departed from him, and so his strength had gone too. The practical lesson against the presumption of self-dependence, and the all-importance of a hearty dependence upon God''s Holy Spirit, must not be overlooked.', NULL, NULL, '''13'':12 ''25'':13 ''all-import'':55 ''ascrib'':8 ''depart'':33 ''depend'':52,61 ''e'':9 ''extraordinari'':5 ''g'':10 ''god'':63 ''gone'':41 ''hearti'':60 ''holi'':65 ''import'':57 ''judg'':11 ''lesson'':45 ''lord'':22,25,31 ''must'':67 ''overlook'':70 ''possess'':2 ''practic'':44 ''presenc'':16 ''presumpt'':48 ''self'':51 ''self-depend'':50 ''spirit'':19,28,66 ''strength'':6,39 ''upon'':62', NULL, '227');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('244', '2', '244', NULL, 'This verse implies a long period, probably not less than 20 years, of which we have no account except what is contained in the brief notice in 1 Samuel 7:13-17. The general idea conveyed is of a time of peace and prosperity, analogous to that under other Judges.', NULL, NULL, '''-17'':32 ''1'':28 ''13'':31 ''20'':11 ''7'':30 ''account'':18 ''analog'':45 ''brief'':25 ''contain'':22 ''convey'':36 ''except'':19 ''general'':34 ''idea'':35 ''impli'':3 ''judg'':50 ''less'':9 ''long'':5 ''notic'':26 ''peac'':42 ''period'':6 ''probabl'':7 ''prosper'':44 ''samuel'':29 ''time'':40 ''vers'':2 ''year'':12', NULL, '244');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('233', '2', '233', NULL, 'In the days when the Judges ruled - "Judged." This note of time, like that in Ruth 4:7; Judges 18:1; Judges 17:6, indicates that this Book was written after the rule of the judges had ceased. The genealogy Ruth 4:17-22 points to the time of David as the earliest when the Book of Ruth could have been written.A famine - Caused probably by one of the hostile invasions recorded in the Book of Judges. Most of the Jewish commentators, from the mention of Bethlehem, and the resemblance of the names Boaz and Ibzan, refer this history to the judge Ibzan Judges 12:8, but without probability.The country of Moab - Here, and in Ruth 1:2, Ruth 1:22; Ruth 4:3, literally, "the field" or "fields." As the same word is elsewhere used of the territory of Moab, of the Amalekites, of Edom, and of the Philistines, it would seem to be a term pointedly used with reference to a foreign country, not the country of the speaker, or writer; and to have been specially applied to Moab.', NULL, NULL, '''-22'':44 ''1'':21,117,120 ''12'':105 ''17'':23,43 ''18'':20 ''2'':118 ''22'':121 ''3'':124 ''4'':17,42,123 ''6'':24 ''7'':18 ''8'':106 ''amalekit'':144 ''appli'':179 ''bethlehem'':87 ''boaz'':94 ''book'':28,56,75 ''caus'':64 ''ceas'':38 ''comment'':82 ''could'':59 ''countri'':110,165,168 ''david'':50 ''day'':3 ''earliest'':53 ''edom'':146 ''elsewher'':135 ''famin'':63 ''field'':127,129 ''foreign'':164 ''genealog'':40 ''histori'':99 ''hostil'':70 ''ibzan'':96,103 ''indic'':25 ''invas'':71 ''jewish'':81 ''judg'':6,8,19,22,36,77,102,104 ''like'':13 ''liter'':125 ''mention'':85 ''moab'':112,141,181 ''name'':93 ''note'':10 ''one'':67 ''philistin'':150 ''point'':45,158 ''probability.the'':109 ''probabl'':65 ''record'':72 ''refer'':97,161 ''resembl'':90 ''rule'':7,33 ''ruth'':16,41,58,116,119,122 ''seem'':153 ''speaker'':171 ''special'':178 ''term'':157 ''territori'':139 ''time'':12,48 ''use'':136,159 ''without'':108 ''word'':133 ''would'':152 ''writer'':173 ''written'':30 ''written.a'':62', NULL, '233');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('234', '2', '234', NULL, 'A kinsman - More literally "an acquaintance"; here (and in the feminine, Ruth 3:2) denoting the person with whom one is intimately acquainted, one''s near relation. The next kinsman of Ruth 2:20, etc.   ga''al, is a wholly different word.Boaz - Commonly taken to mean, "strength is in him" (compare 1 Kings 7:21).', NULL, NULL, '''1'':52 ''2'':14,33 ''20'':34 ''21'':55 ''3'':13 ''7'':54 ''acquaint'':6,23 ''al'':37 ''common'':43 ''compar'':51 ''denot'':15 ''differ'':41 ''etc'':35 ''feminin'':11 ''ga'':36 ''intim'':22 ''king'':53 ''kinsman'':2,30 ''liter'':4 ''mean'':46 ''near'':26 ''next'':29 ''one'':20,24 ''person'':17 ''relat'':27 ''ruth'':12,32 ''strength'':47 ''taken'':44 ''wholli'':40 ''word.boaz'':42', NULL, '234');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('235', '2', '235', NULL, 'Who art thou, my daughter? - In the dim twilight Ruth 3:14 her mother was not sure at first who the young woman was, who sought admittance into the house.', NULL, NULL, '''14'':12 ''3'':11 ''admitt'':27 ''art'':2 ''daughter'':5 ''dim'':8 ''first'':19 ''hous'':30 ''mother'':14 ''ruth'':10 ''sought'':26 ''sure'':17 ''thou'':3 ''twilight'':9 ''woman'':23 ''young'':22', NULL, '235');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('236', '2', '236', NULL, 'The gate is the place of concourse, of business, and of justice in Oriental cities (see Judges 19:15 note; Genesis 34:20; Deuteronomy 16:18).Ho, such a one! - Indicating that the name of the kinsman was either unknown or purposely concealed 1 Samuel 21:2; 2 Kings 6:8.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':44 ''15'':19 ''16'':25 ''18'':26 ''19'':18 ''2'':47,48 ''20'':23 ''21'':46 ''34'':22 ''6'':50 ''8'':51 ''busi'':9 ''citi'':15 ''conceal'':43 ''concours'':7 ''deuteronomi'':24 ''either'':39 ''gate'':2 ''genesi'':21 ''ho'':27 ''indic'':31 ''judg'':17 ''justic'':12 ''king'':49 ''kinsman'':37 ''name'':34 ''note'':20 ''one'':30 ''orient'':14 ''place'':5 ''purpos'':42 ''samuel'':45 ''see'':16 ''unknown'':40', NULL, '236');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('237', '2', '237', NULL, 'Ramathaim-zophim may signify "the two hills 1 Samuel 9:11-13 of the watchmen," so called from its being a post from which the watchmen looked out. But since Zuph is the name of the head of the family, it is more probable that Zophin means the Zuphites, the sons of Zuph (see Zophai, 1 Chronicles 6:26), from whom the land about Ramah was called "the land of Zuph," 1 Samuel 9:5.There is reason to believe that Elkanah - an Ephrathite, or inhabitant of Bethlehem 1 Samuel 17:12; Ruth 1:2 and of the territory of the tribe of Ephraim 1 Kings 11:26 - the father of Samuel, represents the fifth generation of settlers in Canaan, and therefore that Samuel was born about 130 years after the entrance into Canaan - four complete generations, or 132 years - and about 40 years before David.', NULL, NULL, '''-13'':13 ''1'':9,57,73,89,94,105 ''11'':12,107 ''12'':92 ''130'':128 ''132'':139 ''17'':91 ''2'':95 ''26'':60,108 ''40'':143 ''5.there'':76 ''6'':59 ''9'':11,75 ''believ'':80 ''bethlehem'':88 ''born'':126 ''call'':18,68 ''canaan'':120,134 ''chronicl'':58 ''complet'':136 ''david'':146 ''elkanah'':82 ''entranc'':132 ''ephraim'':104 ''ephrathit'':84 ''famili'':41 ''father'':110 ''fifth'':115 ''four'':135 ''generat'':116,137 ''head'':38 ''hill'':8 ''inhabit'':86 ''king'':106 ''land'':64,70 ''look'':28 ''may'':4 ''mean'':48 ''name'':35 ''post'':23 ''probabl'':45 ''ramah'':66 ''ramathaim'':2 ''ramathaim-zophim'':1 ''reason'':78 ''repres'':113 ''ruth'':93 ''samuel'':10,74,90,112,124 ''see'':55 ''settler'':118 ''signifi'':5 ''sinc'':31 ''son'':52 ''territori'':99 ''therefor'':122 ''tribe'':102 ''two'':7 ''watchmen'':16,27 ''year'':129,140,144 ''zophai'':56 ''zophim'':3 ''zophin'':47 ''zuph'':32,54,72 ''zuphit'':50', NULL, '237');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('238', '2', '238', NULL, 'The song of Hannah is a prophetic Psalm. It is poetry. and it is prophecy. It takes its place by the side of the songs of Miriam, Deborah, and the Virgin Mary, as well as those of Moses, David, Hezekiah, and other Psalmists and prophets whose inspired odes have been preserved in the Bible. The special feature which these songs have in common is, that springing from, and in their first conception relating to, incidents in the lives of the individuals who composed them, they branch out into magnificent descriptions of the Kingdom and glory of Christ, and the triumphs of the Church, of which those incidents were providentially designed to be the types. The perception of this is essential to the understanding of Hannah''s song. Compare the marginal references throughout.', NULL, NULL, '''bibl'':54 ''branch'':86 ''christ'':97 ''church'':103 ''common'':63 ''compar'':128 ''compos'':83 ''concept'':72 ''david'':39 ''deborah'':28 ''descript'':90 ''design'':110 ''essenti'':120 ''featur'':57 ''first'':71 ''glori'':95 ''hannah'':4,125 ''hezekiah'':40 ''incid'':75,107 ''individu'':81 ''inspir'':47 ''kingdom'':93 ''live'':78 ''magnific'':89 ''margin'':130 ''mari'':32 ''miriam'':27 ''mose'':38 ''ode'':48 ''percept'':116 ''place'':19 ''poetri'':11 ''preserv'':51 ''propheci'':15 ''prophet'':7,45 ''providenti'':109 ''psalm'':8 ''psalmist'':43 ''refer'':131 ''relat'':73 ''side'':22 ''song'':2,25,60,127 ''special'':56 ''spring'':66 ''take'':17 ''throughout'':132 ''triumph'':100 ''type'':114 ''understand'':123 ''virgin'':31 ''well'':34 ''whose'':46', NULL, '238');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('242', '2', '242', NULL, 'The word for "priest" here is the same as that used for the priests of the true God; that for diviners is everywhere used of idolatrous or superstitious divining. Three modes of divination are described Ezekiel 21:21-22, by arrows, by teraphim, and by the entrails of beasts. (Compare Exodus 7:11; Daniel 2:2).', NULL, NULL, '''-22'':39 ''11'':53 ''2'':55,56 ''21'':37,38 ''7'':52 ''arrow'':41 ''beast'':49 ''compar'':50 ''daniel'':54 ''describ'':35 ''divin'':21,29,33 ''entrail'':47 ''everywher'':23 ''exodus'':51 ''ezekiel'':36 ''god'':18 ''idolatr'':26 ''mode'':31 ''priest'':4,14 ''superstiti'':28 ''teraphim'':43 ''three'':30 ''true'':17 ''use'':11,24 ''word'':2', NULL, '242');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('245', '2', '245', NULL, 'The genealogy of Saul is here given as far as Aphiah ("Abiah," 1 Chronicles 7:8), who was of the house of Becher the son of Benjamin Genesis 46:21. "Kish" 1 Chronicles 9:35-39 was the son of "Ner" the son of "Jehiel," (or, "Abiel" here and 1 Samuel 14:51), the first settler ("father," 1 Chronicles 9:35) at Gibeon, or Gibeah of Saul, and who married "Maachah," a daughter or granddaughter of Caleb. If so, it is obvious that the names of several generations are omitted between Kish and Abiel, and among them that from which the family of Matri 1 Samuel 10:21 was called.', NULL, NULL, '''-39'':36 ''1'':13,32,50,58,105 ''10'':107 ''14'':52 ''21'':30,108 ''35'':35,61 ''46'':29 ''51'':53 ''7'':15 ''8'':16 ''9'':34,60 ''abiah'':12 ''abiel'':47,94 ''among'':96 ''aphiah'':11 ''becher'':23 ''benjamin'':27 ''caleb'':77 ''call'':110 ''chronicl'':14,33,59 ''daughter'':73 ''famili'':102 ''far'':9 ''father'':57 ''first'':55 ''genealog'':2 ''generat'':88 ''genesi'':28 ''gibeah'':65 ''gibeon'':63 ''given'':7 ''granddaught'':75 ''hous'':21 ''jehiel'':45 ''kish'':31,92 ''maachah'':71 ''marri'':70 ''matri'':104 ''name'':85 ''ner'':41 ''obvious'':82 ''omit'':90 ''samuel'':51,106 ''saul'':4,67 ''settler'':56 ''sever'':87 ''son'':25,39,43', NULL, '245');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('246', '2', '246', NULL, 'Is it not because ... - Samuel answers Saul''s tacit or expressed wonder, by telling him why he did as he did. (Compare 1 Samuel 9:21.)', NULL, NULL, '''1'':23 ''21'':26 ''9'':25 ''answer'':6 ''compar'':22 ''express'':11 ''samuel'':5,24 ''saul'':7 ''tacit'':9 ''tell'':14 ''wonder'':12', NULL, '246');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('247', '2', '247', NULL, 'Nahash was king of the children of Ammon, as appears from 1 Samuel 12:12. He seems to have been connected with the family of David, since Abigail, David''s sister, was "the daughter (perhaps granddaughter) of Nahash" 2 Samuel 17:25; 1 Chronicles 2:16-17; and, perhaps, in consequence of this connection, he and his family were very friendly to David 2 Samuel 17:27.Jabesh-Gilead must have been re-populated after its destruction (see marginal reference). The Ammonites and Moabites resented the possession of Gilead by the Israelites Judges 10:6-18; 11.', NULL, NULL, '''-17'':47 ''-18'':96 ''1'':12,43 ''10'':94 ''11'':97 ''12'':14,15 ''16'':46 ''17'':41,66 ''2'':39,45,64 ''25'':42 ''27.jabesh'':67 ''6'':95 ''abigail'':28 ''ammon'':8 ''ammonit'':82 ''appear'':10 ''children'':6 ''chronicl'':44 ''connect'':21,54 ''consequ'':51 ''daughter'':34 ''david'':26,29,63 ''destruct'':77 ''famili'':24,58 ''friend'':61 ''gilead'':68,89 ''granddaught'':36 ''israelit'':92 ''judg'':93 ''king'':3 ''margin'':79 ''moabit'':84 ''must'':69 ''nahash'':1,38 ''perhap'':35,49 ''popul'':74 ''possess'':87 ''re'':73 ''re-popul'':72 ''refer'':80 ''resent'':85 ''samuel'':13,40,65 ''see'':78 ''seem'':17 ''sinc'':27 ''sister'':31', NULL, '247');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('248', '2', '248', NULL, 'My sons are with you - Possibly, however, a tinge of mortified feeling at the rejection of himself and his family, mixed with a desire to recommend his sons to the favor and goodwill of the nation, is at the bottom of this mention of them.', NULL, NULL, '''bottom'':40 ''desir'':24 ''famili'':20 ''favor'':31 ''feel'':12 ''goodwil'':33 ''howev'':7 ''mention'':43 ''mix'':21 ''mortifi'':11 ''nation'':36 ''possibl'':6 ''recommend'':26 ''reject'':15 ''son'':2,28 ''ting'':9', NULL, '248');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('249', '2', '249', NULL, 'The text of this verse, omitted by the Septuagint, is held to be corrupt, and the numerals denoting Saul''s age at his accession as well as the duration of his reign, are thought to be omitted or faulty. Saul may have been about 30 at his accession, and have reigned some 32 years, since we know that his grandson Mephibosheth was five years old at Saul''s death 2 Samuel 4:4; and 32 added to the seven and a half years between the death of Saul and that of Ishbosheth, makes up the 40 years assigned to Saul''s dynasty in Acts 13:21. Neither is there any clue to the interval of time between the events recorded in the preceding chapter, and those which follow in this and succeeding chapters. But the appearance of Jonathan as a warrior 1 Samuel 13:2 compared with the mention of Saul as "a young man" 1 Samuel 9:2, implies an interval of not less than ten or fifteen years, perhaps more. The object of the historian is to prepare the way for the history of David''s reign. He therefore passes at once to that incident in Saul''s reign, which led to his rejection by God, as recorded in 1 Samuel 13:13-14.', NULL, NULL, '''-14'':216 ''1'':142,156,212 ''13'':105,144,214,215 ''2'':70,145,159 ''21'':106 ''30'':45 ''32'':53,75 ''4'':72,73 ''40'':96 ''9'':158 ''access'':24,48 ''act'':104 ''add'':76 ''age'':21 ''appear'':136 ''assign'':98 ''chapter'':124,133 ''clue'':111 ''compar'':146 ''corrupt'':14 ''david'':187 ''death'':69,86 ''denot'':18 ''durat'':29 ''dynasti'':102 ''event'':119 ''faulti'':39 ''fifteen'':169 ''five'':63 ''follow'':128 ''god'':208 ''grandson'':60 ''half'':82 ''held'':11 ''histori'':185 ''historian'':177 ''impli'':160 ''incid'':197 ''interv'':114,162 ''ishbosheth'':92 ''jonathan'':138 ''know'':57 ''led'':203 ''less'':165 ''make'':93 ''man'':155 ''may'':41 ''mention'':149 ''mephibosheth'':61 ''neither'':107 ''numer'':17 ''object'':174 ''old'':65 ''omit'':6,37 ''pass'':192 ''perhap'':171 ''preced'':123 ''prepar'':180 ''record'':120,210 ''reign'':32,51,189,201 ''reject'':206 ''samuel'':71,143,157,213 ''saul'':19,40,67,88,100,151,199 ''septuagint'':9 ''seven'':79 ''sinc'':55 ''succeed'':132 ''ten'':167 ''text'':2 ''therefor'':191 ''thought'':34 ''time'':116 ''vers'':5 ''warrior'':141 ''way'':182 ''well'':26 ''year'':54,64,83,97,170 ''young'':154', NULL, '249');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('251', '2', '251', NULL, 'Compare the marginal references. It appears 1 Samuel 14:48 that this expedition against Amalek was not made without fresh provocation. Probably some incursion similar to that described in 1 Samuel 30 was made by them upon the south country at a time when they thought the Israelites were weakened by their contests with the Philistines.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':7,30 ''14'':9 ''30'':32 ''48'':10 ''amalek'':15 ''appear'':6 ''compar'':1 ''contest'':53 ''countri'':40 ''describ'':28 ''expedit'':13 ''fresh'':20 ''incurs'':24 ''israelit'':48 ''made'':18,34 ''margin'':3 ''philistin'':56 ''probabl'':22 ''provoc'':21 ''refer'':4 ''samuel'':8,31 ''similar'':25 ''south'':39 ''thought'':46 ''time'':43 ''upon'':37 ''weaken'':50 ''without'':19', NULL, '251');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('252', '2', '252', NULL, 'It was the purpose of God that David should be anointed at this time as Saul''s successor, and as the ancestor and the type of His Christ. It was not the purpose of God that Samuel should stir up a civil war, by setting up David as Saul''s rival. Secrecy, therefore, was a necessary part of the transaction. But secrecy and concealment are not the same as duplicity and falsehood. Concealment of a good purpose, for a good purpose, is clearly justifiable. There is therefore nothing in the least inconsistent with truth in the occurrence here related. Compare Exodus 7:16; Exodus 8:1; Exodus 9:13.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':106 ''13'':109 ''16'':103 ''7'':102 ''8'':105 ''9'':108 ''ancestor'':22 ''anoint'':11 ''christ'':28 ''civil'':42 ''clear'':83 ''compar'':100 ''conceal'':64,73 ''david'':8,47 ''duplic'':70 ''exodus'':101,104,107 ''falsehood'':72 ''god'':6,35 ''good'':76,80 ''inconsist'':92 ''justifi'':84 ''least'':91 ''necessari'':56 ''noth'':88 ''occurr'':97 ''part'':57 ''purpos'':4,33,77,81 ''relat'':99 ''rival'':51 ''samuel'':37 ''saul'':16,49 ''secreci'':52,62 ''set'':45 ''stir'':39 ''successor'':18 ''therefor'':53,87 ''time'':14 ''transact'':60 ''truth'':94 ''type'':25 ''war'':43', NULL, '252');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('253', '2', '253', NULL, 'The narrative reverts to the Philistine wars 1 Samuel 14:52; the other introductory details concerning Saul''s rejection, and David''s introduction upon the stage of the history, having been disposed of in the intermediate chapters.Shochoh which belongeth to Judah - See the marginal reference which places Shochoh and Azekah in the "Shephelah" or maritime plain, and 2 Chronicles 28:18, "Shochoh" now "Shuweikeh," "nine miles from Eleutheropolis," Jerome.Ephes-dammim - Called "Happas-dammim" (Pas-dammim, 1 Chronicles 11:13), "the end of bloodshed," now "Damun," about 4 miles northeast of Shuweikeh.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':8,78 ''11'':80 ''13'':81 ''14'':10 ''18'':61 ''2'':58 ''28'':60 ''4'':89 ''52'':11 ''azekah'':50 ''belongeth'':39 ''bloodsh'':85 ''call'':71 ''chapters.shochoh'':37 ''chronicl'':59,79 ''concern'':16 ''dammim'':70,74,77 ''damun'':87 ''david'':21 ''detail'':15 ''dispos'':32 ''eleutheropoli'':68 ''end'':83 ''happa'':73 ''happas-dammim'':72 ''histori'':29 ''intermedi'':36 ''introduct'':23 ''introductori'':14 ''jerome.ephes'':69 ''judah'':41 ''margin'':44 ''maritim'':55 ''mile'':66,90 ''narrat'':2 ''nine'':65 ''northeast'':91 ''pas'':76 ''pas-dammim'':75 ''philistin'':6 ''place'':47 ''plain'':56 ''refer'':45 ''reject'':19 ''revert'':3 ''samuel'':9 ''saul'':17 ''see'':42 ''shephelah'':53 ''shochoh'':48,62 ''shuweikeh'':64,93 ''stage'':26 ''upon'':24 ''war'':7', NULL, '253');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('254', '2', '254', NULL, 'Was knit with the soul of David - The same forcible phrase occurs of Jacob''s love for Benjamin (marginal reference). Jonathan''s truly heroic character is shown in this generous love of David, and admiration of his great deed.', NULL, NULL, '''admir'':35 ''benjamin'':18 ''charact'':25 ''david'':7,33 ''deed'':39 ''forcibl'':10 ''generous'':30 ''great'':38 ''heroic'':24 ''jacob'':14 ''jonathan'':21 ''knit'':2 ''love'':16,31 ''margin'':19 ''occur'':12 ''phrase'':11 ''refer'':20 ''shown'':27 ''soul'':5 ''truli'':23', NULL, '254');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('255', '2', '255', NULL, 'David fled - This was the beginning of David''s life as a fugitive and outcast, though for no "offence or fauIt" of his (Psalm 59:3, Prayer Book Version).', NULL, NULL, '''3'':26 ''59'':25 ''begin'':6 ''book'':28 ''david'':1,8 ''fauit'':21 ''fled'':2 ''fugit'':13 ''life'':10 ''offenc'':19 ''outcast'':15 ''prayer'':27 ''psalm'':24 ''though'':16 ''version'':29', NULL, '255');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('256', '2', '256', NULL, 'While Saul was under the constraining influence of the spirit of prophecy, David escaped from Naioth, and, probably by Samuel''s advice, returned to Saul''s court to commune with Jonathan. Nothing could be a better evidence of his innocence than thus putting himself in Jonathan''s power. Perhaps something passed between Samuel and Saul on the subject, since it appears from 1 Samuel 20:5, 1 Samuel 20:25, 1 Samuel 20:27, that Saul expected David at the feast of the new moon.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':63,67,71 ''20'':65,69,73 ''25'':70 ''27'':74 ''5'':66 ''advic'':22 ''appear'':61 ''better'':36 ''commune'':29 ''constrain'':6 ''could'':33 ''court'':27 ''david'':13,78 ''escap'':14 ''evid'':37 ''expect'':77 ''feast'':81 ''influenc'':7 ''innoc'':40 ''jonathan'':31,46 ''moon'':85 ''naioth'':16 ''new'':84 ''noth'':32 ''pass'':51 ''perhap'':49 ''power'':48 ''probabl'':18 ''propheci'':12 ''put'':43 ''return'':23 ''samuel'':20,53,64,68,72 ''saul'':2,25,55,76 ''sinc'':59 ''someth'':50 ''spirit'':10 ''subject'':58 ''thus'':42', NULL, '256');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('257', '2', '257', NULL, 'Nob was a city of the priests, the high priest resided there, and the tabernacle was pitched there 1 Samuel 21:4, 1 Samuel 21:6,1 Samuel 21:9; 1 Samuel 22:10. It was situated on the road from the north to Jerusalem, near Anathoth, and within sight of the holy city Isaiah 10:32; Nehemiah 11:32. But the site has not been identified with certainty.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':19,23,27,31 ''10'':34,56 ''11'':59 ''21'':21,25,29 ''22'':33 ''32'':57,60 ''4'':22 ''6'':26 ''9'':30 ''anathoth'':47 ''certainti'':69 ''citi'':4,54 ''high'':9 ''holi'':53 ''identifi'':67 ''isaiah'':55 ''jerusalem'':45 ''near'':46 ''nehemiah'':58 ''nob'':1 ''north'':43 ''pitch'':17 ''priest'':7,10 ''resid'':11 ''road'':40 ''samuel'':20,24,28,32 ''sight'':50 ''site'':63 ''situat'':37 ''tabernacl'':15 ''within'':49', NULL, '257');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('258', '2', '258', NULL, 'To the cave Adullam - Or rather "of Adullam." Adullam was the name of a town of Judah in the "Shephelah," not far from Bethlehem, and below it. Innumerable caverns, one nearly 100 feet long, are excavated in the soft limestone hills in the neighborhood of Beit-Jibrin. (The cave is placed by Ganneau and Conder on the hill (500 feet high) over ''Aid el Ma or Miyeh.) David''s brethren and kinsmen joined him partly from sympathy with him, and partly because their own lives were in jeopardy front Saul''s furious enmity.', NULL, NULL, '''100'':32 ''500'':60 ''adullam'':4,8,9 ''aid'':64 ''beit'':47 ''beit-jibrin'':46 ''bethlehem'':24 ''brethren'':71 ''cave'':3,50 ''cavern'':29 ''conder'':56 ''david'':69 ''el'':65 ''enmiti'':94 ''excav'':36 ''far'':22 ''feet'':33,61 ''front'':90 ''furious'':93 ''ganneau'':54 ''high'':62 ''hill'':41,59 ''innumer'':28 ''jeopardi'':89 ''jibrin'':48 ''join'':74 ''judah'':17 ''kinsmen'':73 ''limeston'':40 ''live'':86 ''long'':34 ''ma'':66 ''miyeh'':68 ''name'':12 ''near'':31 ''neighborhood'':44 ''one'':30 ''part'':76,82 ''place'':52 ''rather'':6 ''saul'':91 ''shephelah'':20 ''soft'':39 ''sympathi'':78 ''town'':15', NULL, '258');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('259', '2', '259', NULL, 'David''s growing importance, fugitive as he was, is marked by this appeal to him for deliverance from the Philistines. The "threshing floors" were the natural objects of plunder Judges 6:11. Keilah was in the Shephelah (marginal reference), probably close to the Philistine border, but its site is uncertain.Joshua 2:11 Samuel Joshua 23:1  PP1', NULL, NULL, '''1'':56 ''11'':32,52 ''2'':51 ''23'':55 ''6'':31 ''appeal'':13 ''border'':45 ''close'':41 ''david'':1 ''deliver'':17 ''floor'':23 ''fugit'':5 ''grow'':3 ''import'':4 ''joshua'':54 ''judg'':30 ''keilah'':33 ''margin'':38 ''mark'':10 ''natur'':26 ''object'':27 ''philistin'':20,44 ''plunder'':29 ''pp1'':57 ''probabl'':40 ''refer'':39 ''samuel'':53 ''shephelah'':37 ''site'':48 ''thresh'':22 ''uncertain.joshua'':50', NULL, '259');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('260', '2', '260', NULL, 'Remained in the sides - Rather, "were in the sides of the cave dwelling or abiding there." Some of these caverns are very deep and spacious. Any one near the mouth of the cave would be visible, but those in the recesses would be quite in the dark and invisible, especially if the incident occurred at night. Psalm 67:1-7, according to the title, was composed on this occasion.', NULL, NULL, '''-7'':60 ''1'':59 ''67'':58 ''abid'':15 ''accord'':61 ''cave'':12,33 ''cavern'':20 ''compos'':66 ''dark'':47 ''deep'':23 ''dwell'':13 ''especi'':50 ''incid'':53 ''invis'':49 ''mouth'':30 ''near'':28 ''night'':56 ''occas'':69 ''occur'':54 ''one'':27 ''psalm'':57 ''quit'':44 ''rather'':5 ''recess'':41 ''remain'':1 ''side'':4,9 ''spacious'':25 ''titl'':64 ''visibl'':36 ''would'':34,42', NULL, '260');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('261', '2', '261', NULL, 'In his house at Ramah - Probably in the court or garden attached to his dwelling-house. (Compare 2 Chronicles 33:20; 2 Kings 21:18; John 19:41.)The wilderness of Paran - The Septuagint has the far more probable reading "Maon." The wilderness of Paran lay far off to the south, on the borders of the wilderness of Sinai Numbers 10:12; 1 Kings 11:18, whereas the following verse 1 Samuel 25:2 shows that the scene is laid in the immediate neighborhood of Maon. If, however, Paran be the true reading, we must suppose that in a wide sense the wilderness of Paran extended all the way to the wilderness of Beersheba, and eastward to the mountains of Judah (marginal references).', NULL, NULL, '''1'':64,72 ''10'':62 ''11'':66 ''12'':63 ''18'':26,67 ''19'':28 ''2'':19,23,75 ''20'':22 ''21'':25 ''25'':74 ''33'':21 ''41'':29 ''attach'':12 ''beersheba'':115 ''border'':55 ''chronicl'':20 ''compar'':18 ''court'':9 ''dwell'':16 ''dwelling-hous'':15 ''eastward'':117 ''extend'':107 ''far'':38,48 ''follow'':70 ''garden'':11 ''hous'':3,17 ''howev'':89 ''immedi'':84 ''john'':27 ''judah'':122 ''king'':24,65 ''laid'':81 ''lay'':47 ''maon'':42,87 ''margin'':123 ''mountain'':120 ''must'':96 ''neighborhood'':85 ''number'':61 ''paran'':33,46,90,106 ''probabl'':6,40 ''ramah'':5 ''read'':41,94 ''refer'':124 ''samuel'':73 ''scene'':79 ''sens'':102 ''septuagint'':35 ''show'':76 ''sinai'':60 ''south'':52 ''suppos'':97 ''true'':93 ''vers'':71 ''way'':110 ''wherea'':68 ''wide'':101 ''wilder'':31,44,58,104,113', NULL, '261');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('262', '2', '262', NULL, 'The incident related in this chapter of the meeting between Saul and David bears a strong general resemblance to that recorded in 1 Samuel 24, and is of a nature unlikely to have occurred more than once. Existing discrepancies are explained by the supposition that one narrative relates fully some incidents on which the other is silent. On the whole the most probable conclusion is that the two narratives relate to one and the same event. (Compare the two narratives of the Creation, Genesis 1; Genesis 2:4 ff; the two narratives of David''s war, 2 Samuel 8; and 2 Samuel 10; and those of the death of Ahaziah, 2 Kings 9:27 ff; and 2 Chronicles 22:9.)', NULL, NULL, '''1'':23,85 ''10'':103 ''2'':87,97,101,111,117 ''22'':119 ''24'':25 ''27'':114 ''4'':88 ''8'':99 ''9'':113,120 ''ahaziah'':110 ''bear'':14 ''chapter'':6 ''chronicl'':118 ''compar'':77 ''conclus'':64 ''creation'':83 ''david'':13,94 ''death'':108 ''discrep'':39 ''event'':76 ''exist'':38 ''explain'':41 ''ff'':89,115 ''fulli'':49 ''general'':17 ''genesi'':84,86 ''incid'':2,51 ''king'':112 ''meet'':9 ''narrat'':47,69,80,92 ''natur'':30 ''occur'':34 ''one'':46,72 ''probabl'':63 ''record'':21 ''relat'':3,48,70 ''resembl'':18 ''samuel'':24,98,102 ''saul'':11 ''silent'':57 ''strong'':16 ''supposit'':44 ''two'':68,79,91 ''unlik'':31 ''war'':96 ''whole'':60', NULL, '262');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('263', '2', '263', NULL, 'David, with characteristic Oriental subtlety (compare 1 Samuel 21:2), suggests as a reason for leaving Gath that his presence was burdensome and expensive to the king. His real motive was to be more out of the way of observation and control, so as to act the part of an enemy of Saul, without really lifting up his hand against him and his own countrymen of Israel.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':7 ''2'':10 ''21'':9 ''act'':46 ''burdensom'':22 ''characterist'':3 ''compar'':6 ''control'':42 ''countrymen'':65 ''david'':1 ''enemi'':51 ''expens'':24 ''gath'':17 ''hand'':59 ''israel'':67 ''king'':27 ''leav'':16 ''lift'':56 ''motiv'':30 ''observ'':40 ''orient'':4 ''part'':48 ''presenc'':20 ''real'':29 ''realli'':55 ''reason'':14 ''samuel'':8 ''saul'':53 ''subtleti'':5 ''suggest'':11 ''way'':38 ''without'':54', NULL, '263');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('264', '2', '264', NULL, 'Thou shalt know ... - David dissembled (compare also 1 Samuel 29:8), hoping, no doubt, that something would happen to prevent his fighting against his king and country.Keeper of mine head - Captain of his bodyguard.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':8 ''29'':10 ''8'':11 ''also'':7 ''bodyguard'':34 ''captain'':31 ''compar'':6 ''country.keeper'':27 ''david'':4 ''dissembl'':5 ''doubt'':14 ''fight'':22 ''happen'':18 ''head'':30 ''hope'':12 ''king'':25 ''know'':3 ''mine'':29 ''prevent'':20 ''samuel'':9 ''shalt'':2 ''someth'':16 ''thou'':1 ''would'':17', NULL, '264');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('265', '2', '265', NULL, 'A fountain - Probably, the fine spring Ain-Jalud. It is impossible to say what the special circumstances were which led to the struggle between Israel and the Philistines taking place so far north as the plain of Jezreel. Possibly it was connected with some movements of the Aramaic tribes to the north of Palestine. See 2 Samuel 8.', NULL, NULL, '''2'':57 ''8'':59 ''ain'':8 ''ain-jalud'':7 ''arama'':49 ''circumst'':18 ''connect'':43 ''far'':33 ''fine'':5 ''fountain'':2 ''imposs'':12 ''israel'':26 ''jalud'':9 ''jezreel'':39 ''led'':21 ''movement'':46 ''north'':34,53 ''palestin'':55 ''philistin'':29 ''place'':31 ''plain'':37 ''possibl'':40 ''probabl'':3 ''samuel'':58 ''say'':14 ''see'':56 ''special'':17 ''spring'':6 ''struggl'':24 ''take'':30 ''tribe'':50', NULL, '265');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('266', '2', '266', NULL, 'On the third day - This indicates that Aphek was three days'' march from Ziklag, say about 50 miles, which agrees very well with the probable situation of Aphek (1 Samuel 4:1 note). From Ziklag to Shunem would not be less than 80 or 90 miles.The Amalekites, in retaliation of David''s raids 1 Samuel 27:8-9, invaded "the south" of Judah Joshua 15:21; but owing to the absence of all the men with David there was no resistance, and consequently the women and children were carried off as prey, and uninjured.', NULL, NULL, '''-9'':58 ''1'':29,32,54 ''15'':65 ''21'':66 ''27'':56 ''4'':31 ''50'':17 ''8'':57 ''80'':43 ''90'':45 ''absenc'':71 ''agre'':20 ''amalekit'':47 ''aphek'':8,28 ''carri'':89 ''children'':87 ''consequ'':83 ''david'':51,77 ''day'':4,11 ''indic'':6 ''invad'':59 ''joshua'':64 ''judah'':63 ''less'':41 ''march'':12 ''men'':75 ''mile'':18 ''miles.the'':46 ''note'':33 ''owe'':68 ''prey'':92 ''probabl'':25 ''raid'':53 ''resist'':81 ''retali'':49 ''samuel'':30,55 ''say'':15 ''shunem'':37 ''situat'':26 ''south'':61 ''third'':3 ''three'':10 ''uninjur'':94 ''well'':22 ''women'':85 ''would'':38 ''ziklag'':14,35', NULL, '266');
INSERT INTO sections (id, source_id, ordinal, heading, body, period_start_year, period_end_year, tsv, source_url, unit_ordinal) VALUES ('267', '2', '267', NULL, 'He was sore wounded - Better, "he was sore afraid" (compare Deuteronomy 2:25). Saul''s fear is explained in 1 Samuel 31:4.', NULL, NULL, '''1'':20 ''2'':12 ''25'':13 ''31'':22 ''4'':23 ''afraid'':9 ''better'':5 ''compar'':10 ''deuteronomi'':11 ''explain'':18 ''fear'':16 ''samuel'':21 ''saul'':14 ''sore'':3,8 ''wound'':4', NULL, '267');
